USBC-2026-0628-0003,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-jvy9-mzkf,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes are long overdue, should have been enacted in 2020.  The racial questions in particular are irrelevant to modern society as the courts have finally moved to mandate race-neutral behaviors by governments and their agents.  Similarly - as noncitizens are disqualified from most aspects of social duties and benefits - they should not be an element to increase the voting/benefits powers of any jurisdiction.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0004,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-k50q-pfca,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Demographic information, including race and ethnicity, is vital to ensure that the equal protections of the voting rights act are enacted during the apportionment process. Further, the information is important to measure and reduce gerrymandering in states where such practices are illegal. Demographic information is indeed a core responsibility of the apportionment process. Claiming otherwise is disingenuous. <br/><br/>Demographic information also serves many federal, state, and local research, funding, and grant purposes that would be jeopardized without reliable information. I can appreciate wanting to shorten the questionnaires, but demographic information is not the place to do so. There are other, more discretionary questions that could be removed to shorten the questionnaires without putting the most basic data sets at risk.<br/><br/>As an AICP Planner, I know how important this information is to small and large communities alike. It is both necessary for apportionment and relied upon by the country at large for numerous other purposes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0005,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-kkza-g77v,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&quot;If someone is not lawfully recognized as being here, they are not legal &quot;inhabitants&quot; and should be excluded from the count&quot;<br/>Having no provision of law in establishing a countable status precludes such populations from being counted in the US Census: so says US enumerated law, so tells US history.<br/>The stuborn rebelliousness of trying to reinstutute a former and ever rejected standard has worked distructively agsinst US interests, US citizen populations interests, and affected peoples interests.<br/>As the USA exists in representation of the interests of people within the context of rule of law (her own long standing and carefully devoloped &amp; Gods unchanging and unchallengable), it behoves her to return/reaffirm her status as/unto lawful order in the interests of humanity. Unlawfully present foreign nationals should not be counted in the US census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0006,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-lled-uduj,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen that is concerned with our national debt, spending and fair representation.<br/><br/>Please add this change to the Census.<br/><br/>I am in favor of the proposed changes on citizenship and residence.<br/>These changes will help address these issues.<br/> ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0008,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-n76q-d7ua,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes are long overdue.  Non-citizens, being disqualified from most aspects of social duties and benefits, should NOT be used to increase the voting or benefits power of any jurisdiction.   Neither should race be included in the census, as the courts have moved to mandate race-neutral behavior by the government and its agents.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0007,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-mzqv-mkqn,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I write in opposition to the changes to the Census that are contained in this proposed rule. Specifically, I object to the limitations on Residence Criteria and the restrictions on the collection of demographic information in the short-form Census.<br/>1. The U.S. Constitution requires the enumeration of all persons resident in the United States. Historically, the criteria for inclusion as a resident has included immigrants legally resident in the United States, regardless of whether they hold a valid green card. Any attempt to change this criteria is in contravention of the U.S. Constitution. There is no valid purpose for the proposed redefinition of residency, and one must conclude that the proposed changed is motivated by animus toward those immigrants who are in the country legally but have pending green card applications. I object.<br/>2. Similarly, I can only conclude that the restrictions on the collection of demographic information on the short form is motivated by animus toward certain groups of persons living in the United States. The proposed justification of wanting to protect against disclosure of personally-identifiable information is specious; to date, there is no evidence that such information has been disclosed. Moreover, there are many existing ways to safeguard against disclosure of this information, and there may be additional means by the time the processes for data collection, management, and reporting for future censuses are established. I object.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0009,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-nn50-cuhl,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This proposal is a direct violation of the U.S Constitution!!  The Constitution states the &quot;whole Number of free Persons&quot; and &quot;three fifths of all other Persons&quot; (the Three-Fifths Compromise), while completely excluding &quot;Indians not taxed&quot;. shall be counted.  Note two things: 1) the founders used the word &quot;persons&quot; NOT citizens even though it is obvious the difference was known to them at the time, so they did indeed mean to count non-citizens;  2) They used the word &quot;Indians&quot; which clearly indicates they knew who (which human beings) they did not want counted.  Taken together it is obvious non-citizens were meant to be counted.  The text is simple and clear.  Weird, obfuscating arguments need to simply be ignored.  The text, meaning, and intent of the Constitution is CLEAR!!<br/>THROW THIS PROPOSAL OUT!!   Thank you, Dan Beausoleil",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0010,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-nn6c-p5yu,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The 14th amendment was never meant to be used for millions of non citizens from everywhere in the world here illegally year after a year - it was meant for the sons daughters and grandchildren of slaves so they could be represented. Since slavery is at least two generations removed it has no purpose anymore except to illegally pad representation to non citizens who should not have a say in our congressional legislative process and certain political parties have used this loop hole in representation to import in congressional representation by the millions. Congressional representation should be reserved for citizens and not to pad funding for sanctuary states or maliciously elevate populations for nefarious political motives. Repeal the clause that allows non-citizens to be counted in the cenus and count only American citizens.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0011,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-nwm2-zbd2,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I write in strong opposition to the proposed rule as a citizen and statistician. <br/><br/>The Census is the gold standard for counting every person and when they are. It was so important the founders established it alongside the post office as one of the two most important duties of government. <br/><br/>The proposed rule makes a mockery of that sacred responsibility, inserting inappropriate questions about citizenship into a purely demographic process. Information about citizenship is sensitive, and better accounted for in properly rigorous, administrative contexts rather than being jammed into the Census count. It will function solely to depress the count of persons from racial and ethnic minority groups, for the purpose of advancing a political ambition towards illegal and unconstitutional ideology of white supremacy. <br/><br/>The proposed change has been telegraphed as an obvious attempt by a political faction to manipulate the Census for partisan advantage in Congressional appointment. This proposal approaches an organized, conscious conspiracy to subvert the will of the Founders, the public legitimacy of our democracy, and the ability of our republic to function. <br/><br/>It should be removed, and the public is owe a thorough account of who and what motivated its pproposal. <br/><br/>With outrage and grave concern,<br/>Kevin McGrath",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0013,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-pgma-fgbm,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Attached is a request for an extension on the comment period.,,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0013/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0012,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-p5ql-nced,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Comment of Matthew C. Kuhn, MSN, MPA, AGACNP-BC<br/>Docket USBC-2026-0628; RIN 0607-AA75<br/><br/>I oppose proposed Sec. 60.4(b) and urge the Bureau not to place a legal-status question on the short-form questionnaire. A detailed comment is attached.<br/><br/>I am a board-certified acute care nurse practitioner in critical care, previously a paramedic and flight nurse, licensed in North Carolina, Virginia, Georgia, Tennessee, and South Carolina, practicing largely in rural and community hospitals. I hold an MPA in public health and emergency management. I comment on what this rule would do to the population figures hospitals, EMS systems, and emergency managers use to plan.<br/><br/>1. The rule is framed as a targeted exclusion, but its mechanism produces a general undercount. The Bureau enumerates households. Roughly one in seven U.S. households with an immigrant member is mixed-status and contains citizens, most often citizen children. A household that fears an adverse status determination does not omit one member; it does not respond. The uncounted then include citizens and lawful permanent residents. The Bureau&#39;s own research predicted this differential decline in self-response. Under State Farm, the Bureau must confront that evidence; the preamble does not.<br/><br/>2. The rule is internally inconsistent. Part 70 removes questions on the premise that sensitive items depress response and that the criminal penalty under 13 U.S.C. 221 compounds the burden. Part 60 then invites comment on adding a question requiring respondents to declare immigration status to the federal government under that same penalty. The Bureau cannot rely on that reasoning for one part and disregard it for the other.<br/><br/>3. Section 60.4(b) sweeps far beyond the preamble&#39;s own rationale. The preamble grounds exclusion in allegiance, durable permission, and domicile, but the operative text excludes every foreign citizen who is not a lawful permanent resident: refugees and asylees the United States itself admitted, long-tenured TPS holders, DACA recipients, and H-1B physicians and nurses staffing the rural hospitals where I work. Answering the Bureau&#39;s invited question directly, several of these statuses are materially similar to LPR status on the Bureau&#39;s own criteria.<br/><br/>4. Administrative-record status determination will misfire on citizens. Linkage failure clusters among people with thin record footprints: the rural poor, the elderly, frequent movers, people with inconsistent name records. The rule states no accuracy standard, no error rate, no notice, and no correction or appeal mechanism. A citizen dropped by a failed match would never know.<br/><br/>5. The public health and emergency management costs are concrete, and they fall on everyone. In FY2017, 316 federal programs used census-derived data to distribute $1.504 trillion, including Medicaid, Title V, HRSA health center funding, HPSA and MUA designations, the Hospital Preparedness Program, and rural hospital programs. These formulas run on population denominators. Understate the denominator and capacity is planned short: beds, ventilators, PPE caches, blood products, ICU staffing. Disease incidence and vaccination coverage are computed on a wrong denominator, understating risk exactly when intervention matters. And cost lands where the count does not: under EMTALA every emergency department must screen and stabilize every person who presents, regardless of status, and public health must investigate a tuberculosis or measles case regardless of the patient&#39;s status. The obligation is universal; the funding denominator would not be. After Hurricane Helene, shelter capacity, water distribution, and strike team staging were planned against population figures. A flood does not check status.<br/><br/>6. The assurance at footnote 5 is not sufficient. It is a preamble statement, not a regulatory obligation. The enumeration is a single collection event, so depressed response degrades every downstream product. And ACS margins of error at tract and block-group geography are frequently too wide for operational use.<br/><br/>I ask the Bureau to: withdraw Sec. 60.4(b); decline to add a legal-status question to the short form; publish projected response-rate, undercount, and funding effects by county before finalizing; commit in regulatory text, not preamble, to producing a full resident-population count at block geography for all non-apportionment uses; and extend the comment period, which currently affords 33 days for a rule of this consequence.<br/><br/>The Bureau has stated its commitment to counting every person once, only once, and in the right place. I share that goal. My concern is that this mechanism will not produce a cleaner count but a smaller and less accurate one, in ways that fall on citizens in rural and safety-net communities as surely as on anyone else.<br/><br/>Respectfully submitted,<br/>Matthew C. Kuhn, MSN, MPA, AGACNP-BC<br/>Critical Care Nurse Practitioner<br/>Keystone Critical Care Consulting, Eagle Springs, North Carolina<br/>",,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0012/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0014,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-pgqd-a1oz,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Extension request from Census Funder Collaborative,,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0014/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0015,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-qe2l-fv7a,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My family comes from the Pickstown/Fort Randall Dam area of South Dakota. Reading a mid-1800s town history, I was struck to learn the town held Irish and English immigrants alongside families from New York, Wisconsin, and Missouri. We know this only because the census asked about people&#39;s origins. Eliminating the race and ethnicity questions would rob future generations of that same understanding of their own communities. These questions also underpin enforcement of anti-discrimination laws and the fair allocation of federal funding. I urge the Bureau to retain them, and to continue counting every person residing in the United States, as the 14th Amendment requires.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0016,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-rp21-7kb4,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"To Whom It May Concern,<br/><br/>I am writing to submit my formal public comment regarding the proposed regulations for the 2030 Decennial Census. I strongly urge the U.S. Census Bureau to update its criteria and include a mandatory question regarding legal citizenship status, with the ultimate goal of restricting the final apportionment count exclusively to citizens of the United States. <br/><br/>The current framework, which relies on interpreting the 14th Amendment&rsquo;s phrase &quot;counting the whole number of persons in each State&quot; to include everyone physically present regardless of legal status, does not reflect the modern realities of the United States. The framers of the Constitution and the authors of the 14th Amendment operated in a fundamentally different historical era and did not anticipate the scale or nature of modern illegal immigration. <br/><br/>When the foundational text was established, the intent was to account for the lawful population loyal to the nation. The framers could not have foreseen contemporary national security challenges, including the potential for hostile actors or subversives from various global regions entering the country unlawfully. Furthermore, the current system improperly counts economic migrants who enter the country to send remittances back to their home countries rather than to integrate into the American body politic.<br/><br/>As a matter of national sovereignty and fair representation, different times call for different measures. Including non-citizens in the census figures used for congressional apportionment dilutes the voting power and representation of actual legal U.S. citizens. For these reasons, the 2030 Census must formally distinguish legal status and restrict apportionment metrics strictly to citizens of the United States.<br/><br/>Sincerely,<br/><br/>A Concerned U.S. Citizen",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0017,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-rw79-6fpk,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This proposed regulation&#39;s contorted logic appeals to a peculiarly restrictive definition of &#39;person&#39;, and to John Adams&#39; distinction between &#39;Strangers&#39; and &#39;inhabitants&#39;.  The implied question is who qualifies as a &#39;Stranger&#39;.  The straightforward answer is that people who have made their homes in the United States are residents, whether legal or removable.  They are not mere visitors.  In the language of the proposed regulation, such people are indeed &quot;establishing a residence within a jurisdiction and an intent to remain there indefinitely&quot;, and therefore they must be counted.  The census exists not to count voters, but to count population; this is why children, people who lack capacity, and people who are not registered to vote are nevertheless counted.  And indeed states that disproportionately bear the public services burden of the United States&#39; broken immigration and removal system ought to receive corresponding weight, not only in terms of congressional representation but in terms of allotment of federal funds.  To quote the Census Bureau&#39;s own web site (https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial-census/about/why.html), the count &quot;helps communities get its fair share for schools, hospitals, roads, and public works.&quot;<br/><br/>It is for this very reason that no a legal-status question should be included on the decennial census questionnaire.  Because no illegal alien in their right mind would return such a self-incriminating questionnaire response, the result would be a gross undercounting of those residing in the United States illegally, and therefore a loss of services to those communities that most bear the burden.  Indeed, such probably is the intent of the authors of this proposed regulation!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0018,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-sr4w-e2vg,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Well here we go again another another constitutionally based right, article 1 section 2, being subverted by the GOP under fealty to their unstable genius leader. Birthright citizenship, and the states role in our elections are the first two other that come to my mind.as clear violation of all their oaths to protect and defend  the constitution. .    ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0021,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-usyb-h68w,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a US citizen, I do not favor any changes in the way our census is administered if it reduces the likelihood of fair treatment for ALL residents of our country. The US Constitution clearly states that EVERY person living in this country be counted and that should not change!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0019,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-tbmd-kuu5,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Fidel declared that racism was over in 1962. Somehow, it was only Black Cubans who were sent to die in Angola. <br/><br/>More importantly, the justification for removing questions about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation make very clear that adding a question about citizenship - or changing the non-citizen respondent section to state &quot;legal permanent resident&quot; - is not justified. This change would make the data a nightmare to use, and release. It also makes a whole lot harder to know how many non-citizens are residing in the United States, which would make a lot more difficult to know how many people you are trying to deport. The expected lack of response from households also increases the distortions in housing availability in the area. Furthermore, the definition restricts non-citizens respondents to only permanent residents, ignoring the wide array of temporary legal residents who are living and working in the area, and who may change their residency status later. Someone need not become a permanent resident to continue living in the United States for many years, on a wide array of legal visas. They just intend to one day go home. <br/><br/>It is also worth pointing out that in 1907 the Expatriation Act declared that a married woman&#39;s nationality was that of their husband. Women&#39;s citizenship had been recognized as derived from her husband as far back as 1804, but this act specifically de-naturalized married women, while having no effect on married men. At no point in history has our nation not counted women in the census, nor have women ever been excluded from the definition of &quot;inhabitants&quot; or &quot;persons&quot; for purposes of apportionment. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0023,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-vfwu-ylyp,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,It is insane and ignorant to say there is no longer value in collecting race and ethnicity information.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0024,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-vl7b-7wlc,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Based on the 14th Amendment, &#39;all persons&#39; means what it says or it doesn&#39;t. With a High Court of &#39;Originalists&#39;, this should be easy to interpret. Additionally, as this was enacted by Congress, an Executive Order alone is obviously insufficient to change plain meaning of the text.<br/><br/>I believe this is yet another method by which the Administration looks to &#39;bury its head in the sand&#39;. Yes we have an illegal immigrant problem; but not counting them then suddenly saying, &#39;Look, all our deportation efforts are working, just look at the numbers&#39;, doesn&#39;t seem like a stretch.<br/><br/>In conclusion, I am against this change, not only because it is clearly illegal, but it will disenfranchise many.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0020,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-ujuu-jfff,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"we pride ourselves as a land of freedom, of representation of those valuable lives that find refuge here like many of our ancestors. <br/><br/>This rule would clearly take away the representations of these peoples valuable and equal existences when we are determining how to achieve our goal for a land of life, liberty, and happiness. <br/><br/>How? So many laws and actions are based on taking into account the different population&#39;s demographics to know how to better serve the pot of people in that area. Getting rid of the information about them would overrepresent certain populations, likely to the detriment of those who would be under-represented. <br/><br/>This rule must be struck down in order to maintain what integrity we still hold as a country.<br/><br/>30 Days is not enough for a commenting session. Extend this as well, hear the voice of the people. <br/><br/>To claim this defends what the constitution stands for in the summary page is ridiculous. Being &quot;colorblind&quot; is a philosophy to overcome racism that has proven harmful and unproductive over and over again. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0022,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-vfej-vi7a,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Comment on Proposed Rule: Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions (Docket No. USBC-2026-0628)<br/><br/>To Whom It May Concern:<br/><br/>I respectfully oppose the proposed rule entitled Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions.<br/><br/>The decennial census is one of the most important functions of the federal government. Its legitimacy depends on producing a complete, accurate, and broadly trusted count of people residing in the United States. While I support efforts to improve efficiency, protect privacy, and enhance data quality, I believe several provisions of this proposal would undermine those objectives and create substantial legal, operational, and policy concerns.<br/><br/>1. The proposal departs from the longstanding practice of counting all residents.<br/><br/>For more than two centuries, the Census Bureau has counted persons residing in the United States regardless of citizenship status. The proposed exclusion of many categories of non-citizens from apportionment would represent a fundamental change in census practice and would likely generate extensive litigation, uncertainty, and public confusion.<br/><br/>Many non-citizens live, work, pay taxes, raise families, attend school, and participate in their communities for extended periods of time. Excluding them from apportionment would alter the representation of communities and states based on legal classifications rather than actual population.<br/><br/>2. Expanded use of administrative records raises significant privacy concerns.<br/><br/>The proposal contemplates increasing reliance on extensive federal, state, local, tribal, and commercial datasets.<br/><br/>Although the Census Bureau has a strong history of protecting confidential information, many residents may be concerned about the government&#39;s use of multiple administrative data sources to determine legal status, residence, or eligibility for inclusion in the census.<br/><br/>Maintaining public trust is critical to census participation. Policies perceived as expanding government surveillance or data integration may discourage response and reduce data quality.<br/><br/>3. Eliminating race and ethnicity questions would significantly reduce the value of census data.<br/><br/>Race and ethnicity data collected through the census have long served important governmental, academic, business, and community purposes.<br/><br/>This data is used to:<br/><br/>Monitor compliance with civil rights laws.<br/>Evaluate disparities in health, education, housing, and employment.<br/>Support Voting Rights Act implementation.<br/>Inform public policy, research, and resource allocation.<br/>Help local governments and community organizations understand demographic change.<br/><br/>Removing race and ethnicity questions from the decennial census would eliminate one of the nation&#39;s most important and consistent demographic datasets.<br/><br/>4. The proposal would reduce transparency and comparability over time.<br/><br/>One of the greatest strengths of census data is the ability to compare results across decades.<br/><br/>Concentrating demographic information in administrative records may create new data quality issues.<br/><br/>The Census Bureau&#39;s mission is to count every person once, only once, and in the right place. While modernization and efficiency are worthwhile goals, the proposed rule would introduce significant legal uncertainty, operational complexity, privacy concerns, and potential reductions in data quality and public trust.<br/><br/>For these reasons, I urge the Census Bureau to withdraw or substantially revise the proposed rule and continue to rely on broadly inclusive counting practices, established residence criteria, and the collection of demographic data necessary to support civil rights enforcement, public policy, research, and informed decision-making.<br/><br/>Thank you for the opportunity to comment.<br/><br/>Respectfully submitted,<br/>Elizabeth A.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0027,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-x37a-6fbg,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed rule change excluding undocumented immigrants and those with temporary legal status from the census. When the original rules were written I don&#39;t believe anyone would have imagined officials protecting undocumented immigrants over citizens and then their state benefiting from inflated numbers through Federal dollars.<br/><br/>I also support eliminating questions on race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation.<br/> ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0025,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-vloi-qao4,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is a complete and total violation of the US Constitution. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0026,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-w68j-8h4e,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen interested in the true and honest facts of life in the United States. Like our founders, I believe that the government should gather data on its residents based on taxation status. Because all adults are subject to taxation, all adults should be included in the census. No changes should be made to the current form of the census questions. Data on race is necessary to prevent discrimination and to research and prevent social and civic inequality. Data on immigration status is not necessary to obtain and should not be obtained. This is necessary to protect immigrants from unlawful but highly likely governmental harassment and obsession. Thus I reject all elements of this proposal. <br/><br/>&ldquo; Representatives shall be apportioned among the several States according to their respective numbers, counting the whole number of persons in each State, excluding Indians *not taxed*.&rdquo; Clearly taxation is the key element for counting people. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0029,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-xi9m-7ca9,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I strongly oppose the proposed changes to the way the dicennial Census collects information and counts residents of the United States. It is not for the Census Bureau to decide who should be counted and who may be excluded from the count. It is in everyone&#39;s interest--including corporations who depend on accurate information to plan their investments--to have full and accurate data on every single person who resides in the country. It is also dangerous when cities, counties, and government agencies lack true information on where folks within their jurisdictions live. The Census is not a political football that should be played to the benefit of one party or the other. Please ensure that the Census Bureau counts every person regardless of their immigration status, and gathers every bit of information that is has always gathered about the people who live in America. Demographic information about race, ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, etc. are also extremely valuable pieces of data relied upon by businesses and government entities alike. Without having the fullest amount of data available every ten years, the United States and its economic and government sectors will be operating blind, to the detriment of all. Please reject these changes in their entirety. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0028,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-xdvz-r6te,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I submit this comment in response to the Census Bureau&rsquo;s proposed rule that would limit the apportionment population to U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents and otherwise redefine who counts as a &ldquo;person&rdquo; for census apportionment purposes. I am an individual member of the public with a strong interest in constitutional governance, accurate representation, and the lawful administration of the decennial census. My comment addresses the rule&rsquo;s consistency with the U.S. Constitution and controlling Supreme Court precedent.<br/><br/>The proposed rule&rsquo;s core premise&mdash;that individuals who are not U.S. citizens or green card holders are not &ldquo;persons&rdquo; to be counted for apportionment under Article I, &sect;2 and the Fourteenth Amendment&mdash;is inconsistent with: (1) the Constitution&rsquo;s text (&ldquo;whole number of persons in each State&rdquo;); (2) Supreme Court precedent defining &ldquo;person&rdquo; to include all individuals within U.S. jurisdiction, regardless of citizenship or immigration status; and (3) more than two centuries of uniform practice implementing the Census Clause by counting all residents at their usual residence. For these reasons, the rule should be withdrawn or revised to conform to the Constitution and binding judicial authority.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0031,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-yehr-ymo2,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Stop changing our documents.  Article I, Section 2 of the Constitution requires an actual count (enumeration) of the U.S. population every 10 years. Section 2 of the 14th Amendment updated the census rules in 1868 to end the &quot;Three-Fifths Clause&quot; and mandated that the census must count the &quot;whole number of persons in each state.&quot;  That is quite clear!  What Trump is proposing is unconstitutional.  The United States does not exist under the fantasy world of our impaired president.  The United States exists under the power of the people and the people&#39;s constitution!  So stop it already!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0033,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-2ju4-5t0y,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed rule relating to residence criteria and demographic questions in the decennial census would have a negative impact on my work as a librarian at a university library who supports data, economics, and finance subject areas.  It would also negatively impact the education and research of students and faculty members who I support in my work.  The decennial census is one of the most frequently used data resources in my own work and in the work and education of students and faculty who I support.  The proposed rule would make the decennial census data less useful because going forward, census population counts would not be comparable, before and after the rule on residence criteria was implemented.  Since much research involving census data looks across time, and since population is a common variable for understanding underlying conditions, this would hamper future research.  Race and ethnicity are also frequent underlying variables that help researchers control for general trends and identify better estimates for the variables they are focused on.  The proposed rule for demographic questions would lead to less precise research and statistical estimates in research using census data going forward. Overall, the proposed rule would make decennial census data less useful in my work and in the education and research of those I support at the university.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0030,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-xqdr-4edf,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I oppose a U.S. Census Bureau rule proposed on September 10, 2026, that would exclude millions of people from the population count and end the collection of demographic data on race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation. Under the proposal, only U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents would count toward apportionment, excluding refugees, asylees, and people with Temporary Protected Status. Section Two of the Fourteenth Amendment states clearly that &ldquo;Representatives shall be apportioned among the several States according to their respective numbers, counting the whole number of persons in each State&hellip;&rdquo; While the drafters of the Fourteenth Amendment made an exclusion for &#39;Indians not taxed, &#39; there was never any mention of excluding someone based on citizenship or immigration status.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0032,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtv-zs9w-487n,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Re: Docket USBC-2026-0628<br/>Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions<br/><br/>I am writing as a resident of Morganfield, a small community in rural western Kentucky, to oppose the proposed residence criteria and restrictions on demographic questions.<br/><br/>I am disabled, receive Social Security Disability Insurance and Medicare, and live on a limited fixed income. I depend on public institutions making decisions using complete, accurate information about the people and communities they serve. Census policy may sound abstract, but representation, public planning, civil-rights enforcement, and the distribution of public resources have real consequences for people like me.<br/><br/>I oppose excluding undocumented residents and most temporary lawful residents from the population used for congressional apportionment. The Constitution&rsquo;s Fourteenth Amendment requires representatives to be apportioned according to the &ldquo;whole number of persons in each State,&rdquo; not only citizens or lawful permanent residents. Every person living in a community affects its schools, hospitals, emergency services, roads, housing needs, and economy. Their presence creates real responsibilities for state and local governments regardless of immigration status.<br/><br/>Excluding residents from the apportionment population would make some communities politically smaller on paper than they are in reality. That could reduce their representation even though public agencies must still serve the people who were excluded. It could also discourage participation in the census among mixed-status families and others who fear how government information may be used, making an accurate count more difficult.<br/><br/>I also oppose the proposed prohibition on questions about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation on questionnaires used for the decennial enumeration.<br/><br/>I understand that the proposed rule would still permit the Census Bureau to collect demographic information through the American Community Survey, administrative records, and other sources. That limitation should be stated accurately. It does not, however, eliminate my concern.<br/><br/>Race and ethnicity data help the public, researchers, courts, and government agencies identify unequal treatment and disparities involving voting, housing, healthcare, education, employment, environmental hazards, and access to public services. Sexual-orientation data can likewise help document whether LGBTQ Americans are being served fairly or experiencing disparities that would otherwise remain hidden.<br/><br/>Removing demographic questions from the decennial enumeration risks making the nation&rsquo;s most comprehensive population record less useful for understanding who lives here and whether different communities are being treated equally. Other surveys may not provide the same coverage, geographic detail, or public confidence as information collected through the census itself.<br/><br/>A government cannot effectively protect people it chooses not to measure. Less demographic evidence can mean less accountability, because discrimination and unequal outcomes become harder to demonstrate when the underlying data are incomplete.<br/><br/>The Census Bureau describes demographic characteristics such as race as immaterial to the constitutional purpose of apportionment. But the census has responsibilities and consequences beyond calculating a single national population total. Congress requires census information for redistricting, and census data support decisions throughout federal, state, and local government. Accuracy should mean accurately understanding the population&mdash;not merely producing a number stripped of information needed to evaluate fairness.<br/><br/>I respectfully request that the Census Bureau withdraw the proposed restrictions on who is included in the apportionment population and withdraw the proposed prohibition on race, ethnicity, and sexual-orientation questions. At minimum, the Bureau should conduct and publish a thorough analysis of how these changes could affect census participation, data quality, redistricting, civil-rights enforcement, rural and underserved communities, and the government&rsquo;s ability to identify disparities.<br/><br/>The census should count the people who live in this country and preserve the information necessary to ensure that all of them can be seen, represented, and protected.<br/><br/>Bruce Coffman<br/>Morganfield, Kentucky<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0034,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-5qq2-3840,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Department of Commerce, Census Bureau<br/>USBC-2026-0628<br/>RIN 0607-AA75<br/><br/>I support this Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (&quot;NPRM&quot;). I believe it is proper and consistent with the original intent of the Census to capture an accurate number of US Citizens or lawfully present individuals for apportionment, among other reasons. <br/><br/>First, illegal aliens should not be counted in the Census. Period. Their presence should not be weighted similar to (nor have any bearing on the results of) how US Citizens or those lawfully present are counted and will be impacted by the results of their illegal presence. Liberal and sanctuary hell holes would love for illegals to be counted as part of the Census to further illegally attempt to extort benefits from programs or initiatives the Census data is used for. <br/><br/>As this NPRM mentions, there are methods of verifying legal or illegal aliens so not to be counted towards the Census. Several federal agencies are mentioned but the two with the highest probative data would be the Social Security Administration and certain components and initiatives through the Department of Homeland Security. <br/><br/>DEI-type data collection in the Census is generally irrelevant. Unless there is a legitimate purpose that can be backed by scientific evidence [I&#39;m referring to &quot;real science&quot; - not Tony Fauci-type &quot;science&quot;] for collecting such information, it shouldn&#39;t be a priority. As this NPRM alludes to, it would reduce the public burden. <br/><br/>The Census Bureau is encouraged to implement this NPRM with my above input in mind. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0038,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-7hzh-jf3v,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,How can you serve as he community if you don&rsquo;t know who they are!!<br/>Keep the diversity questions in the census. Spend on those who need it!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0039,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-7n8k-ugns,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is a clearly unconstitutional proposal. Article I, Section 2, Clause 3 states that &quot;Representatives and direct Taxes shall be apportioned among the several States which may be included within this Union, according to their respective Numbers, which shall be determined by adding to the whole Number of free Persons ...&quot; It does not exclude non-citizens or immigrants (legal or otherwise) and only excludes Indians (Native Americans) not subject to taxes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0036,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-61e8-y4wb,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support all of the new residence criteria and proposed regulations be used for the 2030 Decennial Census. Only US citizens have a true vested interest in the laws and taxes enacted by Congress. Some people say that our census counts have included questions on race since the 1790 census. Of course they did early on in our nation&rsquo;s history. In 1790 the Three-Fifths Compromise was in effect - thus the need for racial data. The thirteenth amendment (1865) and Fourteenth Amendment (1868) made the racial count mute. The seats in the House of Representatives should be an awarded  purely through the count of US citizens.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0037,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-7efm-d37d,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I object to the changing the current way the census is taken regarding the inhabitants of this county regardless of citizenship. The Constitution clearly states that all inhabitants of the nation are to be counted.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0035,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-5sgh-524e,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,See attached file(s),,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0035/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0041,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-9272-7e7a,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I strongly oppose FDMS Docket No. USBC-2026-0628 and urge the Census Bureau to<br/>withdraw it. See attached file.,,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0041/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0044,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-95wv-3it8,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I object to the proposed changes in the data the US Census collects.  No politicalization of the Census.  Leave data collection as is,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0040,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-8duq-0j92,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed regulations appear clearly designed to serve political purposes and not to accurately accumulate numeration of the people in the United States and its constituent states. This clearly violates both the letter and the intent of the constitutional mandate. Instead of creating another entry into a valuable record of data that is extremely beneficial to understanding the course of development of our democracy, this would serve to detour that record in ways that not only render the data collect in the next census of little beneficial use beyond purely partisan political malignancy, it will also render the value of the existing data significantly less beneficial by reducing the merit of historical comparison. For these reasons, I must vehemently object to the proposed regulatory changes. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0042,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-95b7-lsct,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The Constitution states that all people in the United States are counted for representation. It doesn&#39;t specify citizens. Many people live here with a visa, work permit, etc. They pay taxes, are here under asylum or protection and contribute to the communities that they live in. They deserve representation also. If this is to be changed, it should be changed by Amendment with votes from the states. The legal way.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0043,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-95bl-134v,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The Constitution states that all people in the United States are counted for representation. It doesn&#39;t specify citizens. Many people live here with a visa, work permit, etc. They pay taxes, are here under asylum or protection and contribute to the communities that they live in. They deserve representation also. If this is to be changed, it should be changed by Amendment with votes from the states. The legal way.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0045,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-97ff-tzwo,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes fundamentally undermine the ability to gather data needed to accurately understand communities. For public health and community needs assessments, and economic development, for example, we need to know the race/ethnicity denominators for the overall populations and about subpopulations to understand a range of metrics like incidence rates, mortality rates, fertility rates, employment rates, etc. The removal of race-ethnicity question in particular, would eliminate the ability to conduct population weighting and produce population estimates with precision. This would negatively affect every US and State survey --  public health surveillance, polls, market research -- that relies on Census denominators for critical weighting and calibration. In short, these changes would prevent us from being able to provide reliable data on communities, states, markets, and the broader US population.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0046,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-9kft-84k7,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"In addition to the transparently nativist and racist intent of this new rule, which is its own problem, but not one I expect the fascists running this agency to care about, it seems to have an unintended consequence: the exclusion of native-born Americans who happen to be homeless. It may surprise the billionaires who wrote this rule to learn that homeless people do not neatly line up at hotels to make them their residences. Many have a &quot;usual residence&quot; that is illegal, because it is on a public sidewalk or in a public park that does not allow setting up residence. If this rule were put in place, it would exclude many American citizens, and is thus a clear violation of the Constitution. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0047,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-9rr5-uw1g,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am writing to express strong opposition to any policy, rule, or administrative attempt to exclude non-citizens&mdash;including undocumented individuals, &quot;removable aliens,&quot; &quot;deportable aliens,&quot; or &quot;inadmissible aliens&quot;&mdash;from the decennial Census population count used for congressional apportionment and the distribution of Electoral College votes.<br/><br/>Historical Precedent: Dred Scott v. Sandford Demonstrates &quot;Persons&quot; Exceeds &quot;Citizens&quot;<br/>The distinction between constitutional &quot;personhood&quot; for Census enumeration and legal &quot;citizenship&quot; is decisively proven by the nation&#39;s early constitutional history and Supreme Court jurisprudence.<br/>Under the original text of Article I, Section 2, enslaved individuals were explicitly included in the apportionment base (at the three-fifths ratio) as &quot;persons&quot; (&quot;three fifths of all other Persons&quot;), despite being completely denied citizenship, legal rights, and political representation or the ability to vote.<br/><br/>Dred Scott v. Sandford, 60 U.S. (19 How.) 393 (1857)  affirmed and held that enslaved AND free Black individuals were NOT &quot;citizens&quot; under the U.S. Constitution possessing no rights of citizenship. Yet, despite holding that Black individuals were entirely excluded from citizenship, the legal framework of the Constitution simultaneously required them to be enumerated as &quot;persons&quot; for the purpose of state population counts and legislative apportionment.<br/>Dred Scott stands as definitive historical evidence that under the U.S. Constitution, citizenship has never been a prerequisite for inclusion in the Census apportionment base.<br/><br/>Article I, Section 2, Clause 3 of the United States Constitution establishes that congressional representation and direct taxes shall be apportioned among the states according to their respective numbers of &quot;all Persons&quot;&mdash;not &quot;citizens&quot; or &quot;voters.&quot; When the Fourteenth Amendment superseded the Three-Fifths Clause in 1868, Section 2 reaffirmed this standard using identical language:<br/><br/>&quot;Representatives shall be apportioned among the several States according to their respective numbers, counting the whole number of persons in each State, excluding Indians not taxed.&quot; (U.S. Const. amend. XIV, &sect; 2)<br/><br/>The Framers of the Constitution and the authors of the Fourteenth Amendment deliberately selected the word &quot;persons&quot; rather than &quot;citizens&quot; or &quot;voters.&quot; Where the Constitution intends to restrict a right for to only citizens (such as qualifications for the Presidency or Congress, or voting rights in the 15th, 19th, and 26th Amendments), it uses the term &quot;citizen.&quot; Its choice of &quot;persons&quot; for the Census mandate reflects a clear intent to measure the entire physical population inhabiting each state.<br/><br/>If individuals who were explicitly held by the Supreme Court to be without citizenship were constitutionally mandated to be counted as &quot;persons&quot; for apportionment, the executive branch cannot now redefine &quot;persons&quot; excluding non-citizens today. At least without a constitutional amendment or convention. <br/><br/>3. Application to Modern Non-Citizen Classifications<br/>Administrative classifications such as &quot;removable aliens,&quot; &quot;deportable aliens,&quot; or &quot;inadmissible aliens&quot; are statutory immigration distinctions. They do not alter an individual&#39;s constitutional status as a &quot;person&quot; physically residing within a state.<br/>Excluding any class of residents based on immigration or citizenship status violates the plain reading of the Fourteenth Amendment, overturns more than two centuries of unbroken administrative practice, and undermines the constitutional mechanism governing the distribution of House seats and Electoral College votes.<br/><br/>Conclusion<br/>The executive branch lacks the authority to alter the constitutional apportionment base by substituting &quot;citizenship&quot; or &quot;legal status&quot; requirement for the Constitution&#39;s clear &quot;whole number of persons&quot; standard. That is clearly defined and understood by history. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0048,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-ayi6-5jqa,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This proposed rule is contrary to the specific language with which the Founding Fathers defined the Census&#39; requirements. Originalists need to be consistent in their treatment of those gentlemens&#39; words! That means counting everyone, regardless of immigration or citizenship status. I STRONGLY oppose this proposal!!!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0050,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-bx85-zgwb,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hard no on the proposed rule or rules. I&#39;m not interested in the Trump regime&#39;s attempts to rig elections, and demonize immigrants.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0049,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-bvqu-6pyj,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I do not support this rule. After reviewing the language, this rule will not help census accuracy and is a power-grab by this administration.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0054,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-vwq3-va20,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I oppose this new rule.<br/>College students should continue to be counted where they attend school, not their &quot;usual residence&quot; if that means their parent&#39;s home.<br/><br/>Individuals living in the United States under TPS, green card, as citizens, or otherwise should count in the census. &quot;The whole number of persons&quot; is what the Constitution mandates. An individual&#39;s status does not negate the need for municipal services, etc. If those individuals are not counted, cities/counties with large numbers of such people will be disadvantaged in the distribution of funds.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0051,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-esln-fmt0,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-10T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"After a careful reading of the proposed changes I respectfully disagree with both their rationale and implementation.  As a person with several degrees in human geography I believe there is insufficient merit to them.  And on a personal level I dislike the term &quot;illegal alien&quot; as a pejorative and biased labeling of the people who will be impacted.  The easiest way to approach this topic is by considering some of the reasoning that was presented in the proposal:<br/><br/>&quot;... illegal aliens (among others) should not be included in the apportionment count, as they are not true inhabitants, members of the body politic, or persons with a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; in the United States due to their lack of a sufficient tie and allegiance to the United States.&quot;   Please specify under what basis a person who may have lived here for many decades is not a &quot;true&quot; inhabitant, nor a usual resident.  And by what mind reading ability is the Bureau able to know they do not have ties and allegiance here?  Many have married and raised families within this country and deeply appreciate what it has provided for them, and what they have given it in return.<br/> <br/>&quot;...excluding from the apportionment base illegal aliens and aliens whose legal status is less durable and indefinite in length than lawful permanent resident status.&quot;  At least you are asking for comments on whether &quot;other forms&quot; of residence may be equivalent to legal status.  But where does one stop?  When people with the right to work here, who are doing everything correctly, are dragged off by masked armed men, there is no status, there are no rights.  This is a matter that is far beyond what the Bureau can manage and should not be opened.<br/><br/>I do not expect the comments that I, or anyone else, makes will have the slightest influence.  However, I will not stand passively by and keep quiet as numbers are fiddled to satisfy the whim of one person.  Data is important, and playing politics with it is never right. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0052,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-gj9k-qb8o,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Proposed Sec. 60.2(c) fixes the &quot;census enumeration period&quot; as January 3 to April 1, anchoring the start date to the convening of a new Congress under the Twentieth Amendment and the end date to the April 1 reference date already set by 13 U.S.C. 141(a). That gives the residence test in Sec. 60.2(a) a bounded window to work from rather than an open-ended one.<br/><br/>The same section 60.2(a) standard -- &quot;the residence at which they have lawfully spent the greatest number of days during the census enumeration period&quot; -- is the baseline every category in Sec. Sec. 60.5 through 60.10 builds on, but Sec. 60.4 replaces it with a binary rule for foreign citizens: subsection (a) counts &quot;citizens or lawful permanent residents,&quot; and subsection (b) excludes &quot;[a]ll other citizens of foreign countries.&quot; The preamble&#39;s legal argument for treating a person as lacking a &quot;usual residence&quot; is built specifically around illegal presence: it explains that illegal aliens should be excluded because of &quot;their lack of a sufficient tie and allegiance to the United States.&quot; Sec. 60.4(b) is not limited to illegal presence, though -- on its face it excludes every foreign national who is not a citizen or lawful permanent resident, including people lawfully present on temporary or humanitarian status, some of whom have lived continuously in the same state for years. The preamble does acknowledge this broader reach in a single reference to &quot;aliens whose legal status is less durable and indefinite in length than lawful permanent resident status,&quot; but the constitutional and case-law discussion built around allegiance and enduring ties addresses illegal presence, not this wider group. A rule this broad needs a stated basis, specific to lawfully present non-permanent residents, for concluding that they too lack the tie &quot;usual residence&quot; requires -- not an extension by omission from an argument built for a different population.<br/><br/>Section 60.2(b)&#39;s day-counting rule -- a day counts if a person was &quot;physically and lawfully present&quot; at a residence for &quot;at least one hour&quot; -- does not say what &quot;lawfully present&quot; excludes beyond the population already addressed in Sec. 60.4, and Sec. 60.2(a) anchors the &quot;usual residence&quot; finding to tax records (&quot;should be consistent with, and evidenced by, their tax records (e.g., tax returns, W-2 forms)&quot;). Neither provision states how the Bureau would determine usual residence for a person with no tax record at all, a category that includes non-filers below the income threshold and people paid in cash. The rule states a preferred form of evidence without stating what governs when that evidence does not exist.<br/><br/>Section 60.2(b) also states that &quot;[a] person may spend a certain day at more than one residence,&quot; and Sec. 60.2(a) resolves usual residence by whichever address has the &quot;greatest number of days.&quot; Neither provision addresses a tie -- two residences with an equal count of qualifying days during the enumeration period -- even though allowing multiple residences per day makes a tie mathematically more likely than it would be under a rule limited to one residence per day. The notice opens by stating the Bureau&#39;s goal of counting every person &quot;once, only once, and in the right place&quot;; a tie-breaker rule, even a simple one such as the residence where the person spent the most recent qualifying day, would keep the regulation itself consistent with that stated goal.<br/><br/>Section 60.5 sorts Americans abroad on Census Day into two outcomes only for the categories it defines: subsections (b) through (e) count military and civilian government employees, and their dependents, at a U.S. residence or as part of &quot;the U.S. federally affiliated overseas population.&quot; Subsection (a) covers everyone else living outside the United States who is not a government employee or their dependent, and states, in full: &quot;Not counted in the stateside census.&quot; Nothing in that subsection or elsewhere in Sec. 60.5 says whether these private citizens are counted anywhere for apportionment, the way overseas government personnel are counted through their tie to a home state. A private citizen working abroad long-term retains the same kind of state tie the rule credits government employees with; the rule does not explain why that tie stops mattering once the employer is not the government.<br/><br/>None of this is a case against setting residence criteria by regulation rather than case-by-case guidance, which the preamble is correct that the Constitution leaves largely to agency discretion. It is a case for matching the breadth of Sec. 60.4(b)&#39;s exclusion to a stated basis for excluding lawfully present non-permanent residents specifically, and for stating what evidence governs a usual-residence determination when tax records do not exist.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0053,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-h75y-vn0r,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"In Texas, even immigrants use the roads. So we need to count immigrants in the census in order to get Texas its fair share of highway maintenance money.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0055,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-w3db-iv5e,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a Christian. I am also a US citizen. I would like to see my government support immigrants living in our nation, whether legally or illegally. There are many people living in the US illegally who have good reason to be here, whether they were brought at a young age and had no choice in the matter or their situation in their home country was not amenable to human flourishing. In many, if not most, cases these people are productive members of our society. They are human beings whom I believe to be made in the image of God. I believe that all criminals, whether citizens, green card holders, or illegal immigrants should be prosecuted under the law. However, I believe that the proposed changes to the Census would harm not only individuals, but also the states where they live, work, and attempt to flourish.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0057,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-xit6-qyql,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I fully support the proposed changes in the residence criteria and demographic questions. The Census should be a neutral fact-gathering agency. It has become, under the Obama and Biden administrations, an instrument for political purpose, as is best illustrated by the 2020 undercount that avantage some states and political parties to the detriment of others. This undercount has never been corrected to the detriment of many citizens whose votes are not worth less. The purpose of the constitutional purpose of the Census is to count citizens, for purposes of apportionment of representation. Non-citizens are irrelevant for this purpose, and counting them distorts the apportionment process. Questions about race and ethnicity are also not necessary for this purpose and in an age of mixed races only serves to provide opportunities for political mischief.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0059,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-xwdr-y86v,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Ever since the beginning of the census immigration status has not been a factor in the decision to count someone, why now in 2026 is it all of a sudden an issue? This will have ripple effects on the feasibility of jobs, allocations of funds, and it IS now leading to inaccurate data. The census is conducted to provide an accurate picture of who lives in America, whether they do so legally is not the task that the census has been created to perform. If you would like to know the difference between citizens and non citizens that badly I would suggest creating a way to tally that separately, but I would not change the very meaning of the census?",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0058,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-xwdr-t1tk,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Ever since the beginning of the census immigration status has not been a factor in the decision to count someone, why now in 2026 is it all of a sudden an issue? This will have ripple effects on the feasibility of jobs, allocations of funds, and it IS now leading to inaccurate data. The census is conducted to provide an accurate picture of who lives in America, whether they do so legally is not the task that the census has been created to perform. If you would like to know the difference between citizens and non citizens that badly I would suggest creating a way to tally that separately, but I would not change the very meaning of the census?",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0056,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-x0xw-oo70,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am submitting this comment as an active member of the American Association for Public Opinion Research (AAPOR) and as a researcher who relies on Census data to produce accurate, reliable, and representative information about the United States population.<br/><br/>I strongly oppose the proposal to prohibit the collection of race and ethnicity data on the decennial census questionnaire. While the proposed changes raise numerous policy and societal concerns, my opposition is grounded primarily in their profound consequences for the quality, accuracy, and usefulness of the nation&#39;s statistical infrastructure.<br/><br/>The decennial census serves as the foundational benchmark for an enormous range of federal, state, academic, nonprofit, public health, and private-sector data collection efforts. Race and ethnicity data collected through the census are not merely descriptive variables. They provide the population denominators that allow researchers, policymakers, health systems, businesses, and governments to accurately understand the characteristics and needs of communities across the country. The proposed rule would prohibit questions about race and ethnicity on the decennial census short-form questionnaire.<br/><br/>Without high-quality census-based race and ethnicity population counts, researchers would lose the ability to calculate reliable rates and estimates that are critical for decision making. Public health agencies depend on these data to assess disease incidence, mortality rates, maternal and infant health outcomes, vaccination coverage, and other key indicators across population groups. Community needs assessments rely on accurate population benchmarks to identify disparities and allocate resources appropriately. Economic development organizations, educational institutions, and social service providers similarly depend on these statistics to understand the populations they serve.<br/><br/>The effects would extend far beyond the census itself. Virtually every major survey conducted in the United States relies on census benchmarks to weight data and ensure that survey results accurately represent the population. Public opinion surveys, public health surveillance systems, academic studies, market research, and government statistical programs all use race and ethnicity population distributions for weighting, calibration, estimation, and nonresponse adjustment. Removing these measures from the decennial census would substantially degrade the quality of the benchmark data used throughout the research ecosystem.<br/><br/>The proposal notes that demographic information may continue to be available through administrative records and other sources and that demographic questions could continue to be asked on the American Community Survey and other surveys. However, these sources are not substitutes for comprehensive race and ethnicity data collected through the decennial census. The census provides the only universal population enumeration that serves as the basis for national and subnational population controls. Administrative records vary substantially in coverage, quality, consistency, and comparability across jurisdictions and demographic groups. Likewise, sample surveys cannot replace the census as the primary benchmark for population estimation.<br/><br/>The loss of race and ethnicity data from the decennial census would therefore have cascading effects throughout the nation&#39;s statistical system. It would reduce the precision of population estimates, weaken the quality of survey weighting and calibration procedures, impair longitudinal comparability with prior censuses, and diminish our collective ability to measure demographic change over time. Researchers would have fewer tools to evaluate whether public programs are reaching intended populations, whether health outcomes differ across communities, and whether social and economic conditions are improving or declining.<br/><br/>Most importantly, these changes would make it more difficult to provide reliable and accurate information about communities, states, markets, and the nation as a whole. The Census Bureau&#39;s mission is not only to count the population but also to provide the statistical foundation upon which countless public and private decisions depend. Eliminating race and ethnicity questions from the decennial census would significantly weaken that foundation.<br/><br/>For these reasons, I respectfully urge the Census Bureau to reject the proposed prohibition on race and ethnicity questions and to retain the collection of these essential demographic measures in the 2030 Census.<br/><br/>Thank you for considering these comments.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0060,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtw-y3hv-53l3,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The constitution says every PERSON must be counted. Businesses and other organizations rely on census data accurately reflecting our population. This change will sew chaos. I am 100% opposed to this change.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0061,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-027b-maqp,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I have serious concerns about the proposed exclusions of race/ethnicity and sexual orientation questions in section II. Proposed Restrictions on the Inclusion of Certain Demographic Questions in the Decennial Census Operation. These demographics are important for understanding the population and ensuring comparability to previous data. I urge the Census Bureau to seek guidance from those with great expertise in data collection and statistical methodology to ensure that the quality of Census data can remain as high as possible.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0062,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-1k7y-3r54,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Simple. Follow the Constitution, all persons to be counted. Nothing about citizen status. Count every person living in this country!!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0065,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-3ch5-js1n,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"When you &quot;prohibit the inclusion of questions about race and ethnicity or sexual orientation on the decennial census short-form questionnaire or any questionnaire used for purposes of the enumeration&quot; then we -- being folks of all races, ethnicities, and sexualities -- cannot prove we exist; you are making us statistically invisible.<br/><br/>Obscuring race, ethnicity, and sexuality patterns across the US also makes it more difficult for us to see correlations in demographically linked data, whether it&#39;s public health information, economic trends, voting power, funding disparities, etc.  It also makes it more difficult for organizations (nonprofits, etc) working with target populations to find those populations and provide often lifesaving support.  If we want our resources to be thoughtfully allocated and data-informed - WHICH I DO - then we should collect this necessary data.<br/><br/>If &quot;census questionnaires historically have also been used as a vehicle for collecting demographic information about the population of the United States&quot; then collect our general demographic information!  While privacy concerns are of course very real, we also have the freedom to opt out.  Let us make our own decisions regarding what information to disclose; to choose it for us is overreach.<br/><br/>Race and ethnicity have been tracked since 1790.  To exclude these data points would be a departure from our historical practices, and a disservice to all those who have relied on that data since our founding.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0063,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-2w6e-oq9m,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please see attached letter. ,,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0063/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0064,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-35p2-zkv0,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Of note is reference to the inclusion of slaves when determining the number of persons residing in a designated area. Resort to that element of the standards to suggest no historical intent to count all without reference to citizenship simply displays the lack of intellectual honesty undergirding the proposals.<br/>Lashing out at non-citizens and illegals is playing a game of whack-a-mole and renders effective allocation of representation and financial aid unresponsive to the needs of locales comprising our Republic.<br/>Our Founding Fathers often show wisdom of which our current representatives do not share.    ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0066,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-3h6k-vocp,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I have 40+ decades in the research profession and I am very troubled by the proposed changes to the Decennial Census. How can the Census possibly claim to achieve &quot;a more complete population count&quot; by eliminating essential demographic questions from the Decennial Census? Not only does this limit the usefulness of the Census outcomes, it gravely defeats researchers&#39; ability to conduct accurate and meaningful investigations on critical topic areas facing our country and beyond. I sincerely urge the Census to reject the Proposed Restrictions on the Inclusion of Certain Demographic Questions in the Decennial Census Operation.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0067,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-3i6z-96vy,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am writing to strongly oppose the Census Bureau&rsquo;s proposed rule modifying residence criteria to exclude noncitizens. This proposal violates the plain text of the U.S. Constitution, breaks with over two centuries of unbroken historical precedent, and threatens the foundational reliability of census data.<br/>1. Plain Text of the Constitution and Statutory Mandate<br/>Article I, Section 2, Clause 3 of the United States Constitution, as amended by Section 2 of the Fourteenth Amendment, explicitly requires that representatives be apportioned by counting the &quot;whole number of persons in each State.&quot; The Framers deliberately chose the term persons&mdash;not citizens, voters, or lawful permanent residents. Where the Constitution intends to restrict a clause to citizens, it does so explicitly (e.g., Privileges or Immunities, qualifications for office). Redefining &quot;usual residence&quot; to artificially exclude entire segments of the population living within a state&#39;s borders is incompatible with the Constitution&#39;s plain language.<br/>2. Over Two Centuries of Precedent<br/>Since the inaugural census of 1790, the United States has counted every resident residing in a jurisdiction, regardless of citizenship status. Every decennial census across multiple administrations has adhered to this principle. Altering this standard via agency rulemaking contradicts the longstanding understanding of the Enumeration Clause and federal census statutes.<br/>3. Inaccurate and Distorted Population Data<br/>The decennial census is not merely an apportionment mechanism; it is the statistical backbone of the United States. Census data dictates hundreds of billions of dollars in federal funding, guides emergency management, informs infrastructure planning, and powers economic forecasting. Noncitizens live in our neighborhoods, travel on local roads, send children to local schools, utilize emergency services, and contribute to state and local economies. Excluding them produces a distorted, artificially deflated count of actual community populations, leaving state and municipal leaders without an accurate picture of how many people actually reside in their jurisdictions.<br/>An inaccurate count guarantees flawed resource distribution, strained local infrastructure, and compromised public data. I urge the Census Bureau to withdraw this proposed rule and adhere to the constitutional mandate to count every person living in the United States.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0069,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-43lv-ah3l,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The proposed changes to the census are a clear threat to democracy as we have known it in the USA. I see this as an explicit attempt to further destroy the integrity of elections and an intentional erosion of the capacity of the nation to faithfully serve our neighbors living within its boundaries. The quality of life of all who live here should be our top priority and we won&#39;t stand by for something with such grave implications.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0070,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-5vbe-9rdx,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"American citizens should not lose House seats and electoral votes because another state has a large population of people who entered or stayed in violation of federal law. That is what happens under the old &ldquo;count everyone living here&rdquo; approach. The proposed rule correctly includes U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents and excludes illegal aliens and those with less durable status. That is fair to the citizens who vote, pay taxes as members of the body politic, and live under our laws. I urge the Bureau to finalize these criteria and use administrative records from DHS and Treasury so the count is accurate and not based on self-reports that can be gamed.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0068,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-41dy-di19,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"If enacted, this proposal creates a difficult paradigm for Decennial Census data users and American Democracy.  The Census has asked questions about race since the very first census in 1790.  Race and ethnicity are critical to the understanding and measurement of &quot;one person one vote&quot;.  The fourteenth amendment guarantees each person &quot;equal protection of the laws&quot;.  The founding fathers understood that without measurement, there is no knowledge.  Without measuring race in the census, we cannot know how our society is addressing &#39;equal protection&#39;.  So race and ethnicity measures part of who we are as Americans.  Leaving it out of the 2030 Census is a true threat to our democracy.  This suggestion is a misdirected plan to limit our knowledge on diversity and would never be supported by career statisticians regardless of political affiliation.<br/><br/>One could argue along similar lines that one does need exact measurement in science or manufacturing.  Imagine the automobile industry proposing that measuring tolerances within automobile engines is an unnecessary part of production...  Its a ludicrous proposal, as is removing the questions on race from the Census. Countless programs in the public AND private sector depend on this information.  Just as the political parties depend on these data for a successful Gerrymander.  Does the administration realize that without these data, a redistricting plan that benefits Republicans cannot be created?<br/><br/>On the subject of excluding certain people based on residency rules.  This suggestion is also poorly justified.  When our country was founded, no person was a citizen. The Constitution recognizes that immigration is part of America&#39;s fabric.  Its part of who we are.  Representation in our congress should include all those who reside within each district.  Whether they are citizens or not is irrelevant. Each person who considers our country &#39;home&#39; is part of our society.  Good or bad.  Excluding them from the census ignores the data they represent.  Good decisions in our country cannot be made without good information.  Excluding certain people because of their immigration status ignores the intent of the Constitution when the phrase &quot;all persons&quot; is referenced.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0071,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-6kl3-sxf7,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0075,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-72t5-t1cd,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"American citizens should not lose House seats and electoral votes because another state has a large population of people who entered or stayed in violation of federal law. That is what happens under the old &ldquo;count everyone living here&rdquo; approach. The proposed rule correctly includes U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents and excludes illegal aliens and those with less durable status. That is fair to the citizens who vote, pay taxes as members of the body politic, and live under our laws. I urge the Bureau to finalize these criteria and use administrative records from DHS and Treasury so the count is accurate and not based on self-reports that can be gamed.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0073,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-6opa-p3ro,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Thank you for the opportunity to comment on this rule. Thank you for proposing these important changes. I support them.<br/><br/>Re: Constitutional purpose<br/><br/>I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.<br/><br/>Re: Fairness to citizens and states<br/><br/>American citizens should not lose House seats and electoral votes because another state has a large population of people who entered or stayed in violation of federal law. That is what happens under the old &ldquo;count everyone living here&rdquo; approach. The proposed rule correctly includes U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents and excludes illegal aliens and those with less durable status. That is fair to the citizens who vote, pay taxes as members of the body politic, and live under our laws. I urge the Bureau to finalize these criteria and use administrative records from DHS and Treasury so the count is accurate and not based on self-reports that can be gamed.<br/><br/>Re: Process and records<br/>I support both parts of this proposal. The census should not be turned into a social survey about race or sexual orientation when its core job is a headcount for apportionment. More important, the residence rules should stop treating illegal presence as if it created a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; equal to that of a citizen or green-card holder. I am concerned that past counts mixed people with no lawful claim to remain into the numbers that decide political power. Using tax records and immigration files is a better, more objective way to apply the rules. Please keep the exclusion of illegal aliens and non-durable statuses and do not weaken it during the comment period.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0074,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-6t4q-7nfb,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,There is no basis for this proposal. This proposal would violate the 14th Amendment and is a blatant attempt to strip voting rights from millions of Americans.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0072,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-6kyh-ysg6,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I absolutely, positively support limiting census counts to those who are citizens. Would include those in our nation legally if they are tabulated separately so we could determine the distribution of actual citizens vs. here legally.<br/>AM FIRMLY AGAINST COUNTING THOSE WHO ARE IN OUR NATION ILLEGALLY! The only counts of illegals we should be keeping is for deportation purposes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0077,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-7p3t-zfvn,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The Census has counted the whole number of people and their race/ethnicity since the very first Census. You cannot possibly argue that the Constitutional Framers didn&#39;t intend for it to work that way when they were running the original Census themselves. It works exactly the way they intended, and they told us so with their words in the Constitution and with their actions running the early Census. To argue that they were going against their own intentions is absurd. Leave it alone, it works as intended.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0076,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-7p18-9x6l,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support both parts of this proposal. Apportionment should only be determined by the number of legally residing people in a district.  <br/><br/>I support both part of this proposal. Stop treating illegal presence as if it created a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; equal to that of a citizen or green-card holder. <br/>Please finalize these criteria and use administrative records from DHS and Treasury so the count is accurate and not based on self-reports that can be gamed. Do not reward illegal presence and take away our representation.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0078,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-83ah-kwd6,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Stop the disenfranchisement of voters. Those of good conscience will not allow this.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0079,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-8l14-whda,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am submitting this comment as the owner of Research Narrative, a research agency that conducts nationwide and regional surveys throughout the US on a regular basis. Our company relies heavily on racial and ethnic data from the Census Bureau for all of our studies, to make sure we are sampling a representative population that is reflective of market opportunity and the relevant national or regional population. Removing any demographic information from the Census would have a catastrophic impact on our industry, which relies on this data. It&#39;s also important in accurately designing sample frames for accurate political polls and managing investments in public education. Please note that this is not a partisan issue; we have clients across the political spectrum that rely deeply on this information. Census data demographic information (including but not limited to race and ethnicity) is, without a doubt, the most important and most valuable federally-funded resource that I use as a business owner. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0082,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-8vop-ketu,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0080,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-8tnp-f2l0,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"American citizens should not lose House seats and electoral votes because another state has a large population of people who entered or stayed in violation of federal law. That is what happens under the old &ldquo;count everyone living here&rdquo; approach. The proposed rule correctly includes U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents and excludes illegal aliens and those with less durable status. That is fair to the citizens who vote, pay taxes as members of the body politic, and live under our laws. I urge the Bureau to finalize these criteria and use administrative records from DHS and Treasury so the count is accurate and not based on self-reports that can be gamed.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0081,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-8vdm-41qq,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support both parts of this proposal. The census should not be turned into a social survey about race or sexual orientation when its core job is a headcount for apportionment. More important, the residence rules should stop treating illegal presence as if it created a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; equal to that of a citizen or green-card holder. I am concerned that past counts mixed people with no lawful claim to remain into the numbers that decide political power. Using tax records and immigration files is a better, more objective way to apply the rules. Please keep the exclusion of illegal aliens and non-durable statuses and do not weaken it during the comment period.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0085,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-9fve-q5px,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,See attached file(s),,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0085/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0083,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-949w-mp7c,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Somebody stop this man!!! Don&rsquo;t let him keep chipping away at the systems that make America a democracy. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0084,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-95ej-he1z,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support both parts of this proposal. The census should not be turned into a social survey when its core job is a headcount for apportionment.  I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose.  Using tax records and immigration files is a better, more objective way to apply the rules. Please keep the exclusion of people who are not legal citizens and do not weaken it during the comment period.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0086,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-9jsb-6pvm,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This rule should be passed without modification. This matches the original idea of &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; from the first census in 1790, clearly what the founders intended and later court cases that talk about allegiance and a lasting home.<br/><br/>Bear in mind that while courts have bastardized much of the Constitution, this is what was stated at the time the Constitution was written:<br/><br/>&quot;[T]he present Constitution is the standard to which we are to cling. Under its banners, bona fide must we combat our political foes -- rejecting all changes but through the channel itself provides for amendments.&quot;<br/>&mdash;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Alexander Hamilton<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0087,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-9k29-0qyt,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a proud insights professional them demographic data, include race, ethnicity, sexual orientation, and citizenship status is critical in making sure we are representative of all people within the US when conducting researching. Without this information, critical weighting schemes will be lost making our insights less reliable and actionable for the businesses and corporations we support. This is essentially kicking the knees of the entire research profession. Please keep this information included in the Census as it is critical to our industry.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0088,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-9nb7-g6o8,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Considering the purpose of the Proposal, also considering criteria of efficiency and efficacy that US Government must follow and since the resources come from taxpayers, there are two scenarios to comply with the objective:<br/>1.<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Census has the experience, the structure and the questionnaires in order to get information that can be used for different government offices/agencies. So it would be in the best interest of avoiding duplicity on the efforts to obtain information, to make the appropriate agreements with those offices in order to, with the law, giving them the information they need. The agreements could mean also getting the appropriate funds. Or<br/>2.<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>In order to conduct a population count for purposes of congressional, just get the appropriate information from the Internal Revenue Service. That would also imply not doing the decennial census anymore, cutting the funds to Census Bureau for that activity since the information required to conduct a population count for purposes of congressional could be obtained in a more efficient way, avoiding duplicity.<br/>Risks<br/>There are risks avoiding counting all persons living in the United States.<br/>What (or who) is not measured could not be considered in any way (for the things needed to be done).<br/>If there is no information about the total amount of persons living in the United States, and just of certain categories of persons living in, there are social and political risks extremely dangerous: by example in areas where people take law in their own hands, some groups of persons could be apprehended, tortured, killed by those persons taking the law in their own hands. The risk of having persons working in any area of the government tempted to do what was done in Central America last century, killing persons, could increase (like happened in El Salvador, Guatemala). Having people unaccounted gives that opportunity. If they &ldquo;do not exist&rdquo;, how they could be missed? How could it be demonstrated that something happened to them?",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0089,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-adjn-cs75,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is outrageous. It would be the first time the census has not included the &quot;whole number of persons&quot; in each state since 1860, when an enslaved Black person was counted as three-fifths of a person for population purposes. <br/><br/>We are a first world country, not a sad backwards sliding nothingburger sad sack. But this would just change our trajectory towards the latter. I mean you can be stupid if you want to and go down in history books as top ten stupidest if you really want to. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0091,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-art6-je47,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"A simple statement, I disagree with the redefinition though decree of what is clearly outline in the constitution.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0090,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-apdu-ivid,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,&quot;Article I of the Constitution mandates that the census count all &ldquo;Persons&rdquo; living in a state and makes no reference to citizenship being a requirement for inclusion in the tally. The 14th Amendment also requires that congressional districts be assigned based on the &ldquo;whole number of persons in each State&rdquo; with no reference to citizenship.&quot;<br/><br/>It is not up to trump to make any change to the census.  Don&#39;t do it. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0092,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-bjlw-f6we,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support both parts of this proposal. The census should not be turned into a social survey about race or sexual orientation when its core job is a headcount for apportionment. More important, the residence rules should stop treating illegal presence as if it created a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; equal to that of a citizen or green-card holder. I am concerned that past counts mixed people with no lawful claim to remain into the numbers that decide political power. Using tax records and immigration files is a better, more objective way to apply the rules. Please keep the exclusion of illegal aliens and non-durable statuses and do not weaken it during the comment period.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0093,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-bs6k-cs2u,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"OPPOSED! There is so much over-stepping happening to justify not counting immigrants (illegal or otherwise), which will subsequently lead to an invasion of privacy of citizens by abusing access to records on anyone that their citizenship status is questioned. Foreign sounding last name? Best dig up records from the Dept. of Treasury! That&rsquo;s asinine! <br/><br/>Not to mention, as a people of this nation, we are GROSSLY UNDER-REPRESENTED due to the fact that we have failed to keep up the number of representatives as our population has grown. We need more representatives, not fewer. This is clearly a move to justify discrimination, abuse power through allowing access to data that is not relevant, and to continue consolidating power. <br/><br/>WE LIVE IN A REPRESENTATIVE DEMOCRACY, and it should stay that way. I oppose this proposal. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0094,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-c1ph-4h0m,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support both parts of this proposal. The census should not be turned into a social survey about race or sexual orientation when its core job is a headcount for apportionment. More important, the residence rules should stop treating illegal presence as if it created a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; equal to that of a citizen or green-card holder. I am concerned that past counts mixed people with no lawful claim to remain into the numbers that decide political power. Using tax records and immigration files is a better, more objective way to apply the rules. Please keep the exclusion of illegal aliens and non-durable statuses and do not weaken it during the comment period.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0095,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-c98o-kusc,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Specifically, when counting residents for congressional seats, it is ideal to exclude non-citizens from the census count. <br/><br/>I also agree that certain demographic information such as race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation should not be collected because there is no need for the government to collect that information.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0098,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-dfja-k5sl,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This plan is in direct opposition to all established constitutional tradition and goes against the entire purpose of the census. The census must include every person in a given state. Furthermore, removing questions about race, ethnicity ir any other demographic would effectively strip our ability to enforce anti discrimination laws, handicap demographic research, and goes against tradition as well, with that type of question dating back to the earliest American census. <br/><br/>I profoundly disagree with his proposal by any metric, and I believe any American who believes in our constitution and wishes to defend it should also.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0096,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-cb42-9bj9,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This proposal to omit non-citizens is completely illegal since it is specifically un-constitutional. I am against this proposal.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0097,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-cdfk-2zd1,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support both parts of this proposal. The census should not be turned into a social survey about race or sexual orientation when its core job is a headcount for apportionment. More important, the residence rules should stop treating illegal presence as if it created a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; equal to that of a citizen or green-card holder. I am concerned that past counts mixed people with no lawful claim to remain into the numbers that decide political power. Using tax records and immigration files is a better, more objective way to apply the rules. Please keep the exclusion of illegal aliens and non-durable statuses and do not weaken it during the comment period.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0100,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-gcyp-cds6,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,See attached file(s),,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0100/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0099,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-djud-o52s,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I have three strong objections to the proposed revisions to the U.S. Decennial Census. First, the term, &quot;illegal alien&quot; is intentionally insulting and degrading to immigrants, especially those from Latin America. A less politically insensitive term would be &quot;undocumented immigrant&quot; which achieves the same defining objective without the taint of degradation. Use of the term &quot;illegal alien&quot; in this proposal ensures that it will be perceived by many Americans as a partisan, politically motivated attempt to disparage a large segment of our population, including lawful immigrants and those who have lived here their entire lives. The proposal loses significant credibility with the use of that offensive terminology. <br/><br/>My second objection is regarding the definition of an &quot;inhabitant&quot; of the United States. The assertion in the proposal that undocumented immigrants &quot;are not true inhabitants, members of the body politic, or persons with a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; in the United States due to their lack of a sufficient tie and allegiance to the United States&quot; is demonstratively false and intentionally misleading as many of these people have lived in the United States for many years, or even all their lives, and have no other &quot;usual residence&quot; or &quot;sufficient tie&quot; with any place other than the United States. Additionally, it is inherently unwise and fallacious to assume that undocumented immigrants have no &quot;allegiance&quot; to the United States. This statement alone invalidates the entire premise of the proposed change as it is founded on falsehood and unverifiable assumptions. <br/><br/>Finally, the proposed inclusion of a legal-status question on the short-form decennial census questionnaire would (intentionally) result in an undercount of population as many immigrants (both legal and undocumented) would likely choose not to respond to the census at all since it would be presenting itself as an excessively invasive instrument of government surveillance rather than as a non-partisan data collection mechanism to ensure equal representation and provision of government services. This very point is emphasized in Section II of the proposal regarding the reduction of &quot;response burden&quot;.<br/><br/>It is imperative that the United States Census retain its historic role as a politically neutral national data collection process and not be transformed into a partisan mechanism to degrade certain segments of our society and to skew the results of the of data collected to benefit a particular political agenda. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0101,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-hndi-ceqe,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. <br/><br/>Furthermore, American citizens should not lose House seats and electoral votes because another state has a large population of people who entered or stayed in violation of federal law. That is what happens under the old &ldquo;count everyone living here&rdquo; approach. The proposed rule correctly includes U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents and excludes illegal aliens and those with less durable status. That is fair to the citizens who vote, pay taxes as members of the body politic, and live under our laws. I urge the Bureau to finalize these criteria and use administrative records from DHS and Treasury so the count is accurate and not based on self-reports that can be gamed.<br/><br/>Please adopt the proposed rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0102,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-iyvh-j6pk,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,These rule proposals are inappropriate. Everyone must be counted. To not do so strictly for political purposes is an abuse of power.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0104,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-j7g0-r2a4,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,   This proposal is completely contrary to the clear text of the Constitution.<br/>It is also contrary the concept of appropriate resource allocation.<br/>,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0105,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-jt76-g55j,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Much of my career, social, and economic impact as an American is reliant on robust and accurate Census data. Any changes to how race, ethnicity, and residency are tabulated will break an important economic engine for purely political means. I robustly disagree with the changes proposed here. It will lead to poor data that will cause profound failures for survey data calibrated to Census products and distrust of an important engine for understanding the social fabric of America. <br/><br/>Take, for example, my current role. I lead the methodology function at a GovTech start-up, working with hundreds of state and local governments. Our survey product is reliant on an accurate Census count of residents and accurate descriptions of their identity. The decennial Census&#39; count of population and race/ethnic identity questions make our data representative. They form targets for distribution. They are reference criteria for weighting. Without them, we cannot say that the respondents to any of our surveys reflect the population itself. The critique in this rule is that race/ethnicity and residency data isn&#39;t necessary. Our approach, and the approaches of many of our competitors, and hundreds of polling firms and market research firms say otherwise. Whether we like it or not, race and ethnicity and residency are meaningful predictors of lived experience that are necessary for weighting. These changes would break that process, reducing the feasibility of survey products to describe America. <br/><br/>The economic impacts of this change would be large, and negative. Government and Market Research is a multi-billion dollar industry premised on the idea that US Census Bureau data is a true description of reality. It powers sales efforts, public decision making, and understanding. Attacks on the quality of these products, as described above, would lead to severe reductions in quality and therefore the size of the market itself. And economics impacts are minor compared to the irreparable harm of reducing public knowledge of Americans. The Census is an august institution which has spread open data and open understanding of what America is. Willfully blinding ourselves to communities of America is a short-sighted and destructive impulse. This rule should not take effect.<br/><br/>Note: This comment is purely in a personal capacity and does not reflect the opinions of my employer, nor is it a reflection of my employer&#39;s views.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0103,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-iz9v-qfq8,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The attempt by the Trump administration to undermine the census is just another attempt to wrest representation from the citizen. The process of counting our numbers needs to adhere to the 14th amendment. Altered residency questions are a waste and a dissuasive means of reducing the accuracy of the count. Additionally, limiting the count based on citizenship flies in the face of the founders. All are to be counted. Do not adopt these proposed changes. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0108,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-lgn6-ueox,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The proposed changes to the census will leave several populations of tax payers without resources or representation. This betrays the foundational principles of our republic.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0106,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-knvn-e9sb,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The census is mandated to count the number of PEOPLE living in our country, not the number of citizens.  Monkeying with this law is unlawful.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0107,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-kw56-9hl9,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I oppose this regulation change and I urge the U.S. Census Bureau to rescind its proposal immediately, on the grounds that this rule change would violate the U.S. Constitution and that it would go against more than 200 years of precedent by eliminating valuable questions about respondents&#39; race and ethnicity. The 14th Amendment clearly states that &quot;Representatives shall be apportioned among the several states according to their respective numbers, counting the whole number of persons in each state, excluding [Native Americans] not taxed.&quot; Excluding non-citizens from that number would be inaccurate, plain and simple. Non-citizens are whole people, equally deserving of representation for where they live, and that same amendment provides for their same due process under the law as any citizen should receive.<br/><br/>Furthermore, the census has been collecting race and ethnicity data since its beginning. And while the categories have changed over time to reflect new interpretations and more nuanced understandings of these identifiers, that data is still valuable to reflect the full diversity of the nation. Diversity is not a dirty word. It is not a weakness, it is an incredible strength that should be accounted for in congressional representation, public programs, medical research, and educational curriculums. Colorblindness is a fallacy, and contrary to what some in this administration may think, you cannot make people or their identities disappear simply by refusing to acknowledge their presence on paper.<br/><br/>Again, please rescind this proposed regulation and leave wavering political agendas out of the census. This tool and its data are far too valuable to the country&#39;s economic and social future to meddle with. It needs to reflect the true reality of America. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0110,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-m502-aa6d,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Don&#39;t touch the census. There is no reason to change the census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0111,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-mo4r-yul2,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Population counts actual people living in the area.<br/>Support everyone that lives in a community no matter their status. Neglecting anyone will bring the quality of life down for everyone.<br/>We need accurate population polls to fully provide for a community.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0112,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-mr9a-q7x6,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This should have been done long ago.  Federal law prohibits noncitizens from affecting our governance, and that they should affect districting and representation strikes me as an absurd contradiction.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0109,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-lx3t-p5mz,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hello,<br/>I strongly disapprove of the proposed changes to the census data. Demographic information such as race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation is not &quot;immaterial&quot; but in fact matters significantly and needs to remain an important part of how we determine how effectively government resources, policies, and laws are being carried out, and in ensuring fair representation. This data is essential to making informed decisions and should NOT be removed from our official methods. The proposed changes to residency risk not counting people simply because of their stage of citizenship status. They are still people, who contribute to the economy, affect their communities, and need to be considered in the distribution of representation as well as other types of programs, laws, and policies. Changing this aspect of the census makes our numbers inaccurate. If the census data doesn&#39;t actually show everyone, then the data is useless. Please do NOT implement these changes, as they make our system less valuable and deny us the information that the census is designed to collect. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0115,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-nl3d-clt3,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes will have adverse effects on both red and blue states. These changes will reduce the ability of governments and private organizations to conduct&nbsp;public health&nbsp;surveillance, polls, and market research. All of these tasks rely on Census denominators for critical weighting and calibration. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0114,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-n3rl-npyy,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I feel this regulation will cause millions of people living in America to go without adequate representation, and remove desperately-needed funding from struggling school districts, government agencies, and social programs such as foster care, SNAP, and state-run employment agencies. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0113,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-msf9-o8ae,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The point of the census is to gather accurate (as much as possible) information about the population of an area. Obviously this can&#39;t be done by excluding information, such as race or ethnicity, nor by excluding non-citizens, who are part of a population regardless of status. All this can really accomplish is more un-American voter suppression, and further discrimination.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0116,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-nqg2-h94i,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"September 11, 2026<br/><br/>RE: Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions [Docket No. USBC-2026-0628]<br/><br/>To whom it may concern:<br/><br/>We respectfully request an extension of the comment period for the rules published on September 10, 2026 [FRN 2026-18481], which proposes significant changes to some of the U.S. Census Bureau&#39;s most fundamental responsibilities. <br/> <br/>The proposed residence criteria rule would change the way redistricting was done for over a century, and would introduce a citizenship question in the decennial census that was never asked of the entire U.S. population. The proposed Demographic questions rule would eliminate questions on race and ethnicity, a topic that has been part of the decennial census since the first census in 1790 and are essential to understanding the nation&#39;s demographic composition and ensuring that census data meet the needs of governments, communities, researchers, and other data users. <br/> <br/>Given the significance and potential impact of these proposed changes, the comment period from September 10 to October 13 does not provide sufficient time to fully review the proposals and prepare meaningful comments.<br/><br/>Sincerely,<br/><br/>California Demographic Research Unit<br/>California State Data Center<br/>California &ndash; U.S. Census Bureau&rsquo;s Federal-State Cooperative for Population Estimates and for Population Projections (FSCPE/PP)<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0117,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-oaph-xa66,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Do not implement this. We count residents. And we don&#39;t ignore how race interacts with our government. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0118,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-ogc5-zyf3,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is a ruination of our democracy.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0121,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-skmp-12yv,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposed criteria for counting Americans. The census exists to apportion the House of Representatives among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0119,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-qeuu-w2b6,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I find these proposed changes to the voting census to be against the constitutional rights of the American citizen. It allows loose definitions of who is and isn&#39;t a resident in order to lessen the pool of voters and skew bias to certain parties. The changes to our government have been a step backward for everyone, regardless of their heritage or homeland. There are people here of all colors who have known no other land than the USA, and to deem them unworthy of their right to vote because of bias regarding their upbringing is a direct violation of our freedoms. We are the land of the Free, and yet we keep taking away everyone&#39;s freedom, bringing shame to our country&#39;s name.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0120,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-r4rz-hpfn,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only count citizens and legsl residents fir apportionment.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0122,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-t3wl-1gz1,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposed rule changes regarding the census.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0123,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-un3e-5ait,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The census is one of the pillars of our country and it cannot be undermined. Any changes that would not count every person are unacceptable. Changes that don&#39;t count non-citizens are unacceptable. Changes that remove questions about race and ethnicity are unacceptable.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0125,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-vauc-pp4m,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The census is vital to government funding of programs such as SNAP, Medicaid, and more and ensures equitable treatment as well as accurate counts for the House of Representatives! I will not vote for anyone who supports this injustice!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0124,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-ustn-rxvy,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Since the times of the Romans, the census has stood as a permanent record of everyone who lived in a given area. And for just as long, it has been used to determine how money was spent by the government at large. Meddling with this census has the potential to entirely upend not only the common citizen&#39;s way of life, but the entire nation&#39;s as a whole. <br/><br/>Just as much as the vote records popular opinion, so too does the census. If anything, it may be a truer sense of the makeup of the country, being organized by largely objective principles. But it was not always this way. Not two centuries ago, it was used to further enforce the bondage of the enslaved, by marking them not as a whole, but as merely 3/5ths of a person. While this grave mistake has been amended, it has left a stain that will never go away. Removing entire categories of people can too easily replicate this feat of social engineering. These sorts of changes lend themselves almost exclusively to denying the most disadvantaged of our country the help they need. Just as not counting the victims of an illness not make their suffering go away; so too does forgetting vast sums of people not remove their existence from our soil. <br/><br/>In conclusion, I cannot disagree with these proposed changes to our national census enough. There is almost nothing to gain, but all of our self-respect to lose. Neglecting the people does not make us stronger, it makes us immeasurably weaker. Not only this, but it makes us something even worse: a sham, a pathetic shell of a nation that cannot even provide for its people. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0126,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-1h5c-ixc3,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"It would be so easy for influential people on all sides to start making false claims about the demographics of the U.S. if race and ethnicity were removed from the census and if non-citizens were removed from the population. It&rsquo;s not going to stop people from talking about race, ethnicity, and citizenship. Instead, it just means people won&rsquo;t have a reliable federal statistic and will either make things up or find less reputable sources, possibly and likely pushing misinformation. It&rsquo;s frankly asinine to so willfully eschew the collecting of knowledge, information, and statistics. Why are we so eager to continue chipping away at institutions that grant us knowledge and help us make educated and informed arguments and reasonings?",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0128,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-5rzj-bff9,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"grateful this platform for feedback exists, it&#39;s user-friendliness streamlines my providing comment, bigtime;<br/><br/>having worked as a non-response enumerator previously i would like to remind the dept. of commerce the u.s. consititution says count # of whole persons, no qualifications such as citizen necessary; as &#39;person&#39; is concerned it means all persons, any characteristics or particulars; counting the number of people allows jurisdictions to plan effectively for the burdens to be expected; if a census counts only some of the people there could be areas with more people than are budgeted for, roads, hospitals, schools, many, many things<br/><br/>allocation of tax dollars on a per citizen basis instead of per person would harm everywhere tourists are in #&#39;s, those uncounted individuals still use roads, hospitals, and counting foriegn university students alone, a lot of educational resources;<br/><br/>the census is not charged with differentiating or delineating any data or surveys, an accurate timely count is the order of the day &amp; decade;<br/><br/>may my comments provide welcomed insight or fuel for dialogue all the greater, please do enjoy your day and remember to smile when moments permit....<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0127,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-4mlf-idyj,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This change proposes to interpret the constitutional requirement of counting &quot;persons&quot; in the census as instead counting &quot;citizens. When the founders of the Constitution said &quot;persons,&quot; they meant &quot;persons.&quot; If they&#39;d meant citizens, they could have said so. If the Federal Government wishes to change the constitution, there is a legal route laid out for doing so. Deliberately misinterpreting the constitution&#39;s wording in order to force it to fit modern politics is not one of those legal routes. If the words in the constitution can be interpreted to mean whatever we want, then what is the point of having a constitution in the first place? <br/><br/>The actual description of what this change would do is buried in the verbiage on this page. The summary actually does not contain the words &quot;citizens&quot; or &quot;persons.&quot; What it says is &#39;the Census Bureau is proposing to add a part outlining the residence criteria used for identifying each person&#39;s &ldquo;usual residence,&rdquo; as needed to determine whether and where to count them in the census of the population throughout the United States.&#39; As you&#39;ll note, this says nothing about citizens or persons. Only in the &quot;additional information,&quot; hidden under paragraphs and paragraphs of justification, does it say this: &#39;The Census Bureau has preliminarily determined that illegal aliens (among others) should not be included in the apportionment count, as they are not true inhabitants, members of the body politic, or persons with a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; in the United States due to their lack of a sufficient tie and allegiance to the United States.&#39; Surely the basic effects of a proposal should be included in the summary; this information is not additional, it is core. If this change is something the public shouldn&#39;t object to, then why is it being hidden away towards the end of sixteen paragraphs that most people wouldn&#39;t read? If you&#39;re not ashamed of it, why not put it front and center? <br/><br/>Moreover, this lengthy justification for removing illegal aliens from the count sneaks in an alarming qualification: that only people who have &quot;allegiance&quot; to the United States should count as &quot;residents&quot; for the purposes of the census. Why is the government making judgement on which people should &quot;count&quot; based on their &quot;allegiance&quot;? We have the right to freedom of speech in this country. Is the government proposing to take away that right in the event that someone says &quot;I pledge allegiance to the United Kingdom&quot;? What about declarations of loyalty or support to enemy nations, or even to people who, in the past, took up arms against the United States? Will they remove everyone who owns a Confederate flag from the census? There is simply no clear-cut, legal definition of what constitutes &quot;allegiance,&quot; and creating a situation where the Federal Government is allowed to define who does and does not have certain rights (such as the right to be counted), much less who counts as a &quot;person,&quot; sets an extremely dangerous precedent. In short, the government should never be allowed to decide that people who lack &quot;allegiance&quot; to the United States have lesser or different status than others, because it opens the door for the government to persecute anyone who criticizes the government. <br/><br/>The Federal Government should not be making any decisions whatsoever based on who has &quot;allegiance&quot; to the Federal Government. It is contrary to our entire way of life and system of government. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0130,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-czbb-unvs,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The importance of accurate census information per the bodies and residents of a town, county, state cannot be understated. Trying to cherry pick who counts or redefine what it means to live in a place is an egregious slight not only to the individuals and communities being overlooked but to the principles of democracy, liberty, and freedom which make the United States breeding ground for hope and improvement. Transition periods are hard enough without people being dehumanized and scorned by any system meant to validate and support them through the tumultuous times of &quot;figuring it out.&quot;<br/>A vote for these alteration is a vote for regression, for cruelty, for erasure of experience and therefore for never even attempting to attain &quot;liberty and justice for all.&quot;",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0132,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-dozm-yjmv,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The US census is supposed to collect information about *ALL* people living in the US&mdash;not just citizens. It&rsquo;s also a record of everyone including race, gender, etc. Erasing groups of people to serve your needs is unconstitutional and goes against the history of the Census. I disagree with these proposed changes. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0129,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-7coy-poji,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support adding a citizenship question to the census, and I disagree with removing all of the questions to identify personal characteristics such as race, sex, nationality.  I think that identifying persons living in the U.S. who are citizens and non-citizens is critical to apportioning the correct number of Congressional Representatives in each state. I believe that such representation should be limited to those persons who are U.S. Citizens. I believe that identify a more accurate count of citizens and non-citizens will allow better apportionment of federal assistance funds to states. I believe that understanding where non-citizens are residing will assist to better focus efforts of ICE in removing non-citizens from the U.S., and assist federal and state legislatures in developing more effective legislation to curb sanctuary locations within the U.S.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0131,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-de1j-ccgt,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I worked for the Census and had to quit because of racist abuse because I&#39;m white. Managers who are Latino, LGBTQ are particularly problematic. The Census also misrepresents salary issues with field representatives. Most employees think they are going to be working close to full time. In reality this never happens. My suggestion for change to the surveys would be to remove the ethnicity question. The Latinos are the only ones who use it and then they are expected to say that their race is White. I think it&#39;s important to ask race/nationality questions for historical, political, and health reasons. Most people I talked to don&#39;t identify as being part of a continent. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0133,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-dulp-jym9,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please allow the proposed rule to become policy.  The only people who should have a say in how our country is run are those who are actually citizens of the U.S. <br/>Thank you.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0134,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-wfjm-hgz2,Public Submission,2026-09-15T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"fedreg Agency name,This is a word [X]",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0135,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-e2nb-qlai,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a physician and epidemiologist who has worked in medical/public health research for over 40 years on both sides of the Atlantic. This has included extensive work using the (previously) amazing US Census data. I have used it in particular to explore the health and wellbeing of the US worker population in peer reviewed research funded by and in collaboration with NIOSH (CDC), NIH and other organisations.  <br/><br/>The current proposed changes to the US Census will irrevocably damage these important data not only for the purposes of public health research but also lead to a complete misunderstanding of the needs of the US Population.  These changes include:<br/>1.) Only count US citizens and lawful permanent residents (green card holders) in assigning the number of Representatives per state. <br/>2.) Vulnerable population, including survivors of domestic abuse, the homeless, nursing home residents, disabled, divorced women, and transgendered individuals whose names have changed, risk not being included when the number of representatives for each state are calculated.<br/>3.) The Census Bureau may substitute their judgment about where an individual lives, and whether they get counted, for an individual&#39;s statement sby exchanging data with DOJ, DHS, USPS, third-party commercial vendors, and other data sources. <br/>4.) They appear to be preparing to add a question directly asking about citizenship or legal status to the census form.<br/>5.) Questions about race, ethnicitiy, and sexual orientation are explicitly barred. (Biological) sex, date of birth, and household relationship questions are being made mandatory.<br/>6.) All of the above changes will be permanent.<br/>These changes go against decades of existing US Census data (which will make connecting the data or comparing the data with prior decades almost impossible) and it will lead to a significant undercount of many vulnerable populations.  Furthermore, these changes will completely underestimate the needs of US citizens which will in turn lead to inadequate funding and support by the US government.<br/>And the addition of questions concerning citizenship etc will lead to decreased Census data collection participation.<br/>I am against all of the proposed changes to the US Census on both scientific and moral grounds<br/>Professor Lora E Fleming MD PhD MPH MSc Hon CBD<br/>Portland ME USA<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0136,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-fwr9-6jbw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"All persons should be counted. Even illegals who should not be here. Due to the fact they are still using the roads, the infrastructures, and that must be counted. The determines resource allocation to emergency services, Schools, hospitals. And race should be included for generations the Census has been used for genealogy purposes. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0138,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ipou-fvpd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is a terrible idea. You can&#39;t just re-define what it means for the census to be accurate, what the hell were you thinking?!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0137,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-hrou-pdfh,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I strongly oppose these changes to the Census.  They do not reflect our country and are biased.  The data collected are used by many to make decisions for their State, as well as nationally.  The elimination of this data will be harmful.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0139,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-jjp6-wgzd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Withdrawing the documentation of key components of the American population endangers the ability of Federal leadership, Federal and state governments, business and industry, healthcare and pharma organizations and nonprofits to effectively create, develop and produce products and services for its citizns. Limiting an understanding of the American population endangers the ability of all emergency serivces to direct key essential services to impacted populations in needs of services - remember Katrina?  <br/><br/>The proposal to eleminiate key population queries would impact critical health and medical eresearch that will put America&#39;s security at risk. If the United States becomes dependent on foreign medical research, findings and products, the country risks being dependent for all medical improvements and solutions.  This is a NATIONAL SECURITY RISK which the US Military and Security Agencies repeatedly identifies as a serious issue. <br/><br/>To not know who you are, you do not know who you can become period. This puts everyone at risk.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0140,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-jtoo-rxdp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am opposed to the new regulations based on the fact that it is designed to hurt blue states. The following information is clear that all persons are to be counted.<br/><br/>Article I of the Constitution mandates that the census count all &ldquo;Persons&rdquo; living in a state and makes no reference to citizenship being a requirement for inclusion in the tally. The 14th Amendment also requires that congressional districts be assigned based on the &ldquo;whole number of persons in each State&rdquo; with no reference to citizenship.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0141,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-khad-r3vm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I work in marketing research, the foundarion of every corporate strategy  decision and data-backed capitalist enterprise. We use census data to calibrate our research to the total pool of consumers. The accuracy of the full census to represent ALL residents, consumers, and buyers is critical to us making accurate decisions about our businesses and the success of American capitalist enterprise. These changes will hurt businesses and diminish profits. I strongly oppose. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0142,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-l6b3-l90d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Decennial census of the Populations of Americans<br/><br/>The proposed changes to the census are in direct violation of the constitution. In the future, if these changes are &quot;allowed,&quot; there will be an extended litigation using up tens of thousands (maybe millions?) of taxpayer dollars to defend the constitution against this blatant attempt to disenfranchise thousands of eligible voters. This is a partisan attempt to &quot;rig&quot; elections and the distribution of taxpayer-funded resources that belong to all people living America. I suggest that the current regime rethink this and withdraw these changes. Again, these changes are in direct violation of our constitution.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0143,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-lwqb-peo6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree that only people that have a legitimate stake in US should be counted.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0144,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-n2eg-ke2r,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This proposal is an absurd and transparent ruse animated by politics. Stop using bureaucratic chicanery to make everyone outside your constituency into a non-person. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0145,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-o1mg-85tk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I suspect nefarious intent behind this Proposed Rule. I am not trusting of it, and I do not wish to see it done. The concept of a &quot;usual residence&quot; sounds like an excuse to split hairs for said nefarious reasons...",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0146,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-o8ls-x9x4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I wholeheartedly endorse ELIMINATING questions about race and ethnicity from the census form. I haven&#39;t answered these questions for the past 3 decennial censuses. If one is a citizen, everyone is an American. Race and ethnicity are immaterial. They are outdated classifications and have no use in the United States. As Chief Justice Roberts ruled in Parents Involved v. Seattle School in 2007 &quot;The way to stop discrimination on the basis of race is to stop discriminating on the basis of race.&quot; If we consider everyone to be an American first and only, no one can be discriminated against. Asking these questions violates the principles upon which the nation was founded. &quot;We hold these truths to be self-evident, that ALL MEN ARE CREATED EQUAL, that they are endowed by their Creator with certain unalienable Rights.&quot; This means externalities like race and ethnicity are irrelevant factors in the United States. It is the same principle that is followed in our courts - Lady Justice is blind. One of the reasons the United States has been so successful is because we are the great &quot;melting pot&quot; of people from around the world. I believe &quot;American&quot; should be a new classification for race/ethnicity. After 9-10 generations since our founding, it is time. Somebody born over 100 years ago has no bearing on how I live my life today. I am responsible for myself. We should learn from ancient culture clashes and hatreds we can observe still happening today in other parts of the world to know to give that up. E pluribus unum - American! ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0147,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-q1hp-2u8b,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes to the national census are not only unconstitutional, but also deeply disturbing on a personal level. Before 1860, enslaved people were only counted as three-fifths of a person. The proposed change would treat non-citizens as if they were less than slaves from the 1800s. How could anyone justify such a change?<br/><br/>Census numbers are used to determine everything from funding for public schools to representation in Congress. The proposal is obviously a power grab to disenfranchise communities with a high population of immigrants. But immigrants are people. They still go to school. They still need infrastructure like roads and public services. And even if you&#39;re a ghoul who doesn&#39;t care about immigrants, lowering the funding available to areas with immigrants hurts *everyone.* Public education is a human right. The strength of our democracy depends on it. Weakening public education is something only and autocratic and fascist government would want to achieve. <br/><br/>Census data also provide information about race and ethnicity, data which are vital for tracking systemic inequality on many fronts, including education and healthcare. The proposed change would eliminate those data, making the inequalities across racial and ethnic lines invisible. This can only harm marginalized communities. When my black friend&#39;s child goes to school in one area and has fewer resources than my white friend&#39;s child in a different school district, we need to be able to articulate those differences and see the problem for what it is. Anything less is an Injustice.<br/><br/>There are a lot of immigrants in my state. These people are my neighbors, friends, and relatives. As a US citizen, I recognize that it is the government&#39;s duty to take care of all the people who rely on it, not pick and choose certain groups to prioritize (like whites vs slaves in the 1800s). Immigrants pay taxes!! They contribute to our economy and society! To be quite frank, they do jobs that a lot of white citizens turn their noses up at. We absolutely need our crop harvesters, gardeners, cleaners, and service people, which are &quot;undesirable&quot; jobs filled by people the government also wants to label as &quot;undesirable.&quot; But these people are the backbone of our economy, and treating them like dirt is not only morally abhorrent, but also a bad move economically. Our economy would collapse if these people left the country. It just doesn&#39;t make sense to pretend they don&#39;t exist. How dare you!!!!<br/><br/>Reverting the official government census to something worse than the racist policies of the 1800s is ludicrous for both its callous disregard for humans AND its atrocious economic and societal impact. This change cannot be allowed.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0148,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-q2wb-hb2y,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This would remove a primary source of key data for economic and market research, undermining accuracy and precision of findings. The wrong findings would have an impact on decades ahead, resulting in hundreds of millions of dollars going to waste in both the private and public sectors. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0149,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-q3py-hrrm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The census should exclude all illegal aliens.  Illegal aliens who met a long residency definition should not be excluded.  A question of citizenship should be on the census as well as a question asking about legal status on the &#39;short form.&#39;    ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0151,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-qmyx-3p1p,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I have a comment regarding the proposed 15 CFR 60.4, regarding &quot;citizens of foreign countries&quot;. The regulations do not provide the definition of the term &quot;citizens of foreign countries&quot;, and a literal reading can lead to unintuitive results in some cases.<br/><br/>For example, is a stateless person (i.e. someone who doesn&#39;t have the nationality of any country) a &quot;citizen of a foreign country&quot;? If the proposed 15 CFR 60.4 is taken literally, it seems not to apply to this case. But does that mean they would be counted for apportionment, even if they are not a lawful permanent resident?<br/><br/>Also, what about someone who is only a &quot;national&quot;, but not a &quot;citizen&quot; of a foreign country, under that country&#39;s legal terminology? If the proposed 15 CFR 60.4 is taken literally, it seems not to apply to this case. But does that mean they would be counted for apportionment, even if they are not a US citizen or a lawful permanent resident?<br/><br/>And what about a citizen of a foreign country who is also a non-citizen US national (e.g. people from American Samoa)? If the proposed 15 CFR 60.4(a) is taken literally, it seems not to apply to this case. But does that mean that 15 CFR 60.4(b) applies, and they would *not* be counted for apportionment?",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0150,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-q4d1-mqmm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I think people living long term in the US should be counted in the census, regardless if they are &quot;illegal aliens&quot; as no person is illegal. And given the recent violence US residents have faced from ICE, disqualifying people from the census contributes to this violence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0153,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-rst2-ech1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"It only makes sense. Illegal immigrants are not supposed to be here, they are not anyone&#39;s &quot;constituents&quot; and only waste federal money that could be used to help Americans. By removing illegal immigrants from the census, we can guarantee Congress will be apportioned to the number of AMERICANS living in districts ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0152,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-rrap-1wi4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am writing to express that I am opposed to these proposed changes to how the Census Bureau counts people. Given the focus on so-called illegal aliens and other foreign persons that have traditionally been counted, this seems like nothing more than an attempt to reduce representation by states with larger foreign populations rather than an attempt to more accurately count the number of people residing in all states. Given the fact that the number of members of the House of Representatives hasn&#39;t been increased in over a century despite the ever-increasing populations of many states in the country, this would result in an even worse true representation in that body of Congress than exists today. Please reconsider this proposal.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0154,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-rxbx-jul4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am against both proposed changes to the US Census. These modifications would NOT add to the integrity of the US Census nor fidelity to the US Constitution, because no data has been provided to justify these modifications, only political rhetoric. Even the statements on race are inaccurate and frivolous. So many, federal government policies have historically had racial exclusions, which have caused harm and NEVER been rectified. Not only are there written historical documentation to confirm this, but there are empirical  data that confirm this unaddress harm. Inserting language to ignore race does NOT in any way nullify its pernicious effects on the citizenry.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0155,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ss0h-nnbv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The government is proposing changes to Part 15 of the Code of Federal Regulations (C.F.R.) on the residence reporting criteria and demographic questions that will be asked for the 2030 Decennial Census; The criteria and questions are used to measure the population of Americans and apportion congressional seats.<br/>I fully support the narrowing of foreign citizenship being counted in the census for determining congressional seats. Legal IS citizenship votes should NOT be diluted.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0156,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-st9h-czjp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only people who are citizens of the United States or legal residents of the United States should be counted in the census.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0157,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-sugi-whxf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Ed Moseley, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0159,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-sweb-lepu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Patrick Schreiber, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. I believe only legal US citizens should be counted in the census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0160,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-swht-3kt1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I approve of the proposed residence criteria language changes. I approve of the proposed residence criteria changes. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0158,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-sux6-sapm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"These rule changes are a long time coming. We dont need to be counted by our race or identity, as we are all Americans. No state should get extra seats in congress because they allow illegals to flout federal laws.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0161,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-sx3v-7i9k,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is _____Kathy________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0162,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-sy0l-u7h2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Charlesweaver, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0163,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-sy3x-7crn,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree with the new census rule. Foreigners and illegals should not be counted.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0164,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-sy91-o8gr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Steven Laux.  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: 60.4 ensures that only citizens and legal residents are included for apportionment which is appropriate. 70.2 stops the divisive practice of dividing Americans by immutable characteristics. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0165,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-sz4v-6frw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is how the Census should be take .  I am in favor of this rule.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0166,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-t0y8-291g,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only CITIZENS of the United States of America should be counted in the census. STOP rewarding states who break immigration laws. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0169,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-t4tx-gaia,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John Heiple, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0167,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-t1gf-q6y8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0168,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-t2ho-qxjd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a Navy Veteran and American citizen, I fully support this rule change. In fact, anyone illegally in the country that claims to be a citizen should face felony charges. It is not fair for &quot;sanctuary&quot; jurisdictions to load up on illegals to gain unfair overrepresentation in the House and electoral college.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0171,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-t71v-kqyb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposed changes to the taking of the census ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0172,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-t95v-l1yx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is James Hunter, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. Those who break our laws should not benefit fro, representation that are due illegally.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0170,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-t6qt-sr3r,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I feel that the rules changes are adequate and should proceed. I think the changes are important and should happen.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0173,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tagi-tmwv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Andrew L. Solow, I am a citizen of the United States of America; and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. I strongly believe that these sections should be adopted into Part 15 of the C.F.R. when the final rule on the 2030 Census is published later this year. Only American citizens should be included in the US Census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0174,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tbv3-fg0c,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is James Kitchens, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;  As citizens of the USA we are all equal and should be counted as one group of Americans.   People here illegally should not be counted or represented in any way for any purpose other than deportation.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0175,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tcd7-kl3g,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  It is high time that these changes be made to restore the integrity of US elections.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0177,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tcyb-avn9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is a good start...  Next do Census workers who falsify documents &amp; make it a felony!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0178,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-temc-yi0m,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert Tracy Thomas, I am a citizen of the United State of America, and I am commenting as an individual.  I am addressing sections 60.4 AND 70.2 of the NPRM.  I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published.  I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  The reasons for my support as as follows:  1.  Only U.S. Citizens should be counted in the census because this is our country &quot;We the People&quot; and people who are not here legally should not be counted asbeing  part of our country.  2.  As citizens of this country, we are a united people, we should not be segregated based upon our race and ethnicity in the census and counted as such.  We are all Citizens of the United States of America.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0176,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tcjf-9xfm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I agree 100% with the NEW proposed census regulation 60.4 &amp; 70.2.  I believe it will more accurately depict the actual American populace that have a stake in keeping America special. People who are not citizens should never be counted when that count helps make decisions of distributing that money back to it&#39;s citizen. Also there is no reason to allow non-citizens to dictate our Congressional apportionment! There is a lot more to say, but I think that is enough to suffice!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0179,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tf8y-mtww,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing proposed 15 CFR &sect; 60.4 and proposed 15 CFR &sect; 70.2. I support both sections as published.<br/>Section 60.4:  Foreign citizens<br/>I support counting for apportionment only U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents as of Census Day, and not counting other foreign citizens.<br/>The constitutional purpose of the decennial census is to apportion House seats among the States according to the number of persons who belong to those States as inhabitants. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has never meant mere physical presence. From the First Census Act forward it has included an element of settled connection and allegiance. A person whose legal right to remain is temporary, conditional, or nonexistent does not have that connection in the same way a citizen or green-card holder does.<br/>Apportionment is not a social-services headcount. It decides who holds political power for a decade. Including people who cannot vote, who may be required to leave, and who have no durable stake in the laws of the United States shifts seats and Electoral College votes toward jurisdictions that happen to contain large temporary or illegal populations. That is a political outcome, not a neutral count. Other federal surveys and administrative records can still measure how many people are physically present for planning and funding formulas that Congress writes separately. The apportionment base should not be one of those formulas.<br/>Legal permanent residents have been granted an open-ended right to live here and a path that can lead to citizenship. That is a bright, administrable line. Temporary visa holders, students, and persons here illegally are not in the same category. Treating them as interchangeable with citizens for representation rewards illegal presence and temporary presence with permanent political weight. Incentives work in both directions. If presence alone confers representation, the incentive to control the border and to distinguish citizens from non-citizens weakens.<br/>Section 70.2: Restrictions on certain demographic questions<br/>I support the rule that no question about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation shall appear on the short-form decennial questionnaire or any questionnaire used for the enumeration of the population.<br/>The job of the enumeration is to count persons once, in the right place, for apportionment. Race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation are not required for that job. They have been collected for other purposes on other instruments, including the American Community Survey. Those instruments can continue. Putting identity categories on the form that produces the official national count invites the census to become a tool for sorting Americans into official groups rather than a count of equal persons.<br/>Granular identity data has costs as well as uses. It trains institutions and citizens to treat ancestry and orientation as the primary facts about a person. It also creates a standing demand that every future policy be scored by those categories. That is a choice about political culture, not a requirement of Article I or the Fourteenth Amendment. A color-blind enumeration does not erase history or prevent later analysis. It simply refuses to make group classification part of the constitutional headcount.<br/>Advances in record linkage and commercial data already allow detailed demographic profiles without putting those questions on the short form. The Bureau itself notes that. The short form should stay short and limited to what the Constitution requires.<br/>Conclusion<br/>I urge the Department of Commerce and the Census Bureau to adopt &sect; 60.4 and &sect; 70.2 as proposed. The 2030 Census should count citizens and lawful permanent residents for apportionment, and it should not use the enumeration questionnaire to classify the population by race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation.<br/>Respectfully submitted.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0181,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tjbd-gtvz,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The only individuals that should be counted in the 2030 US Census are US citizens and Lawful Permanent Residents. I&rsquo;m tired of having my citizenship value diluted by illegal alien criminals who shouldn&rsquo;t be here in the first place much less factor into the formula for congressional apportionment. Enough is enough.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0180,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tj4t-eotb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is William Moraca, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The major reason for my support is that I believe the concept of &quot;usual residence&quot; should be limited to citizens of the United States.  As a result, those in the USA illegally or legally via Visa or as an employee of a foreign government should not be counted.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0183,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tjzs-jw6j,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposed changes to 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2. These changes will make the census better represents the population who should be represented in the federal government.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0184,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tkwx-whf3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I approve of this rules change. This is vital to our Republic. Non-citizens should not be able to vote, and they should not be counted for apportionment of Congressional seats. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0182,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tjkz-6sne,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Illegal immigrants should NOT be counted.  They are not American citizens and should not sway Congressional representation. Citizens, by our Constitution, are the ones who allowed to vote and since they vote for Congress they should be the only ones counted. Remember it&rsquo;s the illegal immigrants that should not be counted. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0186,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tm32-1c8n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Charles Dockter of New Palestine Indiana wholly and completely agrees with the proposed Federal regulations as written for review. God save America.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0187,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tm38-artk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My name is Dora Peery and I am commenting as an American  citizen. I believe that only citizens should be counted for apportionment of representation in government. Only citizens should be entitled to government benefits. I support 60.4 and 70.2 as written. Thanks,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0185,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tljo-4q9j,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes to the census would harm the fabric of our society as Americans. Not since 1860, when Black slaves were counted as 3/5 of a person, has the US failed to count each member of its populace as a whole person. Undocumented immigrants are people who live here, and there is no reason not to count them. In fact, we have never left information about race and ethnicity off of the census. This change is meant to disenfranchise and lose track of whole groups of Americans. The changes would affect both voting rights and federal funding for numerous programs, such as Medicaid, Medicare, and SNAP, along with school funding. We need accurate data about every person in the US to have as the foundation of the rest of the work the government does to serve us.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0188,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-to0t-z482,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Joseph Myers, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo; Non citizens should not have any influence over how the United States of America conducts its business.  Thank you for your attention to this matter.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0189,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tq3z-rs0n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Richard Norman Boutin II, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. No one who is not a legal resident of the United States of America should be counted or included in any fashion in a legal census of citizens in this country. Doing so dilutes the importance of being a citizen.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0191,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tsn5-xp26,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Christin Hale , I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: Citizens of the United States of America have the right to elect our representative government. Non-citizens have no right to be elected. The Federal government was created to be by and for the Citizens of our great country, not for visitors, Non-citizens, or illegal aliens. Allowing proportioning of seats in our House of Representatives, or proportioning of any other Federal programs or benefits, according to the presence of aliens is unconstitutional on its face.<br/><br/>Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0190,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tsd0-8k05,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I fully support these changes to the census. Only those legally residing here should be counted. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0194,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tv3s-169b,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Non-citizens should not be counted toward the assignment of legislative districting.  Non-citizens should also not be counted toward the distribution of government funds or resources.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0192,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tt36-v22t,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"We are Dale and Sue Thompson and we are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as a family. Specifically, we are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for our support are as follows.  We believe that only United State citizens should be counted in the census data to apportion representatives to the states. Only citizens should be allowed to participate since they are the ones who can vote. We also believe that requiring granular details for each person by requesting their race, ethnicity, etc. is not needed for the census.  Other agencies already collect this data regularly at the state and federal lever and are far more accurate.  This would be redundant and provide no better information. Thank you for this opportunity to comment on these proposed changes.  We welcome their addition to the NPRM.  Sincerely, Dale and Sue Thompson",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0193,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tusn-e4tj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is R. L. Wheeler, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;  I believe that only citizens should be included in the census of the United States, as this count plays a very important part in the allocation of our goverment activities.  It is not fair that some areas have large illegal immigrant populations and this allows for increased reprsentive in the congress.   ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0195,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tv9o-ek9p,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Yes. Do it. Only citizens ought to get representation. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0196,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tvhd-plvx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Shawn Reed, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are I believe only us citizens should be represented in census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0197,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-twav-z9an,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is a common sense rule change that is LONG overdue.  I wholeheartedly support this change.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0199,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ty8c-p86w,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The census was originally developed to understand the makeup and distribution of the population, and to track changes over time so that the government could be better informed. Asking questions on citizenship and resident status is no different from ethnicity, home type, and all of the other salient data points collected every 10 years. This question should absolutely be addressed as part of the 2030 collection. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0198,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-twhi-vjhd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Alan David McMurry.  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>The proposed language of the revised NPRM conforms to the intentions of the Founding Fathers as prescribed in the U.S. Constitution.<br/>Think you for your attention to this matter.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0200,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tyjt-m4dv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. Only citizens of the United States should be counted in our census. It is unfair to the citizens of the United States if their representation is changed by counting non citizens. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0202,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tyob-bz11,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Michelle Reed, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are I believe only us citizens should be represented in census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0203,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tzwm-wo4z,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,AMERICAN BORN CITIZENS ONLY !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0201,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-tylv-7q13,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Clayton L. Williamson, Jr., I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>- Apportionment of Congressional seats should only recognize US citizens and legal residents.<br/>- Expenditures of Federal, State, and local funds should only be in support of US citizens and legal residents.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0204,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u0gw-di31,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Only US citizens should be counted in any and all US census, period! Proven by government approved documentation as to citizenship, (birth certificate, etc.). Also, census workers must be vetted, in 2020, a census worker asked me how many people lived at an address that I own and I said none and I was told that they counted 4 people at the house so they would just enter that. Of course the census worker spoke broken English.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0206,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u1a1-s3ym,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is CELESTE ACHTERHOF ____________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>THEY MAKE COMMON SENSE. NO FOREIGNER SHOULD HAVE ANY IMPACT ON THE GOVERNMENT IN ANY MANNER WHATSOEVER IN THIS COUNTRY.  JUST BECAUSE THEY HAVE THE ABILITY TO SNEAK ACROSS THE BORDER, LIVE IN THE COUNTRY AND AVOID DEPORTATION SHOULD NOT ALLOW THEM TO HAVE AN IMPACT ON OUR CENSUS AND APPORTIONMENT.  THIS IS A COMMON SENSE AND MUCH NEEDED CHANGE, LONG OVERDUE.  I FEEL THE SAME WAY ABOUT ALL THE DEMOGRAPHIC INFORMATION.  IT ONLY SERVES TO DIVIDE THE COUNTRY.<br/><br/>THANK YOU,<br/>CELESTE ACHTERHOF<br/>I APOLOGIZE FOR THE ALL CAPS...I HAVE ARTHRITIS MAKING SHIFTING PAINFUL.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0205,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u15x-5y19,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I agree with the changes proposed in Published Document: 2026-18481 (91 FR 57524) and make the following observations:<br/> <br/>-  Non US citizens should never be counted in our census.<br/>-  There are only two human genders,  They are the only ones which should be found on the census (or any other federal/state govt document).<br/>-  There is NO need for the government to know anyone&#39;s sexual orientation.  Zero, Zip, Nada.<br/>-  There is no reason for the government to know anyone&#39;s race or ethnicity.  Zero, Zip, Nada.<br/><br/>The only reason for a government to know such things is to make unconstitutional laws/rules/regulations that treat people differently under the law based on their sex, race, or ethnicity.  In short, that is Apartheid.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0209,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u1hd-qtiu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only count American Citizens and legal permanent residents. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0207,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u1bn-34vl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Edward Whatley, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0208,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u1cm-nzzs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dan Keller, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. I support these changes because they are prudent and in accordance with the intent of the census and resulting federal processes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0212,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u26m-8xdv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only us citizens should be included in the census survey....,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0210,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u1v8-oprn,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I think verifiable demographic data such as race and ethnicity should be collected but it must be that condition at your birth and not an assumed identity. <br/>I fully agree that only legal residents should be included in census as enumerated in our laws.<br/>I will remain anonymous in my comment.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0211,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u1zx-lxjd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Count U.S. citizens only.  Count people and dont do it by itemizing them by race, religion, sexual orientation or anything else.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0213,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u282-vsly,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I would like to stare that O am in favor of the proposed rule changes to 60.4 and 70.2.  American citizens should be the people counted in a census. Counting non- citizens puts legal American citizens at a distinct disadvantage in many ways including congressional representation. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0214,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u3bz-ndiv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Sharon A. Williamson I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>* Apportionment of Congressional seats should only recognize US citizens and legal residents.<br/>* Expenditures of Federal, State, and local funds should only be in support of US citizens and legal residents.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0215,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u3rv-ju1i,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is James Edward Sheehy, and I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual and I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Apportionment of Congressional seats should be based upon the number of citizens in each state and should not be skewed by the inclusion of illegal aliens and other non-citizens in the States. Also, the racial, ethnicity or sexual orientation of citizens should not be collected by the Census since it is irrelevant to the apportionment process. <br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0216,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u3rv-lm1g,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is James Edward Sheehy, and I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual and I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Apportionment of Congressional seats should be based upon the number of citizens in each state and should not be skewed by the inclusion of illegal aliens and other non-citizens in the States. Also, the racial, ethnicity or sexual orientation of citizens should not be collected by the Census since it is irrelevant to the apportionment process. <br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0218,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u4b1-kabk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am a born and bred Citizen of the USA. I strongly agree that ONLY US Citizens be included in every Census. States and municipalities should never receive compensation for importing noncitizens in order to increase their representation in Congress. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0217,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u40b-zoj9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are James L and Elizabeth W Studer.  We are a citizens of the United States of America, and are commenting as a a married couple. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: Illegal aliens unfairly remove representation from actual US citizens, Limits resources available for same, and is long overdue. In summary, we support the proposed changes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0219,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u73g-apyv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"We fully agree with the new rules that the census has proposed in counting ONLY U.S. citizens in the 2030 census!  Wayne R. and Debra A. Dubbs, both born and raised in the United States and full citizens!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0221,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u8iq-tvcv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Bruce Crane, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>On &sect; 60.4 (counting only citizens and lawful permanent residents for apportionment):<br/><br/>Apportionment is a political act, and representation should track a political community. Congressional seats and Electoral College votes are allocated based on the census count. Limiting that count to citizens and lawful permanent residents ties representation to the population that is legally part of the American political community, rather than to transient or unauthorized populations who have no lasting stake in that district&#39;s governance.<br/>It corrects a known distortion from prior censuses. Under the 2020 methodology, states with large unauthorized or temporary-visa populations gained congressional seats and Electoral College weight that states with smaller such populations did not, even though those additional residents cannot vote. This shifts political power away from the citizen population that the census is constitutionally meant to represent.<br/>It affects federal funding formulas as much as it affects seats. Census counts drive the distribution of federal dollars for infrastructure, healthcare, and social programs. A count restricted to citizens and legal residents ensures that formula-based federal spending is calibrated to the population with a permanent legal claim on those resources, rather than inflated by populations whose presence may be temporary or unauthorized.<br/>It removes an incentive structure some states have exploited. Because the prior rule counted &quot;all persons&quot; regardless of status, some jurisdictions had a financial and political incentive to avoid cooperating with federal immigration enforcement, since a larger uncounted or unauthorized population could still translate into more seats and funding. Restricting the count to citizens and LPRs removes that incentive.<br/>It is consistent with how most of the developed world treats census-based political apportionment, where legal residency or citizenship status is a standard threshold for inclusion in figures used to allocate political power, even though total population may be measured separately for other statistical purposes.<br/><br/>On &sect; 70.2 (barring race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation questions from the census form):<br/><br/>It reduces the government&#39;s collection of sensitive personal data. Race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation are among the most sensitive categories of personal information a person can disclose. Removing these questions from a mandatory federal form limits the government&#39;s footprint in collecting data that, if breached or misused, could expose individuals to real harm.<br/>Granular demographic data is increasingly redundant with private data. Commercial databases, other federal surveys (like the American Community Survey), and third-party data brokers already collect this information from those who choose to provide it. A mandatory decennial form is not the only or best vehicle for it.<br/>It reduces the risk of political manipulation of census categories. Historically, how racial and ethnic categories are defined and subdivided has itself become a politically contested exercise, with categories added, split, or removed based on advocacy pressure rather than a neutral statistical need. Removing these questions from the core enumeration removes that battleground from the census process.<br/>It reflects growing concern about re-identification risk. As statistical techniques improve, even anonymized granular demographic data becomes easier to reverse-engineer into personally identifiable profiles, particularly at the neighborhood or block level. A simpler, less granular form limits this exposure.<br/>It refocuses the census on its constitutional purpose. The core function of the decennial census is an accurate headcount for apportionment, not a demographic profiling exercise. Streamlining the mandatory questionnaire toward that core purpose is a reasonable narrowing of scope.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0222,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u9bj-qae2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"From Oklahoma, with all due respect&hellip;<br/><br/>Just because you Ameri-can use the Three-Fifths Compromise to legally justify changing rules you don&rsquo;t like, doesn&rsquo;t mean you Ameri-should. On the most basic, fundamental human level, it&rsquo;s asinine that it needs to be said that legislation that decided that enslaved people could be counted as less than a whole person has no place being cited as a legal precedent. Regardless of someone&rsquo;s documented status, no human deserves to be dehumanized just because you don&rsquo;t like them. <br/><br/>In The Year of Our Lord 2026, that shouldn&rsquo;t need to be said to adults who are responsible for anything more consequential than a pet rock. I&rsquo;m sure some lawyer around the office feels proud of themselves for that legal argument, but honestly y&rsquo;all should be ashamed of yourselves. Who raised you?<br/><br/>And then, imagine invoking the Three-Fifths Compromise, and later in the same document saying that the Constitution is colorblind, that everyone in America is treated equally, with respect, and that DEI initiatives are unnecessary. Do you actually believe the things you say? <br/><br/>The demographic information about race, sexual orientation, gender, etc. that these changes wish to cut out is vital to identifying the systemic issues that perpetuate health disparities and other social injustices. The authors of these proposed changes are either painfully naive to this reality and therefore unqualified to hold their positions, or willfully playing a role in ignoring the existence of the people whose identities they don&rsquo;t want to know about. Internalized stigma is painful and I hope you find the strength to work through that in therapy. <br/><br/>These proposed changes are dumb and frankly, I&rsquo;m mad that I had to spend time on my Saturday on this. <br/><br/>With all due respect.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0220,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-u8f8-v38b,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree with this change. We should never have allowed illegal aliens to be counted in 2020. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0225,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ua58-71e6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Daniel L. Pratt, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0223,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ua3d-78g2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Richard Lambright I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language CSS  Vaof section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0224,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ua4g-bua4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes should be enacted. Counting illegal aliens, non-citizens, and any other people not specifically identified in the US Constitution, i.e. American Citizens, should be illegal.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0226,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uaud-q28r,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"<br/>I support the proposed language of sections 60.4 and 70.2 as published.<br/><br/>My name is Stephen David Leonard.  I am a native-born citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM regarding residence and demographic criteria for inclusion in the 2030 and subsequent census enumerations. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>The results of the census determine representation in Congress, relative influence of states in electing the President, and distribution of federal funds  for myriad federal programs. All of these functions should only be determined by citiens and legal, permanent residents of the United States. It is a travesty that past policy changes have widened these benefits to illegal aliens -- by definition people who should not and have no right even to be in this country, let alone participate in its governance. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0228,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ud24-s763,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I urge you to approve all changes being considered for the 2030 census. It is imperative that foreigners whose true allegiance is to countries othe than the United States are denied the opportunity to influence the representation of true United States Citizens. Thank you. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0227,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uci6-a3br,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Allison Patterson__, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>Only citizens of this country should receive the benefits of this great country.  Every eligible / legal person should be counted.   They should not be marginalized because so many people are counted that have no right to be counted.  <br/><br/>Resources should be assigned accordingly.  The House of Representatives should be delegated according to citizens home location.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0230,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uf3v-9tfx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I completely support the proposed changes in the Residence Criteria and Demographic Questions for the 2030 Census. For reasons of apportionment, especially, we should not be counting individuals who are here on temporary assignment from another country, vacation (tourist visa), or have broken our laws by being in the country illegally. Nor should those here on student visas be counted. All of those are not subject to the jurisdiction of the USA ultimately, and should not have representation in Congress or for federal fund appropriations.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0229,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ueuj-dfup,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Richard Perry Taintor, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>US citizens are the ones who should decide US issues, therefore, representation should be proportional to the count of citizens. Since we are all equal, there is no value to collecting attributes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0231,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ufax-edv1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only American citizens should be counted in the Census. And you ask too many personal questions that are none of your business.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0234,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ui3m-thda,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name TJ Moore. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I, however, do not support the language of section 70.2 as published. The reasons for my support or lack of support are as follows.<br/><br/>It is common sense that anyone in the U.S. illegally does not get counted in the U.S. census. Our government represents U.S. citizens and those in the country legally ONLY. Criminal illegal aliens do not receive representation of the U.S. government. Yes, all illegal aliens are criminals as they broke our immigration laws to enter our country illegally.<br/><br/>As for section 70.2, demographics of the United States as a country as well as the state, county, and city levels should include race and ethnicity. But not sexual orientation as that is a personal choice and it&#39;s not the governm business who someone is having sex with. Although I support the elimination of racism, ethnicity and race are used to determine many important facts to help disadvantaged individuals (i.e. education and poverty levels). If we don&#39;t know that more people of a certain demographic need more help than other demographics,  how can we concentrate on helping them???",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0232,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ug6k-5dwj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Michael Robinson, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>Regarding 60.4.<br/>The US census should only account for individuals legally and permanently residing in the United States. To include any other persons is to basically negate the validity of the census.<br/><br/>Regarding 70.2.<br/>The purpose of the census has nothing to do with demographics. There is absolutely no needs to collect any information beyond a head count of each US citizens. Period.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0233,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uhr8-8ep3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Licata, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>1. Citizens, and only citizens should be entitled to vote in elections in the United States and its territories. Only those who are legally and permanently in the United States should be counted for the purposes of representation in Congress or any state or local legislative division. <br/><br/>2. Those who are in violation of the law, or who are in the United States for a specific purpose with only temporary residence should not be counted and affect the apportionment of representation as they may not (probably should not) be present in the country at all during the full term of apportionment. Thus, the territory in which they are registered receives an unfair advantage in apportionment and an unfair advantage in distribution of legislative power. This violates the Supreme Court mandated principal of &quot;one man one vote.&quot;<br/><br/>3. Further, non-citizens should not be permitted to take advantage of the social programs for monetary or other support that is offered to citizens. These &quot;free-riders&quot; take the tax dollars of American citizens. If a state votes to give their tax dollars to non-citizens, that is a choice they are allowed to make under the principal of &quot;states rights.&quot; But, such a state has no right to take money from the citizens of other states to distribute to persons not legally in the country. Therefore, non-citizens (excluding those who are legal permanent residents) must not be counted in the census so that social program money is not apportioned to them.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0235,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ui5u-pi5f,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jon Prisbe, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: only citizens of the United States should be counted in the census and United States citizens should all be equal in the eyes of the government. Identifying demographics should be ignored to ensure this equality.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0236,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ujdx-tq0i,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Ed Thomas and  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>The blessings of this country were created, earned, and are the property of US Citizens and should not be given away to non-citizens who have no long term interest in protecting our way of life.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0237,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ujqa-afso,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,If you count anyone besides citizens you are traitors and should get capital punishment for treason. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0238,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ul9y-xwoo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert Trey Cannon and I am a citizen of the USA, and I am here as an individual. Thank you for the proposed changes 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2. Census should be for US Citizens and not those who were let in illegally to this country.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0239,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ulpb-xlhe,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Richard A Daniels, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published except for it extending beyond &quot;Bona Fide&quot; Citizens.  Legal Permanent Residents (LPR) or any non &quot;Citizen&quot; should not be counted unless they have obtained Citizenship through naturalization.<br/> I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>The allegiance and loyalty of LPR&#39;s is not a guarantee to the extent of a born or naturalized citizen. A review of the current LPR eligibility reveals to me participant groups that are involved in the current wave of fraud, crime and cultural revision being revealed by our government.  Representation is for citizens, not maybe citizens, therefore only citizens should vote.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0242,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-umlu-qe96,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Terryl Hedrich, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>1. Those who entered our country illegally should not have a voice equal to mine as an American citizen.  <br/>2. The census numbers are used in many ways to affect my life as an individual - determining the number of representatives that will represent my and my state at the federal level, as well as the state and local level.<br/>3. I love my country and am a patriot.  Those who come here illegally have no such love for my country by the mere fact they didn&rsquo;t come through legal channels.  Consequently their voice negates the voice of every American.<br/>4. Those who arrive in our country illegally don&rsquo;t get assimilated into our culture, the culture of the United States.  They remain separate from and desire to change the culture of the US to align with their own native country. <br/>5. Identifying ethnicity on the census merely fosters and promotes separatism, racism, and fuels hostility among the races.  We are Americans.  We should be counting Americans regardless of their race or ethnicity.<br/><br/>Thank you for your attention to my comments.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0240,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ulw2-vc20,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Bruce Shipley,  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. These are good changes to the regulations and brings some level of sanity and balance back to the true purpose of the Census.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0241,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ulxh-yhb7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I fully support changes to the census. If you can&#39;t legally vote it&#39;s ludicrous you should be counted for congressional apportionment or electoral votes. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0245,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-un7r-xd70,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I 100% agree with the proposed change to the census rules. Only citizens should be represented in our nations policy decisions. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0243,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-umv6-1ait,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposed changes in the census counting and questioning. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0244,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-umzb-aofm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This rule change is welcome.  Apportionment should be by citizens who have a firm connection to the United States.<br/><br/>Should states or other political entities choose to conduct their own investigative survey, they will be free to do that.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0246,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-unzu-n9oo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This proposed change is long overdue. Not only should we protect the sovereignty of our voting processes, but we must also protect our citizens who have paid taxes, supported our government and way of life, sometimes with the last full measure of devotion. Our government must not be weakened by allowing the power of our own citizens to be weakened by allowing those without legal status the same level of belonging.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0249,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uoyp-4les,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Peter Klein. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reason for my support is as follows: only citizens should be counted for representative apportionment.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0247,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uoaa-dnz8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,No non-citizen should be counted for apportionments to Congress. Citizens only to be represented by duly elected representatives.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0248,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uocq-g4zs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jeffery Brent Olsen, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:  Citizens of foreign nations do not need to be counted as citizens of the sovereign United States of America.  Only citizens of the United States of America should be counted for the apportionment of Representatives to the Congress, foreign citizens and illegal aliens should not impact the representation of the people of the United States in the legislative branch.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0251,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uqyh-covs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree the only U.S. citizens are counted in the 2030 census. How does anything else make sense? Their is a reason being a citizen is so special. We don&#39;t give the right to vote (or be counted to effect our representative numbers freely to non citizen. All you have to do is read the founders letters to one another to understand how to interpret the constitution in this and many other matters.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0250,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uqne-nk90,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am concerned that our census doesn&#39;t accurately count US Citizens.  To ensure this occurs, please restrict the census to US Citizens only, or add the question to the census - Are you a US Citizen?  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0252,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-urre-gq2e,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Frank Panzica, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>I agree that the purpose of the census is to determine congressional representation of citizens.  Counting others dilutes one of the key rights of a citizen, the election of a representative.  Further I agree that making the short form as simple and non-controversial as possible supports this first purpose.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0255,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ushy-1mss,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>We are all created with unalienable rights. There is no reason for the US Census to collect more information that stated. The personal information collected in recent previous census years has been invasive, unnecessary, dehumanizing and divisive. This new language corrects prior over reach of the census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0253,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-urzv-i138,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The census should have two categories.  Number 1: legal residents and citizens.  Number 2: Other. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0254,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uscz-cwd9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is George Bailey, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Apportionment should be done based on the number of people that are residents of an area. People that are visiting or there illegally should not be counted. Counting them allows for influence on the operation of the country by foreign actors that do not have the best interest of the United States, its citizens or its legal residents and can relocate or concentrate a portion of legislative power to areas where people do not reside. This apportionment should be done without regard to race or religion. I agree with the aspiration that we are created equal and that none of us are special unless all of us are special together. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0256,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uvzq-og16,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Re: Docket No. USBC-2026-0628 &mdash; Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions<br/><br/>My name is Stephen Colebrook, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing &sect; 60.4 and &sect; 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the Census Bureau&rsquo;s proposed regulations at 15 CFR part 60, including proposed &sect; 60.4, and 15 CFR part 70, including proposed &sect; 70.2.<br/><br/>Support for proposed &sect; 60.4<br/>The decennial census exists first and foremost to support the constitutional allocation of seats in the House of Representatives among the States. The apportionment population should reflect persons whose usual and lawful home is in the United States and who have a durable connection to the Nation&rsquo;s political community.<br/><br/>Proposed &sect; 60.4 advances that purpose by distinguishing foreign citizens who are lawfully admitted for permanent residence, approved asylees, and approved refugees from other foreign citizens temporarily present in the United States. A visitor, temporary worker, student, tourist, or other person present in the country on a time-limited basis ordinarily maintains his or her permanent home outside the United States. Such persons should not be treated as residents for congressional-apportionment purposes merely because they happen to spend time here during the enumeration period.<br/><br/>This distinction is sensible because congressional representation is fundamentally about representation of a State&rsquo;s permanent population. People whose permanent residence remains abroad retain their primary civic and political connection to the country of their permanent home. Counting temporary foreign visitors for apportionment can increase a State&rsquo;s congressional representation even though those individuals are not part of the State&rsquo;s enduring resident population and have no long-term stake in its political representation.<br/><br/>The proposed rule provides a clearer, more administrable standard than a system that attempts to infer &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; from temporary physical presence alone. The rule appropriately recognizes lawful permanent residents, approved asylees, and approved refugees as persons whose lawful residence and durable ties are in the United States, while treating other foreign citizens as residents of their foreign country unless the regulation&rsquo;s criteria provide otherwise.<br/><br/>I therefore support adoption of proposed &sect; 60.4.<br/><br/>Support for proposed &sect; 70.2<br/>I also support proposed &sect; 70.2, which would prohibit questions concerning race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation on the short-form decennial census questionnaire and on questionnaires used to enumerate the population.<br/><br/>The constitutional purpose of the decennial census is an enumeration for apportionment. Questions about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation are not needed to determine the number of people who should be counted or the State in which they should be counted for that purpose. Removing questions that are not necessary to enumeration helps keep the decennial census focused, understandable, and more likely to receive complete and accurate responses.<br/><br/>This change would also reduce the amount of sensitive personal information collected directly by the federal government in connection with a mandatory population count. In an environment where personal data are widely collected, shared, and exposed through commercial and institutional databases, the government should collect only information demonstrably necessary for the specific statutory and constitutional function at issue. For the decennial census, that core function is counting people and locating their proper residence for apportionment&mdash;not compiling demographic profiles unrelated to the enumeration itself.<br/><br/>The Census Bureau may still use limited questions, such as biological sex, age or date of birth, and household relationships, when they materially assist with identity resolution, household matching, data-quality checks, or prevention of duplicate counting. That is consistent with proposed &sect; 70.2(b). In contrast, the questions prohibited by proposed &sect; 70.2(a) do not appear necessary to establish an accurate apportionment count.<br/><br/>For these reasons, I support adoption of proposed &sect; 70.2.<br/><br/>Thank you for the opportunity to comment.<br/><br/>Sincerely,<br/><br/>Stephen Colebrook<br/>Lexington, South Carolina",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0257,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uw04-wgkj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Citizens only on census,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0258,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ux4h-aojb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I concur: Only US citizens and  green card holders need be counted for the purposes of apportionment. Foreign citizens here temporarily or illegally need not be counted for purposes of apportionment.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0259,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ux62-366f,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America and of the Commonwealth of Virginia, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published because it seems to ensure that illegal aliens will not be counted for apportionment. I consider that important because they are not voters and should have no say or influence on elections or politics. In fact, they should be deported. I further support the language of section 70.2, also as published because I do not want my country further balkanized into irrelevant interest groups.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0260,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uywq-wijy,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Thank you for listening to our input on these much needed rule changes. <br/><br/>I believe these rule changes make absolute sense and will benefit this country and its citizens who will be properly represented in Congress.   <br/><br/>Please move forward with these changes. <br/><br/>Please do not be fooled by lobbyists and special interest groups who will also be commenting negatively against these changes. <br/><br/>They only care for themselves and not our country.<br/><br/>Thank you,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0261,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-uztg-ze9n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only count us citizens on census,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0262,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-v3pp-8e8d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Kerry Crouse, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0263,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-v3sd-hzw6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I have now read about the census rule changes from multiple sources and I am in complete and absolute support of these simple changes. Illegal foreign invaders and all other non-citizens who do not have specific legal status to be here should be counted in the census. We should not be electing representatives to promote the interests of those who have no right to receive benefits at the taxpayers expense. This is a ridiculous, ruinous policy and must be stopped. It is just another means of corrupting our system by the organized crime syndicate known as the democrap party.  As to the race, ethnicity, sexual orgientation issue I can only say, THANK GOD! We are constantly told we should not consider these identifiers in any way, yet, every time we turn around we are told we MUST consider these qualifiers first and foremost over all else. Every form we fill out asks us to identify our race, sexual orientation, ethnicity, etc., even though we are also told it&#39;s not important. Make up your minds. I refuse to answer those questions because none of it IS supposed to matter and it&#39;s nobody else&#39;s business. Thank you for bringing sanity back to the census. YES, I ABSOLUTELY SUPPORT THESE CHANGES.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0264,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-v646-z43n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a US Citizen who has seen the value of being a US citizen eroded due to the massive number of non-citizens or illegal residents in this country, I fully support the changes indicated.  By allowing those who broke the law to come here and remain here to be included in representation and allotment of tax dollar appropriation is not a representative government.  Only American citizens and those who reside here legally deserve to have a say in how the government is run and how our tax dollars are spent.  Illegal immigrants, students and business executives who do not owe an allegiance to this country but rather the country they legally call their own, will not reflect what is best for Americans.  We have seen this in the massive riots taking place in colleges across the country by students who owe their allegiance to those who are trying to exterminate the Jewish culture and population.  We fought a war to prevent this from happening overseas.  We see this in areas of the country where representatives running for office support extreme policies not endorsed or encourage by the majority of American citizens.  They are in fact in opposition of the very country they seek to pass laws for and in some cases represent the benefit of their home country over the USA.  Finally, it removes the ability for individuals who reside within a state to have the power to influence decisions within the state by relocating to a state that is more in line with their views and concerns.  If states lose population because they put illegal aliens above their own citizens, they should lose representation in Congress and the ability to funnel tax dollars to support illegal immigrants. In the end the Census should be used for what it was originally designed to do, provide an accurate count for the purposes of allowing the representative government of the American Citizen and Legal Residents, not the ability for a large number of induvial who are not vested in the core values and culture of America and its people.<br/><br/>As to the demographic questions, I don&#39;t see why we need such granular information to handle the intent behind the census.  Representation and tax dollar allocations should not be based on demographic information.  It in fact supports tribalism and not unity which is central to our American founding.  One nation under God, does not care about race, gender or any other distinguishing characteristic of an induvial, but rather means we stand together under a government that represents the people who have an allegiance to this country and the success of it.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0265,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-v6d5-t2ra,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Chris Moore, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Counting non-citizens in the census disenfranchises the apportionment of resources and the representation of the citizenry, It gives undeserved and unfair allocation to states with large non-legal populations, and greatly skews resources in their favor. Focusing the census away from those who are not legally a part of the long-term fabric of the United States will curtail this practice and allow for a more just process.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0267,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-va40-ssel,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only US citizens should be counted by the census. Illegal aliens should never be counted.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0266,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-v8xl-7nb7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I believe this is a long overdue change to our census process.  The people that should be counted in the census should be Only American citizens.  Those who are here legally.  I have several friends who went through the American Citizenship process Legally.  I am appalled at how our country has given free reign to illegal citizens who abuse the generosity of our American system.  I know actually American citizens who Need help, yet are denied, when illegals seem to receive it at the drop of a hat, depending on which city or state they are in.  Please make these changes as proposed to protect Real Americans.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0268,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vb8s-v58v,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Laura Savage, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reason for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>The American citizen will be properly counted and be properly represented in the United States Congress.<br/><br/>Thank you<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0269,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vbg9-2vzh,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is ____heidi evans _________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0270,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vci4-0f0w,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert Breglio, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>   I feel that only citizens and legal permanent residents should be counted to decide how representation and its attendant federal aid should be apportioned.  To count others, especially those who are here illegally, will dilute my representation.  This country should look after its own and the census should reflect that focus. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0271,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vcw3-htwk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I&rsquo;m the grandchild of Danish immigrants and I a born native citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>Section 60.4 correctly ties apportionment to people who hold a lawful, durable stake in the United States: U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents. Apportionment determines how political power and federal resources are allocated for a decade. Those decisions should rest on the population that the Constitution and our laws treat as members of the political community, not on temporary presence. Counting only citizens and green-card holders for apportionment is a practical way to reserve sovereign political rights to those who belong to the nation, rather than to anyone who happens to be inside the borders on Census Day.<br/>That rule also produces a cleaner, more defensible count. It reduces the incentive to treat illegal presence or short-term status as a source of extra representation and funding. It aligns the Census more closely with who can vote, serve on juries, and hold the long-term obligations of citizenship. For a citizen who wants the government to look after its own people first, this is the straightforward, workable standard.<br/>I also support section 70.2. Removing race, ethnicity, and sexual-orientation questions from the short-form decennial questionnaire keeps the count focused on the constitutional purpose of enumeration. Those detailed identity categories can be collected in other surveys if Congress or agencies need them. Putting them on the official population count invites the Census to become a tool for group claims rather than a simple headcount of the people who constitute the nation. A shorter, citizenship-centered form is more practical, less politicized, and more consistent with equal citizenship.<br/>I urge the Department of Commerce and the Census Bureau to adopt sections 60.4 and 70.2 as proposed.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0272,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vdg8-mvrm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support this long overdue rule change to the census. It will ensure that the representation of American citizens and lawful residents is not diluted by unlawful and temporary residents. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0274,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vduk-2dal,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Carol Mills, I am a natural born citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reason for my support is as follows.: I believe places like California, New York, and Illinois as well as all blue states have been encouraging illegal aliens to come to their states so that they can be counted and then those numbers be used for apportionment in Congress. If this were no longer possible then the democrat/socialist ( read&quot;communists&quot; ) trying to take over the Republic would not have one of the most important arrows in their quiver for regaining control over our lives. Make no mistake that the DSA now leading the democrat party loathes natural born Americans andwishes to flood this country with as many illegals as it takes to overwhelm and crush our Republic. Not allowing the count of illegals will put a good crimp in their plans.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0273,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vds7-lil2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dan Savage. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reason for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>The American citizen will be properly counted and be properly represented in the United States Congress.<br/><br/>Thank you<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0275,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vffh-g1h0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is how I would like the U.S. Census to be conducted in 2030:  ONLY U.S. citizens, including dual citizens and green card holders (legal permanent residents), WILL BE COUNTED in 2030 for apportionment. ALL other foreigners will NOT.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0278,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vheq-18vk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Since the government spends our tax dollars, Representation should be for citizens only, not the rest of the world. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0276,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vg22-u8w4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Clearly the founding fathers did not intend to count persons who were not supposed to be here in the census. removing them will make a the perverse wanting them in certain states to go away and allow them to start caring for citizens instead of those here illegally,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0277,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vggo-p85v,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I SUPPORT the Proposed Rule in its entirety.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0279,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vhyh-4akf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Cindy Mattern, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I also support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are that I feel very strongly that we need to clean-up the census information to represent ONLY U.S. citizens, especially for those allowed to vote; and we don&#39;t need to collect race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation which can further provide divisiveness.  None of that should matter, we are All AMERICANS!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0280,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vi9y-shjw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only persons residing in the United States of America LAWFULLY can be counted in the census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0281,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vifz-gh80,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed changes to the 2030 Census, including changes that would ensure the census provides a clear and accurate picture of the U.S. citizen population.<br/><br/>In my view, U.S. citizens should be the primary population counted for purposes of determining political representation and allocating government resources. The number of citizens in a community is directly relevant to determining the needs and interests of the people who are eligible to participate fully in our political system. Including large numbers of noncitizens in population-based measures can affect congressional representation and the distribution of federal and state resources without accurately reflecting the citizen population that those systems are intended to serve.<br/><br/>A modern census should use updated methods and technology to improve accuracy, reduce unnecessary costs, and protect the confidentiality of census information. I encourage the Census Bureau to move forward with changes that make the 2030 Census accurate, efficient, secure, and appropriately focused on the U.S. citizen population.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0283,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vkhc-nei0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Martha J. Drennan, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>To section 60.4 I firmly believe that only American Citizens should be counted in our census. People who have dual US citizenship or have legal permanent residency are the only foreign citizens who should be counted in the census for purposes of apportionment. Our legislators do not represent foreigners. They only represent American citizens, those with dual American and another citizenship, or those with permanent legal residence status. NO EXCEPTIONS!<br/><br/>As to section #70.2, those demographic questions regarding race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation are irrelevant and superfluous. For the purposes of counting how many human persons live in each location within the US borders, only the fact that there are legal citizens there is relevant. The legislators represent ALL human beings in their district or jurisdiction. PERIOD. They do not only represent certain sectors of that population. They represent all the people so they only need to know how many people are in their district. The additional data can only be used to segment the population and result in legislators only representing various factions. This is divisive and destroys the unity of our populace. It does not serve the people, it only serves special interests. We are not identity groups. We are Americans. We are human beings who are Americans. Our representatives and senators represent all of us equally or they are failing to uphold their oath to the Constitution of the United States. I want no legislators that think that way. This will be a great start to get &quot;special interests&quot; out of certain aspects of government. Thanks for allowing me to speak my mind as an American citizen and a Veteran of a foreign war.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0282,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vipo-p2cs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I&#39;m addressing section 60.4 and 70.2 of the notice of proposed rule making.I support section 60.4 and 70.2 as published.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0284,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vlmp-63ay,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Its common sense; only American citizens should be included in the Census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0285,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vnb0-2ts6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,, Count only US citizens!!!!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0286,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vnd1-s6s0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Emily Anne Boykin, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:  The United States of America Census and its use should only apply to United States citizens , and the demographic information should not matter for the Census.  Thank you",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0288,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vo4q-alzj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Cynthia Cullen, I am a citizen of the United States and am commenting as an individual. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I support the language of section 70.2 as published.  The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>60.4) People who are here temporarily and not a citizen/lawfully permanent resident will unfairly impact the number of congressional seats in States. We must look out for Americans first.<br/><br/>70.2) We are all created equal. Stop dividing us into special/not special groups. We are all Americans. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0289,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vq92-gaal,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mary Antonetti, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  <br/><br/>In section 60.4 I believe that only citizens of the United States should have a legal say in the representation of their wishes. If non-citizens would like to have their opinions heard they should become legal citizens and then they may participate in the election of their representative. I believe non-citizens should not have a representation in how this country is run. <br/><br/>In Section 70.2 I believe the granulation of previous census is detrimental to the laws for all. By further separating citizens into smaller and smaller groups the laws which should affect all are only for a small minority. And by focusing on minority populations (by whatever divisions are made) representatives are elected that only cater to those minorities. Our laws should be for the whole citizenry, not small parts. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0287,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vns2-m4bs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Melinda Colvin. I was born in the United States and am a citizen, commenting as an individual. I am giving my opinion on the NPRM for sections 60.4 and 70.2. I support both of these changes. Section 60.4, relating to citizenship makes sense. It should not include counting those who are in the country illegally. They are not entitled to representation in our congress. As for Section 70.2, it makes no sense to include questions that are irrelevant to representation. These personal answers can be ascertained by other means.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0293,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vtsw-85p4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is in regard to USBC-2026-0628. I feel that only US citizens should be included in the census. People who are in our country illegally should not be included in ANY reporting done regarding US citizens. Our country is at the brink of financial ruin because of the billions of dollars spent on non citizens who did not pay taxes to cover monies given to them via food stamps, free phones, free rent, free healthcare. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0292,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vtrm-p4g9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only citizens should be counted. Please make this rule change.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0290,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vqgg-9yuh,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am reaching out concerning the proposed rules which are under consideration.  <br/><br/>Ooncerning CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2 I whole heartly agree with the changes.<br/><br/>I put myself on the line for this country via military service to hold the line for this Great Republic.<br/><br/>I believe that only AMERICANS should be counted in the census.  Citizens and green card holders (legal permanent residents), should be counted in 2030 for the purposes of citizenship.<br/><br/>Thank you for the opportunity to respond.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0291,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vt7p-pqs7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Thank you for this proposed rule. Unfortunately, I have watched as certain Supreme Court justices mistakenly interpreted the 14th amendment to our nations detriment. In terms of apportionment, only permanent legal residents and US citizens should ever be counted in a census. I whole heartedly support the proposed change to accomplish this.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0294,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vvkr-7728,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I (Carol Huddleston) am commenting on CFR 60.4 and CFR 70.2.  Only U.S. citizens and legal green card holders should be counted in the census.  I was appalled that it wasn&rsquo;t done that way in the first place for isn&rsquo;t the census supposed to count people to apportion federal aide?  I approve of the rewritten sections 60.4 and 70.2.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0296,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vxtg-gqev,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"In 2030, count only U.S. citizens for the Census. No foreigners (non-U.S. citizens). No LPRs (non-U.S. citizens). No illegal aliens (definitely non-U.S. citizens).<br/><br/>In 2030, count only U.S. citizens. Period. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0295,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vx5r-wg50,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Tim Oakley, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>60.4<br/>Foreign citizens in the United States<br/>I agree only legal foreign citizens ( green card ) should be counted. Illegal should not. (why would you? ).<br/><br/>70.2<br/>Nothing past actual identifying facts: Male / Female/ DOB<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0297,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vyl8-zm3y,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Greg Grider, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reason for my support is that only US Citizens should be counted for the purposed of Congressional Representation, as only US citizens are legally allowed to vote. It is a quite simple proposition; I fail to see why anyone would have trouble grasping it.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0298,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vzd7-qrx5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I would like to see the changes 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2 implemented. This will assure that only citizens or legal residents are represented.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0299,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vzd9-5qqe,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I would like to see the changes 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2 implemented. This will assure that only citizens or legal residents are represented.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0300,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vzls-xiq0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"<br/><br/>My name is Michelle Crandall. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Besides the power of the citizen&rsquo;s vote, is the power to &ldquo;vote with their feet&rdquo;. The consequences of citizens moving out of a state in large numbers, cause the state to lose representation. The state where the citizens move in large numbers, then increases representation in another state. By apportioning representation based solely upon number of individuals in the state alone, neuters that power. <br/><br/>It is not the role of the Census to count race, sex, etc. The emphasis should be citizens and permanent residents. By emphasizing demographics, citizens are segregated into groups which divide us rather than unite us. The consequences of counting these in the past, have weakened the United States. There are other means by which demographic information can be gathered. The role of the government should be to undergird and strengthen the principles contained in our Declaration that all men are created equal. The constant segregation of individuals into groups erodes that principle in the minds of the citizens. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0301,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-vzq7-jzq2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"<br/>My name is Grant Laughlin I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. We cannot allow law breakers to influence and interfere with our country. Only actual US citizens should be counted. This is just common sense. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0302,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w09c-uuoz,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am a citizen of the United States and support the two new proposals for the 2030 census.  Keeping the count to citizens only is important to me,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0305,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w4ir-5cb9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposed changes regarding not counting illegal aliens for representation purposes and requesting minimal information on race,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0303,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w09d-cj4m,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am a citizen of the United States and support the two new proposals for the 2030 census.  Keeping the count to citizens only is important to me,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0304,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w4fv-pv5w,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only US citizens should be counted in the census. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0308,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w6dc-5265,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I would like to say that I support both of the proposed changes for the 2030 census.<br/><br/>Since representation in our government is based on population numbers provided by the census, I think it is appropriate that ONLY American citizens be counted.  Foreigners and, especially illegals, shouldn&#39;t be included just because they happen to be in our country at the time of the census.  The effects are long ranging when people like that are counted and reported in the census.<br/><br/>I also agree with proposed section 70.2.  The census is to count Americans, not ethnic groups, sexual preferences etc.  If we are all Americans we should be considered that in our official census and not a hodge-podge of groups based on criteria that really have nothing to do with how many citizens we have in our country.<br/><br/>Thanks for letting me have this opportunity to express my views on this important matter.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0306,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w5fw-3xbl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is John F. Hinkle, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;  Only citizens of the United States of America should be counted since this census is used to determine how we are represented.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0307,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w5oo-j67i,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I totally agree with this rule change.  it is long overdue.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0309,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w6vi-3d6c,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only citizens of the USA should be counted in the upcoming census. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0311,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w7uz-znrk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is __Laura Hoffmeier___________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo; Foreigners have no allegiance to the United States and thereby should not receive the benefit of representation. I would go further and deny any dual citizen from being counted in the census. A split allegiance should not be rewarded in either country with representation. Additionally, race, creed, sex orientation and any other mental or physical health issues should not be included. We are to be one nation, under our Sovereign God and not separated by immutable or fake characteristic traits. Thank you",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0310,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-w6w9-6ovy,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Only American Citizens should be counted in the Census.  Representation, Federal funding apportionments, and other services are benefits of Citizenship and should be accurately reflected in the Census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0313,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wb0z-49l8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Patricia Hubel. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>Only citizens of the United States should be counted for apportionment representation,<br/>I also support removing ancestry identity as it is irrelevant, as well as multiple gender identities and sexual orientation.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0314,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wb1a-1ctc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,," I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. There is absolutely no reason to apportion representation based on the number on non-citizens in a given area.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0312,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wa5d-x8o9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Bill, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>USA CITIZENS are entitled to representation in our elected government NOT non-citizens as defined in the regulations.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0317,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wfrh-x66e,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Lawrence Feldman I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: basing representation on people with no legal right to be in this country dilutes the will and voting power of Citizens as well as of people who entered this country through time/effort/expense in a Legal manner (respecting the sovereignty &amp; laws of the US)",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0315,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wdly-iu7a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My wife and I (Arthur and Danielle Wilson) are in agreement on this issue and would like to make a comment as a married couple. We are citizens of the United States of America and are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for our support are as follows:<br/><br/>1)  When non-citizens are factored into apportionment, it artificially boosts the population counts of states with high numbers of temporary or undocumented residents. This unfairly favors the allocation of resources to areas that desire to wrongly utilize resources for non citizens and illegal immigrants that should be applied to legal full time citizens of the USA. It also reduces the voting power of legal citizens in states with smaller immigrant populations.  We feel strongly that representation in the U.S. House of Representatives should reflect the actual legally voting political community.<br/><br/>2)  We favor a historical interpretation of the Constitution&#39;s phrase &quot;whole number of persons&quot;. We believe that the Framers intended &quot;persons&quot; to mean permanent &quot;inhabitants&quot; who hold allegiance to the United States, rather than individuals who are temporarily or unlawfully present.<br/><br/>3) We believe that allocating congressional seats based on non-citizen populations rewards states for policies that attract or shelter non-permanent populations, shifting federal power away from states relying on legal and natural citizen growth.  This would allow for the empowerment, acceleration, and perpetuation of an &quot;illegal and migrant state&quot; that again takes resources away from other states that prefer not to squander power and taxpayer money on non-taxpaying individuals. If states choose to support this kind of growth, the citizens of other states should not have to subsidize it or have their representative power diminished because of it.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0316,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-we5h-v8cp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I wholeheartedly agree and endorse this proposal.  These simple and constitutional changes bring the census back to what it was originally intended to do.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0318,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wfup-xrj4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jeffrey Warren Johnston, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>It is simply, pure common sense!t",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0321,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wgrw-y6wm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Tommie Martin, a citizen of the United States.<br/> I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;  I believe that only citizens should be counted.  I have lost family members to wars that the United States has fought.  <br/><br/>We need only those who have or are US citizens.  Who follow the rules set by Congress.  <br/><br/>I see what is happening in Great Britain and want no part of such a situation. <br/><br/>And then you can go on as much as you like about",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0320,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wg1w-675q,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"No undocumented immigrants, illegal aliens, or any similar non citizens ought be counted in each census. My representation is unfairly diluted by those persons. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0319,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wfvq-etcl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am in total agreement for the new 2030 census rule changes, Only citizens and authorized persons residing in the United State of America should be counted. God bless the USA.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0322,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wgs1-e41n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Tommie Martin, a citizen of the United States.<br/> I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;  I believe that only citizens should be counted.  I have lost family members to wars that the United States has fought.  <br/><br/>We need only those who have or are US citizens.  Who follow the rules set by Congress.  <br/><br/>I see what is happening in Great Britain and want no part of such a situation. <br/><br/>And then you can go on as much as you like about",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0323,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wh7o-r9hl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Hebert, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I fully support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  Apportionment of congressional seats and funding must only be accomplished based on the accurate number of American citizens in each area.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0325,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wino-f8i4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,," My name is Daniel F Belcher I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows - I believe only American citizens and legal residents have the right to be counted for purposes of asset management and distribution of the taxes we have paid to the government in good faith. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0324,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wial-4jf3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jon Dougherty, I am a citizen and veteran of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. <br/><br/>I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: <br/><br/>In my opinion, one of the most devastating consequences of the 2020 Census emphasis on granularity is that it encouraged an unhealthy pattern of valuing self-segregation in one&rsquo;s identity in U.S. society over the core values in our Declaration of Independence, in which we held the aspiration to the truth that we are all created equal.<br/><br/>Thank you for your attention to this.<br/><br/>Cordially,<br/><br/>Jon Dougherty",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0326,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wkoh-vysp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Joetta Forsyth, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>Since the Census determines house of representative numbers it should be based on citizens who can vote. We also should not divide citizens by race, ethnicity, etc.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0328,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wncy-h1fq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,There is no doubt that citizens and permanent legal residents alone should be counted for apportionment.  Many states are illegally aiding illigal aliens to stay in their state just so the can be counted for their power.  People in this country illegally are not constituents and should not counted toward apportionment.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0329,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-won4-e5ev,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Doyle Kaufman. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reason for my support is that the U.S. census was created to distribute political representation in Congress and apportion taxes fairly among the states based on their population size.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0327,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wmwr-pikk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hello. My name is Alberto Rodriguez and I am in favor of the proposed new language for 60.4 &amp; 70.2. I believe the Founding Fathers wanted congressional representation based on legal residents and were not interested in specific demographic information such as race, gender, let alone sexual orientation ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0331,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wpy6-9koa,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Bill Freeman, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo; Illegal Aliens are not citizens and should not be counted towards the Census for apportionments.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0332,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wpy6-pbz9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: only citizens should be counted for representation since only those citizens can eventually vote in the resulting voting districts. Additionally, the separation of citizens into racial categories can create pernicious outcomes if used improperly. Removing those categories removes the possibility of pernicious results.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0330,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wotc-yduc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,," I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: 60.4 should go much further then it does, no one, except Americans should be counted in the census because foreigners do not have a vested interested in protecting this nation and given that counting those who are foreigners but not Americans will change electoral counts that could potentially put this nation at risk! I also support 70.2 , the census concerns only Americans and by ending the questions that focus on anything but being American makes clear, there is no discrimiatnation, it also makes clear to all , that we are all equal in the eyes of the law. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0333,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wqv7-pg40,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Gerald Holl, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;  I believe the adoption of these changes will provide a much more more just country for the true citizens. I also think they will make the country less divisive. These changes will also help preserve the values of what America was founded upon and not allow undue influence from those who are not citizens and yet are striving to change the values and character of this country. The values that have made it the most prosperous country the world has ever known. <br/><br/>Gerald Holl",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0335,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wrqp-f8tc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dan Shapiro, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:  Representation in Congress should be based on the number of qualified citizens residing in each state.  Federal financial support for the states should also be based on the number of citizens in each state.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0334,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wrmi-nsl8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only count US citizens!!!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0336,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wtxz-q1he,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John S. Sturges, I am a citizen of the United States of America, born here [X] and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>Both my parents came from immigrant parents. My father was descended directly from the Sturges&#39; who founded Fairfield, CT in 1610. My mother born in Slovakia, 1921, immigrated in 1931 and became a citizen due to her father John Ridzi gaining citizenship earlier and bringing his family to the US after several years of hard work during the Great Depression.<br/><br/>I believe that only citizens should be counted in the US Census. This includes those who have immigrated and gained citizenship though working here. Neither race nor ethnicity should matter in the Census.<br/><br/>Thank you,<br/><br/>John S. Sturges",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0338,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wuwf-q7ju,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I wholeheartedly support the changes to the US census to only count legal residents and citizens for apportionment. Counting untraceable, undocumented individuals is rife with problems. It incentivizes political parties to encourage illegal immigration to boost their delegations, and it makes it a temptation to fraudulently boost the census numbers by creating fictional identities that cannot be verified. <br/><br/>Counting the illegal immigrant population would of course be valuable for other aspects, but not for apportionment.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0337,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wu63-vae5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Sheryll Jeffers. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>1. Lawful permanent residents should be counted in the US census. <br/>2. Those individuals who are here illegally should not be counted as residents since they have no lawful permanent status and should not, therefore, be counted in the US census for the purposes of apportionment or representative government. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0339,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wvd3-2u0k,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I fully support the rule changes that exclude foreigners from the counting for apportionment. Only American citizens are allowed to vote and do only American citizens should be counted for apportionment.  Such a rule is consistent with the historical past, especially in the founding era and neerly the first century of American history. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0341,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wwgt-vi3e,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jeffrey L. Garrett, and I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting for myself and my wife, Diane K. Garrett.  We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 60.4 as published. The reasons for our support are as follows:<br/>* We believe that the census should accurately reflect the legal citizens of the USA, not illegals somehow living within our borders. We understand that the census helps determine the representation of each State in the House of Representatives, which should only reflect actual, true citizens of this country. This is particularly an issue with the huge amount of illegals now residing unlawfully within the USA.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0340,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wvif-fmuf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Re: Docket No. USBC-2026-0628 &mdash; Proposed 15 CFR &sect;&sect; 60.4 and 70.2<br/><br/>Preamble and Basis for Comment<br/>I submit this comment as a citizen of the United States, a registered voter in the State of Texas, and a longstanding participant in our democratic and civic processes. For more than thirty years, I have consistently exercised my right to vote in statewide and national elections. Throughout my adult life, I have also participated in each applicable decennial census and taken seriously my responsibility to ensure my household was accurately represented.<br/><br/>I offer that background to explain the perspective from which I comment. I regard voting and participation in the decennial census as important civic responsibilities. How the census determines who is counted, where they are counted, and what information is required directly affects representation and public confidence in the process.<br/><br/>With that perspective, I respectfully submit this comment in support of proposed &sect;&sect; 60.4 and 70.2.<br/><br/>Section 60.4 &mdash; Foreign Citizens in the United States<br/>I support proposed &sect; 60.4, including the distinction between U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents, who would be counted at their usual residence, and other foreign citizens, who would not be included in the apportionment count.<br/><br/>In my view, this approach better reflects the constitutional purpose of the decennial census and the historic concept of &ldquo;usual residence.&rdquo; Representation in Congress should be based on persons who have a durable and established connection to the United States and to the State in which they are counted. Citizenship provides the clearest such connection, while lawful permanent residence represents legal authorization to settle in the United States on an indefinite and lasting basis.<br/><br/>I therefore agree with the proposed rule&rsquo;s distinction between lawful permanent residence and immigration statuses that are temporary, transitory, or do not provide lawful authorization for permanent settlement. Physical presence alone should not establish the kind of enduring residence contemplated when population is used to allocate political representation among the States.<br/><br/>I encourage the Census Bureau to adopt &sect; 60.4 substantially as proposed and retain a clear standard centered on citizenship, lawful permanent residence, and a durable legal connection to the United States.<br/><br/>Section 70.2 &mdash; Restrictions on Certain Demographic Questions<br/>I strongly support proposed &sect; 70.2(a), which provides that questions concerning race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation will not appear on questionnaires used to enumerate the population.<br/><br/>The fundamental task of the decennial census is to count the population accurately for representation. Separating that core function from collecting race, ethnicity, sexual orientation, and similar characteristics returns the census to a simpler and more unifying purpose: counting people rather than unnecessarily categorizing them.<br/><br/>Americans have many different backgrounds and characteristics, but the constitutionally required enumeration need not emphasize those differences. Removing these questions treats respondents first as individual members of a common national community rather than primarily as members of demographic categories.<br/><br/>I also believe &sect; 70.2 has important privacy and data-minimization benefits. Detailed demographic data can increasingly be combined, segmented, analyzed, and used for purposes far removed from the basic responsibility to conduct an enumeration. Limiting information collected through the decennial enumeration reduces opportunities for government, commercial interests, political interests, or others to parse the population into ever-narrower identity categories using data gathered as part of a constitutionally mandated count.<br/><br/>This does not prevent legitimate demographic research. As contemplated by proposed &sect; 70.4, the Census Bureau may continue collecting demographic information through the American Community Survey and other surveys and data sources. Demographic research can continue without making such classifications part of the basic decennial enumeration.<br/><br/>For these reasons, I encourage the Census Bureau to finalize &sect; 70.2 as proposed. A census focused on determining who should be counted, where they should be counted, and accurately enumerating that population better serves its constitutional function while promoting privacy, simplicity, and a greater sense of common national identity.<br/><br/>Conclusion<br/>I support adoption of proposed &sect;&sect; 60.4 and 70.2. Section 60.4 appropriately ties apportionment to persons with a durable and lawful connection to the United States, while &sect; 70.2 separates enumeration from classifications based on race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation. Together, these provisions would produce a census more closely focused on its foundational purpose: an accurate count for representative government.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0342,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-wx4g-qtz4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Peter Kamper. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. My reason is that only legal citizens of the United States of America and illegal residents shall not be allowed to vote in the United States of America.<br/>Respecfully submitted SEptember 12, 2026 ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0343,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x076-2uhm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only natural born and naturalized citizens and legal residents should be counted in the census.  Persons who are citizens of other countries or only in the USA for work assignments or school should NOT be included.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0344,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x0v8-x6ft,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Brian Robinson, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/><br/>The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo; A man&#39;s responsibility is to the welfare of his family first and then if at all possible he is to assist his neighbor&#39;s. A man who robs his children of bread and causes them to go hungry even in the name of feeding a stranger is not a good father. The same goes for our  nation. Until the lawful citizens of this great land have all been cared for which is definitely not the case we cannot in good conscience give its resources to non citizens. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0345,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x1ht-iook,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Catherine L. Klouda. I am a citizen of the United States of America, of the state of Connecticut, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows...<br/>Just as it held true when the Constitution was written, non-citizens should not be used to amass power. Elected representatives should represent only citizens and legal residents. If immigrants want to be included under representation, then they should come here legally. <br/>Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0346,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x1wu-bj1g,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Steven Hall.  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>I believe only those people that legally and permanently reside in the US as US citizens should be counted for purposes of apportionment for representation in Congress.  <br/>Anyone residing in the US without legal status should not be counted for apportionment purposes because, due to their lack of legal status, they have not demonstrated a willingness to abide by the laws enacted by those people apportionment intends to represent them.  Additionally, if non-legal residents are not counted for apportionment, the impetus for certain state governments to attempt to flout US immigration laws and policies is minimized.  It should not be incumbent upon legal US residents and citizens to forfeit a portion of their vested interest in the self-governing principles outlined in the Constitution to someone that chooses not to participate fully in adherence to the law, and accept the responsibilities inherent therein. <br/>Regarding the proposal to not include demographic questions on the Census questionnaire, while that information may have been useful in the past, the government&#39;s access to various databases in the modern era no longer warrant the collection of that data.  And, if indeed &quot;all men are created equal&quot;, and we no longer count certain persons as less than one, that information is no longer applicable.  <br/>The collection of Census data should be strictly limited to that information necessary to determine the number of people eligible to participate legally in the process of self governance in the United States, as outlined in our founding documents, so that states may be properly represented according the number of legal residents and citizens that reside therein.  And states that choose not to participate in the enforcement of federal immigration law as currently written should not be rewarded and strengthened for their lawlessness.  For these reasons, I support these rule changes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0347,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x4gh-nsv4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is M.L. Rowe. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>Our Founding Fathers never imagined there would be a faction in our representative government who would work to subvert the Constitution and twist the language used to mean something they clearly did not intend. Yet now we have nearly half of our country seeming to believe that any person in our country at the time of census should be counted - legal, illegal, visitor, student, or what have you. Neither could they imagine that same faction would encourage citizens of other nations to come by the millions illegally or that leaders of other countries would unleash their portions of the worst of humanity on our borders to receive the benefits reserved for our citizens once they made it in country. Thank you for making these changes to clarify what should be obvious to all. I pray you are successful.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0349,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x5pz-rsew,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Hamilton, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.<br/>I believe the only people that should be counted are US citizens and legal residents. It is that simple. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0350,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x70e-wtvx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Andrew Stich, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are simple. We are a nation of laws. By allowing states who encourage people to enter the country illegally and reside in their state illegally to increase their representation in the Federal government are flaunting our laws and our Constitution. Only Citizens can legally vote. So if a state gets extra Congressional representatives due to non citizens then their Citizens have a heaver weight in their Federal representatives votes than Citizens in other states. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0348,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x4ha-qpdr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Why in heaven&rsquo;s name would be better to have people that don&rsquo;t actually live here LEGALLY? It was always a dumb ass effort by liberals to have all of these people that aren&rsquo;t eligible to vote so they can illegally vote for some dumb ass Democrat! How&rsquo;s that for a common?,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0351,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x76r-lqj0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Justin, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0353,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x7xm-3we6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Travis, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: <br/><br/>1) Regarding section 60.4, only US Citizens, dual US citizens and lawful permanent residents should be counted in the census. This is a welcomed change. Having other categories including those in the country illegally count towards congressional seat apportionment only encourages more illegal immigration and dilutes the representation of US citizens. <br/><br/>2) Regarding section 70.2, the count should be race, gender, sexual preference  blind. This kind of granular detail only encourages us to focus on what our differences are instead of what we have in common. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0352,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x7wm-j95m,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jerry, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. As a voting legal citizen I feel that it is wrong for non citizens to be counted in the census. It throws the balance of the house of representatives off because it should be based on the people that can vote for them, not people that just happen to be in their state visiting or illegally or any other reason. States with a higher number of non citizens should not get more representation than states with less non ciizens.   ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0354,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-x9s6-wqum,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I My name is Chad K. Caswell , I am a citizen of the United States of America   and I am commenting as an individual.  I am addressing Sections 60.4 and70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of Section 60.4 as published, I further support the proposed language of Section 70.2 as published. <br/>I believe these changes will have a very positive effect for the population enumeration . <br/>I <br/>I<br/> <br/>My name is Chad K. Caswell, I am a citizen of The United States of America, and am commenting as an individual.  I am addressing Sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  I support the language  of Section 60.4 as published.  I further support the language of Section 70.2 as published.  I  believe only Citizens of the United States or lawful residents of the United States should be counted in the census.  Citizens of forign countries as of April 1 of a decennial census year should not be counted for apportionment.  I also believe questions about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation are divisive and should not be included in the census.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0356,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xdro-5n18,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only American citizens should be counted in the American census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0355,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xawt-ynxv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Comment on Docket No. USBC-2026-0628 / RIN 0607-AA75<br/>Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions<br/><br/>I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing proposed 15 CFR &sect;&sect; 60.4 and 70.2.<br/>I support the proposed language of &sect; 60.4 as published. I further support the language of &sect; 70.2 as published.<br/>On &sect; 60.4 (Foreign citizens in the United States)<br/>I support counting only U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents at their usual residence for apportionment, and not counting all other foreign citizens present on Census Day.<br/>The Constitution requires an enumeration of persons &ldquo;in each State&rdquo; for the purpose of apportioning the House of Representatives. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of enduring tie and allegiance, not mere physical presence. Temporary visitors, persons here on nonimmigrant visas, and persons present without lawful status do not have that tie in the same way citizens and green-card holders do.<br/>Including everyone who happens to be on U.S. soil on April 1 distorts representation and the later distribution of federal resources. States and localities with large temporary or unlawful populations gain seats and funding that should belong to communities of citizens and legal permanent residents. The 2020 approach treated presence as sufficient. The proposed rule correctly reverses that and prioritizes people who are here legally and permanently.<br/>This change does not prevent the government from collecting other data through ACS or administrative records. It simply keeps the constitutionally required apportionment count focused on the population that has a lasting stake in the country.<br/>On &sect; 70.2 (Restrictions on certain demographic questions)<br/>I support the proposed ban on questions about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation on the short-form decennial questionnaire and any other questionnaire used for the actual enumeration of the population.<br/>The core constitutional job of the decennial census is to count persons for apportionment. Race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation are not needed for that count. Putting those questions on the short form turns a population count into an identity survey. That encourages people to sort themselves into official categories and then treat those categories as the basis for political claims.<br/>The Bureau already has other vehicles (ACS, surveys, commercial data) if detailed demographic statistics are required for other programs. Those vehicles should stay separate from the enumeration used for House seats. Keeping the short form limited also reduces respondent burden and the risk that granular answers can be combined into identifiable profiles.<br/>A color-blind, characteristic-blind enumeration better matches the principle that we are equal as persons before the law, not as members of officially recognized identity groups.<br/>I urge the Census Bureau to adopt both &sect; 60.4 and &sect; 70.2 as proposed, without weakening the language or adding exceptions that would put race, ethnicity, or sexual-orientation questions back on the enumeration form or that would restore counting of non-citizens and non-LPRs for apportionment.<br/>Thank you for the opportunity to comment.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0358,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xf69-ef4l,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I think only citizens of the United States should be included in the census due to impact on my representatives in congress and how they are apportioned.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0357,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xf3p-5a2o,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Get it Done!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0359,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xglo-8u4e,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Craig O&rsquo;Connor, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are simple: only U.S. citizens, including dual citizens and green card holders (legal permanent residents), should be counted in 2030 for the purposes of apportionment. All other foreigners should not. By definition, this excludes several categories of persons who were included in the 2020 Census; namely, all undocumented persons, foreign students, foreign executives assigned to their companies&#39; U.S. offices, and, the oddball category of 2020: foreigners in the U.S. on temporary work permits living in California.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0362,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xihz-02hj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Maurice. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. 1) I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. 2) I support the language of section 70.2 as published. <br/><br/>With respect to 60.4, in the language of the regulation &quot;The sole Constitutional purpose of the decennial census is to apportion the seats in the U.S. House of Representatives among the States based on the number of qualifying persons &ldquo;in each State.&rdquo;&quot; Those not here on a permanent legal basis should not be considered as qualifying. They are legally prohibited from voting, they are not entitled to representation, and thus they should not affect the apportionment of seats. Further there is so much potential for error in counting those not here legally, so devoting resources to attempting this count is wasteful.<br/><br/>With respect to 70.2, in the regulations words &quot;The sole Constitutional purpose of the decennial census is to apportion the seats in the U.S. House of Representatives among the States based on the number of qualifying persons &ldquo;in each State.&rdquo; &quot; The ethnicity, race, sexual preferences, and any other supposedly distinguishing characteristics of citizens have no bearing on the count. Therefore at best asking the questions results in every person who completes the form wasting their time, and the tabulation and analysis of this unnecessary data wastes the resources of the Census Bureau, and hence wastes our tax dollars.   <br/><br/>Thank you for your consideration.<br/><br/>David Maurice",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0360,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xgqy-iemo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Under NO circumstances should foreign students we included in CENSUS 2030! It is violation of USA CITIZENS RIGHTS to include CHINESE STUDENTS WHO STEAL FROM USA,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0361,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xic9-op3g,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Make the changes to 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0363,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xjha-qoyg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Steve Jarvis.  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>1.  All eligible persons should be counted as Americans solely and not on ethnic, or identity groups. <br/>2.  Persons should only be included in the census if they are productive citizens or legal residents adding to the American economy. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0364,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xjoa-b8n0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Edward Paul Brownlee, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>I concur in thinking illegal residents and temporarily legal residents are not to be allocated a piece of congressional representation.<br/><br/>I concur in thinking previous censuses were overly and unnecessarily granular in parsing the population.<br/><br/>I do not think the comments of residents of other nations who do not live in this nation should have their say in determining questions in the American national census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0365,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xk49-i5np,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a citizen of these United States, I approve of the language that rids us of taking micro minutiae data on race or ethnicity in the census, it is irrelevant data and only useful to the people and organizations that seek to divide and non-profit from the data.  I also approve of excluding those who are here without the protection of dual citizenship, from the opportunity to be counted in the Census.  Do not count illegal and unauthorized persons in the census of These United States.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0366,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xkll-16dd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Joanne Hamilton, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. Only American citizens should be counted !!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0367,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xlvi-evzc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I fully support NOT counting noncitizens in the 2030 census, especially as I live in a &quot;sanctuary&quot; state that houses a lot of people who are not supposed to be here. In order to be counted, everyone that is counted should have a vested interest in our country and only our country. Please pass the proposed rule to count only actual citizens whose primary allegiance is to the USA. We need our government to support legislation that helps citizens of the USA, not foreigners. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0368,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xm5e-rzdw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Only legal resident US Citizens should be counted in future census activities.<br/><br/>Fred Tolen<br/>9826 Carmel Ct<br/>Lone Tree, Colorado 80124<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0369,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xmhm-5cua,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mark Bopp, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>(a) census results are used by the government for many reasons that affect US citizens (and legal permanent residents)....and it should therefore be those same people (US citizens and legal permanent residents) counted in the survey, and<br/>(b) personal matters (some opinion-based) should not be collected by the government and counted as factual data.<br/><br/>Sincerely,<br/><br/>Mark R. Bopp<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0370,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xryl-fj97,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are Charles and Katrina Brothers , we are natural-born  citizens of the United States of America, and are commenting as a married couple. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  Only citizen should be counted!!!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0373,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xwcp-q6n1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Cameron Schlehuber, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>America should look out for our own first. All undocumented persons, foreign students, foreign executives assigned to their companies&#39; U.S. offices should not be counted any more than people visiting the U.S. on tourist visas or persons here for business trips. Their homes are NOT in the U.S. and given the purposes of the Census should not be counted in the Census. The Census should count only lawful permanent residents.<br/><br/>Questions on the short form about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation are divisive in a country that is great need of unity. Eliminate those questions.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0371,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xs9d-zfur,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hi , I am a united states citizen , and I would like to comment on the proposed rule changes of 60.4 and 70.2.  I support those  two changes , because the original intention of the united states census was to count people for the apportionment of congressional seats. I am a resident of Ohio, and I have seen each census Ohio, lose congressional seats to states like California and New York. Illegal immigrants should not be counted because they are not eligible to vote.  We need to get back to the original intention of the United States census, which is to count people so congressional seats can be apportioned to each state. There is also no reason to ask what someone&#39;s ethnicity is either. I also helped do the 2010 Census as a temporary enumerator and having to ask too many invasive questions like that make people not want to do the census.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0372,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xuma-uoe5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I have read the changes and I support and approve. Please move forward with the changes. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0374,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xwxa-ofp1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The Census is supposed to be a head count of Americans and nothing more. For representation purposes it should only count those of voting age since those elected represent them.  When other information is gathered it leads to much effort and irrelevant information being gathered that become ammunition for various interest groups.  In other words keep it simple.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0375,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-xzav-pzjc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Non-citizens should NOT be counted in the census.  If they are, those who are here illegally or temporarily certainly should NOT be included. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0376,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y3du-mbjj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Albert P. Franco, II, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>1) As apportionment of representation is a critical Constitutional requirement of the Census and since counting of illegal aliens and other people that are not entitled to live in USA permanently dilutes the representation of the citizens and other lawful permanent residents, the change is not only necessary, it is long overdue.<br/>2) The census should be closely limited to its historic Constitutional purposes. By adding countless questions to filter the population based on ideological, sexual preference, and other non-essential characteristics, the census becomes more time consuming to complete, more invasive and indeed, prejudicial through implied government support through recognition of irrelevant data. The Census should be narrowly tailored and focused to accomplish its essential purpose.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0377,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y3rb-1rfx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mark Houde, a US citizen. In regards to sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM, I&#39;m in firm agreement to the proposed rules as written. I feel these rules will give the census a true indication of the population of US citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0378,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y42r-5dgm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is William Hodges, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0379,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y43t-g1h4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Thomas Herrmann, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Only c<br/><br/>US citizens should be voting for US leaders. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0381,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y56f-4uqb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The census should only count citizens. It should not count visitors. It should not count illegals. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0380,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y46y-m168,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is _____________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:  1 since the census is used to establish the number of members of the House of Representatives it should only include citizens and others in the country legally.  2 Since it data is used in allocating many of the monetary benefits a state receives from the federal government, iibelieve that these monete4y benefits should be only go to those in this country legally.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0382,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y66h-i2kd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Lucy Spencer, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  I firmly support the Constitution that only citizens of The United States have the right to vote and this needs to be clarified and firmly held.  Allowing anyone who is not a citizen is not only illegal but those aliens do not have a vested interest in the success and survival of our country.  I also do not believe &quot;specialty&quot; groups should be represented any differently than any other citizen.  No one is more special than any other citizen.  That being said, I do have a suggestion.  As a genealogist, some questions from the old census forms have a lot of valuable information, i.e., place of birth of the citizen and their parents, occupation, level of education, etc.  But that is just my wish list. Thank you and have a great day.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0383,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y7mx-f3ki,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please ensure that only people in our country legally are counted.  To do otherwise is against the purpose of the census and against our country. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0384,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-y9rt-m5rs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I believe there should be a change to the 2030 census. <br/>Foreign citizens in the United States.<br/><br/>(a) Citizens of foreign countries who are also either citizens or lawful permanent residents of the United States as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Counted at their usual residence in accordance with &sect;&thinsp;60.2.<br/><br/>(b) All other citizens of foreign countries as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Not counted for apportionment.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0385,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yalu-496q,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Lee. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I fully support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further fully support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0386,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ycyp-o4ry,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"A congressional representative should represent the citizens of the United States. Including non-citizens in the census gives representatives an incentive to allow illegal immigration to swell their districts with numbers to outweigh American citizens&#39; influence and true representation. Diminishing the effect of one man, one vote. Demographic information ends up slicing and dicing the populace, identifying groups to give unequal attention.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0387,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ydjw-b77k,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is ___Gary H________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>It is unfair to US Citizens that illegal non citizens are included in the census count in that congressional representation will be skewed towards a greater representation of areas where large populations of non citizens and illegal non citizens are residing. Federal resources will also be skewed towards these areas thereby reducing any benefit they may have been provided to actual citizens. Counting non citizens and illegal non citizens dilutes what is available to actual citizens. In a representative government such as ours, only the US Citizen and legal non citizen should be counted so that valuable resources can be allocated to serve US Citizens and legal non citizens only. <br/>Thank You for your consideration of this comment. <br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0388,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yedl-wplo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dennis Patrick Rodgers, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: I strongy contend that only citizens should be counted in our census and een condent that counting non-citizens is so horrible that it should constitute a felony. These numbers determine representation in both Congress and the electoral process, and ONLY citizens have any right to participate in governance. Thus, disproportionate representation based on the inclusion of non citizens, including but not limited to those illegally in the US is anathema to American values and American Sovereignty. Further, tracking ethnic, religious and other &quot;discriminating factors simply provides fodder for those who wish to divide us along those lines and is therefore decidedly un-American.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0389,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yhk4-7kxn,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,It&#39;s about time some common sense is brought to the census.  These changes would be a good start.  Counting only those that are here and eligible to vote is how it should be.  Not dividing people into divisive groups is also how it should be.  I encourage using these proposed regulations.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0390,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yhk5-auy6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,It&#39;s about time some common sense is brought to the census.  These changes would be a good start.  Counting only those that are here and eligible to vote is how it should be.  Not dividing people into divisive groups is also how it should be.  I encourage using these proposed regulations.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0391,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yi1e-9slo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is James M. Meek and I am a citizen of the United States of America and I am commenting as an individual citizen. I would like to voice my support for the proposed changes in section 60.4 of the NPRM as published.  I feel counting illegal aliens for apportionment purposes effectively diminishes the value of my citizenship. I also support the proposed changes in section 70.2 as I see no need to differentiate citizens by race, ethnicity or sexual preference.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0393,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yj5r-n36b,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Diana Brandy Lestat, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Since apportionment of electors and Congressional representatives is based on the census, and since non-citizens should have no voice in the country&#39;s politics, they should not be counted. Further, choosing to delineate such characteristics as race and religion, which are non of government&#39;s business, simply provides information that can be used to divide us.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0394,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yj6i-wgud,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I fully support the proposed changes to the census questions, in particular that no person having illegally entered the United States has any right or portion in the determination of representation. This is the purview of US citizens&mdash;it is a stretch, in my opinion to include green card holders as they have no voting rights. I was a GC holder and had no expectation of either representation considerations participation nor that I could vote in any local, state or federal election! I also agree that the granular demographic data is irrelevant to the mandated purpose of the census and that is has been used to divide and facilitate discrimination among many demographic groups, which is counter to our &ldquo;one nation under God&rdquo; core ethic, meaning that it&rsquo;s not just a union of States but a oneness across ethnic and racial delineators. Thank you for not facilitating division by these changes and defeating the overt policy of vote dilution and representation falsification by prior census and administration policies, to our corporate detriment.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0392,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yiyq-uezj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Katherine, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual.  I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published.  I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>Firstly, about funding:<br/>I think that one of the main purposes of the census is to determine how many people live in each state, county and township of the United States so that the government may apportion funds to each jurisdiction for the aid of citizens of the United States.    I do not think these federal funds should go to people who are here temporarily or illegally, nor should states and jurisdictions who choose to give federal money to these types of people be rewarded by increased representation or funding. . If some jurisdictions wish to give taxpayer money to foreigners or illegal residents, those jurisdictions should use money they raise from their own localities, not commandeer federal funds from other jurisdictions who are not interested in using their money to support such people.<br/><br/>Secondly, about representation:<br/>I think representatives who are sent to the House of Representatives should only be representing U.S. citizens and legal permanent residents of the United States, not foreigners.  Foreigners often have different priorities than citizens and it would not be fair to the citizens to have their priorities overruled by those of foreigners, simply because more foreigners live (temporarily or illegally) in their neighborhoods.<br/><br/>Thirdly, about detailed questions about an individual person&rsquo;s identity:<br/><span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>I think this is unnecessary data collection because this information is not needed to figure out how much money is allotted to each jurisdiction.  Nor is it needed to determine the number or representatives each jurisdiction may sent to Congress.  It is very intrusive and makes me feel like someone is spying on me to be asked to reveal such information.  Please don&rsquo;t do so any longer.  <br/>And, fourthly, get rid of that ridiculous loophole in California!  Why does that state get special treatment?  That is blatantly unfair.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0396,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yrv2-13as,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Thomas Boley.  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  Not following this method of gathering information about US residents has resulted in skewing of the numbers which favors illegal residents over citizens and other legal residents,  That is simply wrong and making this change is brilliant as it will result in more accurate numbers, those numbers which should be counted in each state.  Further, questions about race, ethnicity, etc, are irrelevant for a US census and are rightly eliminated.  Thank you for your attention to this matter.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0397,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ysbf-43el,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are Linda &amp; John White. We are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as a couple. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.<br/><br/>Only citizens and permanent guests should be counted for apportionment.  No distinguishing individuals by race, color, religion or creed should be attempted.  <br/><br/>The only distinguishing feature that would be acceptable is single citizenship vs dual citizenship vs non-citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0398,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yvrr-k2h7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Donald Shoemaker, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/><br/>The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>&bull; Citizens are the members of the political community. They owe permanent allegiance to the United States, can vote in federal elections, and are eligible for the full rights and responsibilities of citizenship.<br/>&bull; Representation is supposed to serve the electorate. Noncitizens cannot vote for the federal officials whose districts are enlarged by their presence, so counting them can give citizens in one state more representation than citizens elsewhere.<br/>&bull; It prevents immigration policy from changing political power without conferring political rights. A state could gain additional House seats by having a large noncitizen population even though many of those residents cannot participate in the federal political process.<br/>&bull; It aligns representation with democratic consent. Government derives legitimacy from the people who are recognized as members of the polity&mdash;not everyone who happens to be temporarily or unlawfully present.<br/>&bull; It avoids rewarding illegal immigration politically. If people residing in violation of immigration law increase a state&rsquo;s congressional representation, the system creates an unintended incentive for states to tolerate or attract unlawful migration.<br/>&bull; It makes &ldquo;one person, one vote&rdquo; more closely reflect &ldquo;one citizen, one political voice.&rdquo; <br/>Citizens in a district with a large noncitizen population may otherwise have their voting power diluted relative to citizens in districts with fewer noncitizens.<br/><br/>Thank you for your attention to this matter.<br/><br/>Don Shoemaker<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0395,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yl8l-sdo3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are David Quimby and Chunsin Quimby, we are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting jointly as individual citizens. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for our support are as follows. <br/><br/>Regarding section 60.4, we agree with the published rationale of the Census Bureau in excluding illegal aliens for apportionment purposes.  The prior practice of including such individuals rewarded so called &quot;sanctuary&quot; jurisdictions by allowing their usurpation and illegal nullification of federal immigration law to be rewarded by extra representation via the apportionment process, especially in comparison to jurisdictions that were in compliance with federal immigration law and did not offer &quot;sanctuary&quot; to individuals here illegally.  This has had the impact of distorting the balance of representatives across multiple states and has in effect penalized those states in compliance with the law.  Further, illegal immigrants are subject to deportation and, assuming that states comply with federal immigration law, these individuals would leave the state and there would no longer be any justification for &quot;representing&quot; them, as has been proffered as a competing rationale by certain parties.  Excluding illegals would also encourage jurisdictions to comply with multiple federal laws and work to improve the quality of life for their citizens and legal residents such that their locations would be viewed as an attractive place to live and work by many citizens and residents.<br/>Regarding section 70.2, a person&#39;s race, color, creed, or other characteristic has no bearing on their rights and responsibilities as a citizen or legal resident of a place. We do not need to find ways to further divide us as Americans, rather, the role of government is to treat everyone equally under the law WITHOUT REGARD to such characteristics.  Also, the Census Bureau&#39;s published rationale supporting this rule change is very comprehensive, thorough, and logical and, once implemented, would probably enhance the value of the data collected by the Census Bureau.  <br/><br/>Thank you very much.  May God Bless America!<br/><br/>Sincerely,<br/><br/>Chunsin Quimby and David Quimby",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0399,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yw71-fcxm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I fully support the proposed rule change.<br/><br/>1) Only people who are entitled to vote in the US should be counted for purposes of apportionment.  If people are counted who are not entitled to vote then their presence dilutes the value of the votes of those who are valid voters. <br/><br/>2) The US government should not collect information about the race or sexual orientation of anyone.  It&#39;s simply none of the government&#39;s business. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0400,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yxmw-8abh,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. It makes sense to count only citizens and those lawfully present in the United States on the Census. Also, it is better not to have to answer additional questions about oneself in addition to Census itself.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0401,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yytj-ir1j,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Bryce Ford, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>Only American Citizens should be counted in any census, especially ones that impact voting.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0402,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yyx8-9whe,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America and commenting as an individual.  I write in support of the proposed residence criteria in 15 CFR Part 60, including &sect; 60.4, and the proposed restrictions on demographic questions in 15 CFR Part 70, including &sect; 70.2.  Each part should be adopted on its own merits.<br/><br/>Docket No. USBC-2026-0628 / RIN 0607-AA75<br/><br/>Document 2026-18481<br/><br/>I write in support of the proposed residence criteria in 15 CFR Part 60, including &sect; 60.4, and the proposed restrictions on demographic questions in 15 CFR Part 70, including &sect; 70.2. Each part should be adopted on its own merits.<br/><br/>1. Support for &sect; 60.4 (foreign citizens and the apportionment base)<br/><br/>The constitutional purpose of the decennial census is to apportion House seats among the States according to the number of qualifying persons &ldquo;in each State.&rdquo; Since 1790 that phrase has been implemented through &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; or &ldquo;usual place of abode,&rdquo; not mere physical presence on Census Day. The Supreme Court in Franklin v. Massachusetts recognized that usual residence can include an element of allegiance or an enduring tie to a place, and that the Secretary has substantial discretion in applying that concept.<br/><br/>Proposed &sect; 60.4 draws a workable line consistent with that history:<br/><br/>- Dual U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents are counted at their usual residence. They have durable, lawful permission to remain and a settled domicile. <br/><br/>- Other foreign citizens&mdash;those without lawful permanent resident status&mdash;are not counted for apportionment.<br/><br/>That line matches how the census already excludes some people who are physically present (tourists, many short-term visitors, certain foreign diplomatic personnel). Unlawful or purely temporary presence is not the same as inhabiting a State as a member of the political community that the House represents. Including people whose presence is not lawful or not durable inflates some States&rsquo; representation relative to others based on federal immigration outcomes rather than on inhabitants the Framers associated with &ldquo;usual residence.&rdquo;<br/><br/>Section 60.4 does not pretend to rewrite the word &ldquo;persons&rdquo; in the Fourteenth Amendment. It applies the longstanding residence filter Congress and the Bureau have always used to decide which persons are &ldquo;in&rdquo; a State for apportionment. Lawful permanent residents remain in the base. The proposal asks only that status less durable than LPR not confer House seats.<br/><br/>If the Bureau finalizes &sect; 60.4, it should:<br/><br/>- State clearly that the exclusion is for the apportionment tabulation required by 2 U.S.C. &sect; 2a and 13 U.S.C. &sect; 141, and describe how other census products (if any) will be handled so users are not confused.<br/><br/>- Rely on administrative records and existing legal-status categories to the extent feasible, so the Bureau is not forced into a door-to-door immigration adjudication.<br/><br/>- Keep the severability clause in &sect; 60.11 so a court ruling on one category does not collapse the rest of the residence rules.<br/><br/>2. Support for &sect; 70.2 (short-form demographic questions)<br/><br/>The short-form questionnaire exists to produce an actual enumeration for apportionment and a clean household roster. Race, ethnicity, and sexual-orientation questions are not required for that constitutional task. Proposed &sect; 70.2 correctly keeps the form focused:<br/><br/>No race, ethnicity, or sexual-orientation question on the short form or any questionnaire used for the population enumeration.<br/>Biological sex, date of birth, and household relationship may remain because they help verify who lives where and prevent double-counting.<br/>Other demographic items are not mandatory on the decennial short form.<br/>Questions needed to apply the residence rules are not treated as banned &ldquo;demographic&rdquo; questions.<br/><br/>That split is sound. Detailed demographic statistics can continue through the American Community Survey and other vehicles that are not the apportionment instrument. Loading contested identity questions onto the short form raises nonresponse risk, litigation risk, and the appearance that the count is a social survey rather than a constitutional headcount.<br/><br/>If finalized, &sect; 70.2 should confirm that ACS and other Title 13 programs remain available for race and ethnicity data that federal agencies need under separate statutes, so those programs are not accidentally disabled.<br/><br/>3. Independence of the two parts<br/><br/>The Bureau should adopt each part even if the other is delayed or narrowed. Residence rules for apportionment and the content of the short form are related but legally distinct. Severability language already in the draft should be retained.<br/><br/>Thank you for the opportunity to comment.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0403,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yz2u-ty4u,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is ___SR______________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;  People in our country temporarily and/or unlawfully do not have a vested interest in our nation.  Their position should not be represented in Congress.<br/>Additionally, information such as race, ethnicity, etc. is unnecessary.  Having this information for the government to work with only causes division and pandering rather than the equal treatment demanded in the US Constitution.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0404,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yzco-wlp1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Lawrence Dudzik. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:  Representation in Congress and allocation of federal resources should be determined solely by citizenship.  To consider any other status in the census dilutes the rights and benefits of of citizenship, blurring the lines of America as that of a world state rather than a nation state.  And equally important, in America we all are men and women created equal under God despite race or creed.  Differentiating groups or categories of Americans within a census questionnaire is actively promoting tribal differences which has lead and will lead to division amongst us rather than promoting unity amongst unique individuals who look foremost to each other &quot;for the content of their character rather than the color of their skin&quot;.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0405,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-yzes-dld0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This sounds like a logical change to the census   Citizens should be counted and represented in government.  Visitors to our country should not have representation in our government.  Visitors to our country have embassies that represent them. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0406,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-z0mg-0cxu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Joel Peterson, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the  I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  I believe that only United States citizens and certain others lawfully present on the census date should be counted for purposes of representation.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0409,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-z36j-gg0a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Ann Truitt , I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows, i am proud to be an AMERICAN and want to put my country first. As a mother and now grandmother it is vital we focus on on the health of our country and our people.  We spend too much socially.  Lets focus our dollars on our citizens.  This is a start to getting that right. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0407,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-z0p2-ros3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Keith Garner, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. I believe only citizens of the United States of America should be counted in the census. Thank you for you time and attention to this matter",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0408,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-z2h2-kw5p,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This coming 2030 Census should only count legal citizens, resident aliens (Green Card holders) and all US Military personnel residing overseas<br/>in their home state they choose.<br/>With regards to the purposed Census rule changes, I support excluding all illegal aliens currently present illegally in our country.<br/>Especially all foreign non citizens serving prison sentences in either the Federal Prison system or any part of a States prison/jail <br/>facility.<br/>Also no foreign students attending any type of school or training center within the US borders be allowed to be included in our National<br/>2030 Census.<br/>Thank you, <br/>Signed - Dennis V.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0410,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-z4a7-bbpm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jay Blackburn.  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I also support the language of section 70.2 as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>I like the rule change because I believe the census should not be counting any illegal aliens, foreign students, foreign executives, or any foreigners in the U.S. on temporary work permits. Federal dollar apportionment needs to be focused on U.S. citizens and residents only.<br/>Also, I like the next change because we as a country need to focus on what we have in common.  Previous questions about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation encourage us to focus on these characteristics that tend to divide us as a nation.  Also, when the questions were asked previously people will find ways to use the data to apportion federal dollars toward these groups are away from these groups.  This is unfair.  Thank you for your consideration.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0411,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-z712-u8q9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am a citizen born in the state of West Virginia and I be leave only legal US citizens either born here or naturalized should be included in the 2030 census,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0412,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-z9y4-qrd1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only legal US Citizens should be counted in a US Census. Common sense must prevail.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0413,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zbjc-qgtz,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I want the 2030 census to include us citizens and lawful permanent residents only. I do not want it to include any undocumented or foreign students or visa holders. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0415,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zcuc-j7cg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Illegals should absolutely NOT be counted in the census. That dilutes the votes of American citizens and is plainly unconstitutional.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0414,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zbqb-3dec,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My name is William Ables Jr. and I am a citizen of the Unitied States and I am commenting on 60.4 and 70.2 concerning proposed residence criteria and demographic regulations accordingly. I believe that the wording in both sections is as it should be because only citizens and noncitizens here legally should be counted for apportionment and demographic questions should not be used to delineate any special groups. Thank you,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0416,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zdqh-97ff,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Bruce W. Spitler, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>And then you can go on as much as you like about why you believe these sections should be adopted into Part 15 of the C.F.R. when the final rule on the 2030 Census is published later this year.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0418,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zjop-hq72,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I agree with the proposed changes to the census, that only citizens of the United States are counted.  Since the census results in benefits and representation for citizens, citizens should be the only ones counted in my opinion.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0419,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zlbz-2d0n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I feel only legal residents of the USA should be counted. No foreign resident who is not a fully legal resident of the USA should be counted.<br/>No resident can be counted more than once. There should only be one legal (primary)residence allowed.<br/>,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0417,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zhft-1m4s,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I fully support the proposed changes to the U.S. census regarding the counting of only legal citizens and resident aliens. &sect;60.4 <br/>I further more support not asking questions about race, ethnicity or sexual orientation. &sect;70.2",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0421,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zn2y-c6ix,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As to &quot;I. Proposed Residence Criteria&quot; I concur wholeheartedly.  Visitors (short or long term) are not members of the body politic and should not be counted toward that end.  As to the presence of other non-citizens without legal full time resident status, they should not be here.  The inability of one branch of government to do its job (that of properly regulating immigration and visiting foreign citizens) should not be construed as license to corrupt otherwise legal processes such as the Census.  <br/>As to &quot;II. Proposed Restrictions on the Inclusion of Certain Demographic Questions in the Decennial Census Operation&quot; I also concur with the proposed change.  Although it is pleasant and potentially useful to track demographic changes over time, the primary purpose of the census remains proper apportionment of legislative representation and this change is a reasonable adjustment to improve response rate and thus better provide for the designated purpose of the Census overall.  <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0422,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zpf3-fbdz,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Glenna Kathleen Ford, and I am commenting as an individual addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  I am in support of the proposed language of section 60.4 as published.  i further support the language of 70.2 also as published. My reasons for support, as a life long citizen of the United States I have watched States welcome people who should not be in the U.S. to increase the Federal funding for their states.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0420,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zmll-i4fl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Susan McConnell.  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. <br/><br/>I am addressing section 60.4<br/><br/>and section 70.2 of the NPRM. <br/><br/>I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. <br/><br/>I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.<br/><br/>THANK YOU!!!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0423,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zq3y-j8ci,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree with this.  Illegals should not be counted for the purposes of the congressional representation.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0424,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zsb8-7579,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Counting unauthorized immigrants in the census encourages certain states to have lax enforcement if immigration laws as they achieve more electoral power. Removing this incentive will go a long way to enforcing immigration laws. <br/><br/>This rule change must be upheld. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0425,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-ztr5-9bk4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Karen. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am submitting this comment as an individual. I am addressing Sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the Notice of Proposed Rulemaking.<br/><br/>I support the proposed language of Section 60.4 as published. I also support the proposed language of Section 70.2 as published.<br/><br/>I believe representation in Congress should be based on the population of United States citizens and lawful residents, rather than individuals who are present in the country unlawfully. Including individuals who have entered the United States unlawfully or remained after their authorized period of admission can increase a state&rsquo;s representation even though those individuals are not legally eligible to vote in federal elections.<br/><br/>In my view, this can dilute the political representation of citizens and lawful residents in other states and communities. Congressional representation should reflect the people who are legally part of the communities being represented and who participate in our system under the laws of the United States.<br/><br/>Congressional representation affects the allocation of political power, federal attention, and Electoral College votes among the states. When a state&rsquo;s representation is increased based on residents who are not lawfully present and cannot vote, citizens in other states receive proportionally less representation. I support the proposed provisions because I believe they would promote a fairer allocation of political representation among American citizens.<br/><br/>For these reasons, I support Sections 60.4 and 70.2 as proposed and respectfully request that the Department retain this language in the final rule.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0426,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zu3n-i4mh,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the rule changes.<br/><br/>NO illegals should be counted and used to determine how many House seats a state gets because they have a huge population of illegals.<br/>California and any other state with lots of illegals should NOT benefit.<br/><br/>Our Constitution was written to benefit the citizens of this nation, not anyone who snuck in or crossed illegally.<br/><br/>The Census should benefit the states that follow the laws and not any of the states that break those laws, seeking to gain an advantage over other states when it comes to federal funding or how many seats they will have in the House after the Census is completed.<br/><br/>THANK YOU!!!<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0428,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0138-vp5y,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dennis E. Ford, I am a citizen of the United States of America and am commenting as an individual.  I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I also support the langtof section 70.2 as published.<br/>Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0427,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-zygu-e7ff,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Great idea! Why  hasn&#39;t this already been implemented before? Birth tourism is crazy to accept. Having 100&#39;s of thousand foreign citizens who  have no linkage to the USA only that of their mom being here temporarily should not automatically grant citizenship to the child, imo.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0430,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-030e-f64z,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I, Barbara A Dakan, am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0431,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-03rp-a48a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I wish the changes to the exclusion of non-citizens be passed and in force for the 2030 census. I don&rsquo;t believe those in the country illegally or temporarily should have the same repeats citizens and those immigrants that are trying to become citizens. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0429,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-02ai-q22d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,We should allow only US citizens and green card holders to count towards our congressional apportionment.   It is a disgrace that illegals and visitors are some how counted as if they are citizens screwing the monies that go to states as will as congressional representatives. Please make this correction!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0434,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-05y6-d24k,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Matthew Bruce Kaufman, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0433,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-05g5-ky34,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The counting of people who entered our country illegally goes against everything our Constitution stands for!  NO!  NO!  NO! to this proposal!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0432,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0519-s54h,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, born and raised her, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. There is NO country documented in the GLOBALCIT Migrant Electoral Rights dataset that permits undocumented or unlawfully present migrants to vote in any election &mdash; local, regional, or national. Voting rights for migrants always require lawful residence status. The USA should protect our nation with the same level of respect for LEGAL citizen, resident voters as the entire world does.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0435,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0639-eifj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are Jim and April Wagner,  we are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting together. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows, only US legal citizens should be counted as this will reduce fraud and misrepresentation by individuals and foreign governments!  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0437,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-07ww-ltcw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The census is to count US citizens to be represented in Congress . It is not for people here illegally, or on visas etc. it is for Citizens only! Congressional representatives only represent citizens!!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0438,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-09ls-vmc2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree with the changes to the 2030 Census in that only those people who are American citizens &amp; legal &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; should be counted as the only fair way for Congressional representation.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0436,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-075n-g1u4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am completely in favor of these rules changes. For far too long, we Americans are ignored for those who hold no allegiance to the USA. They should not be counted",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0440,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0cgf-cmwr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My names are James and Nancy Hale,we are both citizens of the U.S.A, and we are commenting as a married couple. We are addressing section 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We both support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We both further support the language of 70.2 also as published.<br/>These sections should be adopted into Section 15 of the C.F.R. when the final rule on the 2030 census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0441,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0dyo-bexc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Yes, yes and yes. Please enact these rules. We need a clearer understanding and definitions as to who should be included in our census. The apportiionment of Representatives and Federal taxpayer resources need to go to states and cities based on these proposed difinitions. Also, I don&#39;t care what a person&#39;s classification is. Those are dumb questions that should have no bearing on government apportionments. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0439,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-09wo-yl38,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only citizens should be counted for apportionment!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0442,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0iym-5gyu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please see letter attached,,,,,,"https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0442/attachment_1.docx,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0442/attachment_1.pdf",
USBC-2026-0628-0443,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0krj-7u0e,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Kurt Polenz, I am a citizen of the United States of America in the state of Texas, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0445,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0lq5-nmhk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dollie Martinez and as a citizen of the United States of America I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.<br/><br/>We will not long remain a sovereign nation if we don&#39;t restrict the allocation of representation to only citizenry and permanent legal residents.<br/><br/>Questions regarding gender, race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation serve only to divide and/or separate our citizenry and have no place on the census.<br/><br/>I will also notify my representatives that these rule changes should be codified into law so that no future administration may be able politicize the US Census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0444,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0liu-ksrs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I fully support the proposed changes to the 2030 census criteria regarding residency and demographics. (15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2.)<br/><br/>Rosemary Boyle<br/>10757 S Haven Vista Dr.<br/>South Jordan, Utah   84095",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0446,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0muo-099c,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I was reading about the proposed changes to 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2. These are positive changes which should help minimize non citizens from effecting representation and keeping information that should not be public out of the government database. I strongly support both changes. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0447,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0njs-5hv0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I believe the rules should be adopted. It&#39;s important only legal residents should be counted. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0448,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0or8-9i0w,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Kevin McElroy, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows; The question about section 60.4, which I see as who should be counted is easy for me, in that if a person is not a US citizen or a lawful resident, they are a visitor to the US, and should not be counted for any purpose towards apportionment to a local population, nor for any benefits that are meant solely for US citizens.  For section 70.2, if we are to count all US citizens and lawful residents, then we are recognized as Americans, and no other characteristic supercedes our standing as Americans, for we are one nation under God.  The Declaration of Independence and the US Constitution clearly states these criteria, and there is no reason to deviate from that, as citizens of the USA.  I strongly stand behind both sections as presented, and God Bless America.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0449,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0p0p-wjms,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a citizen of the United States, i support adoption of changes to rule 70.2. My main concern is for citizen representation in Congress as designed by the writers of the Constitution to ensure citizens and/or LEGAL residents directly impact that makeup of the House of Representatives. Visitors, foreign students and illegal aliens should not count towards addes representation in Congress.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0451,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0pnh-b30c,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Michael Ivy.  I am an American citizen.  I write in support of the language published particularly in Sections 60.4 and 70.2.  I feel strongly that illegal aliens should not be counted in our census.  I believe there is no place in our country for those who broke into our country.  I further strongly feel that information collected on citizens should be limited.  Too much information allows some organizations to further divide citizens into groups, taking away from us the simple importance we feel as a citizen.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0450,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0pbo-snhv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the changes I see for the upcoming 2030 census. For far too long the census has been used to segregate and validate groups that should only be listed as members of American society and nothing more and nothing less. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0452,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0q42-rnas,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,ONLY American citizens should be counted in the census. Foreigners legal or illegal should not be counted. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0453,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0r18-8w19,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Craig Richard Covey, Sr. I am a US citizen, commenting as an individual. Regarding sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM, I support the proposed language of both sections as published. My reasons are as follows:  <br/><br/>Section 60.4(a)&#39;s provision for both green card holders and dual citizens covers all those who obeyed the law and have become, or are becoming, American citizens.  That&#39;s as it should be.  <br/><br/>Section 60.4(b) is the true meat of the matter:  there is absolutely NO REASON for anyone who is not an American citizen (or lawful green card holder) to have any input into US governance.  It&#39;s long past time to eliminate the voting fraud and a major incentive for certain jurisdictions to import illegal aliens.<br/><br/>Section 70.2 is simple.  There&#39;s no legitimate justification for the government to collect data on citizens&#39; sexual preferences, and the practice of gerrymandering based upon race/ethnicity needs to stop ... at least the federal government should not make it easy.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0454,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0r49-gkx3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"9/12/2026  My name is John, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/>As a U.S Veteran, I believe that only permanent U.S. citizens should be counted on the upcoming census and that the questions should be colorblind and not contain immaterial personal questions about race, sex or ethnicity etc.<br/>Although these two parts are somewhat related and complementary, each is being proposed independently based on its own merits. These proposals are intended to ensure fidelity to the Constitution, improve and protect the core decennial census data, and promote efficiency. These changes should be permanent going forward.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0455,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0s52-ntzu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,As a natural born citizen of the US I support the wording of 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. The constitution requires the enumeration of the population. The Declaration notes that all are created equal. This change lives up to our most important founding documents. Citizens must determine representation. That too is constitutional. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0458,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-10dw-5gbn,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Linda McElroy, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows; concerning section 60.4, if a person is not a US citizen or a lawful resident, they are a visitor to the US, and should not be counted for any representation by elected politicians, and are not eligible for taxpayer-funded benefits meant for US citizens.  For section 70.2, if we are all to be equal, then we should all be counted as the same.  The only way to do that is to use the only common denominator, which is that we are US citizens.  I strongly stand behind both sections as presented, thank you for allowing my voice to be heard.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0456,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0xba-tnkm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Harry K Oburn, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support  the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0457,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-0ycj-3vg5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is ______ _____________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>1. We the people who are citizens should be the only ones counted for purposes of the census. We are a sovereign country and we have suffered, bled and struggled to build this country for citizens  of this country, not foreign invaders seeking to rob us of the fruits of our labors. We have fought Wars to defend our country and defend our freedom. <br/>Now those who can only be defined as traitors wish to destroy our country from within by inviting in foreign invaders and using census data to elect traitors to further their aims of destroying our country.<br/>This must stop and these rules are a good start. Only traitors seeking to destroy their own country would oppose these rules that will deny them power and wealth from the corruption made possible by the current  rules.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0459,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-11sx-pmm7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Counting illegal aliens in the census distorts congressional apportionment.  This amounts to taxation without representation for millions of citizens.  Sanctuary states like California get an excessive amount of representatives while non sanctuary states are punished with less representatives.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0460,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-131r-nnwm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,CFR&rsquo;s 60.4 &amp; 70.2 should be adopted and used in the census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0462,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-166k-8n5v,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Andrew ONeill, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0461,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1401-m842,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the idea that the census should only count citizens of the United States of America, especially for apportionment.  Also, the race of the people for apportionment has no reason to be included.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0463,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-16t9-lvug,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is  Roy F. Engman , I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. 1. For reasons of Political Apportionment only Lawful US Residents and Citizens should be counted. The counting of persons Illegally or otherwise on US Territory dilutes the Self-Governing power of Citizens and Lawful Residents.  2. The inclusion of demographic questions on the Census is unnecessary and since all Citizens and Lawful Residents are one Homologous Group. No further division of Sub or Special Interest groups is necessary.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0465,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1afu-4m1j,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support these rule changes for the 2030 census. This returns common sense logic and legal integrity to the census.  Citizens and legal residents should be the only numbers used to determine representation and allocations. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0464,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-170e-or5l,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Heather ONeill, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0466,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1ali-c426,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Linda K. Matthews, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: <br/>The purpose of the decennial census is to count noses for the purpose of apportionment of representation. Those being represented are meant to be those resident in and with allegiance to the USA and the particular state. Non-citizens with any status other than Permanent Resident must not be included for consideration of apportionment; this includes illegal aliens--visa-overstays included (regardless of work or tax-paying status--the IRS will give anyone an ITIN), foreign students, tourists, business travellers, diplomats, etc.. These non-citizens are not regularly domiciled within the United States and any responses from them on a census document must be segregated from the apportionment process in order to avoid the dilution of the voices of those who ARE regularly domiciled here. Also, the Constitutionally-mandated census should be as lean as possible and should only collect information necessary to its purpose. It would be reasonable to ask whether the respondent is citizen or permanent resident, if permanent resident how long they have had this status, where and when a respondent was born, where his parents were born, where he resides, how long he has resided there, whether he considers some other place to be his permanent home, whether he holds allegiance to the United States. All of this speaks to qualifying factors in determining whether a person should be considered for apportionment, and if so, where (which state).<br/>All manner of other demographic information can be collected on the Community Survey, which is also the place to count these non-permanent resident non-citizens of whatever type. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0468,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1c2b-7qcw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The only people that should be counted are legal citizens, permanent t residents, any any others that are here legally.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0467,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1bpt-gpw1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Constitutionally only citizens of the United States of America are eligible for benefits from taxpayer dollar, thus the thought or assumption that anyone who is counted in a census is eligible is in violation of of the original intent of the constitution as well as fraud perpetrated against actual citizens of America, thus proposed rule must stand for the integrity of our constitution.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0469,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1chf-qrb8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is James and I&rsquo;m a citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I fully support the proposed language of sections 60.4 and 70.2 as published.  The reasons for my support are simple- the census is of primary importance in determining representation of the various states in the House.  The Republic is founded on this ideal of representation as one half of Congress.  The only people who should determine the state representation population are the legal citizens and others with the legal authority and responsibility to vote.  This is not a racial issue, a religious issue, or a gender issue, it is an American issue.  The full and fair representation of American citizens should be at the heart of all government functions. The will of the citizens should be accurately represented for each state in the Union and a fair census is needed to accomplish this.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0470,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1ckj-mxv6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposala,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0471,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1gbd-02ay,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Using common sense it is apparent that illegals should not be counted in the census because the numbers are used to allocate the House of Representatives seats and illegals cannot vote nor are they represented by Congress.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0472,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1i2t-jb2f,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I&#39;m a U.S. citizen and I&#39;m commenting as an individual.  I&#39;m addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of sections 60.4 and 70.2 as published.  The 2020 census is one of the core causes of the ongoing damage the U.S. is continuing to experience and these proposed changes will go a long way towards stopping that ongoing damage.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0473,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1iuz-evdf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert C. Petrilak I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0474,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1krg-f1ov,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert Penna. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0476,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1nad-haku,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Donald F Flintoff, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>I feel allowing non-citizens to counted for apportionment dilutes my representation at the national level and reduces my vote to less than whole in all elections.<br/>Thank you,",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0478,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1pme-k9q1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please ONLY include full Citizens for apportionment.  Undocumented or illegally present should NOT be included.  And Green Card holders should not be included for representation as they should not be voting.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0477,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1p57-zs1r,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The census should count only legal citizens of the United States (not legal residents or undocumented migrants) and should collect zero demographic information. All citizens are equal and it is not the job of the Census to separate us by categories.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0475,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1lr6-on0b,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Yes proceed with this change ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0479,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1qei-gmi0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only legal American citizens should be counted for the purpose of the number of representatives each state is allowed.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0481,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1tgk-bspq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hi I&#39;m Jeremy Abrams, a US citizen, and I support sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM as published. I also support the language of section 70.2 also as published. If we disaggregate ourselves by race in the Census, we will disaggregate ourselves by race as a nation. Race is a false category, and should be ignored for all legal purposes. The published sections are the right way to go. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0480,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1syr-5zkb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am John Torelli, a citizen of the United State and I&#39;m making my comment as an individual.  This concerns sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the notice of proposed rule making.  I support the language of 60.4 as published and I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  My reasons are that I believe ONLY United States citizens should be counted in the census and that questions about race, ethnicity and sexual orientation are irrelevant, demeaning and obscures the fact that we are all Americans regardless of the answers to those unnecessary questions.   Please consider my comments in your decision.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0482,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1ve7-wkgr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Regarding the Proposed Residence Criteria, I would like to see illegal aliens excluded from any census or other measurement of population going forward. Illegal aliens have no permanent ties or allegiance to the United States and are often migratory within the borders of the United States. This should disqualify them from being counted as the term &ldquo;inhabitants,&rdquo; which was historically understood to refer to members of the body politic. In my opinion, only lawful citizens and residents of the United Sates should be counted as part of that body politic.<br/><br/>Regarding the Proposed Restrictions on the Inclusion of Certain Demographic Questions in the Decennial Census Operation, I believe that no demographic information should be collected because doing so does not advance the fundamental, Constitutional purpose of the decennial census, which is to conduct an enumeration to ascertain the population count for apportionment. Other methods should be employed to gather demographic statistics as necessary but the census should not be that vehicle. The intent was not to determine the granular structure of American society but to apportion Representatives among the States and that original intent should be honored as the Founder intended.  <br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0484,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-20zd-l2td,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0483,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-1xv0-gkp0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I completely support these proposed changes. The census should only count citizens and lawful permanent residents. Further, race or ethnicity should not be part of any government data collection as its anti-constitutional on its face. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0485,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-22ec-gr8p,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As an American citizen born in Joliet, Illinois to two American born parents I resent the premise that non-citizens be counted in the next government census. Why should illegal aliens be counted when they legally have no right to be in our country. Why should sanctuary states benefit from this illegal population with additional House seats?",,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0485/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0486,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-22wn-kkcf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The census should exclude all non-permanent residents for the purpose of apportionment.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0487,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-243n-e1zm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are that I do not believe that individuals in this country illegally are entitled to representation in congress. I also do not believe that they should receive any benefits for being here illegally.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0490,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-27l7-tllc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Only US Citizens or those naturalized or legally permanently resident in the US, must be the only person counted in the census. Any others have no allegiance to the US, and have shown this by their continued unlawful presence. They should have no say in the composition of the government of the US, which was created to represent the American people.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0488,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-24n9-b5ik,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert Maynard Johnston, I am a native born Connecticuter, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>1) Due to the criminal invasion of millions of foreigners with no interest in supporting our cultural heritage and the constitution of the united States of America, we must exclude such parties from influencing our apportionment and consequently our elections.<br/>2) It is my opinion that asking questions regarding sexual orientation or gender or race is not germane to the constitutional purpose of the census, and such data has been included in the past for political purposes only by those intent on dividing us.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0489,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-275a-tx1y,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Michael Scott Mince, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are that only natural born and naturalized citizens should be counted for apportionment of funds.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0491,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-29bv-n3dq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Sheryl, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  Only American Citizens deserve to be counted as we are the ones voting.  Allowing illegals and non-citizens into the census skews the data towards higher representation in the House of Representatives for states with a very high percentage of immigrants who do not deserve to be considered, as well as possible redistricting that favors 1 major Party over another.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0492,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2efm-073f,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Yes yes yes let&rsquo;s take our republic back&hellip;election integrity starts here.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0493,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2fvw-g579,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mark Jones, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>  I believe that ONLY citizens of the United States should be counted in a census for the purpose of representation, but I&#39;ll accept the counting, reluctantly, of legal permanent residents. <br/>  In re 70.2, let the special interest groups do their own demographic surveys with their own money. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0494,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2hwf-bgai,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My name is James Wayne Middleton. I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of both 60.4 and 70.2 as published. Here is why I support the sections as published:<br/>1) I believe the primary purpose of the census is to provide data to apportion representation in the House of Representatives and that only citizens and lawful permanent residents should be counted for that purpose. Representation in the House should not be affected by those in our country temporarily or illegally. <br/>2) Discrimination is a vile practice.  Governments should not demean themselves by collecting data which can be used to practice discrimination. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0495,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2i3h-lmys,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,It&rsquo;s about time that American citizens are being counted in the census and not foreigners. This is long overdue.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0496,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2k6x-ax7t,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Proceed with proposed rule changes for the census.  I am in favor of the changes.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0499,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2nnq-x8sw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Brian, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>The update to section 60.4 is necessary because only people who will reasonably be in the USA for the foreseeable future should be counted in the census. They have a stake in the country presumably. Those who are reasonably expected to be in the USA temporary, such as illegal aliens, those who over stay visas, or are here on education visas, should not be counted. They do not have a long term stake in the future of the country. They should not have an effect on electoral representation or appropriation of funds.<br/>The update to section 70.2 is necessary because America needs to move past racial identity. An American is an American, we should not Balkanize based on race, ethnicity, or orientation. Omitting unnecessary questions from the census, such as questions on race, will help prevent the destruction on one American identity.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0500,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2ohw-q8s8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Appears to be a reasonable approach to the problem of diluting representations in Congress by counting and providing Representatives that are serving non-citizens.<br/><br/>If it is decided to count these non-citizens, their status whould be noted and not used in the identification of Congressional Reprsentatives.<br/><br/>Thank you",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0497,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2laj-8kep,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Peter G Welsch, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>Section 60.4 should provide the guidance that only U.S. citizens, including dual citizens and green card holders (legal permanent residents), be counted in 2030 for the purposes of apportionment. All other foreigners will not.  Non-citizens, who should not be voting, should not be counted for the apportionment of congressional seats so they don&rsquo;t skew representative seats. <br/> <br/>Section 70.2.  The purpose of the census should not be to collect personal demographic information that could then be used to segregate populations and/or ethnic groups.  The Declaration of Independence puts forth the ideal that we are all created equal and the census should not be used to gather information that can be used to divide and separate us into &lsquo;special&rsquo; groups.  The Supreme Court recently struck down attempts to adjust congressional districts based racial/ethnic groupings.  The census should not be party to the collection and provision of the data to assist in such unauthorized actions.  <br/><br/>Both of these Sections should be implemented as written. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0498,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2lce-iekv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is __Bob Scheuber___________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: only United States citizens should be counted.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0501,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2rpx-fpi0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Most comments on this rule will likely focus on the obvious issues with pretending the 14th Amendment doesn&#39;t say what it says, and the proposed rule&#39;s facially absurd assertion that the Constitutional requirement to count all persons living in the United State somehow doesn&#39;t include those with differing documentation or citizenship statuses. Those comments are correct, and such a claim is absurd and undemocratic. I&#39;m sure many additional comments will raise excellent concerns regarding the implications of the proposed count changes to a wide variety of federal fund distributions to infrastructural and other programs that serve whole populations, regardless of documentation status; those comments are also correct. <br/><br/>But in addition to all of those excellent points, I&#39;d also like to point out that this proposed rule creates a scenario of taxation without representation for millions of American citizens living abroad. Many US citizens live abroad outside of a military or government-employee scenario, whether it&#39;s for work, for education, for family, for retirement, or merely because they enjoy long-term travel. They nevertheless retain their citizenship, including tax obligations and voting rights, under US law. By denying their count to their home state, that unjustly reduces said state&#39;s share of representation in Congress -- denying US citizens living abroad their full, proportional representation under the law and creating a burden of taxation without representation that is unjust and unAmerican. It is not what the founders, who rose up against Great Britain for just such an outrage, would want. This proposed rule change should not go through.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0503,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2uvz-x99l,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support only US citizens being counted in the census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0502,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2smn-fum9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John Vair, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:  the census should be counting American citizens because the resulting data has significant impacts on citizens of the United States for Congressional apportionments, Federal funding, and understanding the meaningful demographics of our country.  <br/><br/>Thank you for your consideration.<br/><br/>~John Vair<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0504,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2v12-omk0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree with new rules that lawful permanent residence should be counted not someone here unlawfully and temporary ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0506,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-31vr-pb00,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Jack A. Otto, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>Representation should be based upon citizenship/legal residency.  Congressional representation, and by extension electoral college votes, by rights should be based on representing people who are either US citizens or who have been granted legal residency.  People who have committed a crime by being here illegally are not entitled to have their presence here counted in determining congressional representation - Period!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0505,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-2v9s-oncv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I approve and agree 100% with the proposed rule change for the 2030 census. Is a natural born citizen. It is only common sense that only United States citizens, persons holding dual citizenship, and green card holders with permanent resident status be counted in the 2030 census.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0507,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-35ne-day4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Annamarie Gervais, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>I believe these sections should be adopted into Part 15 of the C.F.R. when the final rule on the 2030 Census is published later this year because only citizens and legal residents should benefit from the tax dollars that are allocated based on census numbers.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0508,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-36i2-9an8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I totally agree with and support the proposed rule changes.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0509,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-36sz-zd7o,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Since the purpose of the census is to decide how many representatives a state has in Congress, only U.S. citizens and legal residents should be counted in the census. Including non-citizens dilutes the rights of U.S. citizens by possibly allocating more representation than is proper to a particular area or state.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0510,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-3ish-0pwl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Wallace K. Aiken, III. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>Only citizens of the United States are guaranteed the rights and privileges vouchsafed by the Constitution. Those here illegally can claim no such right or privilege and should therefore not be counted for representation in Congress.<br/><br/>Thank you for your time.<br/><br/>WK Aiken",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0512,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-3ll0-414r,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Absolutely love the new proposed rules.  This should be a survey about the American people, for the American people!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0513,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-3mqt-qsit,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Census for citizens only.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0511,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-3j95-lopm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Diana Miller, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. I support the changes proposed as they more clearly represent what The Constitution of The United States of America intended for the collection of census data for Americans.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0515,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-3np9-j6ym,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Only , only, only US citizens should be counted in the census?",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0516,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-40a0-yyrd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Alan Shaw, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  It is my view that counting non-citizens allows representation to be established in a manner that is not consistent with what our founders established. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0514,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-3n7w-kpqy,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hello.<br/><br/>Hello.<br/><br/>Hello. My name is Mike Hillhouse. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/><br/>I support the proposed changes due to their strengthening of the Census process. It is imperative to accurately count  actual, legal citizens,<br/>of the United States for proper accountability of public expenditures. These changes should reduce opportunities for non-citizens to take advantage of American taxpayers. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0517,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-43q0-v91x,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Sandy D., I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0518,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-45ce-s8xs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Murff, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows<br/>Apportionment. The purpose of Congress is to represent the citizens of this country in the government.<br/>Non-discrimination. All citizens should be treated equally, regardless of any category otherwise applied. People are individuals, not insects with specific classes. Just because a label is applied, it doesn&#39;t mean that it fits everyone it can be applied to",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0519,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-45rh-5be8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hello,<br/><br/>As a US citizen, I am for this change to the Census rules.  I am not sure why we ever counted people in the census for the purpose of congressional district allocation.<br/>The only possible reason to count non citizens might be for government funding  to states and districts for non voting related services. But something like drawing congressional seat allocation, which impacts presidential elections, should only be counting people who can actually vote in federal elections.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0522,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-533n-0ay7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Kevin Winter, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. I firmly believe that ONLY U.S. citizens and LEGAL immigrants should be counted.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0520,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-4f6p-f5v2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only us citizens should be counted.    Also race and ethnicity information should not be collected ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0521,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-4ue2-mflk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please pass the new residence rules for the census. I strongly believe only those categories of people mentioned should be counted. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0523,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-54cx-nbk9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jim, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows,  only United States citizens or lawful residents should be counted for the census and the update to curtail subjective language about who a person is that is filling out the census, it simplified language is better that the 2020 census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0524,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-59l7-schk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only US citizens should count in our countries census. Not anyone but only citizen or those legally within our borders.<br/><br/>Thank you,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0525,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5e68-clf2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is George G Simbles, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. <br/>Per the Constitution, we should all be treated equally and ONLY AMERICAN CITIZENS ARE COUNTED.  As for race, gender, religions etc....That is irrelevant,  AS CITIZENS OF THE USA WE SHOULD ALL BE TREATED EQUALLY. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0528,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5gxn-nh17,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is George Taylor, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: only lawful, legal and permanent U.S. citizens of the United States of American should be included and counted. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0529,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5ksx-mpne,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John Hudson.  I am a native-born citizen of the United States of America, and both both parents and grandparents were born in the USA.  My comments are my own, and informed by my PhD training and expertise in psychometrics and surveying.  I am specifically commenting on sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  I fully support both sections exactly as written and published for comments.  Only bona fide American-born and fully naturalized citizens should be allowed to complete the census.  Given the purpose of the census is a count -- the inclusion of demographic characteristics like sex, race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation are not necessary, can be seen as offensive or divisive, an invasion of privacy, and adds time to take the survey.  While I am sure the demographers love playing with such data -- but that is not the legal purpose of the census.  Thank you for considering my perspective.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0526,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5fju-yvf4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Christopher Baumgartner, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows;<br/><br/>Section 60.4. Counting non citizens is by its very nature discriminatory to US citizens as we should not be charged in any way for their non contributory persons. <br/><br/>Section 70.2. In my opinion, one of the most devastating consequences of the 2020 Census emphasis on granularity is that it encouraged an unhealthy pattern of valuing self-segregation in one&rsquo;s identity in U.S. society over the core values in our Declaration of Independence, in which we held the aspiration to the truth that we are all created equal.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0527,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5fle-6bk1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Nancy Brown, and I am a citizen of the United States. I am commenting as an individual regarding the proposed language as published for sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the language of Section 60.4 as published, and further support the language of Section 70.2 as published. I have read the proposals in their entirety and believe these changes will promote a more equitable distribution of representation among all states while representing only citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Thus, I believe these sections should be adopted into Section 15 of the C.F.R. when the final rule on the 2030 census is published later this year. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0531,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5o74-dyyc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"ONLY US citizens should be counted in the census. Non us citizens deserve should not be granted any representation. Non US citizens have no rights, our constitution is for US citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0530,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5njs-bf8y,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Davide C. Marney, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing the proposed Residence Criteria and Restrictions on the Inclusion of Certain Demographic Questions in the Decennial Census Operation. I support the proposed language of both as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>As to the first proposed rule, the primary purpose for the census as authorized by the Constitution is to support apportionment of Congressional representatives. As only a US citizen may vote for a representative, they are the only persons who should be counted for this purpose. <br/><br/>As to the second proposed rule, although demographics are interesting and useful for other purposes, they are of no use for counting the number of citizens to support apportionment. <br/><br/>Any organization must focus itself on its core mission if it is to be successful. The census bureau has many other opportunities to catalog non-citizens and to collect demographics, which are interesting, but not core. Including those side projects in the core census task is inefficient and a distraction.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0532,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5tq0-pp5a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Destrianne Swegel, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0535,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5y1c-1qke,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Brian Kay, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Because only citizens should be counted, especially those currently eligible to vote or will be able to vote once they reach majority. Apportionment should be based on eligible voters/citizens. resident aliens and Illegals should not have representation in the US; they can claim in their homelands. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0533,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5x1j-1p9p,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I think ONLY USA citizens should be counted!!!!!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0534,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-5x2l-s4c5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Samuel M. Townsend I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: it is my firm belief that only citizens of the United States should be counted in the census.  The census data is used in apportioning representatives in Congress, and I believe only citizens should be represented. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0536,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-610m-kriq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is James Spaude, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.<br/>I believe this reverses long time U.S. practices of inclusion by mere presence within our borders to a policy that focuses on making people who are here legally the priority. I believe America should look out for our own first, this is what turning that sentiment into the legal obligation of the United States of America to its people.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0537,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-6207-jxf0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0538,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-66dv-0e3y,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Karl B. Manrodt, Ph.D. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. Thank you for making these rational, logical changes to the process and only counting legal citizens of our country.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0540,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-6933-rvjp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am an individual who is a consultant to nonprofit organizations, and my work often touches on data analysis including the kind of data hygiene and data enhancement that leans on Census data. The rules as currently proposed will just flat out be terrible for business. Accurately determining nationwide demographics is a &quot;public good&quot; in economic terms; like an electricity grid or a railroad network, maintaining a single one is both more efficient and more effective. We don&#39;t need multiple private organizations doing a separate  demographic analysis of U.S. consumers, as the most likely outcome would be incomplete, less accurate and more expensive data. Notwithstanding the fact that the U.S. Constitution requires you to count all persons, not just all citizens, the idea of putting in the immense amount of work that a census requires only to get subpar data back is downright idiotic.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0541,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-6ah4-9gqh,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Donald Lowe, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support is because people who were born here and have sworn to support and follow our culture and constitution should never have their votes and representation diluted from those who are not citizens and likely have other conflicting interest.  These large numbers of non-citizen people have a tenancy to congregate in states that give away federal money and benefits to them while at the same time, if counted, provide  more power the the states that support them and shift the balance of power towards states that adopt sanctuary policies.  So citizens lose power to political systems that openly disobey and disrespect our laws and we are forced to pay for those here illegally. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0539,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-66ea-gab7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Persons who are in this country illegally should never be counted. They are not citizens of this country and shouldn&rsquo;t be able to sway the house of representatives in any way. <br/><br/>Citizens who are out of country serving their country either in the military or through some other form of government service should be counted in the census in their home state. <br/><br/>It matters not to me about the race or ethnicity of a person. I do believe if you don&rsquo;t know if you are a male or female you more problems than a census can handle. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0543,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-6kwu-yffp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are Rebecca and Rick Collodi, we are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as individuals.  We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for our support are as follows:  America is for American citizens and Legal Immigrants (green card holders).  Congressional apportionment should only be based on citizens and legal immigrants.  In addition,  demographic data is overdone and we support the limitations on the collection of that data.  Thank you.   ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0544,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-6m18-3b29,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Fredrick W Myers, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. <br/>I am addressing section 60.4 of the NPRM.  and Franklin (505 U.S. at 792-93) as described in the Proposed Census Bureau Rule of 10 Sep 2026. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>Only citizens of the USA are permitted to legally vote.  Apportionment should be based on the number of valid voters (or legal residents) in each state.<br/><br/>There is also another anomaly in the apportionment system which is NOT addressed in the NPRM though mentioned in part in the Proposed Rule Making.  I am registered to vote in the state of Delaware and subject to USA taxation on my worldwide income.  However, I have been legally resident overseas (ie outside the boundaries of the USA) since 1977. It is mentioned that US citizen military and Department of War employees ARE included in the apportionment process when located overseas on the census date. <br/>I do not see any method of the USA Census process permitting &quot;normal&quot; overseas residents to be included in the apportionment system. However, there should be little (non-political) difficulty in adding a question to the IRS form 1040 (Income tax) or Form 2555 (foreign income exclusion) for persons subject to USA taxation who are  resident overseas:<br/><br/>For the purposes of Congressional decennial apportionment, what state was you last legal residence in the USA? None would be a valid answer (children born overseas to USA parents//foreign spouses of USA citizens)<br/>The total number of overseas USA citizens (by state/territory) for the last tax year prior to the census shall be included in the US Census reporting.<br/><br/>Thank you<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0542,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-6jke-h214,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This whole thing reeks of a scheme to falsify data. How can we trust the Trump administration to accurately tell who is and is not a citizen when ICE has shown so clearly that the government cannot accurately track and identify citizenship? How can we trust that this overzealous targeting of minority groups is not being hidden in the sudden lack of demographic data in a census that no longer asks about one&#39;s ethnicity? We simply cannot. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0545,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-6ndr-ienl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only US citizens should be counted in the census. Only US citizens should be eligible to vote.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0546,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-6wcx-hyjp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,the census should count ONLY US citizens and green card holders. no questions about sexual orientation or gender or race or ethnic identity. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0547,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-7es9-c18u,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Brian, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are: Regarding 60.4, the Founders of the United States of America clearly intended for this great country to be governed for the people and by the people. The people are the lawful citizens of the USA. No foreign entity should have any input or influence on who the American people choose to govern themselves, regardless of whether they are inside our borders or otherwise.<br/>Regarding 70.2, in order for every single American to be considered equal, we must BE equal. Therefore the race, ethnicity and sexual orientation of American citizens is entirely irrelevant. This information could only be used against citizens and this information should absolutely NOT be included in any question concerning the census. <br/>In summary, for all US Citizens to be equal, there must not be any information included about them that could possibly be used to enhance or detract from their individual equality to every other American. <br/>Furthermore, for every citizen&#39;s vote to be counted and weighed with equal measure, ONLY legal citizens of the United States of America should be considered residents. Every single vote cast in any US election that is not from a legal US citizen, voids the rightful vote of one American who absolutely deserves to have their vote counted and deserves to be represented by the leaders they choose. The census is the mechanism that decides the number of representatives governing the entire United States of America. It is imperative that only legal American citizens choose their representatives.<br/>For these reasons, I strongly support the proposed language in the NPRM and encourage the proposed language changes to be adopted for the 2030 census in order to ensure that American is governed for the people and by the people. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0550,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-7p8o-vexu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I&#39;m in favor of these changes. <br/>There is no need to count people who have no business being here in the first place.<br/>,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0549,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-7map-8l9k,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Deborah Baker, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. There is no justification for counting people in this country illegally.  They are not eligible to vote and should not be represented in Congress.  There is no need for you to have information about race and gender as part of the census",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0548,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-7iho-j2fr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2, also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: These are common-sense proposals that are well grounded in historical practice and law. Citizens of foreign countries living in the U.S. as temporary workers or for other reasons, or who are living in the U.S. illegally, should not be counted in the Census for apportionment. Citizens of foreign countries, in my opinion, should be encouraged to participate in their own countries&#39; democratic processes and thus should not be counted for apportionment in the U.S. For example, an Egyptian or a Swede should be encouraged to participate in the democratic processes of Egypt and Sweden, and thus should be excluded from being counted in the Census apportionment in the U.S.  Americans should not be involved in the democratic processes of other countries, and non-citizens of the U.S. should not be counted in our Census for apportionment. That&#39;s common sense.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0551,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-7z2i-3d8b,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Stephen Chapman, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing section 60.4 of the NPRM regarding the proposed changes to the 2030 Census, specifically the exclusion of immigrants in the U.S. illegally from being counted for apportionment purposes.<br/><br/>I would like to address the constitutional context of this proposal. The 14th Amendment, written in 1868, states that &quot;Representatives shall be apportioned among the several states according to their respective numbers, counting the whole number of persons in each state, excluding Indians not taxed.&quot; However, when the 14th Amendment was drafted, the framers could not have anticipated two critical aspects of our modern world:<br/><br/>The unprecedented ease with which people can now travel from anywhere in the world to enter the United States, a reality made possible by advances in transportation and technology.<br/>The massive influx of illegal immigrants across our largely unguarded borders, as has occurred from 2021 to 2025, resulting in millions of individuals residing in the country without legal status.<br/><br/>Given these realities, I believe it is reasonable and necessary to reconsider how we define &quot;the whole number of persons&quot; for the purposes of congressional apportionment. The proposed rule, which would count only citizens and lawful permanent residents, better reflects the intent of ensuring that political representation and federal resources are allocated to those who are legally part of our national community. This change would help align the census with the interests and obligations of American citizens and legal residents, and address challenges that the framers of the 14th Amendment could not have foreseen.<br/><br/>Thank you for considering my comment",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0553,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-80o9-zh41,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Stephen Chapman, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing section 60.4 of the NPRM regarding the proposed changes to the 2030 Census, specifically the exclusion of immigrants in the U.S. illegally from being counted for apportionment purposes.<br/><br/>I would like to address the constitutional context of this proposal. The 14th Amendment, written in 1868, states that &quot;Representatives shall be apportioned among the several states according to their respective numbers, counting the whole number of persons in each state, excluding Indians not taxed.&quot; However, when the 14th Amendment was drafted, the framers could not have anticipated two critical aspects of our modern world:<br/><br/>The unprecedented ease with which people can now travel from anywhere in the world to enter the United States, a reality made possible by advances in transportation and technology.<br/>The massive influx of illegal immigrants across our largely unguarded borders, as has occurred from 2021 to 2025, resulting in millions of individuals residing in the country without legal status.<br/><br/>Given these realities, I believe it is reasonable and necessary to reconsider how we define &quot;the whole number of persons&quot; for the purposes of congressional apportionment. The proposed rule, which would count only citizens and lawful permanent residents, better reflects the intent of ensuring that political representation and federal resources are allocated to those who are legally part of our national community. This change would help align the census with the interests and obligations of American citizens and legal residents, and address challenges that the framers of the 14th Amendment could not have foreseen.<br/><br/>Thank you for considering my comment",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0554,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-80tp-pl1a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John McClary and I am a citizen of the United States of America, commenting as an individual and commenting on sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are that only American citizens should be proportionally represented in the number of representatives fior their state. Others can and will be represented by elected officials but only American citizens should be represented proportionally. Also, in support of section 70.2 I believe that we are all Americans and should be counted and represented on that basis alone. No other data (such as race or country of origin) matters.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0552,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-7z89-9olq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Bradley Baker, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:  my vote should not be diluted by the votes of people not entitled to be in the country.  They do not need to be counted.  There is compelling government interest and thus no need for you to question people about their race or sexual identity or interests",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0556,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-83nq-b5b6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Stephen Chapman, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing section 60.4 of the NPRM regarding the proposed changes to the 2030 Census, specifically the exclusion of immigrants in the U.S. illegally from being counted for apportionment purposes.<br/><br/>I would like to address the constitutional context of this proposal. The 14th Amendment, written in 1868, states that &quot;Representatives shall be apportioned among the several states according to their respective numbers, counting the whole number of persons in each state, excluding Indians not taxed.&quot; However, when the 14th Amendment was drafted, the framers could not have anticipated two critical aspects of our modern world:<br/><br/>The unprecedented ease with which people can now travel from anywhere in the world to enter the United States, a reality made possible by advances in transportation and technology.<br/>The massive influx of illegal immigrants across our largely unguarded borders, as has occurred from 2021 to 2025, resulting in millions of individuals residing in the country without legal status.<br/><br/>Given these realities, I believe it is reasonable and necessary to reconsider how we define &quot;the whole number of persons&quot; for the purposes of congressional apportionment. The proposed rule, which would count only citizens and lawful permanent residents, better reflects the intent of ensuring that political representation and federal resources are allocated to those who are legally part of our national community. This change would help align the census with the interests and obligations of American citizens and legal residents, and address challenges that the framers of the 14th Amendment could not have foreseen.<br/><br/>Thank you for considering my comment",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0555,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-82tc-1rng,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Nancy Burhans. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. <br/><br/>The census is used to measure the AMERICAN population and to apportion congressional seats.  No foreign citizens should be included in either of these activities because that would wrongly distort the results to the detriment of the AMERICAN people.  <br/><br/>The census &#39; purpose is not and should not be to collect sectional, separating data which can be used to segregate by tribe, etc rather than ensuring the core value of our Declaration of Independence that we are all created equal.<br/><br/>t",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0557,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-84mc-idtb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert Stieneker, I am a citizen of the United States of America, a 30 year retiree of the US Army, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0558,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-8ap4-dzzq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I think only citizens should be counted on a census, because the census results are used to apportion congressional representation.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0559,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-8azc-cuww,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I applaud these changes.  However, for apportionment, ONLY Citizens should be counted.  Apportionment should be based upon the Citizens resident in the individual State.  And none of the manipulation that took place in the last Census based on &quot;privacy&quot; should be allowed.  It should be clean and clear, apportionment should be based on true and accurate count of Citizens within the specific geographic area  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0561,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-8tjn-bbbo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I believe that only U. S. citizens and legal permanent residents should be counted for apportionment in the 2030 U. S. census. This is a policy that should continue in perpetuity.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0563,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-9jgd-gp2c,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Any attempt to tamper with or control the census is an attempt to undermine vital government programs and infrastructure. Keep the census as it is.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0562,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-8xu8-28lk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only true American citizens should be counted in the census. Counting illegals and people who have not yet received true citizenship is bogus. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0566,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ahib-u4s3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"We are the Graul family of Shillington, Pa, We are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as a family. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Only citizens of the USA should be counted in the census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0565,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-a5mk-t2q2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Kris Johnson, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting on behalf of myself. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of both sections 60.4 and 70.2 as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. Government accountability starts with citizens. Correct counting of citizens regardless of their gender, race, or religious background, is the first step to properly allocate representatives. These representatives can then be held accountable for stewardship of our resources by the citizens voting for them. Sanctuary states with large illegal populations should not benefit at the expense of other states that work with the federal government to enforce immigration law. The counting of anyone other than citizens and legal residents as well as trying to learn every detail about race, religion, and gender, has drifted very far from the framers original intent of mere apportionment.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0564,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-9jjx-n9xy,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Katherine Thompson, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  I support this because representation should be based only on voters.  There is a perverse incentive currently to manipulate movement of non-citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0570,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-bh2p-jc6x,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Brian Pope, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>I firmly believe only US citizens have the right to decide how this country is governed, by the people, for the people. If not only citizens, then why not the entire world?  It makes no sense as then we would not have a country. <br/><br/>Thank you for your attention to this matter. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0567,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-andq-d90k,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed rule is reasonable and necessary. Congressional representation should be apportioned on the basis of only those persons who are eligible to vote in federal elections. This rule is not perfect in this regard, but it is a massive improvement from past practice and I support its adoption.  Further, demographic questions are irrelevant to the purpose of the census, which is to ascertain populations within each state as required by the apportionment clause of the Constitution. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0568,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-atyl-etnx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,It is essential that the census only count American citizens and legal permanent residents. Why should we include foreigners and illegals to determine the number of representatives (who should represent ONLY American citizens)?,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0569,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-az8q-n8ye,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John Edward Frost, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>On 60.4, noncitizens should have no governmental representation within our country. Noncitizens should not get citizens tax dollars in any form, whether it be health care, food or infrastructure. Those areas that choose to allow noncitizens should bear the burden of extra support for them not the federal government or state government. <br/><br/>On 70.4 representation should be independent of any identifying trait such as race, nationality or sex.<br/><br/>I guess common sense is really not as common as it should be. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0571,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-bl48-jqql,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,," The Constitution is a compact between the government and the people. Representation is core to that compact. And those who are present illegally and/or temporarily are neither party to that compact nor entitled to representation under it.<br/>Section 2 of the 14th Amendment, which sets forth the basis for apportioning House seats, instructs that &ldquo;Representatives shall be apportioned among the several States according to their respective numbers, counting the whole number of persons in each State, excluding Indians not taxed.&rdquo; It&#39;s a compelling case, based on an extensive review of legal precedent and history, that the &ldquo;whole number of persons&rdquo; in each state refers to a state&rsquo;s &ldquo;inhabitants;&rdquo; that a state&rsquo;s inhabitants consist of those &ldquo;legally domiciled&rdquo; therein; and that to be so domiciled, a person must have a &ldquo;lawful intent to permanently remain&rdquo; in a state, and an ability under the law to do so. Illegal aliens and temporary visa holders therefore may not be counted for purposes of apportionment. Drafts of the Constitution&rsquo;s original apportionment clause &mdash; which would be modified by the 14th Amendment in 1868 to encompass freed slaves &mdash; referenced &ldquo;inhabitants.&rdquo; Also, the Federalist Papers indicated that the census count and apportionment figures would include &ldquo;inhabitants&rdquo; within each state. And every census statute passed by Congress prior to the enactment of the 14th Amendment &ldquo;provided for an enumeration of the &lsquo;inhabitants&rsquo; of the United States.&rdquo; Federal law prohibits illegal aliens and temporary visa holders from permanently residing in the U.S., the argument goes, they are therefore not legally domiciled, not inhabitants, and not entitled to representation through their inclusion in the census and apportionment. Neither the executive branch nor Congress has ever interpreted Section 2 of the 14th Amendment to mandate the counting of everyone in the U.S. at a time the census is taken. Authorities have consistently excluded &ldquo;foreign tourists&rdquo; from census tabulation. If the distinction between such sojourners and illegal aliens is simply the duration of their trip to the U.S., or their intent to remain in the U.S., then defenders of the status quo would &ldquo;provide representation even to spies who enter the U.S.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0572,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-c4ao-1e1n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Please don&#39;t count foreign citizens here illegally, or those here temporarilysuch as students or temporary workers in the census calculations.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0575,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-cq6x-gbkc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Joel R. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. People who are here illegally or are not legal residents should not be counted for the purposes of apportionment, since they are not eligible to vote. Apportionment should be solely based on eligibility to vote. Thank you!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0573,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-clua-2dju,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mitchell Snow, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/>one of the most devastating consequences of the 2020 Census emphasis on granularity is that it encouraged an unhealthy pattern of valuing self-segregation in one&rsquo;s identity in U.S. society over the core values in our Declaration of Independence, in which we held the aspiration to the truth that we are all created equal. Subtle things have profound effects on the social harmony of our nation. This has not been a healthy decade for our people. In my view, reminding everyone that none of us is special unless all of us are special together is a paramount element of U.S. national interest, the further we get into the mid 21st-century. Those 27 words are a statement about what we aspire to be.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0576,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ddmf-y9j1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0574,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-cn2e-xks6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,No non citizens should be counted in the census. None!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0578,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-fr28-8iyd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0577,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ew84-rh76,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support these proposed changes.  No one not a citizen or lawful resident alien should ever be considered in a US Census that determines political representation in our government.  To do otherwise would be detrimental to the lawful citizens of the United States.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0579,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-hc84-acql,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Illegal aliens, foreign students should not be counted for allocating congressional representation.  Congressional representatives are intended to act for the US citizens within a congressional district; not cater to the whims of those temporarily or illegally within the country.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0580,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-hurx-r92d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is AndreaLepitzki, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0581,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-hwtd-zoof,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,We should ONLY count legal citizens for the census!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0583,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-iyg0-ojc0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. We need changes like this to our laws  to encourage individuals to enter our country legally and become citizens rather than illegally and hide in the shadows.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0582,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-i2mu-9105,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Date: September 13, 2026<br/>To: Daniel Sweeney, Deputy General Counsel for Economic, Statistical, and Regulatory Affairs<br/>Office of the General Counsel, Department of Commerce / U.S. Census Bureau<br/>Docket ID: USBC-2026-0628<br/>Regulation Identifier / Document Number: 2026-18481<br/>Title: Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions<br/>Submission Portal: Federal eRulemaking Portal (Regulations.gov)<br/>Subject: Public Comment in Strong Support of Proposed Rulemaking on Residence Criteria and Decennial Questionnaire Streamlining<br/><br/>Dear Mr. Sweeney:<br/><br/>I submit this comment in strong support of the Department of Commerce and U.S. Census Bureau&rsquo;s Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (NPRM) published on September 10, 2026, regarding the Decennial Census of the Population of Americans. The regulatory updates set forth under Docket No. USBC-2026-0628 represent a necessary and long-overdue alignment of decennial enumeration practices with foundational constitutional principles, statutory intent, and administrative efficiency.<br/><br/>1. Integrity of Political Representation and Apportionment<br/><br/>The proposed revision to the Census Bureau&rsquo;s &quot;usual residence&quot; criteria properly recognizes that congressional apportionment and political representation belong to lawful members of the American body politic.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Equitable Representation: Apportioning seats in the U.S. House of Representatives and allocations in the Electoral College based on populations that include temporary non-immigrants or unlawful residents dilutes the constitutional franchise of American citizens and lawful permanent residents.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Rule of Law: Establishing clear residence criteria tied to lawful status restores public confidence in the fairness and integrity of national legislative apportionment.<br/><br/>2. Streamlining the Decennial Form and Minimizing Respondent Burden<br/>Proposed 15 CFR &sect; 70.2 establishes a disciplined boundary by excluding demographic inquiries&mdash;such as race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation&mdash;from the decennial short-form count.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Focus on Core Enumeration: The primary constitutional purpose of the decennial count is an accurate head count for apportionment, not expansive sociological inquiry.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Higher Response Rates: Lengthy or intrusive demographic inquiries increase respondent fatigue and privacy apprehensions. A concise, neutral questionnaire will improve voluntary completion rates and reduce the logistical cost of non-response follow-up.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Neutrality and Privacy: The federal government should treat all residents equally under the law rather than categorizing citizens by demographic sub-divisions on mandatory headcount forms.<br/><br/>3. Appropriate Utilization of Specialized Surveys<br/>Removing demographic categorization from the mandatory decennial headcount does not impede statistical research, as the federal government already maintains specialized tools designed for in-depth data collection.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Role of the American Community Survey (ACS): Detailed socio-economic, linguistic, and demographic data can continue to be evaluated through rolling statistical samplings such as the ACS rather than overburdening the mandatory ten-year headcount.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Standardization Across Agencies: Codifying these standards into the Code of Federal Regulations provides long-term administrative predictability across cycles leading into the 2030 decennial census.<br/><br/>Conclusion<br/><br/>The reforms outlined in this proposed rule prioritize constitutional apportionment integrity, respect individual privacy, and streamline operations for the 2030 count. I urge the Department of Commerce and the Census Bureau to adopt these proposed regulations without alteration before the close of the comment period on October 13, 2026.<br/><br/>Respectfully submitted,<br/>John Francis Sargent, Jr.<br/>U.S. Citizen<br/>Palm Harbor, FL 34683<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0584,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-jzg7-v3a7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"<br/>Constitutional purpose<br/><br/>I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0586,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-m5ae-qw16,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is T. Weeks, I am a native born citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual specifically about sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. Regarding 60.4: while I agree with most of that section as written, I do not believe that green card holders should be counted in the census. These legal residents are prohibited by law from voting. For that reason I believe that their presence  in the US should be included. I am also troubled by the inclusion of dual citizenship residents, It is my belief that only loyal citizens should be included in the census.Personally I believe that the concept of dual citizenship should be eliminated: how can a sane person declare fidelity to two different countries? It is nonsensical.<br/><br/>As far as section 70.2: this is a change, an improvement, that is long overdue as written in the proposed regulations. The census has huge impacts upon every citizen, And it is supposed to count American citizens, not special interest groups that are designed to divide this nation into competing tribes. In my opinion, this is a critical rule going forward.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0587,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-mofl-wfzs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Herbert L. Schneider, I am a citizen of the United States, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published.  I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  The reason for my support is that only legal citizens of the United States should be counted in the census and those who are not legal citizens of the United States should not be counted. It just makes sense.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0585,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-lz7u-eo4b,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am for the proposed changes especially the one regarding immigration status (I am a naturalized citizen). As a naturalized citizen I am a part of &quot;We the People&quot; that is stated in the preamble to the constitution. Persons who are here illegally are not a part of &quot;We the People&quot; and thus may not benefit from this country&#39;s treasures. As far as the type of people that are here it is of zero consequence to the government, for the purpose of a census, as to who/what they are beyond just the amount present.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0588,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-mqzh-iyd7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Patrick Sheridan, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0590,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-myu7-etdv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I&rsquo;m Chris Bernhart , I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;. I believe that only US citizens should be allowed to be counted in the census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0591,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-nr49-n2b5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Have we forgotten about the 3/5 compromise? That in the original Constitution, slaves would be counted as only 3/5 of a person?<br/><br/>What group was enslaved in the US at the time? At what time were these slaves counted?<br/><br/>The argument that the census was never intended to include demographics is incorrect. The census has always included demographics for political purposes. That is not new, and as the politics that involve demographics change so should the census.<br/><br/>Demographics are useful to track registered voters vs the adult population in a state, changes in energy costs over time, completion of higher education over time, etc. If a state considers it in its interest to track a type of statistic I see no reason why it should not use the census to do so. It provides a justification for the laws they choose to pass to their peoples&rsquo; benefit.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0589,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-my48-d72d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a citizen of the United States, I want to comment about the 2030 census. About sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. I think that congressional representation should be based on actual US citizens living in that district.  I also think that if we are US citizens it does not make any difference what race or religion we are and it should not make any difference to government either.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0594,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-obyz-xym7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,100% wholeheartedly approve of:<br/> (a) Citizens of foreign countries who are also either citizens or lawful permanent residents of the United States as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Counted at their usual residence in accordance with &sect;&thinsp;60.2.<br/><br/>(b) All other citizens of foreign countries as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Not counted for apportionment.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0592,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-o4mz-05t8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Eric J. Pflugradt, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: I have read the proposed rule changes and agree that the rule changes should take place so as to have correct apportionment and not to dilute the representative votes of other citizens representatives.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0593,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-o8o3-rizj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Lawrence Mitchell of Sarasota, Florida, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>I feel that counting illegal immigrant as part of the population for the purpose of apportioning representation in Congress has the affect of states and cities creating illegal sanctuary policies.  This will create a dangerous situation for real Americans from the dangerous criminal element of illegal immigration.  States that would protect illegal immigrants are attempting to influence financial apportionment away from states that otherwise obey the law.  Additionally, real Americans then would have to compete with these illegal immigrants for housing and employment.   ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0595,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ohfm-otyo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Comment on Proposed Rule: Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions<br/><br/>RIN 0607&ndash;AA75 &middot; Docket No. USBC-2026-0628 &middot; 91 Fed. Reg. 57524 (Sept. 10, 2026)<br/><br/>I support the Census Bureau&rsquo;s proposal to exclude illegal aliens from the apportionment base, and I urge the Bureau to finalize &sect; 60.4 to that effect.<br/><br/>1. The Constitution requires a count of inhabitants, not a headcount of everyone physically present.<br/><br/>Article I, Section 2 directs an enumeration of the &ldquo;whole Number of free Persons&rdquo; in each State for apportionment. The Fourteenth Amendment uses &ldquo;persons&rdquo; and &ldquo;inhabitants of the several States.&rdquo; From the First Census Act of 1790 forward, the governing idea has been usual residence &mdash; the place where someone actually belongs &mdash; not mere presence on a given night. Franklin v. Massachusetts, 505 U.S. 788 (1992), recognized that &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; can include an element of allegiance and an enduring tie to a place. A person who is here in violation of federal law has not been permitted to settle. That person is present. That person is not an inhabitant in the sense the apportionment clauses use the word.<br/><br/>The choice of &ldquo;persons&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;citizens&rdquo; was about the Three-Fifths Compromise and about counting inhabitants who were not voters. It was not a command to treat unlawful presence as equivalent to lawful domicile. Visitors were not counted in 2020. Diplomats are treated differently. Overseas Americans are treated differently. The Bureau has always drawn lines around who is &ldquo;in&rdquo; a State. Drawing that line at lawful, durable residence is consistent with the text and with Franklin.<br/><br/>2. Including illegal aliens distorts representation.<br/>House seats and Electoral College votes are a zero-sum allocation among the States. Counting people who have no legal right to remain, and who the United States is affirmatively trying to remove, shifts political power toward jurisdictions that attract or tolerate unlawful presence. That is not &ldquo;counting everyone once.&rdquo; It is letting a violation of federal immigration law change the makeup of Congress. States that enforce the law are penalized; States that do not are rewarded. That result cannot be what &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; was for.<br/>3. The Bureau has the discretion and the data.<br/>Wisconsin v. City of New York, 517 U.S. 1 (1996), and Franklin confirm that Congress gave the Secretary broad authority over how the census is conducted. Residence criteria have changed before &mdash; overseas military personnel are the obvious example. Formalizing a rule that illegal aliens are not usual residents of any State is a judgment &ldquo;consonant with, though not dictated by, the text and history of the Constitution.&rdquo; That is the Franklin standard.<br/>The Bureau now has administrative records from DHS, State, Treasury, and other agencies that it did not have in usable form in 2020. It should use them. If a legal-status question on the short form improves the match, I support including it. Department of Commerce v. New York, 588 U.S. 752 (2019), held that the Enumeration Clause permits a citizenship inquiry. A status question in service of a stated residence rule is not the pretextual VRA story the Court rejected in that case. State the real reason: determining who is a usual resident for apportionment.<br/><br/>4. Lawful permanent residents are a reasonable line.<br/>I agree with the Bureau&rsquo;s distinction between LPRs and illegal aliens. LPRs have been admitted for permanent residence. They have a durable permission from the sovereign to settle. Illegal aliens do not. Temporary or contingent statuses are closer to presence than to inhabitance; the Bureau asked for comment on those categories, and I would exclude them from the apportionment base as well unless the status is legally equivalent to permanent residence.<br/><br/>5. This does not require pretending illegal aliens do not exist.<br/>The Bureau can still estimate that population for statistical publications, the ACS, and administrative research. Proposed &sect; 70.4 already preserves that. The question here is only who belongs in the number that awards House seats. Those two products should not be confused.<br/>I urge the Bureau to adopt Part 60 as proposed, keep illegal aliens out of the apportionment count, and use tax and immigration records to apply the rule consistently in every State.<br/><br/>Respectfully submitted,<br/><br/>Justin Moses<br/>Mashpee, MA<br/>JMo321456@gmail.com",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0596,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-or6d-c39w,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert Wright.  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  I support this proposed language because it will make the census results more accurate and fair.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0597,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-p09x-s119,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,," I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. I am for these changes because I Don&#39;t think people here illegally or temporarily should affect how funds and congressional seats are apportioned.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0599,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-p4vu-g4c9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Charles Michael Re. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. On;y current USA citizens should be counted when determining representation in our government.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0598,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-p4af-sayj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Margaret Shelby, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>Because the Census is used to structure the framework of representation of the United States government, those who are not legal citizens of the United States should not be included as part of the count.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0600,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-p7az-bhfb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is Mark and Margaret Stolzenberg, We are citizens of the United States of America, and are commenting as a married couple. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0601,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-q1ad-uhc3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Troy J Albina, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>1. The representation in Congress is for American citizens only, not folks here illegally or not completely naturalized.<br/>2. Too many areas of the federal government are developed/funded as a result of the decennial census, certain states that harbor illegal aliens as a matter of policy should not be rewarded for breaking the law at the expense of states that follow the law.<br/>3. Regarding 70.2, I agree with eliminating the questions about race, etc. as the founding documents state we are all created equal, so there is no reason to continue to worry about and collect this divisive information.<br/><br/>Thank you for your consideration.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0603,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-q4xx-0lxu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"In regards to proposed changes to 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2 , I agree these changes are needed.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0602,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-q3sg-sulm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Kevin T, Cooksley, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: This country was populated by, built by, and has flourished by the efforts of people who came here from all points of the globe, and when they arrived here, they became &quot;Americans&quot;, not some hyphenated, partial American. We have been dividing Americans for too long by inserting hyphens in their identities (ie: African-American, Irish-American). We need to return to the policy of, that once you become a citizen, you are no longer anything other than an &quot;American&quot;. Furthermore, people who are in this country illegally, and people who are in this country on a temporary basis, should not be counted, because they are being counted as citizens in whatever country they came from, and counting them here too, just double-counts them and gives undeserved leverage to the elected officials who benefit from those undeserved counts. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0604,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-qgjb-ji5l,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John Schmalstieg, I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. In my opinion, only citizens and legal migrants should be considered for representation and the benefits determined by the census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0605,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-qjwr-8bii,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Paul Holz. I am a citizen of the United States of America, residing in the great state of Georgia, and I am commenting as an individual regarding rule changes for Decennial Census demographic questions. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>No questions regarding race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation should be included or allowed to appear on the Decennial Census.<br/>It is inappropriate to count illegal aliens who will be voluntarily or forcefully deported.  Skin color nor national origin have nothing to do with US citizenship and the right to vote and receive congressional representation and government benefits. <br/><br/>I agree with and wholeheartedly support the text of the new rules as published and as written below. <br/><br/>&sect;&thinsp;60.4 <br/><br/>Foreign citizens in the United States.<br/><br/>(a) Citizens of foreign countries who are also either citizens or lawful permanent residents of the United States as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Counted at their usual residence in accordance with &sect;&thinsp;60.2.<br/><br/>(b) All other citizens of foreign countries as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Not counted for apportionment.<br/><br/>&sect;&thinsp;70.2<br/><br/>Restrictions on the inclusion of certain demographic questions.<br/><br/>No question about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation shall appear on the short form decennial census questionnaire or any questionnaire used for the enumeration of the population.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0606,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-qsbc-33cw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I strongly support the proposed changes to the wording and data collection for the 2030 census.  <br/><br/>The census should only collect information on US citizens, not simply those residing here, and should be limited to numbers only, not data which doesn&rsquo;t apply to determining the number of people within the United States. We don&rsquo;t need information on race and gender or gender identity.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0607,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-qu7x-4n1b,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposed rule changes for the census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0608,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-qunv-upzs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I completely approve of the proposed rule change.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0609,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-qzfh-79t5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Julia Dye and I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>Since the census is used to decide the number of representatives each state can elect to Congress, then only persons who are legally eligible to vote should be counted in each state. One of the most devastating consequences of the 2020 Census emphasis on granularity is that it encouraged an unhealthy pattern of valuing self-segregation in one&rsquo;s identity in U.S. society over the core values in our Declaration of Independence, in which we held the aspiration to the truth that we are all created equal.<br/><br/>The wealth of the United States should be spent helping the domestic citizen population of the country.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0613,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-rpgg-5sj1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only U.S. citizens should be counted.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0610,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-rdna-39bo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposal fully. <br/><br/>This is a common sense proposal that brings the census in line with its intended purpose and current realities.   <br/><br/>The number of person with only foreign allegiance in the country on temporary visas or without visas, being here only until they are removed , has multiplied to numbers that seriously distort congressional apportionment and deprive citizens and permanent residents of due representation.   The previous practice of counting them incentivized active obstruction of federal immigration law by states, undermining our federal republic, creating the greatest crisis since mass resistance to desegregation. <br/><br/>Racial classification is anathema to the principle of equal protection. The growing number of people who are multiracial complicated matters further.  Asking people to classify themselves is an assault on their dignity and serves no lawful purpose, rather fueling illegal discrimination. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0611,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-rlv0-bdyj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only American citizens should be counted in the census. States that harbor illegal immigrants are being granted representation by counting them.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0612,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-rp0e-iv3y,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Tommy Sprinkle, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>Congressional seats should be based on citizens and legal permanent residents, not people here illegally or temporarily.<br/>States should not gain more seats in Congress or Electoral College votes because of people who are not permanent legal residents.<br/>The Census should focus on counting people, not asking unnecessary personal questions about race or ethnicity.<br/>Other Census surveys can still collect demographic information when it is needed.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0614,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-rq4i-mj9c,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Susanna Smith, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: 1) our census should count citizens and persons permanently legal only. Visitors, undocumented persons, and other foreigners should not be counted, and 2) we are a divided country right now, demographically. Does it matter if someone is gay, or black, or whatever? We do a census to determine voting and apportionment, not to say &ldquo;there are x number of x people in a particular county&rdquo;. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0615,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-rr94-vq5v,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This proposed change is a long time coming. Very few if any other countries in the world do it like we have done in the past. I fully support this change and offer it should have been done for the 2020 census. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0616,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ry75-ldrm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"It is about time that our country truly become colorblind, and the best way to do this is to stop collecting data about race and ethnicity.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0617,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-s2fr-8fdo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The Census should only count individuals that are here legally and permanently, such as green card holders. Transients and individuals here in violation of immigration laws should be excluded. As for demographic data on race, sexuality or other factors, it should be excluded as it only serves to encourage division by these factors.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0618,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-s83g-eh56,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Brent Babin. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. I believe representation as well apportionment and distribution of government benefits should be determined based on those who are residing legally within the US to better promote rule of  law and legal immigration policy.  Further, I believe the country is far too divided along identity lines. As far as the US government is concerned, identity should not matter as we are all guaranteed equal protection under the law.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0619,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-saxm-3zl9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0620,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-sgco-9d1a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the addition of the new language and rules for  properly counting citizens and legal aliens only in the next census.  Apportionment decisions should be based on legal residents, not illegal aliens. The actions of  leftists and others to open our borders and allow millions of illegals into the country is a blatant  attempt to dilute the iwll of citizens and legal residents in how to form policy affecting the inhabitants of the USA. only lawful residents and citizens of the USA should be considered in determining the legal enumeration of population for the census. i also support counting illegal aliens separately as a critical aspect of the census so the american people cna see how many of such persons actually reside in the USA and therefore cause an excess burden on taxpayers to support such residents   by way of the inflated costs/ budgets  of education, helath care, infrastructure, and welfare programs. I also support the change in reducing the level of detailed demographic data requested in the census; such data is only used to create competing groups of residents, it does not add any real value to the census mission. thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0621,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-sglw-w40d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Please allow the change to happen. Those who are not in our country legally, or here on a temporary basis should NOT determine representation, electoral college, nor funding.  Because of the current lack of determination one political party can USE the situation, entice illegal aliens to enter our country, in order to control districting structure, gaining seats in the House of Representatives and to predetermine how many Electoral College votes they will gain to control who becomes president.  The Census has become a tool for cheating the American people out of choice. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0622,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-sp8m-1u4k,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Daniel Lockhart of Ellijay, Ga. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. May God Bless America.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0623,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-stp0-9o35,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I being an American citizen support only counting American citizens in trhe 2030 census. It is not fair to count those that are not citizens.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0624,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-stz6-rkno,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The US census creates many thing most important among them is the apportionments of the peoples representatives to congress. As a US citizen and veteran I find it egregious that citizens of foreign countries and even people in the USA illegally can effect these apportionments. It id also egregious that power hungey state governments who create so called sanctuary cities to flood their population can criminally and fraudulently dilute my representation  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0625,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-svnt-bwwa,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My name is Rita Payne. I am commenting as a US citizen about section 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the language of the two sections. Only U.S. citizens should be counted in our census. I support adoption of these sections in part 15 of CFA.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0627,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-t80p-z4gy,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. <br/>1) Re: 60.4; To support the unity of our nation, only those individuals who reside in this country legally should hold the right to vote in our elections. To allow less than this is to invite disharmony and risk the danger of breaking up the United States of America.<br/> 2) Re: 70.2; To require such specific information from each person only encourages and enables government (and others) to create &lsquo;special&rsquo; groups of people, set them apart from their fellow Americans. This inevitably ends in discord.  I believe in one person, one citizen and that is all that is needed for the census.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0628,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-t9rs-5g1e,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Victoria Ogan. I am a citizen of the United States and I am commenting as an individual. I wish to comment on sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the language of both sections as proposed.  America is one of a kind, and representation belongs only to those people who are legal citizens. Including any other category in the counting of a census dilutes the representation of those people who are legal citizens.  For this reason I fully support the proposed language of sections 60,4 and 70.2. Thank you for your attention.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0626,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-t451-bmi8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0629,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-tcn5-vfih,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Joshua Parker, I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. As a citizen and taxpayer I believe that representation and the decisions made thereof should reflect the will of the legal citizens of the country. Geographic concentrations of those in the country illegally should not be able to help either party gain an electoral advantage.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0630,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-tgv2-czzz,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Samuel Frazer, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are that I believe in our US Declaration of Independence and Constitution, that outlines the principles of benefits to CITIZENS of our country. Therefore the counting of citizens is of the utmost importance, NOT counting visitors, no matter how long they have merely resided here. The results of this census have great impacts on the apportionment and distribution of our tax monies. As citizens our opinions should matter, and not be diluted by those persons who are not lawful citizens. Thank you. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0631,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-thbg-4zc8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed changes to the census. In particular, I do not believe that congressional representation should be impacted by people here illegally, or frankly, by people not entitled to vote.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0633,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-tij3-r3sr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is W. Garland Lowe Jr, I am a citizen of the United States of America. Concerning sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I&#39;m in favor the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I also support the proposed language of section 70.2 also as published.  These changes are needed.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0634,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-tkwc-8gb9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please do NOT count those people that are in USA illegally. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0632,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-thza-yrnf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Kevin Franck, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  1) Only US citizens or lawful permanent residents should be considered for the purposes of apportionment  2) It is none of the governments business to know or attempt to classify people by race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation.  We are all Americans and none of this matters for the Census.  It seems this type of information is used to divide the nation verses unite it.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0635,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-tn46-0scv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Marcia Rafferty Zgirta, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support of these changes are that I believe the right to be counted as a US resident should be reserved for legal citizens and those who are adhering to the legal process of becoming citizens as those numbers counted directly affect the allocation of congressional districts and representation of legal citizens therein. These changes protect that representation. Thank you for your consideration of my response. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0636,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-tn98-jpjz,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Schuster, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  I do believe that when it comes to the question of proportionate representation in the government, only those who are legally and permanently here should count.  Thus, counting vacationers, temporary residents and illegal immigrants makes no sense as the US Government has no permanent jurisdiction over them.  Second, intrusive questions about individual demographics are not required by the Constitution and only serve to divide and segregate the population into interest groups and voting blocs.  We are all Americans first and the Census should reflect that.  Thank you very much for your consideration.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0637,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-trh9-lxmf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Katharyn Bishop, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual.  I am addressing section 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published.  I further support the proposed language of section 70.2 as published.  The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>State representation should be based solely on the number of LEGAL U.S. citizens residing in that state.  The race, religion, or any other personal criteria should not be included in the census, as it is irrelevant to the purpose of the census.  Our election process has been highjacked by politicians and individuals who seek to personally benefit from the inclusion of non-citizens in both the census and state and federal elections.  The proposed changes are a necessary step in ensuring that the votes of legal citizens are the only votes that count.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0638,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ts9o-b9b2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Undocumented aliens, illegal aliens or individuals who entered the country illegally or overstayed any type of visa should not be counted in the census to establish congressional representation. Only native born American citizens or naturalized citizens should be counted.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0640,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-tvva-t2j0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I strongly agree that only U.S. citizens should be counted, and NO &ldquo;apportion&rdquo; should ever be given to non U.S. citizens. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0639,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-tufd-xdlu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"In my opinion, one of the most devastating consequences of the 2020 Census emphasis on granularity is that it encouraged an unhealthy pattern of valuing self-segregation in one&rsquo;s identity in U.S. society over the core values in our Declaration of Independence, in which we held the aspiration to the truth that we are all created equal. Subtle things have profound effects on the social harmony of our nation. This has not been a healthy decade for our people. In my view, reminding everyone that none of us is special unless all of us are special together is a paramount element of U.S. national interest, the further we get into the mid 21st-century. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0643,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-u26a-ho2z,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I want to support the rule changes to the upcoming census. I fully support the change that ensures that those ILLEGALLY in this country, and other designated non-citizens are not included in the count for the purpose of apportionment of elected representatives. I further support the change that eliminates the inclusion of race as a part of the census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0641,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-twfv-q6lq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Timothy J Lanier, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. I believe that the proposed rules changes stated above are affirmative for the benefit of the citizens of the United States while also being equitbaly designed.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0642,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-txim-yiqt,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Richard L. Thompson, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. It&#39;s time we got back to common sense in deciding who should be counted for census purposes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0645,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-u34f-yjev,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jalaja McCarty, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo; <br/>Section 60.4 - I would like to omit green card holders and only the citizens of the USA and dual citizens should be there for apportionment.<br/>Sectio. 70.2  - regarding questions, should not ask for race, creed or color sex. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0647,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-u7wy-54u6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I fully agree with non citizens not being included in the census.  It clearly states what is logical here-why would a Illegal Non citizen be considered to have any voice is our representative government?  They aren&rsquo;t supposed to even be here! ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0644,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-u2nf-4iam,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Daniel Downey, a citizen of the United States of America residing in Atlanta, GA and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: The US is a representative republic meaning that our elected representatives are, ideally, supposed to represent the interests of the citizens who elected them to office. If a person is here illegally, they should have no say in how this country is run and their interests should not be represented other than how they may intersect with legal citizens, e.g. crime, government benefits, etc and in each case the interests of the citizen should take precedence. It is already illegal for non-citizens to vote. Counting them in a census which uses that tabulation to determine congressional representation should logically also be illegal.<br/><br/>Given that huge numbers of aliens were illegally allowed to enter the US for the purely political purpose of skewing the apportionment process, determining congressional districts, and determining congressional majority, with the ultimate goal of allowing them to vote in our elections, it is imperative we codify that they will not be counted for apportionment purposes. <br/><br/>The stakes could not be higher.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0646,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-u72v-qpv0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Anthony Niles I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>Since only American citizens are allowed to vote in Federal and State elections those individuals should be the ones counted for purposes of apportionment for seats in the House of Representatives.  It is also important that the incentive for states to encourage illegal immigration for purposes of receiving increased allotments of Federal funding be removed, and that we protect the standing of American citizens first.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0649,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ucug-sh7v,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual and am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. My reasons for support are as follows. To me, the purpose of the census is to get a head count of US citizens. That information can then be used as input for downstream legal purposes to ensure protection of US citizen rights. At it&#39;s most basic level, why would anyone want to count others who aren&#39;t on any kind membership roster? Either I am or am not. As for my race, creed, religion, etcetera, that&#39;s not important to determine at this level. Equal protection under the law will clarify who gets what. And finally, the question of &quot;am I a legal/illegal citizen&quot; or however it&#39;s worded, is not likely to yield very accurate results because people who are not US citizens would likely see the question as a threat.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0648,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ua2z-xdf2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is ____Frank Brattina_________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0650,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-uhr9-hvsq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Kimberly Engleson. I would like to comment as an individual on the purposed language of sections 60.4 and 10.2 of the NPRM. I support both for these reasons. On 60.4, only legal citizens of the United States should be counted in our census for purposes of representation and spending of Federal money.  On 10.2, we do not need to divide our citizens into separate groups. A citizen is a citizen treated equally.<br/>Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0651,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ujtf-s8ng,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I believe all the changes proposed are needed.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0652,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-un1n-yos2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I believe that these new rules should be implemented for this upcoming census!  That those in the United States illegally should not be counted in the census; however, if they are documented they should be categorized as not lawfully in the USA!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0653,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-uo8j-me7c,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the rule changes to census count.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0655,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ury3-ix2x,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I have a question.... Is there recourse for lying on the census form? Yes, we need to know who is here legally AND illegally. People here illegally should NOT be included for representation in Congress in Washington. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0656,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-utnr-nsun,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Kathy R. McGlothlin, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>As a citizens of these United States I approve the proposed changes to subsection 60.4 and subsection 70.2 to the 2030 Census as published.<br/>&sect; 60.4 Foreign citizens in the United States.<br/>(a) Citizens of foreign countries who are also either citizens or lawful permanent residents of the United States as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Counted at their usual residence in accordance with &sect;&thinsp;60.2.<br/>(b) All other citizens of foreign countries as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Not counted for apportionment.<br/><br/> &sect;70.2 Restrictions on the inclusion of certain demographic questions.<br/>a. No question about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation shall appear on the short form decennial census questionnaire or any questionnaire used for the enumeration of the population.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0654,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-up90-lbxb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>Allocation of resources should go to US citizens first.  Representation within the United States needs to be based on citizenship and not just a warm body that may or may not be in the US legally.  For instance, I or anyone else residing in a Home Owners Association would be able to vote in any other HOA without owning property within such boundries.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0657,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-uvkn-i2gn,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is _____________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0659,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-uxv7-nlvj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please institute these changes in the US Census. Thank you.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0658,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-uwq1-gu0r,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Henry Mackey, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0661,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vcyt-kqp8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John Schnake, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reason for my support is that the apportionment of congressional seats and my taxpayer dollars must not be impacted by the presence of illegal aliens in the country.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0662,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vf9x-1n6v,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As in Federal voting, apportionment should only include legal citizens.  Period.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0660,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vaao-upcx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The proposed wording of the census criteria is absolutely correct: citizens of the United States only should be counted.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0663,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vg1x-ahm4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is James A Collinsworth Jr, I am a citizen of the United States and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. I believe that only legal residents as outlined in the proposed change to section 60.4 should be counted for apportionment. We are all citizens of the United States regardless of type of racial, ethnic, sexual orientation. As such I do not believe these are relevant questions for our decadal census. Additionally, this information is readily available using other sources and does not need to be repeating in a census. Thank you for your consideration on this matter.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0664,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vi47-i4v4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Counting only citizens and legal permanent residents is commonsense and I support it fully.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0665,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vim5-5wth,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Brendan O&#39;Neill, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>ONLY US citizens and those with permanent LEGAL residency should be counted in any census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0666,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vjvm-fjke,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am submitting this comment regarding Docket No. USBC-2026-0628.<br/><br/>I work in community health in South Texas, but my concern with this proposal is more fundamental than its impact on any one community.<br/><br/>I am asking the Census Bureau to return to the basic purpose of the Census.<br/><br/>The Census Bureau describes its responsibility as counting every person who should be counted once, only once, and in the right place. The Bureau also reminds us that the framers chose population, not wealth or land, as the basis for sharing political power.<br/><br/>That history matters because it reflects a foundational understanding: representation should be grounded in the people who are actually here, not in judgments about their economic value, social status, legal status, or perceived worth.<br/><br/>The Census Bureau has long understood the importance of counting people as people. That is what gives the Census its legitimacy.<br/><br/>Once the count used for representation begins to distinguish between people who live in a state and people who are deemed worthy of being included for representation, it moves away from that foundational purpose.<br/><br/>The Census should answer a simple question:<br/><br/>**Who lives here?**<br/><br/>Once a person lives in a community, their legal status should not determine whether their presence counts for representation.<br/><br/>Immigration status may be relevant to immigration law and policy. It should not change the factual reality of who lives in a state and is part of its population.<br/><br/>The purpose of the Census is to count the population as it exists, not to redefine the population for purposes of representation.<br/><br/>Immigration policy can and should be debated through the political and legal processes of this country. But the Census should not become another mechanism for resolving that debate.<br/><br/>If a person lives in a community, is governed by its laws, and is part of its population, excluding that person from the count used to determine representation does not make the person disappear. It makes the count less reflective of reality and the community less fully represented.<br/><br/>I am also concerned about changes that could make people afraid to participate in the Census. The proposal considers asking about legal status and using extensive administrative records to determine legal status. A Census that people fear is a Census that risks becoming less accurate. When one member of a household is afraid to participate, entire households may be missed, including U.S. citizens and children.<br/><br/>In addition, removing race and ethnicity questions from the decennial Census questionnaire. If we stop collecting information in this fundamental national count that allows us to see disparities, those disparities do not disappear. They simply become harder to see and easier to ignore.<br/><br/>The impact of these changes would also extend far beyond congressional representation. Census data are used across government, health care, education, research, community planning, infrastructure, and funding decisions. Communities rely on these data to understand population needs, identify disparities, plan services, and make decisions about where resources are needed.<br/><br/>If the underlying data become less complete, less accurate, or less trusted, the consequences will be felt across all of those systems. A change that weakens the integrity of the Census does not stay within the Census. It weakens the information that communities and institutions rely on to make decisions for years afterward.<br/><br/>The proposed changes do not reflect the simple principle of counting the people who live in this country once, only once, and in the right place for purposes of representation.<br/><br/>The credibility of the Census rests on something larger than any administration or political moment. It rests on public confidence that the Census exists to measure the country as it is, accurately and objectively.<br/><br/>Please protect that role.<br/><br/>**Count the people who are here.**<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0668,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vpro-n80n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree with the proposed changes and support full implementation,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0667,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vkfd-5ou1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Ron Copper I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0669,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vqmy-hzl9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed changes to determine representation for US citizens and long term, authorized residents.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0670,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-vrag-iuqo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only United States citizens should be counted. Thank you,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0671,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-w9dw-ysis,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is ___Sharon DiPiazza__________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>Any person who is not a legal resident of the United States of America is not entitled to representation in the House or Senate.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0672,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-wcmx-hu37,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Charles McGlothlin, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>As a citizens of these United States I approve the proposed changes to subsection 60.4 and subsection 70.2 to the 2030 Census as published. I also think people in America who are illegal or not citizens should be counted in a separate census but not included in any federal appropriations. This will guarantee that funding appropriations go for American Citizens only.  <br/><br/>&sect; 60.4 Foreign citizens in the United States.<br/>(a) Citizens of foreign countries who are also either citizens or lawful permanent residents of the United States as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Counted at their usual residence in accordance with &sect;&thinsp;60.2.<br/>(b) All other citizens of foreign countries as of April 1 of a decennial census year. Not counted for apportionment.<br/><br/> &sect;70.2 Restrictions on the inclusion of certain demographic questions.<br/>a. No question about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation shall appear on the short form decennial census questionnaire or any questionnaire used for the enumeration of the population.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0674,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-wlps-srid,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Dear Census Bureau,<br/><br/>Political representation belongs to the American people&mdash;those who hold citizenship and the right to vote. Counting non-citizens in the decennial census inflates the congressional seats and electoral votes of states with large non-citizen populations, transferring power away from citizens elsewhere. This rewards jurisdictions that tolerate unlawful presence and penalizes those that do not. The census should measure the sovereign body that elects and is governed, not every person physically present. Excluding non-citizens aligns apportionment with democratic principle: government derives its just powers from the consent of the governed, not from those outside the political community.<br/><br/>Respectfully submitted",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0673,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-wf2m-7nes,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Count all people but only use US citizens for apportionment.<br/>,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0675,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-wnpr-wsj7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0676,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-wrfy-j407,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Stop counting foreigners as citizens in appointments and to the numbers Of Representatives from a state.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0677,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-wri7-7s41,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed residence criteria that would count U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents (green-card holders) in the decennial census apportionment count, and would not count people who are here illegally or whose legal status is less permanent than a green card. I believe the official count used for representation should include people with a lasting lawful residence in the United States. <br/><br/>Second, I support the proposed limits on demographic questions on the short-form census questionnaire, including questions about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation. I believe the main census form should focus on counting people for representation, not collecting extra identity information.  Please finalize these proposed changes.<br/><br/>It seems like a reasonable rules to me and should be implemented<br/>there shouldn&#39;t be an afvantage to states who support illegal immigration that can inflate its representation. the second rule can surely be used for other means that are contrary to a more equal population<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0678,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-wrki-1sqd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Lois  Vance, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support is that only US citizens should be counted and represented in the governance of our country. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0681,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-x4qi-t54t,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please use these rule changes and make the census about true American citizenship and not include any and all foreigners. <br/>Put America and Americans and <br/>Green Card holders first in all things! ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0679,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-wyjb-ex38,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Gerard Altonji, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. As for section 60.4, it is clear (to me, at least) that the constitution requires only US citizens be allowed to vote in our elections.  The proposal you make here makes sense to me and also properly allows permanent (green card) and duel citizen residents to vote. Every vote cast by non-citizens negates a vote by a valid US citizen.  This must end.  As for rule 70.2, the census is no place for granual detail that woujld allow small groups (or even large groups) the ability to claim victimhood.  Citizenship should be color blind and not matter what ethic, gender, or any other &quot;minority&quot; or &quot;majority&quot; group exists.  If someone is a valid US citizen, no questions asked, they should be allowed to vote. Demographic questions can be asked through other surveys and not be linked to the voter rolls. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0680,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-x3fp-rg5c,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Joseph M. Smyth, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>Congressional representation should be determined by the census of legal U.S. residents. Illegal aliens, foreign workers, and visitors should not influence such representation. Congress represents the citizens and permanent foreign residents (green card holders), not temporary visitors and/or illegal aliens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0683,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-x93z-y71i,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I agree with the rule changes.Only citizens of the U.S. and lawful permanent residents of the United States should be counted in the 2030 census. Questions about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation are irrelevant to counting the census and should be eliminated from the 2030 census questionaire",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0682,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-x7y8-usor,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I entirely support the changes to the rules for obtaining the 2030 census, specifically 15 CFR Parts 60 and 70<br/><br/>[Docket No. 260903-0005].",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0684,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-x9au-nikp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Sean O. Saltzman, I am a citizen of the United States of America, a retired USAF Lt Col, and a retired GS-13 from the Dept of Veterans Affairs.  I am commenting as an individual, addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  I am rather astonished that the changes proposed are even up to question.  OF COURSE only US citizens should be counted for the census.  How is that even a questionable point?  I would even propose that even the lawful permanent residents (i.e., green card holders) should not be counted, but I&#39;ll concede that point if we at least move forward with the changes as proposed.  We simply HAVE to get proper accountability and accurate data in our census count for myriad reasons.  It is painfully obvious why there are those who don&#39;t want these changes and are so adamantly against this proposed rule making -- apportionment of congressional seats and distribution of federal monies.  In short, they want to continue cheating the system.  Cheating ois so much easier.  This all must be fixed to restore integrity to our system.  Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0685,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xgh4-8m00,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The changes to the 2030 census are sorely needed! I am totally in favor of making these changes. Non citizens and especially illegal immigrants SHOULD NOT be counted in the 2030 census. Our tax dollars should not be supporting people who are not citizens and states with so-called &ldquo;sanctuary cities&rdquo; should not have more representation in D.C. because of it. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0686,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xgxx-ep55,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Thomas J Ping, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>Only citizens, Americans, or those legally permitted to be here should be counted. This determines where and on whom taxpayer money should be apportioned. Foreigners should not have any say on that. It also determines how many representatives each state has to send to Congress. That is very important to the very governing of the country. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0687,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xhbh-sfll,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Joseph H. Raborg, Jr., I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>I believe that these changes will support an accurate basis for electoral representation for citizens.<br/><br/>I believe that these changes will help mitigate fraud in federal budget allotments.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0688,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xk8s-hy7l,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I believe this is an excellent idea. Only citizens should be counted in the census. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0689,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xn40-xub0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. I support the proposed language because I believe ONLY American citizens should be counted in the census and that ONLY American citizens be represented in Congress.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0691,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xury-pkqd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I think that only people who were born in the USA or legally naturalized citizens should be counted in the 2030 census<br/> I agree with the proposed resolution.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0690,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xq25-kt5o,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0692,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xw02-4tw8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Pamela Biss, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:  <br/><br/>1. Representation at the state level should reflect the number of people who are in that state legally and should not include those who are there illegally; to do otherwise gives representative power by increased representation in the House of Representatives through breaking immigration laws and rewarding illegal behavior. <br/><br/>2. &ldquo;E pluribus unum,&rdquo; is the phrase chosen by our Founding Fathers to represent the model of a unified republic composed of many diverse states.  Our states are represented by its people who first and foremost are individuals;  gathering information about specific characteristics is irrelevant to being an individual and sets the stage for groups to form based on certain characteristics, and for those groups to seek more power or representation by virtue of those characteristics.<br/><br/>Sincerely,<br/>Pamela Biss<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0694,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xzt0-yecl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"These proposed changes should be rejected immediately. Data about race and ethnicity are important to the federal government as well as state and local governments. Census data are used to make policy, allocate funding, and carry out the research that underlies both. Leaders need to know what is happening in their communities and this includes understanding how race and ethnicity figure into it.<br/><br/>Proposed changes include the elimination of questions about race and ethnicity. Not collecting this type of data would have a severe impact on public health and our ability to track health disparities. As one example, it would become harder to address the Black maternal health crisis.<br/><br/>No, no, no. These changes need to be rejected. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0693,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-xyag-nuz3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0695,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-y5ul-klk2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"1) I believe it is imperative to count only US citizens and green card holders for purposes of determining allocation of seats in the House of Representatives. Only US citizens and green card holders should play a role in how we govern ourselves. Moreover, only such persons should be considered when determining how social benefits to the needy are to be distributed. Including non-US citizens can significantly skew how seats are apportioned among the states, thereby inadvertently disadvantaging some US citizens by allocating more resources to states with higher illegal immigrant populations.<br/>2) I believe we should count non-US citizen visitors, non-US citizen students, and illegal immigrants separately from US citizens and green card holders to identify the number of foreigners living in the United States and to assist US law enforcement personnel in arresting and expelling those who may be in the country unlawfully.<br/>3) I believe that we should minimize granular questions, by which I mean, that we should not continually ask politically charged questions about sexual orientation, sexual preferences, trans status, and the like. In my view, the census serves the valid purpose of determining how to distribute representation among the various states to ensure that we have a government whose laws and policies accord with the will of the majority of our people as determined by their political representatives. Politically motivated questions should be avoided to the greatest extent possible. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0696,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-y6j5-afia,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I believe the Census should separate citizens from non- citizens as people are counted. And, I believe only citizens should be counted for purposes of apportionment ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0699,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-yjnr-mldg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"There is absolutely no reason to count non-citizens for the purposes of allocation of state and federal resources designated for citizens, nor is there any reason to count non-citizens for the apportionment of representatives in state or federal offices.  <br/><br/>Non-citizens are not part of our CITIZEN government, which is supposed to be CITIZENS elected by and CITIZENS, representing CITIZENS. <br/><br/>Non-citizens enjoy in our nation many benefits. Voting and being represented in our legislative bodies IS NOT one of those benefits.  <br/><br/>That this was ever done or allowed is a traitorous disgrace.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0697,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-yiy5-v2h4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  The purpose of the census is for apportionment for Congress; therefore, citizens and lawful permanent residents ONLY should be counted. Extra information, such as race, has no bearing on the headcount, so questions about that data should be excluded from the census forms.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0698,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-yj88-tc5i,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Michael P Breedlove, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. only American citizens should be counted for apportionment in the US Congress.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0702,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ypwn-9x4f,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Gary Strutz.   I am and have been a citizen of the United States of America since my birth in 1951 in Chicago, Illinois, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>I&#39;m a staunch supporter of legal immigration, even though I&#39;ve been opposed to the policy of many administrations since the horrible changes were made to our immigration laws in 1965.  Since then we&#39;ve gone from emphasizing immigrants with exceptional skills not in sufficient supply in the USA to prioritizing family reunions.  We&#39;ve also deflated legal residents&#39; political value by counting those here illegally and those here temporarily by counting them in our census.  We&#39;ve also undermined our pre-1965 common sense approaches to legal immigration and essentially moved illegal aliens to the front of the line in our now destructive immigration policies.  <br/><br/>I would not expect to be counted in the census of a foreign nation I was visiting as a tourist or in which I was residing illegally.  Why should such a dumb policy be followed here?<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0703,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ysr5-j94y,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is an unconstitutional idea. The constitution clearly states the census measures &#39;persons&#39; living within the United States. Not to mention, these persons you want to exclude contribute to the tax base and fund public schools and roads. Don&#39;t succumb to the ideologues.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0700,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ylvx-7m0d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I do not agree with the proposed changes to 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2. Illegal immigrants and unlawful persons here should not be counted in the Census.<br/>Undocumented persons, foreign students, foreign executives and foreigners in the U.S. on temporary work permits living in California or any US State should also not be counted. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0701,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-ympe-xhvo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Long overdue.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0705,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-yua8-2d0a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,as an American citizen i fully support this new rule,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0704,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-yu34-7o5l,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Charles Vahrenkamp, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0706,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-yuei-ksgm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am strongly in favor of NOT COUNTING FOREIGNERS who are not US citizens nor lawful permanent residents.<br/><br/>I view this as a serious flaw in past policy, and I feel that such a correction is long overdue.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0707,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-z6ua-wkg2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Charles K Condray, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  I believe the responsibility of all governments of the United States and the underlying states, counties, cities and other municipalities is to serve the citizens of the United States.  No other persons should be included or counted that may affect the apportionment of elected representatives, or any other services that the various governments may provide.  <br/>The founders of this country held that &quot;all people are created equal&quot;.  This is also understood to mean that all people are treated equally.  i.e. &quot;equal justice under the rule of law&quot;.  Therefore the census should only count citizens and not any subcategories dividing citizens into different groups.  There should be no questions about; race, ethnicity, sex, sexual orientation, color, creed or any other category to divide citizens.  The moment you begin to divide into groups you open the door to begin to break the common bond that unites us all.  We are all equal citizens of the United States.  The census should only determine the number of citizens and their geographic distribution.  The data can then be used only for equal treatment of all citizens of the United States.  Equal apportionment of representation and distribution of resources amongst all citizens thereof.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0709,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-zde7-ztsx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is _____________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>Since the census is used to allocate representation in the national government, locales with high percentages of non citizens should not be rewarded with disproportionate representation that will no doubt advocate for non citizen interests. This is an existing problem created by the current rules. The proposed rule change can begin to remedy this by reducing or eliminating congressional representation that advocates for non citizens over citizens. <br/>Additionally, the country is more divided than ever, the federal government should not add fuel to this division by asking racial, ethnic, or religious affiliation. The only pertinent information needed from the census is how many citizens live in each state so representation and funding can be allocated proportionately. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0708,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-zaog-a9oe,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John Van Hyfte, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0711,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-zgn6-7yka,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed regulation 70.2 because if you are an American, that is all that matters. Identity politics is divisive, not inclusive and what makes America special is that once you are an American, you are an American, no matter what.  So no more unnecessary questions!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0712,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-zjkn-xnmd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My Name is Jean G Nave, I am a registered voter of Tillamook County, Oregon. I support the proposed language of 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I believe that all American are &quot;special&quot; and no groupe is More special or should be treated differently than any other. We are a melting pot that comes together to help each of us find a better life. May we always stay that way.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0713,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-02qn-s1z0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Richard Iascone, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. 1. America should look out for our own first, 2. The issue of race has become very divisive and needs to be tamped down for the good of our country.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0710,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-zelz-oy6q,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of both section 60.4 and section 70.2 as published. My reasons for support are as follows.<br/>Section 60.4 <br/>I feel that only people in our country legally and with a personal stake in the success of our nation should benefit from representation in the legislature. Areas that support; encourage or promote illegal residence in the United States should not be rewarded with a larger voice in congress afforded by the presence of those in our country without legal status.<br/>Section 70.2<br/>I don&rsquo;t think that  race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation should be considered when collecting census data. Among other things the census weighs on representation in congress and in my opinion considering anything other total population and population concentration should be irrelevant.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0714,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-0b0a-k5yg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I strongly support this change to only include U.S. citizens, Green Card holders and Dual Citizens.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0715,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-0cl2-ffmo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I wish to offer my full support to the proposed regulations for demographic questions on the Census form. As an American, I feel that my representatives should be determined by by Americans, not someone passing through and clearly not someone here illegally. I also believe that we count people not special interest groups or specific type of citizens, just people.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0716,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-0o3o-kwe5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hell9, my name is John Wright, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: i support the constitution and desire it to be followed to enforce our laws; the census should reflect the citizens,  not visitors; and further group specificity should mot be pursued. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0718,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-160w-gofg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Scott L Meredith, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  Only legal U.S. Citizens should be counted towards congressional apportionment.  Legal status is the only proper consideration.<br/>Race, ethnicity, sexual preferences, ETC. should not matter one way or the other and thus should not be collected.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0719,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-172t-9v0r,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is unconstitutional and unconscionable. Show some gumption and do the right thing. Do not capitulate to this corrupt administration. For gods sake just do the right thing. This shouldn&rsquo;t even need to be said.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0717,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-0u5t-uv2a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,," I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>They are not documented and should not be included in the census. The census needs to accurately determine how many seats in Congress are needed for the legally documented residents.<br/>The argument that the states with the most illegals would lose the most money is exactly why we need this census to exclude illegals.<br/>The argument is made that they live, work, and play in the USA. Illegally.<br/>They can live, work, and play in their home country.<br/><br/>I agree that this is a return to what the founding fathers intended the once-a-decade headcount to be.<br/><br/>&ldquo;These proposals are intended to ensure fidelity to the Constitution, improve and protect the core decennial census data, and promote efficiency,&rdquo; the draft rules say.<br/><br/>It says undocumented immigrants should be left out because &ldquo;they are not true inhabitants, members of the body politic, or persons with a &lsquo;usual residence&rsquo; in the United States.&rdquo;",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0721,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-1rmj-y7iu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/>I feel U.S. citizens, including dual citizens and green card holders (legal permanent residents) only should be counted in 2030 for the purposes of apportionment.  I also feel certain demographic information such as race, ethnicity and sexual orientation is not pertinent to the census count for usual residents.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0722,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-1rxc-1s42,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,See attached file(s),,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0722/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0720,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-1l0g-ne07,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My wife and I both citizens of the United States of America.  We are commenting as a couple addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for our support are as follows. The census is meant to count the number of US citizens for the purpose of apportioning congressional seats; counting non-citizens skews the apportionment process and disenfranchises citizens.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0725,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-28sz-dxi2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I fully and vigorously support these proposed changes.  Illegal aliens should not be counted in census since they should not even be here. Aside from some very narrowly specific health proclivities more common only amongst certain races and ethnicities, requiring the demographic data merely further divides us and we need badly to knit our people back together.  There are other bettter ways to count those individuals should that need to be done.<br/><br/>I would still like to count the illegal aliens present only so we know how many are actually here but they do not qualify for representation in our gov&#39;t decisions aside from the methods of locating and removing them.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0726,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-2apz-sjfy,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My name is Steven Slattery I&#39;m a citizen of the United States. I&#39;m in favor of the new supposed census for 2030 l believe only legal U.S. citizens should be eligible to file one ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0723,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-1xvl-09d4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only persons that are citizens of the United States should be included in the census numbers used for appropriation of members of Congress. All others counted are for informational purposes only as they should not have representation in the legislature.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0724,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-27ll-d6ji,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Illegals should not be counted in the census because they have no right to vote and thus should not have an impact on the apportionment of house seats which has a direct impact on the political process.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0729,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-2n1k-8t9l,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Charles Henesy, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. It is important to me because I believe it is in the spirit of the ideas express in the U.S. constitution.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0727,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-2fv8-gl1r,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I strongly support this rule change!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0728,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-2lhk-r8s4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support is the census is utilized for apportionment for representation in Congress and electoral votes. The cenus should only reflect lawful citizens of the United States, or those here legally as described in 60.4 and 70.2. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0732,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-329l-v6ap,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0733,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-33pk-dvoo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is _____________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>1.  Only citizens and legal residents of this country should be counted in the 2030 Census for purposes of representation and resource distribution. Americans should come first!<br/>2.  I also strongly approve of the new guideline that no question about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation should be included in the 2030 Census, because that information is irrelevant to counting the population and because we are all created equal according to the US Constitution.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0730,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-2r4u-29h5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"For the decennial Census of the United States, I believe that it is in the best interest of the Citizens of the United States and, therefore, the government of the United States to count all people represented in the country.  It is relevant to citizenry and governmental agencies to know how many people reside in the United States, as well as what their legal status within the country is. Therefore, I support any question to identify which individuals are citizens and which ones are residents (either legal or illegal).<br/><br/>I believe that all censuses should count all people in the country and establish the citizenship of all individuals that are counted.  It is in the best interest of our citizens and government to know how many people are citizens, non-citizens who are legal residents on various forms of visas, and non-citizens here illegally, with no lawful status in the United States.  It is said that one manages what one monitors; therefore, it is important to monitor the number of people within the borders of the United States and their legal status to be here.<br/><br/>There is a very important difference between citizens and residents, and that must be at the forefront of all decisions made regarding the census.  A citizen is a person legally recognized as a member of the country; they have the right to hold a passport from the country, as well as full rights and responsibilities, including the right to vote when they have reached the relevant age.  A resident is an individual who lives in the country but does not have the right to hold a passport from the country and has limited rights, and they do not have the right to vote in state or federal elections.<br/><br/>In specific regard to the decennial census, it is used to apportion seats to the U.S. House of Representatives; the only value that should be used to determine the number of seats each state should have should be the number of CITIZENS that reside in that state.  Only citizens are eligible to vote for our elected officials, such as a Congressman/Congresswoman; therefore, the apportionment should be based on citizens.  To include residents with non-citizen status dilutes the vote of the American people and disenfranchises U.S. Citizens.<br/><br/>Our elected officials&#39; focus should be on U.S. Citizens, to protect our rights and safety.  To dilute the voice of the citizen by apportioning seats in the U.S. House of Representatives by basing that apportionment on the number of residents is a violation of the trust and integrity of the census and election process.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0731,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-2si7-1ztg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Good day, <br/>My name is Scott Sharer, I reside in Florida as an American citizen of 69 years of age. <br/><br/>I believe in these proposed changes and wish to have my words read and understood. <br/><br/>American citizens are the prime control of their federal, state, and local governments. As such, it is imperative that only American citizens ought have voting rights. I believe even non citizens with permanent legal status ought never be included in our elections, let alone any individual that is breaking our laws by living inside our legal borders. <br/><br/>I pray these realities will be the rule of permanent law for our governments before my death. That includes all levels of government. I believe the ultimate need is a federal constitutional amendment is needed as the final rule of law. <br/><br/>These changes are a good first step only in my opinion. Thank you for consideration in this matter. <br/><br/>Scott Sharer <br/>11143 Fountain Lake Blvd<br/>Leesburg, FL 34788<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0735,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-37rg-uaqw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am in support of the proposed changes to the census policy.  In no manner should our policy practices oppose enforcing our laws or provide any individual or group encouragement in violating or opposing the enforcement of our immigration laws.  Excluding individuals that are in the US illegally protects against inflating the voting power of local entities that support &quot;sanctuary&quot; practices and in some manner reduces incentive for providing &quot;sanctuary&quot;.  Now is the time to do this because of the level of materiality that now exists because of past &quot;open borders&quot; policies.  Not local or state entity should benefit in political influence as a result of obstructing US immigration law nor financial benefit by a greater allocation of funding as a result of greater voting influence.  I also support removing demographic, and race based questions that feed the negative direction this country has been drawn to over the past couple decades of divisiveness and rade based  influences.  If every citizen is to have equal rights, then we need to quit viewing ourselves as more aligned to sub-groups for the purposes of national identity.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0734,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-34ud-gdkp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Why would we want to be citizens of a country whose governments places non-citizens at the same level as a citizen?,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0736,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-3ags-uqai,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Patrick O&rsquo;Mahoney, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support of these are that they preserve the intent of the census to allocate representation. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0739,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-3xgi-syi3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a naturally born citizen of the United States of America, with two parents naturally born within the U.S., and I am commenting as an individual. I strongly support the proposed language of both sections 60.4 and 70.2 as published in the NRPM. I believe that only legal U.S. citizens eligible to vote in national elections (and their children) should be counted in the census so as to avoid any confusion as to whom should be represented in Congress.  Counting non-citizens artificially impacts the census as the majority are not in the country legally, therefore they should not be represented in matters concerning the United States.  Sanctuary states should not benefit by increased representation in Congress from a census based on counting those who are in the state illegally - this dilutes the vote of legal citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0737,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-3cf3-52wa,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Brenda Crofoot, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>-Only US Citizens and dual citizens and green card holders (legal permanent residents)<br/>-Questions beyond citizenship serve special interest groups should never receive any of my personal information. The Census is about citizens representation in our government. <br/><br/>Brenda Crofoot",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0738,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-3nnz-si08,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is yet another voter suppression attempt by a fascist regime. The only hosl here is to dilute the pool of voters who might not vote for the current party in power and punish typically liberal states by withholding funding based in population,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0740,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-46td-4dos,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Marc S Cheves, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.\:<br/><br/>ONLY LEGAL CITIZENS SHOULD BE ALLOWED TO VOTE",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0741,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-48r1-a862,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows. An individuals &quot;usual Residence&quot; should NOT pertain to removable aliens as it infringes on all US citizens rights. There are many individuals that should not be included in the apportionment count, as they are not true inhabitants, many crossed into the US border during the Biden administration (&gt;20 million in my estimation) so why would we apportion Representatives for their illegal actions as it subtracts from overall US citizenship rights. Also the census was not created for purposes other than apportionment of Representatives among the States and should NOT be used otherwise.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0742,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-4ewh-nsyp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The census should only reflect actual citizens for reapportionment of the House of Representatives. I would not even include green cards and others included for various reasons so I strongly support the proposed change. I would also support any actions that could be taken to avoid the miscounts that awarded some states representation that deprived other states of their lawful representation for ten years in the 2020 Census.<br/><br/>I also strongly support the elimination of all the extraneous information that has been added to the census over the years. This purpose of this particular census is reapportionment period. Extraneous questions should never have been added and now should be eliminated. If Congress wishes to know the answers to these other things, they can charge the Census Bureau with collecting that information independent of the Apportionment Census. The Dicentenial Census for proper apportionment should be designed to do just that and only that and to do it accurately",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0743,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-4rje-lep7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only  US. citizens should be counted in the census.  Perhaps if you have a green card but only after a period of five years with a green card.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0745,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-4tl1-etb0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree completely with the proposed changes.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0746,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-588u-kpix,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I agree with and approve the proposed changes to the collection of our census. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0744,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-4so3-brew,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Andrea Kolczynski, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows - illegal aliens (among others) should not be included in the apportionment count, as they are not true inhabitants, members of the body politic, or persons with a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; in the United States due to their lack of a sufficient tie and allegiance to the United States. The federal government could &ldquo;weight&rdquo; the number of illegals in a state by settling them where the government wants and therefore change apportionment based on government actions vice the actual state residents. I don&rsquo;t think that is fair.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0747,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-58lw-sg55,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Richard D. Hollis, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>The proposed language confirms that congressional apportionment reflects only U.S. Citizens and legal residents. Questions regarding race, ethnicity, or sex are also irrelevant to a census. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0748,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-5ipy-dh6p,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America.  I am commenting as an individual on Section 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2.  I believe that Section 15 CFR 60.4 is appropriate as written.  Only citizens of the United States and permanent residents should be counted in the census; illegal aliens should not be included in the apportionment count. <br/><br/>Section 15 CFR 70.2, regarding questions about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation.  I believe the questionnaire should not include any questions regarding race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation on the decennial census short-form questionnaire or any questionnaire used for purposes of the enumeration.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0749,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-5pof-fd43,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Joseph R. Schwerzler, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>It is my belief that when the founding fathers, in drafting the Constitution, Articles 1, Section 2 &quot;No Person shall be a Representative who shall not have attained to the Age of twenty five Years, and been seven Years a Citizen of the United States, and who shall not, when elected, be an Inhabitant of that State in which he shall be chosen.&quot; that Citizen and only Citizens were meant to be counted in &quot;The actual Enumeration&quot;, not someone who came into this country illegally. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0750,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-5rtw-51y9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Noncitizens who are counted give states with higher populations of noncitizens an unfair advantage. Take California for example. We know the state has the highest number of noncitizens. The state also has the highest number of congressional seats. The open borders by Biden allows for these people to be counted and that then counters citizens. Every noncitizen counted replaces a citizen&rsquo;s voice and vote - and they don&rsquo;t even need to reside in the same state for this to become a problem. If every person is counted, then apportioned, the unfair advantage states like California has is their representation becomes an advantage. If there are less people in Tennessee for example, TN has a smaller voice in congress. Additionally, state that use noncitizens for their advantage also gain financially. That money is then used to the political party leadership&rsquo;s advantage. Again see California for this living example. California has allowed its state to be flooded, taking resources away from citizens and taking away the voices of citizens. With this unfair advantage, California became a Democrat run state when it was historically red or purple. The state used federally apportioned funds to fund additional unfair advantages such as giving noncitizens healthcare paid for by citizens, housing and cash aid also paid for by citizens, and citizens who do need assistance are not given it. Look what happened in NC after Helene. The state was ignored by Biden - the few housing options available to citizens were given to noncitizens and citizens slept and died in tents in winter. The ability to abuse every system top to bottom relies on the census counting bodies - when the system should only count those legally eligible to be here, and apportionment should not be given or granted for noncitizens, nonresidents, undocumented, temporary status etc. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0751,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-5xdc-rbpd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is KARIN L DAVID, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>I believe that a person should NOT be included in our census by having a mere presence within our borders. Our country needs a policy that focuses on making the people who are U.S. citizens the priority. The census and its impact on apportionment goes beyond assigning the distribution of congressional districts. It affects the how the wealth of the United States is spent when it comes to helping the domestic population of the country.<br/>If you are not a legal citizen you should have no input in the apportionment of money or the assignment of congressional districts. You may work, live and pay taxes, but you are not a citizen and not entitled to any of the wealth or benefits of the United States.<br/>I further believe that inclusion of individuals in our census dilute the importance of citizenship and benefits should be reserved for citizens only. Just because you work and pay taxes does not mean you are a citizen. <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0752,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-65cz-8izl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This decision would mark a major break with more than two centuries of census practice: Race data have been collected in every census since 1790. States with large immigrant populations (for example: Texas, California, New York, Florida) could lose congressional seats and Electoral College votes. This is a way of curbing the influence of immigrant populations. Obscuring demographic information like this makes it more difficult to properly enforce voter rights and anti-discrimination policies, which will mean poor treatment of minorities/communities of color will be easier to hide on a societal level. This proposal should not pass. Period. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0753,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-65hi-6ptp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support President Trumps suggestions for new census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0754,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-6lqm-mepx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,IMy name is Kimberly Gay from Canton Texas. I believe only US citizens should be included in census.  I agree with the Trump administration on the new rule proposal and I will continue to fight against all illegal aliens that have come into my country unlawfully  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0755,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-6sis-ykb8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Absolutely yes, illegal aliens should not count for assigning congressional seats. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0756,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-6stl-trk5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I strongly agree that zero Illegal Aliens, zero temporary residents, and zero non-citizen legal residents, should be counted in the US Census.<br/><br/>The Census should only count legal citizens residing in the USA, legal US citizens serving overseas in military, diplomatic, or similar roles; and legal naturalized citizens living in the USA.<br/><br/>Non-adult &quot;birthright citizens&quot; who do not reside in the USA, should not be counted in the Census.<br/><br/>I strongly agree that the US Census should stop asking demographic questions.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0758,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-6vmi-qr1g,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I have specific concerns on the proposal &quot;..the Census Bureau finds merit in the interpretive argument outlined above and has preliminarily determined that, under the best reading of the Constitutional mandate, illegal aliens (among others) should not be included in the apportionment count, as they are not true inhabitants, members of the body politic, or persons with a &#39;usual residence&#39; in the United States due to their lack of a sufficient tie and allegiance to the United States.&quot; <br/><br/>To exclude certain non‑citizens referred to in the notice as &ldquo;illegal aliens&rdquo; appears to be driven by political motivations rather than by the public interest or sound statistical practice. I state this because of this language in particular &quot;One of the primary legal arguments against including illegal aliens in the apportionment count is rooted in the understanding that the term &ldquo;persons,&rdquo; as used in the Apportionment Clause of Article I, Section 2, was deliberately chosen instead of &ldquo;citizens&rdquo; to reflect the Three-Fifths Compromise but nonetheless was largely informed by (and synonymous with) the term &ldquo;inhabitants,&rdquo; which was historically understood to refer to members of the body politic. See Wesberry v. Sanders, 376 U.S. 1, 13-14 (1964)&quot;<br/><br/>The term &ldquo;illegal alien&rdquo; has a long history of being used in ways that stigmatize communities, confuse the public, and cause collateral harm to innocent families who rely on accurate census data for representation and resources. It is a politically charged label rather than a neutral statistical category, and its use in this proposal departs from longstanding census precedent, which has consistently relied on objective classifications such as foreign‑born, non‑citizen, or lawful permanent resident.<br/><br/>The proposal&rsquo;s reliance on the argument that &ldquo;persons&rdquo; in Article I, Section 2 should be interpreted as synonymous with &ldquo;inhabitants&rdquo; and that &ldquo;inhabitants&rdquo; must be limited to &ldquo;members of the body politic&rdquo; is not only historically contested but selectively applied. For over two centuries, the Census Bureau has interpreted &ldquo;persons&rdquo; to mean all persons residing in the United States, regardless of immigration status, because the constitutional mandate is to count population, not political membership. The citation to Wesberry v. Sanders does not support excluding residents; it addresses equal representation, not redefining who counts as a person for apportionment.<br/><br/>Given this context, the proposal&rsquo;s framing and terminology suggest an effort to reshape census categories for political ends rather than to improve accuracy, neutrality, or public trust. Counting the foreign‑born population, including those without legal status, has been a consistent and essential part of the census. If the Bureau wishes to tabulate subcategories within that population, I think it&rsquo;s fair to do so through statistical methods. But removing or diminishing the count of any residents contradicts constitutional practice, undermines data quality, and introduces bias into a process that must remain nonpartisan and scientifically grounded.<br/><br/>While there is validity in reevaluating a set of residence criteria to determine each person&#39;s &ldquo;usual residence.&rdquo; Any change that reduces the completeness of the count or selectively targets specific groups would harm the integrity of the census and the communities that rely on its data. I urge the Census Bureau to maintain its longstanding practice of counting all persons residing in the United States, including all the foreign‑born and non-resident population, and to reject proposals that compromise neutrality, accuracy, or public trust. As stated in the proposal: &quot; Notably, that residence criterion did not call for any difference in treatment between foreign citizens present in the United States legally and those present illegally&mdash;a category of individuals sometimes referred to as &ldquo;removable aliens.&rdquo; See, e.g.,8 U.S.C. 1229a; see also id.1227 (addressing the subclass of &ldquo;deportable aliens&rdquo;); id.1182 (addressing &ldquo;inadmissible aliens&rdquo;). That criterion has been the subject of multiple legal challenges brought by States, particularly with respect to its application to illegal aliens.4 (This notice incorporates for consideration, without necessarily endorsing, the arguments raised in those challenges.) The counting of illegal aliens for apportionment purposes has also been heavily criticized within the Federal government itself.&quot; <br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0757,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-6t1g-x4n9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed changes to section 60.4 and 70.2 as written regarding the NPRM. Counting citizens and legal residents makes sense, as they have a vested interest in our government, while people here illegally or temporarily, like students, may have negative consequences for some states. For instance, if a state has a large, temporary influx of students who will return home in 4 years, that state will gain unfair representation in Congress. If we count people here illegally, that gives states that allow them to stay an unfair advantage. The 2020 Census is a prime example of the latter. CA should have lost seats, and FL should have gained. Further, the self-segregation of identity politics has no place in the Census. It is merely for apportionment purposes. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0759,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-6yka-5pg6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are Neil and Barbara Snyder, we are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as individuals. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/><br/>The reasons for our support of section 60.4 are based on the legal precedence, moral correctness, and common sense.  Illegal aliens are not part of the body politic given prohibitions on voting in national elections, therefore should have no impact on the apportionment of seats in Congress.  Morally it is unconceivable to bias the apportionment of Congress based on individuals that owe no allegiance to the United States, have demonstrated disregard for its laws by their very presence in the United States, and are unwilling to demonstrate the moral compass required to participate in a Republic form of Government as constructed by the Founders.  Common sense supports the legal and moral arguments and support of 60.4 is the only reasonable conclusion upon application of common sense.<br/><br/>We support 70.2 because the additional demographic questions are not relevant to the purpose of the census.  There is no valid argument in support of these demographic qualifiers.  In fact, one could argue that the questions are unconstitutional.  <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0762,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-739s-grh7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,only americans should be counted. not green card holders.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0760,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-72tg-ybfc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Someone who is not here permanently; whether as a citizen, an illegal immigrant, green card or otherwise, should not be counted.  The count is for those that the government is bound to support.  All of those not here permanent flucuates and varies by state depending on who THEY decide to support.  Not the nation. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0761,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-739m-aqgb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,only americans should be counted. not green card holders.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0763,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-7buk-7dkc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I fully support this change to The residency criteria.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0764,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-7q25-e2uo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are T. Murphy and A.Murphy, we are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as individuals. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for our support are as follows: Census information should be gathered only from U.S. citizens and those here legally. Furthermore, that information should not be devisive or invasive of privacy. Thank you.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0765,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-7wau-94y0,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am Irene Light, a citizen of the United States of America and I am commenting as an individual. In addressing Section 60.4 of the NPRM I support the proposed language as published. In addressing Section 70.2 of the MPRM I support the proposed language as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>In Section 60.4 of the  NPRM I support the census counting only US citizens and legal residents because states with illegal aliens counted should not have more Electoral votes that states having only US citizens in them to be counted.<br/>In Section 70.2 of the NPRM I support the census refraining from counting citizens by race, ethnicity and/or sexual orientation.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0766,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-88oe-f3hn,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mary G. B. Pace, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.  Only US citizens should be counted for purposes of apportionment.  Emphasis on granularity has the subtle and profound effect of people feeling that we are not all created equal.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0768,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-8qdh-vsiu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0767,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-8dtx-tsws,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is James, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>Clearly, it is common sense that people who are not legal citizens should have no impact on apportionment within our government. Further, things such as sexual orientation, ethnicity, etc. should not impact representation in any way!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0770,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-97cd-jjd8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Brian Jones, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:   Illegal Aliens should not be counted in a way that determines representation in the US Federal Goverment (US Representatives) nor to obtain services and money that should be for US citizens only.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0771,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-9lw8-kb0u,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is John A Wakefield, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>Simple: Only US citizens should be represented in congress.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0769,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-969b-k8mm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am in favor of ensuring only citizens are represented in the census since that is how we base our representation in congress.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0772,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-9ma5-jnoy,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I do not want ILLEGAL aliens or tourists counted in the census. Count US citizens and legal immigrants. Counting iloegals  punishes those states that cooperate with ICE and rewards cesspools like CA, where I live, with increased Congressional representation.  Enough of this pandering stupidity",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0774,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-9q0u-fzo6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My name is Mark Hall and I am a citizen of the United States of America by birth. I am commenting as an individual to address sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I fully support the proposed language of section 60.4 and section 70.2 as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>I believe the US Census procedure should be designed to collect data on US citizens and permanent legal residents only. Apportionment of congressional representation and federal financial benefits should not be based on the presence of individuals who are temporary visitors or individuals who entered our country without legal permission. I also believe such representation and benefits should not be based on the demographic background of individual US citizens and permanent legal residents. All US Citizens and permanent legal residents should be counted individually and treated equally. These beliefs are based upon my interpretation and understanding of the US Constitution.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0773,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-9nr2-sh17,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is stupid as hell. All persons are included in the census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0775,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-a074-pwj9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I understand and agree with the proposed changes to 15 CFR 60.4 - Only United States Citizens and Lawful Permanent Residents should be counted in the 2030 Census for apportionment purposes and all others, including undocumented aliens should not be counted for apportionment purposes. I also understand and agree with the Proposed changes to 15 CFR 70.2 regarding questions related to the collection of demographic information in the 2030 Census.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0777,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-b3yb-its4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Census questions should ask as needed to determine each person&#39;s usual place of residence.<br/>Census questions should not ask lineage, religion, income, education of any other questions usable to segment citizens or group citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0778,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-b4cl-lame,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I&#39;ll keep my comment brief.  Only U.S. citizens and others in the country legally should be counted for the purposes of the 2030 census.  Thank you,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0776,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-b0j5-6zgn,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are Raymond W. Gross, Jr. and Kathryn K. Gross, husband and wife.  We are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as a couple.<br/><br/>We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  We support the proposed language of Section 60.4 and Section 70.2 as published except clauses that propose to count non citizens who happen to live within the US.<br/><br/>The reasons for our support are as follows:<br/><span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>The US government was formed to be &lsquo;of the people and by the people.&rsquo;  In this context, the word people means citizens of the US; nobody else.<br/><span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>No non citizen should have any say in who is selected to represent citizens to the national government.<br/><span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Sex, ethnicity and sexual orientation of those counted have no bearing on the count; questions related to these identifiers are irrelevant and should be eliminated.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0781,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-bs8k-uzrg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Please make the changes to USBC-2026-0628-0001, sections &sect;&thinsp;60.4 and &sect;&thinsp;70.2.  I fully support them.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0779,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-bofc-yrl7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I very strongly support the proposed rule change for sections 60.4 and 70.2.  It is irrational to count people who are legally not supposed to be in this country.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0780,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-bon3-vlyd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the proposed changes in sections &sect;&thinsp;60.4 and &sect;&thinsp;70.2,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0783,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-cry3-4st8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Please dont count foreigners here without documentation in US census. They should be considered as any Visitor who is not allowed by law to stay<br/><br/>Perhaps our pathetic state leaders in CA will stop taking our tax dollars to fund programing and protections for undocumented over programs for elderly and vets&hellip;. Maybe politicians will cut those programs if there isnt the benefit of seats and power in it for them. <br/><br/>They replace the numbers of  those  fleeing bad policy, who pay considerable taxes, with those who contribute negligibly to our tax revenue, creatng a growing shortfall year after year<br/><br/>Our leaders then give them free medical care (11 billion), they get housing assist, food assist, in state tuition fee,help with down payments and most recently &ldquo;seed&rdquo; funding to open businesses, all without documentation. Meanwhile to fund it, the take money out of public mental healthand other vital programs<br/><br/>Children of undocumented parents make up 20% of k-12 students in public schools and our schools are failing our kids,  spending more than ever but turning out college freshmen who cant do5th grade math<br/><br/>3 million sitting in hundreds of thousands of our affordable units while vets and families are homeless<br/><br/>We shouldnt get funding based on people who shouldnt be here. Yes it will be still be an uphill battle to convince Dems to cut services to them but its a pain we must tolerate; .the current state is unsustainable <br/><br/>They aren&rsquo;t eligible for state benefit  funds, they shouldnt be counted in census. Their existence and politicians policies are Killing the middle class and darkening our kids futures<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0782,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-bylv-jgsr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The census determines representation in congress. Oue elected leaders represent AMERICANS, not residents from foreign countries who have not become citizens. My great-grandparents immigrated from Canada and became naturalized citizens, they considered it their duty to their new home. Unless a foreigner becomes a citizen, the census bureau should not count them in the upcoming census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0784,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-d4uz-qobk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is _____________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>I served in the US Army to protect USA from foreign and domestic. Therefore I think only US citizens should be counted for census!<br/><br/>Thanks<br/>Homer Stewart",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0786,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-d8b2-nq4x,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Finally.  Praying that this will pass and continue.  Make America Great Again.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0785,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-d787-eu80,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I fully support the Census Bureau&#39;s proposal to ensure fidelity to the Constitution, improve and protect the core decennial census data, to (1.) add a part outlining the residence criteria used for identifying each person&#39;s &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; to determine whether and where to count them in the census of the population throughout the United States. (2.) the Census Bureau&#39;s proposal to add a part establishing standards and restrictions for the inclusion of demographic questions on the decennial census short-form questionnaire and any other questionnaire used for the enumeration of the population.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0787,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-dy4l-huw4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hello and thank you for this excellent rule change for the census going forward! <br/><br/>My name is Alison, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I live in Southern California. I am commenting as an individual. <br/><br/>I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/><br/>I think it is ridiculous that the United States is allowing undocumented immigrants to be included in our Census. In doing so, states that host large populations of undocumented immigrants- who generally have  more allegiance to their country of origin instead of the United States by the way- obtain more power politically. Those states therefore have more members of Congress allocated to them as well as having more Electoral College votes for the US presidential elections. <br/><br/>It was ridiculous that Article 2 of the 14 Amendment was rarely enforced post- Civil War. Because this was not enforced,  the Southern formerly slave holding states added each formerly enslaved Black man to their census but those states still denied the right of that very man to vote. Undocumented immigrants likewise are not allowed to vote in our elections, so therefore they should not be allowed to be included in the Census. <br/><br/>Thank you for your attention. <br/><br/>And then you can go on as much as you like about why you believe these sections should be adopted into Part 15 of the C.F.R. when the final rule on the 2030 Census is published later this year.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0791,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-falx-xdg4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"We are a husband and wife who support the proposed changes to 15 CFR 60.4 and 15 CFR 70.2 . This is common sense, given that apportionment is determined by the result of the census. We find it outrageous that certain states offset their declining populations and loss of influence by hosting and counting illegal immigrants. Thank you for seriously considering new rules to stop this from happening. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0788,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-e6sz-qjnd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, a military retiree, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>In 2010 and particularly in 2020, I was appalled at the amount of information demanded by the census in both those decades. The census should not be used for data mining of information of citizens; the amount of personal information that is available online is patently ridiculous already. The census should be limited expressly to apportionment based on Article I, Section 2 of the U.S. Constitution -- nothing more.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0789,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-e917-apzg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"So race and sexual orientation are off the board, but you still need to check citizenship status and &quot;biological sex&quot; (good job avoiding the scary &quot;gender&quot; word)? <br/><br/>I thought you would want all the data you can get, so that you can tell which districts to punish for daring to have too many brown people or queers. <br/><br/>You are all cowards. You are (rightly) scared that if people were allowed to vote freely, you&#39;d be unemployed in months. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0790,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-f00v-sdcm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"It is imperative that we maintain a proper count of U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents for the purpose of apportionment.<br/><br/>Those here temporarily or illegally should NOT be counted for the purpose of apportionment. <br/><br/>Counting those here temporarily or illegally only encourages states to subvert federal law, attract illegal aliens to pad their congressional and electoral college numbers, while stealing tax dollars from the &ccedil;itizenry to use for fraudulent purposes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0792,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-fnkd-rerj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"These changes would fundamentally change the census, making it cease performing the task envisioned by our founders. <br/><br/>We have always counted *all* the people, even when enslaved Black people noted by the census were only counted as 3/5ths of a person. Any changes that restrict that work are a betrayal of our founding fathers. <br/><br/>In addition, the proposed changes would make the census almost useless as a data collection device. Please don&rsquo;t waste our time. <br/><br/>As an individual, a mom, an educator, and a school board member, I implore you to consider the original spirit in which the decennial census was adopted, as well as the damage these changes will cause to the preservation of knowledge and the ability to serve constituents. <br/><br/>Thanks so much for your time!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0793,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-g6jv-gq11,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I do not want none citizens count in determining congressional apportionment.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0794,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-hfpd-i6nz,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is a colossally bad idea and is quintessentially un-American. It&#39;s downright SHAMEFUL to even consider excluding people from the census due to citizenship status or any of these other criteria. People do not magically disappear if you don&#39;t count them! They don&#39;t stop being part of our communities where they contribute to community wellbeing, local economies, and general happiness, who equally deserve care, compassion, and help when they need it. <br/><br/>Census counts are vital for determining how much resources each area needs, in so many ways this would be an essay if I named them. If an area is suddenly determined to need a vastly lower amount of resources while the actual living population there hasn&#39;t changed, suddenly you&#39;ve got people suffering from not having enough to go round. This proposal is designed to make vulnerable people suffer on purpose for no reason. It cannot be allowed to pass.<br/><br/>--signed, an Extremely Concerned US Citizen ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0796,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-hxy6-nsgm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Linda Hoffman and I am a citizen of the United States of America residing in Arizona. I am commenting as an individual, addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are  the census is intended to reflect the population of Americans. The last census expanded the scope of this inquiry in a way that distorted the outcome and I want it stopped. Inaccurate information in results in badly designed programs out. And clusters of power transferring from where it belongs -- with we the citizens of America -- to others not entitled.  Trust you will be responsible. <br/><br/>Linda Hoffman<br/>Tucson, AZ 85750<br/><br/>And then you can go on as much as you like about why you believe these sections should be adopted into Part 15 of the C.F.R. when the final rule on the 2030 Census is published later this year.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0795,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-hve1-h7ur,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,US citizens ONLY counted on the 2030 census. Racial identity irrelevant,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0797,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-ic67-5280,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed changes to the Census Bureau regulations regarding the 10-year census, beginning in 2030. Both areas of interest are in need of change.  First, changing to apportionment based on citizenship is long overdue. Since only citizens of the United States may vote in Congressional &amp; Presidential elections; apportionment should be restricted in similar fashion - citizenship.<br/>That being said, collection of census data based on residency, to include non-citizens may be allowed; but only for purposes of allocating resources, not apportionment.<br/>As related to the other proposed change - collection of demographic data. It seems intrusive to ask questions of race, ethnicity, sexual identity or health. Yet, from my memory of past participation, I remember being required to provide such information &amp; exclusion carried the risk of penalty prescribed by force of law.<br/>Jeffrey Morris<br/>1917 Wind Walker Dr<br/>Reno NV 89521",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0799,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-lbu8-46uh,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dave Warn, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>The census is used to measure the AMERICAN population and to apportion congressional seats. No foreign citizens should be included in either of these activities because that would wrongly distort the results to the detriment of the AMERICAN people.<br/>The census&#39; purpose is not and should not be to collect sectional, separating data which can be used to segregate by tribe, etc rather than ensuring a core value of our Declaration of Independence that we are all created equal.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0798,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-k51b-t1nr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am in favor of the proposed rule change, to only count full US citizens and not ask other demographic information. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0800,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-lcci-xui4,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only US Citizens should be counted in the US census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0801,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-lnn4-lnkp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am writing, as a public health scientist, as a mother of three children who are both white and Asian, and as a citizen of this country, to strongly oppose the proposed changes to the Decennial Census outlined in 2026-18481 (91 FR 57524). Specifically, I write to oppose the removal of race categories from the census. Removing this information will be detrimental across many areas. As a public health scientist, I will focus on concerns from that viewpoint. <br/><br/>Even though Race has no inherent biological meaning, the categorization system is deeply embedded in our culture and society. As a result, these categories impact health outcomes. We must continue to collect race data in order to conduct public health research, evaluate public health programs, and track substantial and as yet persistent health disparities. <br/><br/>The framework for the race categories we use today was invented by Carl Linnaeus in 1735, just as the trans-Atlantic slave trade was escalating toward its peak. The Linnaean race categories were hierarchical, dividing humanity based on skin color and geography, and ranking the groups. Linnaeus place Homo Europaeus albescens (translating to white European man) at the top, followed by Homo Americanus rubescens (translating to &ldquo;red American man,&rdquo; referring to people indigenous to the Americas), then Homo Asiaticus fuscus (translating to &ldquo;brown Asian&rdquo; man), and finally, at the bottom of the hierarchy, Homo Africanus niger (or Black African man). This purported biological hierarchy was used to justify and excuse the increasingly profitable exploitation and brutal treatment of African people through the trans-Atlantic slave trade and American chattel slavery. The same racial classification system has endured over centuries, with only minor changes to the names and the addition of a. Few categories. The mapping of the human genome provided undeniable evidence that race categories are not biologically meaningful. Humans genes, regardless of race, are 99% the same. In fact, greater genetic heterogeneity exists among people descended from different parts of Africa (all racialized in the US as Black) than between certain African and European populations. If humans were grouped into categories based on genetic similarity, the resulting groups would look nothing like our current racial classification system.<br/><br/>Yet, the hierarchical nature of race groups has remained deeply embedded in American culture. As recently as 1954, schools were legally segregated by race until Brown v Board of Education determined separate was not equal. Alachua County, where I attended college, did not desegregate its schools until 1970, 16 years after Brown v Board of Education. The last segregated school in the US did not desegregate until 2017. 2017! That is the year my 9 year old son was born.<br/><br/>Race matters because we made it matter, through policies that provide different access to opportunity based on the color of one&rsquo;s skin. These discriminatory policies have yet to be fully eradicated. Those that have been corrected in recent decades still have impacts on those who experienced the discriminatory policies. These policies have contributed to substantial racial disparities in health outcomes. Black Americans are 4 times more likely to progress to kidney failure, 60% more likely to develop diabetes, and twice as likely to develop Alzheimer&#39;s Disease. <br/><br/>Removing race categories from the Census will leave us blind to these disparities. But being  unable to measure a problem does not make the problem disappear. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0802,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-mnj8-rl74,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is a terrible law, support for which would instantly lose any representative my vote. It is vital that we have accurate data on who is in this country and that we accurately represent Americans in Congress. The only use for this proposal is to take away power from the states and erase people from our information systems.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0803,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-n2bc-hcfg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mark Jones, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>  I believe that ONLY citizens of the United States should be counted in a census for the purpose of representation, but I&#39;ll accept the counting, reluctantly, of legal permanent residents. <br/>  In re 70.2, let the special interest groups do their own demographic surveys with their own money. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0806,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-o4o5-hnjx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"It is time to count ONLY legal American Citizens in the census count for the purpose of true and fair representation. No foreign students, visitors, or illegals should be included in this count. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0804,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-n2u4-k6q3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,These changes are UNCONSTITUTIONAL. The constitution calls for the census to include ALL living in the state. This is a terrible attempt to decrease the democratic voices in half the states. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0805,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-nzx1-8150,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a US citizen, I oppose this proposed rule. Excluding many noncitizen residents from congressional apportionment, despite their residence in our communities, risks producing population counts that do not fully represent the people states serve. I am also concerned about expanded reliance on administrative records to determine residence and legal status and the proposed removal of race and ethnicity questions from the decennial census. Accurate, transparent demographic data are essential for identifying disparities, allocating resources, planning public services, and protecting population health. I urge the Census Bureau to preserve an inclusive, equitable and rigorous census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0807,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-oj0k-d99x,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The US government, it&#39;s politicians and funds shouldn&#39;t be subject to foreigners in the United States of America. Only lawful permanent residents and US citizens should be counted when it comes to delineating funds for various locations (states, counties and cities) and populations for US Representatives. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0809,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-pnbp-uuoa,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Non citizens should not be counted in the census . Only american citizens so americans representation in congress is not diluted by counting non residents. Representation in congress should only be based on the number of american citizens for each state,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0810,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-qqzq-42hd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My nane is Joseph Marshall IV, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Regarding rule 70.2. In my opinion, one of the most devastating consequences of the 2020 Census emphasis on granularity is that it encouraged an unhealthy pattern of valuing self-segregation in one&rsquo;s identity in U.S. society over the core values in our Declaration of Independence, in which we held the aspiration to the truth that we are all created equal.<br/><span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Regarding 60.4, I appreciate that it focuses on making people who are here legally the priority. The census and its impact on apportionment goes beyond assigning the distribution of congressional districts. It affects the how the wealth of the United States is spent when it comes to helping the domestic population of the country. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0808,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-op0n-j48z,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am an immigrant and US citizen. I totally support this rule change. Since the main purpose of this census is political, only citizens and permanent residents should be counted. Also if a person who is here illegally is encountered during census, he or she should be promptly deported.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0813,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-t5dm-3b2u,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the Census Bureau&rsquo;s Notice of Proposed Rule Making (NPRM). I support the proposed language of sections 60.4 and 70.2 as published. Only U.S. citizens, including dual citizens and green card holders, should be counted in 2030 for the purposes of apportionment. Since citizens of other countries, including undocumented persons, foreign students, foreign executives assigned to their companies&#39; U.S. offices, individuals here on business trips or visiting on tourist visas, etc., are not personally invested in the future of our country, they don&rsquo;t get a say in how we govern our country and therefore should not be counted. Also, since one of our country&rsquo;s main tenets is that we are all created equal, as called out in our Declaration of Independence, there is no need for the census to ask questions regarding race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation. Collecting such information would just be one more way to further divide rather than unite us.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0811,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-rfra-mqkc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"A nation is defined by its borders and its citizens. A nation gets to decide who is a citizen and who is not. The purpose of any government is to serve the interests of its citizens. In a representative system such as ours, the idea of non-citizens counting for the purpose of representation, and thus having some level of indirect influence on the government which is supposed to focus on citizens, is anathema to my first three statements.<br/><br/>Non-citizens should NOT be counted for representation.While it is certainly useful to know just how many there are, that number should not be used for the allocation of representation and other government resources intended for citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0812,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-sryi-yrqe,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,," Having read the proposed changes, I agree 100% with the proposed rule changes and feel strongly it follows the needs of our constitution and our American rights. I will mentioned I worked with for the last Census<br/>  Thank you!<br/> Michael Dunn",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0814,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-t7ay-jtw8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I think it&rsquo;s a great idea. Only Americans should be counted in the census. Counting Americans only will give a true number of illegals in America and will reduce the number of politicians from blue states, which will reduce the amount of money taxpayers have to fund the house.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0815,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-tb1z-07ee,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I oppose not collecting information based on race- the more we know about racial makeup, the better the government can tailor services to that group.  Also, It&#39;s vitally important to count all of the students in NC as being residents here, they definitely use the services we have to offer, so should be counted as residing here for the census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0816,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-vf8m-nsor,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0817,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu0-we5v-9uyp,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Voter suppression plain and simple. No, there is no reason for this other than to rig elections. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0818,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-0r2y-w88d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mark Mendlovitz, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. I am supporting the new rule because including foreigners who are not permanent residents of the United States in the decennial count dilutes the voting power of districts or states with high concentrations of citizens. <br/><br/>As a simple hypothetical, suppose that one state has 10 residents, but only 2 of them are citizens and the other 8 are non-resident aliens, and another state has 10 residents all of whom are citizens. Then the Electoral Vote count and Congressional apportionment for those two states under the old rules would be exactly the same. That means 2 citizens in one state would have exactly the same voting power or Congressional apportionment as 10 citizens in the other state, merely because non-resident aliens also lived among them. That is the antithesis of one man one vote. For that reason, the new rules are much fairer.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0819,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-15a4-nxgy,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hello, we are Terry L and Cheryl C Hawley, we are citizens of the United States of America, and are commenting as individuals.  We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/><br/>We believe these rules are in the best interest of our nation and should be adopted as written.<br/><br/>Thank you,<br/>Terry &amp; Cheryl Hawley",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0820,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-5jcg-039z,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is James McFelea and I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>1. Regarding 60.4, I believe only US citizens should be counted in the census. I find it very difficult to believe that people in this country illegally should be counted. This needs to change.<br/><br/>2. Regarding 70.2, we need to stop counting people based on their race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation needs to stop. We&#39;re all Americans and should be counted as individuals not as part of a group or tribe. That just contributes to pitting one group versus another and has been increasing in this country for years. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0821,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-5maj-8nzb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,These new rules are absolutely necessary. Foreign nationals should never be counted for purposes of a portion.<br/><br/>To think that these people were counted previously in the United States census is horrible.<br/><br/>It smells of political corruption.<br/><br/>These changes are badly needed,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0822,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-5mhj-5aus,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I have read the proposed Residence Criteria and Regulations for Demographic Questions and wish to express my whole-hearted endorsement.<br/>Non-citizens (whether illegal aliens or legally visiting foreign nationals) have no place, whatsoever, in our National Census.<br/>Moreover, it is wonderful to see, for once, our all-encompassing government adhere to the functions that it was created for and stay within the limits of its originally-created authority.  I feel certain that all sorts of &#39;researchers&#39; would love to add probing questions to the census for their own purpose.  Let them petition Congress for the right to include them.  The Bureau of the Census is proposing to do just what it was created for, and I celebrate that!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0824,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-64og-yzl1,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,We need to count only lawful citizens in our numbers and not inflate the numbers to increase representation in Congress.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0825,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-6a6i-vt47,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I wish the proposed changes to 15 CFR 60.4 (Foreign citizens in the United States )and 15 CFR 70.2 (Restrictions on the inclusion of certain demographic questions) to be approved. The US taxpayer should not be granting skewed representative power to states encouraging illegal alien residence. Further, the emphasis on demographic identity serves no purpose other than division. We need to encourage national inclusion not segregation by groups.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0823,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-60ar-bg71,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Count American citizens only. Keep fraud out of it. People living on streets I don&rsquo;t think should be counted. Count Americans count legal citizens only !,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0826,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-6nfu-trfj,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I fully support both of these changes and feel they will be very beneficial to our country.  ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0827,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-6zwu-bkzq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"For public health purposes, the relevant population is the resident population, not the citizen population. Excluding noncitizens produces an inaccurate picture of how many people live in a community, which can distort disease rates, underestimate healthcare and emergency-service needs, and contribute to inadequate allocation of public health resources. Because health risks and healthcare systems are shared across communities, an inaccurate count can ultimately harm citizens and noncitizens alike. Additionally, excluding residents who actively contribute to their communities&mdash;both through the services they provide and the income and economic activity they generate &mdash; creates an incomplete picture of the community itself. These individuals work, pay taxes, support local businesses, provide essential services, and participate in the local economy. Failing to count them can result in an inaccurate assessment of a community&rsquo;s population, needs, and economic activity, ultimately leading to an inequitable allocation of resources. Public resources should reflect the full population that contributes to, relies upon, and is impacted by the systems within that community.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0828,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-7awd-s8jg,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,US citizens ONLY. No duel citizens. No green card holders.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0829,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-7di8-2788,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This should be all anyone talks about for the next four years. I know that sounds dramatic but I am dead serious. He wants to remove questions about race and ethnicity from the census entirely. This has never been done since the very first census ever held in 1790.<br/><br/>He wants non-citizens to be excluded from population numbers, something which again has never been done in U.S. history. It would be the first time the census has not included the &quot;whole number of persons&quot; in each state since 1860, when an enslaved Black person was counted as three-fifths of a person for population purposes.<br/><br/>I cannot overstate this: The census is everything. It&#39;s the whole ballgame.<br/><br/>The number of representatives each state gets in the House of Representatives? The census.<br/><br/>How voting district lines get drawn? The census.<br/><br/>How the Voting Rights Act and anti-discrimination protections are enforced? The census.<br/><br/>The amount of funding each state gets for housing aid, school funding, school lunch programs, SNAP, highway maintenance, Medicaid and Medicare, special education?<br/><br/>The census.<br/><br/>People should be in the streets over this.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0832,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-7owa-lsfw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The proposed changes are appropriate and very necessary.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0830,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-7hi4-gssu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,US CITIZENS ONLY,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0831,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-7n3g-hvn9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mark Jones, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>  I believe that ONLY citizens of the United States should be counted in a census for the purpose of representation, but I&#39;ll accept the counting, reluctantly, of legal permanent residents. <br/>  In re 70.2, let the special interest groups do their own demographic surveys with their own money. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0833,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-8bly-3u1v,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am absolutely OPPOSED to this administration&#39;s effort to omit immigrants in the U.S. illegally and temporary immigrants from the U.S. census.  An article I recently read states the why very well...<br/><br/>&quot;Both of these proposed changes could warp the data used for drawing congressional and state legislative districts, enforcing anti-discrimination law and allocating federal funds for public services.<br/><br/>The effort could have &quot;earth-shattering&quot; effects, leaving cities and states that have relatively high immigrant populations with insufficient federal aid and political representation.<br/><br/>Census data undergirds so much of our democracy and our society. So, we will start seeing a very inaccurate picture of our country, and then we&#39;re making political decisions, business decisions, investment decisions based on data that is flawed.<br/><br/>The U.S. Constitution&#39;s 14th Amendment calls for political representatives to be apportioned based on &quot;the whole number of persons in each State.&quot;  <br/><br/>This proposal is ILLEGAL as well as immoral.  <br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0834,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-8hri-y7v3,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am so happy that this common sense idea is finally moving forward. If you are not American you don&rsquo;t get to vote and you don&rsquo;t count as an American. It&rsquo;s time to put Americans first in our country and not everyone else. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0835,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-8plm-d8rd,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Steward and I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: 1. Regarding 60.4, I believe only US citizens should be counted in the census. I find it unbelievable that people in this country illegally should be counted and have any representation.  This needs to change. 2. Regarding 70.2, we need to stop counting people based on their race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation. We&#39;re all Americans and should be counted as individuals not as part of a group or tribe. That just contributes to pitting one group versus another and this practice has been increasing in this country for years.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0837,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-9ehl-dcl5,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Rodolfo Gonzalez, Jr., I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: Only US citizens should be counted in the census as the census determines representation in congress as well as the electoral vote&rdquo;",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0836,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-93ny-vnrk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is _____________________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>I became a citizen in 1983 at the age of 29 because as I learned about my adopted country, I wanted to be able to vote and make my voice heard as to how it was being run. I learned that even though my parents, having brought me into the USA as a baby, had legal status as green card holders, I had to be a citizen in order to vote. Likewise, any apportionment or number of representatives should be based on the number of citizens within any specific area, not just the bodies residing therein. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0838,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-9o8f-ej1a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,gal aliens should not be counted in the census!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0840,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-aao5-seug,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am in agreement with the proposed change.  illegal immigrants should not be counted because they have no legal interest in our country.  It is unfair to expect hardworking Americans to support these bums that hate us and are here to destroy us.  in the famous words of Senator John Kennedy  &quot; Let them hate us for free.&quot;  Counting them in the census means allowing more money to states to care for them.  Absolutely under any circumstances should they be counted, nor should they receive any American tax dollars or welfare.  The only thing Americans should pay for is their deportations!!!",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0841,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-acgg-dqyc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Heidi S. Nelson, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. I believe only legal residents of the United States of America should be counted in the Census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0839,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-9odc-7hw9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Not counting non-citizens means our census is inaccurate, period. It upsets all calculations made in our country, and already-suffering systems like schools and social services will be gutted. There is no need to change our process after over a century of collecting this data. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0842,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-ag56-axrm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the change to count only legal citizens!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0844,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-aq2h-t4eb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Terry L Coats, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: As a citizen, I support the Constitution as I see the founding fathers interpreted it. Their intention was that citizens and only citizens should be included and counted as part of a census. Sections, 60.4 and 70.2 adhere to that intent. <br/><br/>Terry L Coats",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0845,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-axtd-7x6p,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Jane Coats, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: I feel that sections 60.4 and 70.4 as presented follows the intent of the founding fathers to have legal citizens and only those eligible to be counted zs it pertains to the census.<br/>Jane C. Coats",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0843,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-aksv-blkr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"<br/>**Comments of Garifuna Coalition USA, Inc.**  <br/>**Docket No. USBC-2026-0628**  <br/>**RIN 0607-AA75**  <br/>**Decennial Census of the Population of Americans: Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions**<br/><br/>**Submitted to:**  <br/>U.S. Department of Commerce  <br/>U.S. Census Bureau<br/><br/>**Submitted by:**  <br/>Garifuna Coalition USA, Inc.  <br/>Bronx, New York<br/><br/>**Date:** September 14, 2026<br/><br/>Garifuna Coalition USA, Inc. respectfully submits these comments concerning the Census Bureau&rsquo;s proposed residence criteria and restrictions on demographic questions for the decennial census.<br/><br/>Garifuna Coalition USA is a nonprofit community organization serving and advocating for Garifuna residents, particularly in New York City and the Bronx. The Garifuna are an Afro-Indigenous and Afro-Latino people with ancestral roots in St. Vincent and the Grenadines and historic communities in Honduras, Belize, Guatemala and Nicaragua. New York City is home to a substantial Garifuna diaspora, yet the community remains significantly underrepresented in government statistics.<br/><br/>The proposed rule raises consequential questions about whether Garifuna residents will be fully counted, accurately represented and meaningfully visible in Census 2030 data.<br/><br/>### 1. Proposed restrictions on race and ethnicity questions<br/><br/>Proposed 15 C.F.R. &sect; 70.2 would prohibit questions about race and ethnicity from appearing on the decennial census questionnaire. The proposal would permit the Census Bureau to obtain demographic information through the American Community Survey, administrative records and other sources.<br/><br/>For the Garifuna community, these alternatives are not equivalent to direct self-identification.<br/><br/>In the 2020 Census, residents could identify as Black or African American and provide a detailed response such as &ldquo;Garifuna.&rdquo; This allowed people to communicate that Garifuna identity is simultaneously connected to Black identity, Latino identity, ethnicity, ancestry and a distinct cultural community.<br/><br/>Garifuna Coalition USA&rsquo;s preliminary review of 2020 Census results found substantially different totals depending on whether Garifuna identity was captured through race or ethnicity data. These differences illustrate that question placement, wording and coding rules can determine whether the community is visible or statistically erased.<br/><br/>Administrative records generally do not contain a Garifuna identifier. They also cannot reliably reproduce a person&rsquo;s voluntary identification as Black, Latino and Garifuna. Relying primarily on such records could therefore classify many Garifuna residents under broader categories while eliminating the specific identity needed for culturally responsive outreach, civil-rights analysis and local planning.<br/><br/>The American Community Survey is valuable, but its sample design and margins of error limit its usefulness for measuring smaller populations at neighborhood and census-tract levels. It is not a complete substitute for decennial data covering every household.<br/><br/>Before adopting the proposed restriction, the Coalition asks the Census Bureau to address the following questions:<br/><br/>- How would the Bureau produce race and ethnicity data at the geographic detail required for redistricting and civil-rights analysis?<br/>- How would residents retain the ability to identify voluntarily as Garifuna or another detailed Black or Latino population?<br/>- What evidence demonstrates that administrative records contain sufficiently complete, accurate and current information about small Afro-Latino and immigrant communities?<br/>- How would the Bureau measure and correct differential missingness or misclassification in administrative records?<br/>- How would the proposed system preserve comparability with 2020 Census data?<br/><br/>### 2. Proposed exclusion of certain foreign citizens from apportionment<br/><br/>Proposed 15 C.F.R. &sect; 60.4 would count foreign citizens for congressional apportionment only when they are also U.S. citizens or lawful permanent residents. Other foreign citizens would not be included in the apportionment population.<br/><br/>This represents a significant departure from the Census Bureau&rsquo;s stated 2020 practice, under which citizens and noncitizens living in the United States were included in resident population counts at their usual residence. The Bureau&rsquo;s existing apportionment guidance states that the resident population includes citizens and noncitizens living in the United States.<br/><br/>Garifuna families in New York may include U.S. citizens, lawful permanent residents, people with Temporary Protected Status, asylum applicants, humanitarian parolees, students, temporary workers and other residents. These individuals live in the same households, use the same schools, transportation systems, hospitals and community services, and contribute to the same neighborhoods.<br/><br/>",,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0843/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0848,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-bgvz-0hvf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I fully and wholly support this proposed rule change.  Only citizens as described in the proposal should be counted in the census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0846,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-b3ds-ljrb,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Bill Warren, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/>1) Changes to the census are badly needed after the verified inaccuracies of the 2020 census which came to light only after the census was processed &amp; the apportionment completed. Some States were overcounted &amp; others undercounted resulting in apportionment errors of the number of Representatives &amp; Federal funds allocated to numerous States.<br/>2) The Census should use verifiable methods for determining the actual number of residents &amp; not use mathematical algorithms that extrapolate population statics from small data sets.<br/>3) The Census should only count those legal residents of the United States that pay taxes, are long term legal residents, &amp;/or have the legal right to vote for our Representatives.<br/>4) I would consider adding a section that if the Census is determined to be incorrect within 1 year after the apportionment &amp; has been determined that it is enough to change the apportionment of Representatives, that the apportionment be revised to the correct number of Representatives for each State. It hardly seems fair that an individual State should bear the consequences of reduced representation &amp; reduced Federal funds for a period of 10 years due to an error that is found early in the process.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0847,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-b9nb-8hgm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Dear Census Bureau,<br/><br/>I am writing to oppose the proposed changes to the decennial census residence criteria and demographic questions.<br/><br/>The decennial census serves as a foundational source of data for public health planning, research, and resource allocation across the United States. The proposed elimination of race and ethnicity questions would significantly impair our ability to identify, monitor, and address health disparities affecting communities across the country. The proposal would prohibit race and ethnicity questions on the decennial census short-form questionnaire, removing a critical source of demographic information used by public health agencies, health systems, researchers, and policymakers.<br/><br/>Race and ethnicity data are essential for measuring disparities in maternal and infant health, chronic disease, cancer outcomes, environmental exposures, health care access, and life expectancy. Public health relies on these data not to divide populations, but to ensure that disparities can be identified and addressed. Without consistent census-based demographic information, it will become more difficult to evaluate community needs, direct resources effectively, and measure progress toward improving health outcomes for all populations.<br/><br/>I am also concerned about the proposed residence criteria, which would exclude certain immigrant populations from apportionment counts and increase reliance on administrative records to determine who is counted. Public health systems must plan for and serve all people living in a community, regardless of immigration status. Communities that are less visible in census data may be more likely to be overlooked in needs assessments, health planning efforts, and policy decisions, despite having substantial health care and public health needs.<br/><br/>While the proposal suggests that reducing demographic questions may lessen respondent burden and improve response rates, the loss of information critical to public health surveillance and health equity efforts would outweigh any potential benefits. Effective public health policy depends on accurate, comprehensive data about the populations we serve.<br/><br/>We cannot address disparities that we cannot measure. For these reasons, I respectfully urge the Census Bureau to retain race and ethnicity questions on the decennial census and to avoid changes that would reduce the visibility and representation of populations whose health needs are an important part of our nation&#39;s public health landscape.<br/><br/>Thank you for your consideration.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0850,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-cft4-xfbk,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This appears to be a way to limit true representation of individuals predicated on a selective nature of questioning.  Questions for a census and number of representatives should be as broad-based and inclusive as possible in order to not limit the true understanding of the demographic.  It should also not constrain the rights of each individual to be counted.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0849,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-c6h6-inxl,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I cannot believe that Foreign Citizens would ever have been counted in the past, and therefore completely agree with th proposed changes. Further, I agree with the simplification of the census in not asking any questions about race, ethnicity, or religion.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0851,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-cr00-dz49,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is James C. McConnell, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: <br/>1.<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>My taxes are already too high. Having to give more of my money to support illegal immigrants in our country places an increasing burden on my finances. <br/>2.<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Allowing illegal immigrants to be counted for reasons of Congressional apportionment creates an incentive for fraud. Sanctuary communities, cities, and states act as magnets for illegal immigrants resulting in a plus up to the number of Congressional seats appropriated for those areas. Illegal immigrants thereby gain Congressional protection and tax dollars, further incentivizing more illegal immigration. It&rsquo;s my opinion that anybody, government official or private citizen, providing safe harbor to illegal immigrants is aiding and abetting criminal activities and should be arrested and charged accordingly.<br/>3.<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>As clearly noted in President Lincoln&rsquo;s Gettysburg Address: &ldquo;Four score and seven years ago our fathers brought forth, on this continent, a new nation, conceived in Liberty, and dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal&rdquo;. Consequently, there is no discernable reason why we should differentiate by race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation in the census. The census should only count the number of legal residents for purposes of Congressional apportionment.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0853,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-crto-c8ly,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Richard Wiersma, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/><br/>First, states should not be permitted to &quot;pad&quot; their political influence, power, and federal subsidies by enacting public policies that attract more illegal invaders into the United States for the purpose of increasing their population statistics.<br/><br/>Second, we are all people belonging to the HUMAN race. Stop with this evil nonsense of trying to divide us by ethnicity, gender, sexual preference, etc.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0852,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-crg8-qk5x,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"We are a group of citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as a group. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo; American soldiers have served, bled and died for our freedoms and our form of government. They did not do that to have their representation diluted by people who should not be in this country. We have representatives now who are more interested in representing people who are illegally in this country than they do representing American citizens. It&rsquo;s time for this to stop.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0854,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-cvd9-2d2n,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Ewaldz, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>I believe the changes in 70.2 will simply and fairly count US citizens without regards factors such their race, sex, ethnicity, etc.  Citizens are citizens period. I hope this will prevent special interest groups to unfairly receive funding paid by taxpayers.  I also believe the changes in 60.4 will properly count valid citizens, and eliminate so-called loopholes that allow non citizens be counted. This should help define congressional maps to better reflect actual valid voters.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0856,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-db2h-qeqs,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am a citizen of the United States.  I am in favor of the proposed rule by the Census Bureau on 09/10/2026.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0855,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-d8l3-4y0w,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is William Smit and I am a citizen of the United States of America. I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: 1. Regarding 60.4, I believe only US citizens should be counted in the census. I find it very difficult to believe that people in this country illegally should be counted. This needs to change. 2. Regarding 70.2, we need to stop counting people based on their race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation needs to stop. We&#39;re all Americans and should be counted as individuals not as part of a group or tribe. That just contributes to pitting one group versus another and has been increasing in this country for years. <br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0857,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-do62-42ie,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Excellent! This is a significant beneficial step in protecting the rights and freedoms of US citizens.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0858,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-dq19-5q4z,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Do not change the census, we need an accurate count of EVERYONE living in the United States regardless of immigration status that&#39;s the entire point of the census, we need go know how many people live where - this is a blatant attempt at unpersoning hundreds of thousands of taxpayers for your own benefit.<br/><br/>Jesus was born an immigrant in Bethlehem, was he not? How can any of you call yourselves Christians when you look at the immigrant, the poor, &quot;the least of us&quot; and see an enemy and not God?",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0860,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-dyzi-16v2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As husband and wife, citizens of the United States of America, we are commenting as individuals, addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. In brief, we strongly support the proposed language of sections 60.4 and 70.2 as published. Americans need to be united, not divided by race or ethnicity. Moreover, in the census, only actual American citizens and lawful permanent residents should be counted. It is an outrage that any other class of persons was ever counted in the census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0861,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-e985-imqv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Brad Hunter. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of both sections as published. The reasons for my supoort are as follows: Persons that owe allegiance to a foreign county should not be allowed to be involved in our country&#39;s politics in any way. To me, that is just common sense that they should not be counted in our census. We should also not collect demographic data on citizens. We are all citizens with the same rights, and no class of citizens should be identified for special treatment. Special treatment promotes resentment and division. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0859,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-dyy4-zgr9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The census form should stay the way it is. Changing the current form to only count citizens and green card holders is a violation of the Constitutional requirement to count all whole persons, and not asking people&#39;s race/ethnicity data means erasing important demographic tracking information. These overtly political changes have no place in a nonpartisan census. <br/><br/>The census matters, and these changes are blatantly political. Do not allow this. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0863,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-efyu-n8lx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I totally support these proposed changes. Only citizens should be counted. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0862,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-ebcd-2izo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Roman. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>Only US citizens should be counted and US resources should be available to US citizens ONLY.  Persons who are in our country illegally should not be counted nor take resources meant to assist US citizens or persons living here &quot;legally&quot;.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0864,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-f42y-mluf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My name is Bruce Fykes.  I am a citizen of the United States of America and I am commenting as an individual.  I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published.  I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.  These changes will give a more accurate of American citizens and should not count non-citizens.  Questions about ethnicity and race are not relevant to the purpose of a census.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0867,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-g6yx-8xre,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Barbara Reed, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/><br/>I don&#39;t believe that our representation in Congress should be influenced by the presence of non-citizens in this country.  As for demographic questions on the census, I don&#39;t think that they are at all relevant in how we are to be represented in Congress. All citizens are American, not some category of hyphenated American.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0868,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-gboa-biqv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"A census should measure the population under rules the government can defend regardless of which party controls it.<br/><br/>If an administration changes who counts for representation or removes demographic information used to detect disparities and enforce civil-rights protections, the burden should be more than showing that the change is legally possible. It should demonstrate, publicly and with evidence, that the new rules produce a more accurate constitutional count, preserve the government&rsquo;s ability to detect material disparities, and can be applied consistently across administrations.<br/><br/>Representation and civil-rights enforcement should not depend on definitions that change with the political incentives of the administration conducting the census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0865,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-flls-ugyn,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I strongly support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. <br/>The reason for my support is that only actual citizens or permanent legal aliens should be represented in Congress. Inflating census numbers with visitors, either legal or not, skews the number of representatives for the states and challenges the equal representation of citizens.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0866,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-g5pa-ukkr,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"On Counting Immigrants: The US Census should count and track immigration at each step and phase as each person attempting immigration makes progress of status towards full citizenship with close attention to country of origin. We must improve the documentation of immigration by country of origin, and each category because of the importance of considering global migration patterns. We must count every human, pledging to support every human life in America. That would be the ONLY responsible act of government. So the Census must do its duty to count everyone regardless of status.<br/><br/>Such a policy would also enable a complete overhaul of the Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) vision, mission, policies and objectives. No immigrant or family containing immigrant persons should need to hide from, and avoid being counted by, ICE for any reason. A proper count by appropriate immigration category is the only method to properly arm our legislators for the immigration programs reform data we so desperately need. To avoid counting will, in essence, leave our borders open and the management of them unaccountable and unsecured.<br/><br/>One the concept of Race: Race should not be a category in the Census; but described only as ethnic identity. Therefore, African American, for example, would be an ethnic category - not a &quot;race&quot;. We should remove the category for &ldquo;race&rdquo; but retain all of the categories for ethnicity not tied to the concept of race. &quot;White,&quot; for example, should be removed as a category and replaced with ethnic categories describing global place of family origin or, if indistinguishable, given an appropriate nomenclature.<br/><br/>In the 21st Century, our databanks are sufficiently sophisticated to handle the complexity of the information, and accurate and non-political data can provide a much better data set for research used to inform the drawing of Congressional districts and all that comes from that authority and responsibility in governance. <br/><br/>States that support and nurture new Americans should be given more support from the federal government because these states must fulfill the purposes of developing valued and productive new  citizens. Our nation cannot continue to struggle under the weight of a flawed system of frightened, oppressed and desperate human beings living in an underworld driven by and preyed upon by the supremacy of the few.<br/><br/>With the proposed changes, the approach the Census is taking continues to promote flawed methods of categorization that do not represent the competence and scientific rigor even seen in past administrations, and therefore I recommend that these proposed changes be held back for further  review and consultation through a special review panel of experts with Congressional oversight.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0870,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-gj33-uv7f,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Deborah K McConnell, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0871,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-gocx-gbzw,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I reject this proposed rule change in its entirety.  Do not remove questions about race and gender from census data.  Do not require immigration status to be reported.  The idea that collecting data about race and gender is no longer relevant is fantasy.  And the Constitution is clear that all people should be counted in the census, not just citizens.  This is vital information that our communities need.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0869,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-gdfr-nw2d,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Camden McConnell II, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0874,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-ha3e-onc2,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Congratulations. You have erased more than 200 years of jurisprudence on the meaning of &quot;domicile&quot; and &quot;residence&quot; by bringing in the term &quot;allegiance&quot; (as in allegiance to a domicile) and using it in a way that it was not intended at the time it was used in the documents you quoted. As a law professor, I give you a C- and I would hope it would require better work than this to omit significant parts of the represented body politic (as opposed to the voting body politic). We have not, since the days of slavery, limited our counting of persons based on their right to vote, which seems to be the essence of your argument; nor have we ignored the rights of those here, by whatever means. The idea that the founders had the same concern as reflected here about &quot;illegal aliens&quot; is preposterous given their desire to grow the country and their free distribution of lands to almost anyone who showed up asking. It&#39;s shameful to see what politics is doing to further its own agenda without concern for intellectual or scholarly integrity.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0872,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-gxss-bh3a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,APDU respectfully requests extension of the comment period.,,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0872/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0873,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-h8g2-rilx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes do not meet constitutional requirements and, if<br/>implemented, would undermine the census&#39;s ability to provide a fair and accurate count of the people living in America. This is unconstitutional, biased, and flat out wrong.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0875,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-hmeu-03iq,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Robert L. Wilke. I am a citizen of Alabama and the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. <br/><br/>I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published for this reason. I want only U.S. citizens, including dual citizens and green card holders (legal permanent residents), to be counted in the 2030 Census for the purposes of apportionment. All other foreigners, such as all undocumented persons, foreign students, foreign executives assigned to their companies&#39; U.S. offices, people visiting the U.S. on tourist visas or persons here for business trips, and the oddball category of 2020: foreigners in the U.S. on temporary work permits living in California, must not be counted.<br/><br/>I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published for this reason. One of the most devastating consequences of the 2020 Census emphasis on granularity is that it encouraged an unhealthy pattern of valuing self-segregation in one&rsquo;s identity in U.S. society over the core values in our Declaration of Independence, in which we held the aspiration to the truth that we are all created equal. None of us is special unless all of us are special together is a paramount element of U.S. national interest.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0878,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-jbuv-09qx,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Among many adverse outcomes, a decision to stop collecting and reporting data on race and ethnicity from all persons residing in the United States as part of U.S. Census Bureau data collection activities would be an unmitigated disaster for public health in the United States. Decades of public health disease surveillance, research, and programmatic activities have documented irrefutable evidence of numerous, persistent, and profound racial/ethnic disparities in health burdens and outcomes in the United States.  Without data on race and ethnicity which are used to estimate the population sizes of racial and ethnic groups, rates of diseases will be impossible to estimate; without these estimates, U.S. public health agencies and organizations will be unable to identify and characterize health-related disparities and develop tailored interventions to mitigate associated health impacts in disproportionately affected groups. A decision to stop collecting and reporting data and race and ethnicity will, by reducing the effectiveness and efficiency of public health programs, literally and unequivocally result in increased rates of illness and premature death in the United States. As a public health epidemiologist with over twenty years of experiencing, including over ten years as a CDC epidemiologist, I urge the Census Bureau not to follow through with the proposed change to their racial and ethnic data collection practices.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0877,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-izui-sg2e,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This proposed change harms vulnerable communities by making it more difficult to collect data on disparities. Please do not make these changes.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0879,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-jczi-gwp6,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I think these potential changes to the census are likely to be extremely harmful. They are going to leave out significant populations and key information about everyone, leaving us in the dark about the people living and contributing to our neighborhoods.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0876,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-hmm6-hgh9,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Proposed &sect; 60.4 is the right rule for apportionment: <br/>foreign citizens who are also U.S. citizens or lawful permanent residents as of April 1 are counted at their usual residence; all other foreign citizens are not counted for apportionment.<br/><br/>That line should stay. Including illegal aliens&mdash;and aliens whose status is less durable than LPR status&mdash;in the apportionment base creates a straightforward incentive. A state that attracts a large unlawful population gains House seats and Electoral College votes from people who are not lawful inhabitants and who do not vote. Lawful citizens in that state then wield a larger share of the state&rsquo;s political power than their numbers alone would justify. Neighboring states that do not host the same population lose relative representation. The Constitution&rsquo;s apportionment clause is a rule for inhabitants of the States, not a reward for unauthorized settlement.<br/><br/>There is a second, practical cost. A large non-citizen, non-voting population sitting inside the same jurisdiction as the voters does two things at once. It raises the chance that some ineligible ballots are cast, even if by mistake. It also forces election officials to spend more time, money, and resources screening who is eligible. Both effects lengthen the count and erode public confidence in the result. Apportionment should not be designed in a way that multiplies that pressure.<br/><br/>Those points go to apportionment. They do not go to whether the Bureau should know how many such people are here.<br/><br/>The public numbers now in circulation for the unlawful population are estimates, models, and residual calculations. They are not a count. The Bureau should enumerate persons present who fall under proposed &sect; 60.4(b)&mdash;illegal aliens and other foreign citizens who are not LPRs&mdash;as a distinct tabulated class. That count is how the country learns the actual size and location of the population that the rule correctly excludes from the apportionment base. Without it, the policy debate stays theoretical.<br/><br/>The final rule should therefore state two separate operations, and it should make them explicitly severable:<br/><br/>1. Enumerate foreign citizens who are not U.S. citizens or LPRs (including illegal aliens), using administrative records as the Bureau already proposes, so the published products include an accurate count of that population by State.<br/>2. Exclude that same population from the apportionment totals transmitted under 13 U.S.C. 141.<br/><br/>If a court sets aside (2), (1) should survive. The risk of an adverse ruling is concentrated on the apportionment exclusion. Losing the exclusion should not also erase the first decent national count of the population the exclusion was meant to address. &sect;&sect; 60.11 and 70.5 already speak to severability; the preamble and the regulatory text should make it explicit the enumeration of &sect; 60.4(b) persons is independent of their exclusion from the apportionment base.<br/><br/>On the Bureau&rsquo;s question whether other statuses are &ldquo;materially similar&rdquo; to LPR status: do not widen the apportionment class. Temporary workers, students, parolees, and similar categories are here for a term and a purpose. They are not settled inhabitants. If approved refugees or asylees are later treated as close to LPR status, that should be a narrow, separately justified exception, not a door for every nonimmigrant class.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0880,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-jj7s-l7mf,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"<br/><br/>I oppose the proposed changes to the decennial Census in proposed 15 CFR Parts 60 and 70.<br/><br/>First, I oppose proposed 15 CFR &sect; 60.4(b), which would exclude from the population used for congressional apportionment foreign citizens who are not U.S. citizens or lawful permanent residents. The Census should count people based on where they actually live. People who live in a state are part of that state&rsquo;s population and are affected by its schools, roads, hospitals, emergency services, and other public institutions. Changing the apportionment count based on immigration status would make the Census less reflective of the population actually living in each state.<br/><br/>Second, I oppose proposed 15 CFR &sect; 60.2, which would redefine &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; using where a person has &ldquo;lawfully spent the greatest number of days&rdquo; during the enumeration period and would look to tax records such as tax returns and W-2s as evidence of residence. Census residence should be based primarily on where a person actually lives and sleeps most of the time. Tax records are not designed to determine Census residence and may be outdated, incomplete, or tied to a mailing address that differs from where someone actually lives. I also oppose inserting &ldquo;lawfully&rdquo; into the residence test because it mixes legal-status questions into the basic task of determining where a person lives.<br/><br/>Third, I oppose adding a legal-status question to the decennial Census questionnaire. I understand that this question is not in the proposed regulatory text, but the Census Bureau specifically asks for public comment on whether such a question should be added. I am concerned that asking about legal status could discourage participation or reduce response in some communities. Because an accurate Census depends on obtaining the most complete population count possible, I do not believe the potential benefit of such a question justifies that risk.<br/><br/>Fourth, I oppose proposed 15 CFR &sect; 70.2(a), which would prohibit questions about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation on the decennial Census enumeration questionnaire. Race and ethnicity data from the decennial Census have long been part of the detailed population information used for redistricting and for enforcing civil-rights protections. Removing those questions from the decennial enumeration would reduce the availability of consistent, detailed population data at the very time when such information remains important for understanding representation and demographic change. I also oppose permanently prohibiting a sexual-orientation question; whether such a question is appropriate in a future Census should be decided based on evidence and public need, not barred in advance by regulation.<br/><br/>Fifth, I oppose proposed 15 CFR &sect; 70.2(c), which would make most demographic questions nonmandatory except for biological sex, date of birth, and household relationships. The Census Bureau should be able to determine, through research, testing, and demonstrated data needs, which questions are necessary to produce accurate and useful statistics rather than being constrained by a broad regulatory prohibition.<br/><br/>Finally, I am concerned about proposed 15 CFR &sect; 70.3(b), which would prohibit publication or disclosure of individual demographic data when doing so requires introducing uncertainty into national- or state-level population counts. Protecting confidentiality is essential, but the rule should not unnecessarily reduce the availability or usefulness of demographic data relied on by governments, researchers, courts, community organizations, and the public.<br/><br/>For these reasons, I urge the Department of Commerce and Census Bureau not to adopt proposed &sect;&sect; 60.2, 60.4(b), 70.2, and 70.3(b), and not to add a legal-status question to the 2030 Census questionnaire.<br/><br/>The Census should remain focused on a simple and trusted mission: count everyone who lives here as accurately as possible and provide reliable information about the population.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0881,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-jnl5-vf48,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am writing to oppose the Census Bureau&rsquo;s proposed rule, Docket No. USBC-2026-0628, RIN 0607-AA75, &ldquo;Decennial Census of the Population of Americans; Proposed Residence Criteria and Proposed Regulations for Demographic Questions.&rdquo;<br/><br/>I have always understood the census to be a count of the people who actually live in this country. I am very concerned that this proposal would change that basic principle.<br/><br/>First, I oppose proposed 15 CFR &sect; 60.4(b), which would exclude from the apportionment count foreign citizens who are neither U.S. citizens nor lawful permanent residents. These are still people who live in our communities. They use roads, schools, hospitals, public transportation and other services, and their presence affects the needs of the places where they live. The Fourteenth Amendment says that representation is based on the &ldquo;whole number of persons in each State.&rdquo; It does not say &ldquo;citizens&rdquo; or &ldquo;lawful permanent residents.&rdquo;<br/><br/>I also oppose adding a legal-status question to the decennial census. The Bureau specifically requests comment on that possibility. A question like this could make people afraid to participate, including citizens and lawful residents living in mixed-status households. A census cannot be accurate if large numbers of people are afraid to answer it.<br/><br/>I am also strongly opposed to proposed 15 CFR &sect; 70.2(a), which would prohibit questions about race and ethnicity on the decennial census. Race and ethnicity data are not meaningless information. Census race and Hispanic-origin data have long been included in the redistricting data provided to the states, and this information is important for understanding whether voting districts fairly represent communities and whether voting rights are being protected.<br/><br/>It makes little sense to stop asking people directly about race and ethnicity while at the same time allowing the Census Bureau to obtain demographic information from administrative records and other sources. People should have the opportunity to describe themselves rather than have the government piece together demographic information about them from databases. The proposal expressly leaves that administrative-record collection in place.<br/><br/>The census has enormous consequences for representation, redistricting, public policy and our understanding of who lives in this country. Changes this significant should make the census more accurate and inclusive, not redefine which residents count or eliminate information that has been collected for decades.<br/><br/>I urge the Census Bureau to withdraw proposed &sect; 60.4(b), reject the addition of a legal-status question, and withdraw proposed &sect; 70.2(a)&rsquo;s prohibition on collecting race and ethnicity information.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0883,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-kpdb-ye7a,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"It is important to retain the demographic data for the upcoming US Census, particularly with regard to race. This tactic is not divisive or racist in itself; rather, it is crucial to identify members of each constituency that are being underserved because of the systemic racism that has been been allowed to flourish for far too long in this nation. Government must be for all, not just the few. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0882,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-kmwe-qnhu,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual.  I am addressing Sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.  I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published.  I also support the language of section 70.2 as published.  My comment is as follows:<br/><br/>Since the sole purpose of the decennial census as stated in the Constitution is to allocate the number of representatives in the House of Representatives based on population of each state since these are representatives of the citizens and the lawful permanent residents.  Illegal aliens should not be counted in the apportionment of our elected officals.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0884,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-kvky-beu8,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am concerned that the proposed changes to residence criteria could reduce the accuracy and consistency of Census population counts by weakening the connection between where people live and where they are counted. Accurate residence measurement is fundamental to producing reliable population estimates, demographic benchmarks, and survey weighting controls used throughout the federal statistical system. Any changes should be carefully evaluated for their impact on data comparability, population estimates, and the ability of researchers, policymakers, and communities to understand local population characteristics and trends.<br/><br/>I am concerned that the proposed changes to residence criteria could reduce the accuracy and consistency of Census population counts by weakening the connection between where people live and where they are counted. Accurate residence measurement is fundamental to producing reliable population estimates, demographic benchmarks, and survey weighting controls used throughout the federal statistical system. Any changes should be carefully evaluated for their impact on data comparability, population estimates, and the ability of researchers, policymakers, and communities to understand local population characteristics and trends.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0887,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-lh0s-m5l7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"<br/>I am writing to state my strong opposition to the Census Bureau&rsquo;s proposed changes to the 2030 Decennial Census residence criteria and data collection methods. The proposed regulations distort the purpose of the decennial count, compromise data integrity, and harm local communities.<br/><br/>1. Violation of the Constitutional Mandate for Apportionment. The U.S. Constitution requires an actual enumeration of all persons living in each state for congressional apportionment. Limiting apportionment counts to citizens and lawful permanent residents violates historical precedent and artificially strips states and regions with large immigrant populations of congressional representation.<br/><br/>2. Damage to Public Health Infrastructure and Scientific Research. Accurate public health surveillance, epidemiological modeling, and medical research depend entirely on precise, unmanipulated census baselines.<br/>&bull; Corrupting Research Data: Substituting administrative data matches (from DOJ, DHS, and commercial vendors) for actual self-reported counts introduces massive errors, undermining longitudinal social science and economic research.<br/>&bull; Hurting Health Resources: Federal and state health funding, including disease prevention grants, hospital capacity planning, and community health centers, relies on total resident counts. Undercounting populations directly starves local healthcare systems of necessary funding during health emergencies.<br/>&bull; Erasing Disparities: Barring data collection on race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation prevents public health officials and researchers from identifying, tracking, and addressing health disparities in vulnerable populations.<br/><br/>3. Direct Harm to Community Groups and Non-Profits. Grassroots organizations, labor councils, faith groups, and local non-profits rely on granular census data to allocate community resources, plan language access programs, target worker support services, and organize local civic engagement. Skewing these baselines deprives local organizations of the data needed to serve working families effectively.<br/><br/>4. Privacy Violations and Unreliable Data Matching. Relying on data exchanges with law enforcement and immigration agencies (such as the DOJ and DHS) deters participation by instilling fear of surveillance. Administrative records are notoriously prone to outdated addresses, missing records, and matching errors, resulting in widespread undercounts.<br/><br/>5. Exclusion of Vulnerable Populations. The proposed residence rules risk miscounting or omitting unhoused individuals, survivors of domestic violence, nursing home residents, and individuals with recent legal name changes, systematically excluding those who most need targeted public support.<br/><br/>I urge the Census Bureau to immediately withdraw these proposed regulations and maintain an inclusive, accurate count of every person living in the United States.<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0885,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-l6sd-9z8i,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am absolutely in favor of NOT including non-citizens in the census count for the purpose of Apportionment.  Why would non-citizens be afforded representation in Congress?,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0886,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-l9uj-ulx7,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is ________Ray E Dodd_____________, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo; The census should only count U.S. citizens. Absolutely no non citizens should be counted. If a non citizen wants to be counted, they should do the work to become citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0888,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-lt1m-kdwo,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I urge the Census Bureau to adopt residence criteria that count only U.S. citizens for apportionment purposes. Temporary residents, visa holders, asylum applicants, and other short‑term or conditional categories do not possess the durable legal status, long‑term domicile, or allegiance required to be considered &ldquo;usual residents&rdquo; for apportionment. Including individuals who are present only temporarily distorts representation and undermines the constitutional requirement to apportion seats based on the population of true inhabitants of each State.<br/><br/>Recommended Criteria Adjustments<br/>Count only U.S. citizens for apportionment.<br/><br/>Exclude all temporary categories, including non‑immigrant visa holders, asylum seekers, parolees, and other short‑duration or conditional statuses.<br/><br/>Exclude individuals without durable permission to reside indefinitely, as they do not meet the historical definition of &ldquo;inhabitants&rdquo; or &ldquo;usual residence.&rdquo;<br/><br/>Rationale<br/>Temporary or conditional presence does not establish the long‑term domicile or allegiance historically associated with apportionment.<br/><br/>Counting short‑term foreign residents artificially inflates representation in certain states and misaligns congressional apportionment with the actual citizen population.<br/><br/>Limiting the apportionment base to citizens ensures that representation reflects the population with full political membership and enduring ties to the United States.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0889,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-nafv-3gca,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is a genuinely terrible idea.  The Census is supposed to get information on everyone in the United States of America, and that includes our &quot;undocumented&quot; residents.  They pay taxes and they use public services. If they aren&#39;t part of the Census, then there will be major discrepancies in information.  As far as removing race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation, this is very important information when it comes to funding getting to marginalized folks that need it most, and I&#39;m not just talking about federal funding.  Nonprofits across the country rely on Census data when applying for private grants.  What happened to a more &quot;transparent&quot; government? The government should be focusing on serving our citizens, not obfuscating information. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0890,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-nh28-zn6z,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Colleen Kuehne, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: we are Americans. Matters of &quot;race, ethnicity, sex, age, citizenship, health, education, occupation, housing, military service, radio ownership, age at first marriage, and native tongue&quot; are irrelevant to a census and have been used as a means of &quot;divide and conquer.&quot; That should be discouraged in any and every way possible. Furthermore, refusing to answer such questions should in NO WAY be a federal crime - I submit those questions are intrusive and should be eliminated entirely.<br/>In addition, I request that you do include a question about legal status on the short-form decennial census questionnaire. That should be used to determine who to count - and the ONLY thing the census should do is count.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0892,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-p74k-hcsc,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is a strange way of announcing that the administration is violating federal privacy laws. What &quot;other data sets&quot; are being used, and how? Why is that not explained here? Something really gross is going on here. <br/>I suspect this is why I cannot see any of the Public Comments on this proposed rule. <br/>WHERE ARE THE PUBLIC COMMENTS? <br/><br/>It should also be noted that the two rules contradict each other. <br/>Most importantly - and this is really important - Fidel declared that racism was over in Cuba in 1962. Race data is not collected by the government, and discussion of race can be categorized as counterrevolutionary or subversive. Is that the goal here? ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0893,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-p8h3-rgjm,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dieter Durant, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0891,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-oa32-rpfv,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Dr. Feyi Obamehinti, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reason for my support is because this approach provides a more accurate count for apportionment. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0560,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,,Public Submission,2026-09-16T04:00Z,true,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Commenter requested to anonymize,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0894,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-7usd-f9jg,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This propopal is unconstitutional and another attempt by the corrupt Trump administration to end democracy in America. Kill the proposal.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0895,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-gkou-y1ew,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The Constitution includes &quot;all persons&quot; in the census. A Constitutional amendment is required to codify the rapist&#39;s racist proposed &quot;rule.&quot;,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0896,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtx-ocb1-r9l0,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-11T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is obviously, to anyone with any sense, simply another attack on American democracy by the fascist executive branch. <br/><br/>First they will remove representation for people they declare &quot;illegal&quot;. Next &quot;illegal&quot; will apply to more and more groups until the only people with any power in this country are the dictator and the oligarchs.<br/><br/>This proposal flies in the face of the rule of law. It&#39;s sponsors and all it&#39;s supportors should be held fully accountable for treason against the United States constitution and the promise of inalienable rights that this country is supposed to represent.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0897,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-791y-gchw,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"No.<br/>Just no.<br/>Do you require any other proper comment besides that? No. These proposed changes are racist, bigoted foppery by the corrupt Trump administration. We already said no to his attempts to change the census. We say no again. No.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0899,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mtz-n7r8-fcte,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-13T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am fully in favor of the new census proposal regarding residency and dem questions.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0898,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mty-kfj1-z4yw,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-12T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I&#39;m sure you&#39;re just going to overlook and whipe your X with this comment as yoj do with the law in general. But holy hell must you insist on being idiots throughout every step of your decision making? Dont X remove key identifiers from the census you literal troglodytes,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0900,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-hbsf-f8vk,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Everyone in America should be counted. It&#39;s how we have done it. The census portrays who we are as a nation, regardless of status or color. We are a nation of immigrants and should be proud of this. <br/>My father&#39;s father, Tokio Shirakawa, came from Japan and fully intended to make his money and then return. He decided to stay as his children were born in America. After Pearl Harbor he was picked up by the FBI and taken to several locations (see attached Alien Enemy Docket card - he was sent to Ft Missoula, Ft Sill, Santa Fe, NM, etc) and was eventually cleared of any wrongdoing. <br/>He had not committed any sabotage. He was then sent to the Gila, AZ Internment camp in 1943 and was finally released to freedom in 1945 (see attached WRA Final Accountability Roster). He went back to his farm in Central California to begin anew. Regardless of this treatment, he became an American citizen soon after it was possible to do so (see photo). <br/>Decades after the war, he was celebrated as one of the town&#39;s leading citizens and more recently, a small, grassy park with playgrounds for children now stands in his name, on what used ot be his farm but is now a new housing development (see attached). <br/>My mother&#39;s father and mother ( Kahei and Misao Kaneichi) were not American citizens, as they came from Japan and were barred from citizenship. Their son, Takeo Kaneichi, born on US soil, was an American citizen. He died for this country fighting the Germans on November 8, 1944 in northeastern France with the 442nd Regimental Combat Team, a segregated unit (see attached).<br/>PLEASE COUNT EVERYONE. They are coming here to become Americans.<br/>",,,,,,"https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_2.pdf,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_2.jpg,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_3.jpg,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_3.pdf,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_4.pdf,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_4.jpg,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_5.pdf,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_5.jpg,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_6.jpg,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_6.pdf,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_7.pdf,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0900/attachment_7.jpg",
USBC-2026-0628-0901,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-pdax-45nw,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>We should know the nationalities within our country and how each has changed since the last census, including foreign students and undocumented person.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Our citizens represent many nations, and this impacts American industries.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>AI programming can be more accurate when the American population, citizens or noncitizens, is understood through a census that includes nationalities and citizenship status.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0902,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-pryr-rtjn,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is PaulaAndra Aigner, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support is as follows: 60.4-The United States was created for its legal citizens their decedents and for LEGAL future immigrants. It&#39;s past time that the census recognizes that and allocates OUR taxes and support to our states for the LEGAL citizens only in their legally recognized residences. 70.2- This section was getting long, complicated and ridiculous. The only way to FAIRLY clean it up is to do away with all designations.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0903,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-pvsi-ue0j,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Douglas Caldwell and US citizen in Texas. I support sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. The Census should count only those people as proposed by the new rules. It is especially significant for apportionment of US House members that the states get credit for US citizen or legal residents, not illegal aliens, foreign visitors etc. I also like the wording of proposed language of section 70.2. Thank you for attention on this matter. DougC",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0904,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-pw76-wwi9,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Joseph P (resident of Westchester County, New York).  I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. As the description summary states, I do believe these proposals will ensure fidelity to the Constitution and will efficiently improve and protect the core decennial census data.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0905,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-q8is-ae9g,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I see no legitimate reason to change how the population has been counted by the census. Leave the process as it existed for the last census and stop trying to manipulate that process. No to the proposed changes. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0907,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-rxx0-wlga,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is an unconstitutional power grab by the Trump Administration.  Don&rsquo;t let them pick and choose who gets help from the Federal Government, because as these racists have shown us, they have animus against Black people, Asian people, Hispanic people, Native people and are in a general Genocide against Trans people.  This rule change will make it easier for the racists in this Racist Administration to not help people of color or other minority statuses, such as the ill, the poor, LGBTQIA2S+ people, and women.  ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0908,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-s1lz-st7n,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Christine Cartet. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: the US census must be limited to legal citizens whereby it is only citizens of this country who our government must be accountable to. Non- citizens should have no representation as they are not loyal to our country or our Constitition and should not be afforded their opinions. Further our constition states unequivocally that all men are created equal. Dividing our citizens into special grouping purports that some are more special than others. This is simply not true. We are all Americans first and foremost.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0906,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-rcqb-18j0,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am writing to strongly oppose the proposed changes to the residency criteria for the 2030 Decennial Census. I am deeply concerned about the decision to exclude non-citizens from the apportionment count. The U.S. Constitution mandates a count of the &quot;whole number of persons&quot; in each state, not just citizens or permanent residents. These community members live, work, pay taxes, and utilize local infrastructure. Excluding them will result in an inaccurate population count that directly harms my community by diluting our political representation in Congress and unfairly reducing our share of federal funding for vital services like public schools, road maintenance, and healthcare infrastructure. I urge the Census Bureau to reject these changes and maintain the historic, constitutionally sound practice of counting every resident where they live and sleep.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0909,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-t8f4-f2wm,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,By what constitutional interpretation is the administration redefining the clear language of the Constitution to count all persons in each state? Which past Supreme Court majority opinions support the view of this rule? What has been the historical record of tracking Americans by racial or other categories in the census? ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0910,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-ukq9-opf9,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is stupid. Pretending people aren&#39;t there doesn&#39;t make them go away. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0911,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-wipd-xzkp,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support both parts of this proposal. The census should not be turned into a social survey about race or sexual orientation when its core job is a headcount for apportionment. More important, the residence rules should stop treating illegal presence as if it created a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; equal to that of a citizen or green-card holder. I am concerned that past counts mixed people with no lawful claim to remain into the numbers that decide political power. Using tax records and immigration files is a better, more objective way to apply the rules. Please keep the exclusion of illegal aliens and non-durable statuses and do not weaken it during the comment period.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0912,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-xi27-uyso,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,The census should count only US citizens and those with legal permission to stay here.  Being here does not equate to being equal to or the same as a citizen or legal resident.  Representation for Congress should not include any illegals in the country. Counting illegals only dilutes the base of US citizens.   ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0913,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-xqzv-svp4,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a Syrian-Lebanese American, the proposed removal of race and ethnicity on the census upsets me greatly. I deserve to be counted as an accurately Syrian-Lebanese American, and have a say in my federal and local government as a proud Syrian-Lebanese American. These proposed changes from the Trump administration are unprecedented and should be rejected outright. Race and ethnicity, and undocumented immigrants deserve and should be counted on the census. It&rsquo;s a census of everyone in America, not just the White European American ones.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0914,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu1-yskm-ef3y,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,It is completely baffling that non-citizens would be counted for representation in our system. The census should strictly reflect and serve the legal citizens of this country.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0915,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-00f8-ewd3,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"undocumented/&quot;illegal&quot; people in the US work, pay taxes, and contribute to our society. count them. they are in all 50 states regardless of which party is in power. this doesn&#39;t help republican states, it just leaves people with even less of a voice than they already had. leave them alone",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0916,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-0xm8-il0z,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is really dangerous for the health of our country,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0917,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-2eju-mlov,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support only counting legal US citizens in the census. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0918,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-2fxd-8l7x,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Only American citizens should be enumerated in the US Census.  Foreign students should not be counted. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0920,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-4kl6-fk86,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and I steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0919,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-3qrs-3tru,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I strongly oppose the proposed rule. It seeks to use ancient decisions no longer applicable to modern law and understanding in order to fundamentally alter the Census.<br/>The Constitution is clear - All Persons. Trying to adjudicate who is a person by legal chicanery does nothing except sow fear. <br/>The intent behind this is clear - disenfranchise anyone who may distrust the federal government, which today is an ever growing number. The census must be neutral, factual, and above all, Count All Persons.<br/>I oppose this rule on its face and in its fact. This does not represent America at its best, nor does it benefit anyone.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0922,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-6loj-9cv9,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Gerrymandering and other illegal voter suppression tactics should be a jailable offense. This is nothing but exclusionary politics to achieve a predetermined outcome. No taxation without representation was enough of a reason to start a war over in the past. Now we are repeating the same mistakes this country was founded against. I do not support this census change, as it is intended with malice and manipulation. This should not be the point of a census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0921,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-4mov-17ps,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-14T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Comment 1<br/><br/>I support the proposed residence criteria. The decennial census exists first to apportion the House among the states based on the people who actually belong to the political community of each state. Counting people who are here illegally, or who hold only temporary permission, inflates some states&rsquo; numbers and steals representation from citizens in other states. &ldquo;Usual residence&rdquo; has always included an element of lasting tie and allegiance. People who the government itself says may not remain here do not have that tie. Please adopt the rule so the 2030 count matches the Constitution&rsquo;s purpose instead of rewarding illegal presence.<br/><br/>Comment 2 &mdash; Fairness to citizens and states<br/><br/>American citizens should not lose House seats and electoral votes because another state has a large population of people who entered or stayed in violation of federal law. That is what happens under the old &ldquo;count everyone living here&rdquo; approach. The proposed rule correctly includes U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents and excludes illegal aliens and those with less durable status. That is fair to the citizens who vote, pay taxes as members of the body politic, and live under our laws. I urge the Bureau to finalize these criteria and use administrative records from DHS and Treasury so the count is accurate and not based on self-reports that can be gamed.<br/><br/>Comment 3 &mdash; Process and records<br/><br/>I support both parts of this proposal. The census should not be turned into a social survey about race or sexual orientation when its core job is a headcount for apportionment. More important, the residence rules should stop treating illegal presence as if it created a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; equal to that of a citizen or green-card holder. I am concerned that past counts mixed people with no lawful claim to remain into the numbers that decide political power. Using tax records and immigration files is a better, more objective way to apply the rules. Please keep the exclusion of illegal aliens and non-durable statuses and do not weaken it during the comment period.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0923,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-cxg6-h8a8,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am commenting to express my opposition to the proposed changes to the census. <br/><br/>Removing the demographics questions would lose valuable data that can be used to better understand and support individual states, and both citizens and non-citizens should be counted and further used to understand and support each state. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0924,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-lx33-vb7g,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Our names are Ken and KathrynNass_____________________, we are citizens of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo; non-citzens are not part of this great nation&#39;s decision making process, non-citizens are not a part of the he Census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0925,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-mg7m-yorf,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The ripple effect of removing race and ethnicity from the 2030 Census affects the whole estimation pipeline, and that&#39;s very problematic. The point about needing to be colorblind misses the point. As much as the administration would like to move beyond race and ethnicity, it remains a key variable in helping explain who we are and where we come from. It&#39;s similar to gender, age, income, urbanicity...not there to highlight division, but to understand the factors that make people different types of contributors to our society. Removing it doesn&#39;t make those differences disappear, it just lessens our ability to see and understand life around us.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0926,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-mnci-al62,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The Census is never improved by collecting less data. The Census exists in large part as a method to collect as thorough a demographic picture as possible of the persons living in the United States, and collecting more thorough data is thus inherently superior to collecting less data.<br/>If the government decides it does not want to use a subset of the questions asked by the Census, that is their prerogative (although I believe that, too, to be ill-advised). But there is no excuse to simply not ask the questions. It becomes published, publicly accessible data that any institution can use for the public benefit. The ONLY reason to collect &amp; publish less comprehensive population data is the desire to suppress measurable data and restrict access to information, which is unconscionable for the U.S. Census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0928,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-oo6t-xbm3,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a citizen of the US, I wish to concur with the proposed changes to Part 60, in particular 60.4.  People who broke the law coming to this country have no right to be counted.  They should have no impact on the apportion of the seats in the U.S. House of Representatives.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0927,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-occ0-0ur8,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I wholeheartedly support limiting the Census to those in the United States legally, and preferably just actual citizens.  To allow otherwise creates an incentive to permit illegal migration, to skew the proper representation of our citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0929,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-p8dp-3uhx,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Things are different today than they were in 1868. As with the birthright clause the 14th amendment was meant to assimilate people who were once slaves into the population. I believe had the men who wrote the 14 amendment had known we would have tens of millions of illegal aliens in our country today, they would have used the word &quot;citizen.&quot; Anyone who is not a citizen and is in our country illegally should not have the same rights as a citizen and therefore should not be counted. For too many years California and other states have allowed illegals into our country and they are being counted in the census. They do this so they can have more representatives in the house, which is unfair to law abiding states and the people who live there. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0930,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-qwre-gs4g,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I strongly disagree in striking out race/ethnicity questions. It&#39;s very useful data to know where populations shift, it very much matters and has lots of bearings. Representation is vital and this highlights where gerrymandering occurs. It shows bad district maps, voting districts need representation and this data on race/ethnicity demonstrate that. Many nonprofits rely on this information to improve the wellbeing of communities. See quote below! <br/>&ldquo;This feels like an attack against the American public,&rdquo; Beth Jarosz, a senior fellow in demographic and socioeconomic analysis at the Georgetown University Massive Data Institute, told TPM after reviewing the draft rule. &ldquo;It removes critical data that are used in so many different aspects in public life to understand our communities and to make sure that our communities are healthy, and safe, and prospering",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0931,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-rkp4-yuga,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Good morning. As an academic librarian, I am strongly against this rule. This proposal will serve to reduce the richness of public data available to business, policymakers, researchers, and the broader community. The Decennial Census has, historically, been a bedrock of decision making for all of these stakeholders. Additionally, reducing the richness of Census data forces businesses and other groups to have to rely on expensive primary data collection when it comes to demographics such as race, which drives up costs for them as well as consumers. In sum, this is a step backwards for Commerce, and I urge the Department not to proceed wit this plan.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0933,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-s2oe-bgvw,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Steve Joffrion. I am a US citizen and I wish to comment on NPRM sections 60.4 and 70.2. I support the changes to 60.4 and 70.2 as proposed.<br/>As far as Section 60.4 is concerned, I feel that allowing undocumented persons, foreign students and foreign executives to determine how my tax dollars are spent is an absurdity. It is unfair to the citizens of the United States to permit individuals who just happen to be within our borders to determine the appropriation of funds. Congress should prioritize the domestic population here in the United States.<br/>Currently, the US census asks respondents to identify by race, ethnicity and sexual orientation. These questions are intrusive, divisive and discriminatory and have no place in free and progressive country.<br/><br/>Thank you<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0934,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-s6xn-thkg,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"These proposed changes raise several red flags that people will not be counted in the right place. First, in a total break from every census since 1790 and from requirements in Title 13, the Census Bureau is &ldquo;proposing to define the relevant &lsquo;census enumeration period&rsquo; to run from January 3 to April 1 during decennial census years.&rdquo; The anticipated effect of this proposed rule is that it would shift population from colder, northern states to southern &ldquo;snowbird&rdquo; destinations. This proposed reference period would also cause confusion for anyone who moves during the reference period. Usual residence has always been understood to be where you will live and sleep most of the time as of the census date. Movers in March, for example, may feel compelled to report their prior address rather than their residence on Census Day (April 1). &ldquo;(a) A person&rsquo;s &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; is the residence at which they have lawfully spent the greatest number of days during the census enumeration period. A person&rsquo;s &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; should be consistent with, and evidenced by, their tax records (e.g., tax returns, W-2 forms).&rdquo; However, while that rubric may work for some people, it will fail for many others. Tax documents require a current mailing address. People may file taxes using a post office box, their tax preparer&rsquo;s address, or an address different from their usual address. They may also move, so address information on their W-2 is likely to be out-of-date. A rural resident may be more likely to use a post office box rather than their physical home address for tax purposes. A college student may file taxes using the address of a parent or guardian, but should be counted at their &ldquo;separate usual residence.&rdquo; Similarly, &ldquo;snowbirds&rdquo; may be at their winter residence in January and February, but at their usual residence (the long-term residence where they file taxes) by April 1.These complexities of tax filing create internal inconsistencies within the proposed rules. Will a person&rsquo;s usual residence be accepted as the one they report, or will it be one selected by an algorithm based on tax records? Will individual census responses be tossed out if their response differs from their W-2? The U.S. will no longer have data on race and ethnicity. Removing race and ethnicity data from the census means that the U.S. would no longer have any reliable race/ethnicity data&mdash;whether at the state, federal or local level, whether private or public. While excuses for this prohibition are provided, none are plausible or reasonable. In June 2026 the Department of Commerce issued a sweeping prohibition against use of any modern privacy protections that have previously been applied to protect race and ethnicity. This text as a result suggests that &ldquo;excluding such questions from the decennial census in this manner would reduce response burden as well as the risk of disclosure,&rdquo; but response burden to these questions is minimal. This privacy concern is a self-inflicted injury due to the government&#39;s own policy changes. This is easy to reconcile on a different level than within this proposed change here. The spurious and pretextual arguments ignore the larger reason why race has been asked in some way in every decennial census. Race and ethnicity data are essential for understanding public health, conducting market research, and bolstering workforce development. Without decennial census race and ethnicity data, the weighting of other surveys, public and private, degrade materially. The way to address our society&rsquo;s economic and heath needs is to have the tools that allow us to determine how everyone is doing. Our community&#39;s health and economic needs often vary between groups of ethnic and racial identities. Ending the form of measurement that records this does not make our differing needs go away. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0932,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-s2gr-dtf7,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,See attached file(s),,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0932/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0935,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-s8ba-s1wi,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Do not implement this proposed revision to the regulation. Everyone deserves to be counted and represented accordingly. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0936,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-smkq-2uc2,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Greetings. Native American, American Indian, and Indigenous enrolled members are a very specific population. We are inherent to the land, governed with treaty agreements and our reservation lifestyles are very unique. To say it&#39;s divisive is missing one great fact - we live differently. From economic development to any socio detail ours will vary. How will you as certain information and demographs correctly. I love this equality phase many speak on but we are diverse. Tribal nations are upheld by treaties and the best way to accurately ascertain our demographics your census needs to know who we are <br/><br/>I&#39;m very curious with the funds we pay for these that we get a very big picture of America. I want to know racial variants if they exist, not defining race doesn&#39;t mean there aren&#39;t unique racial problems. It&#39;s seemingly denial to some. <br/><br/>Keep race in the census. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0937,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-snf2-bw0h,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Personally, I find this proposal concerning, especially because of the potential impact on how people are counted and represented. I understand that there may be practical reasons behind some of these changes, but excluding certain people who actually live in a state from the population count could have significant consequences for representation and federal resources. I&rsquo;m also uneasy about relying more heavily on data from other agencies or third parties to determine who is counted, since that could create issues with accuracy and fairness. I&rsquo;m sure there is much more to the proposal that needs to be reviewed, but my initial reaction is that these changes deserve very careful consideration.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0938,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-svxd-auc1,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am submitting this comment as an individual citizen, California resident, taxpayer, and mother. I strongly oppose the proposed changes to the 2030 Census residence criteria and demographic questions.<br/><br/>Americans of every political viewpoint should be able to agree on one principle: we need an accurate count of the people who live in our communities.<br/>Census data are not abstract statistics. They help communities plan schools, roads, hospitals, emergency response, and other essential services. Businesses use them to decide where to invest, and researchers and governments use them to understand population needs and allocate limited resources.<br/><br/>All of this depends on an accurate denominator. If we do not know how many people actually live in a community, the rates, projections, and planning estimates built on that population become less reliable.<br/><br/>I am particularly concerned about excluding people who actually live in the United States from the population count based on immigration status. Whatever one&rsquo;s views on immigration policy, a census should tell us who lives here. People do not disappear from a community&rsquo;s infrastructure needs because of their immigration status. They still live in housing, travel on roads, work, attend school, and use hospitals and emergency services.<br/><br/>I am also concerned that linking census participation to legal status could discourage participation, particularly among mixed-status households. Fear or confusion affecting one household member could result in U.S. citizens and other lawful residents being missed as well.<br/><br/>I strongly oppose removing race and ethnicity questions from the decennial census. These data allow us to measure differences in health, education, housing, employment, and economic opportunity and determine whether disparities are improving or worsening.<br/>Eliminating measurement does not eliminate disparities.<br/><br/>The decennial census also provides foundational population data used to benchmark other surveys and datasets. Removing race and ethnicity from that foundation could weaken demographic statistics at the national, state, and local levels.<br/><br/>I am also concerned about using administrative records to determine residence and legal status. Administrative datasets were created for different purposes and may contain outdated or conflicting information. Tax addresses, for example, do not necessarily reflect where someone actually lives.<br/><br/>More fundamentally, Americans need confidence that responding to the census is safe. Policies that cause people to believe their census responses will be combined with other government records to evaluate immigration status risk undermining decades of public trust. Once lost, that trust will be extraordinarily difficult to rebuild.<br/>These are fundamental changes to our constitutionally required enumeration. They should not proceed without rigorous scientific testing, transparent evaluation, and meaningful public participation.<br/><br/>The 2030 Census will shape political representation, research, planning, and public policy for an entire decade. Getting it wrong cannot simply be corrected the following year.<br/><br/>I respectfully urge the Census Bureau to withdraw the proposed restrictions on race and ethnicity questions and the proposed residence criteria and continue preparing for the 2030 Census using scientifically tested methodologies designed to count everyone once, only once, and in the right place.<br/><br/>You cannot accurately understand, plan for, or serve a population you choose not to count.<br/><br/>Please protect the accuracy, scientific integrity, independence, and public trust of the 2030 Census.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0939,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-tssu-qzfl,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Please do not make the proposed changes to the 2030 census. As a citizen of the United States who wants our number counted accurately for the purposes of government representation, and as a researcher who uses census data, I can tell you that the proposed changes are harmful for our country. The census is incredibly important, since it documents who we are as a country. The proposed changes will make the data collected less accurate and less valuable. It will put us reduce our ability to learn about what Americans need, and it will be harder to keep our country healthy and well-represented as a result. Again, please do not make the proposed changes to the 2030 census.",,,,,,"https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0939/attachment_1.pdf,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0939/attachment_1.png",
USBC-2026-0628-0941,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-vkyx-oa15,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,My name is Shirley Smith.  I am a US citizen.   I would like to comment on sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM.   I support 60.4 as published.  I also support the language of section 70.2 as published.  I support it because the Census should be about US Citizens...plain and simple.   There should be no questions about race or sexual orientation.   ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0942,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-w6en-ht22,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"So, changing the census so that only U.S. citizens are included?  Sounds like a way to take government representation away from areas with higher legal non-citizen residents&hellip;.On paper, it looks pretty normal, but in execution, it&rsquo;s a way to unfairly balance government seats towards, lets face it, the GOP. And those who are being targeted are the ones who need representation most of all, now more than ever. This is a bad bill and I pray it will not pass.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0940,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-udfw-8r8o,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Michael Meriwether, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published.<br/>The reasons for my support are as follows: No benefits of any kind should go to illegal immigrants. They should not be counted in anything that could potentially benefit them or impose any cost whatsoever on the citizens of the United States of America.<br/>Thank you.<br/>Mike Meriwether<br/>merimike@earthlink.net<br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0943,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-wn65-wexr,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Whereas, the purpose of the census is to, first and foremost, establish accurate apportionment of Representatives of the United States to the Congress of the United States, and<br/>Whereas, only verified citizens of these United States should be eligible to vote in electing the Representatives to the Congress to be their Representatives, and<br/>Whereas, no other person or persons should have any means to effect the choice of said Representatives to the Congress, nor should they neither have any means to effect the number of said Representatives apportioned to the States, including the Census,<br/>It is paramount that only American citizens who are legally qualified/authorized to vote should be counted  in any Census that is being used for apportionment of the Representatives to be voted on in election proceedings.<br/>Whereas, the Constitution of the United States states that all men are created equal, endowed by their Creator with certain unalienable rights, the citizens of these United States being counted in a Census to be used for apportionment of the number and location of their Representatives in said States should all be considered equally, with no regards to race, creed, ethnicity, sex, or sexual orientation of said citizen, <br/>Therefore, any and all such questions pertaining to race, creed, ethnicity, sex, or sexual orientation should be prohibited.<br/>Respectfully submitted,<br/>John S. Nowosacki<br/>Belton, TX ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0944,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-wo1i-3aq5,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is David Hughes and Themla Hughes, we are citizens of the United States of America, and we are commenting as an individual group. We are addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. We support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. We further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for our support are as follows.:<br/>We have as a nation, continually strived to protect our citizens from foreign intervention and we do not believe that foreign people or countries should have a voice in our governing.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0945,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-y2hh-m5g7,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I am an American citizen. Only U.S. citizens should be counted in the census for purposes of determining representation in Congress. ,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0946,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-ytcu-ece8,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is Mark Jones, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.<br/>  I believe that ONLY citizens of the United States should be counted in a census for the purpose of representation, but I&#39;ll accept the counting, reluctantly, of legal permanent residents. <br/>  In re 70.2, let the special interest groups do their own demographic surveys with their own money. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0947,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu2-zsg4-i15z,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The term African American must be changed to U.S. American Freedmen which properly identifies and officially classifies under the U.S. Supreme Court and U.S. Congress Bill HR613 established on May 30th , 1866.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0948,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-00nb-82lb,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes to the Census eliminate a number of people who have the right to vote, especiallhy people in elderly residences and veterans. Lesave the census as it is!  And while you&#39;re at it, leave vote by mail alone as this allows many more citizens to participate in our democracy, especislly the elderly, disbaled, rural, and those lacking ytransportation to reach the polls. Thank you, Patricia Mail",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0949,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-0lnm-ekix,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Racial classifications erases identity and collapse people who have no similarities, no shared culture, different political views, different religions etc into a singular broad label. I propose we get rid of race completely and deal with nationalities and/or ethnicities. If a person migrated to America or is the descendant of someone who migrated to America. There should be a category for them to identify as where they came from, not a broad racial label. A Chinese person or the descendant of a Chinese person in America should have a Chinese box to check. An Italian or a descendant of an Italian person should have an Italian box to check and so on. And in the case for Ethnic Black Americans. A Box should be made for them where it does not denote a racial dictation of coming from Africa. Ethnic Black Americans should have their own separate label that people from Africa, the Caribbean or any other considered African or African descended can check. Ethnic Black Americans are their own distinct ethnic group and should be counted as such.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0950,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-0rre-3nth,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,All of the people in this country should be included in the census! By excluding noncitizens the numbers for service will not be enough. The immigrants are seeking a better life just like my grandpa coming here from Greece. We are a country of immigrants. We need them as much or more than they need sanctuary here.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0951,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-0x3m-zl8o,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Get rid of Racial Classifications and replace that with nationalities of where people migrated from to America. Instead of Black, White or Asian. Use China, Nigeria, Ireland, Poland, Jamaica, Italy, Yemen, East India, France etc. And in the Case of Ethnic Black Americans, get rid of the dictation of attaching them to Africa. They are their own Ethnic group that has been shaped in America. They should have their own category/label that is separate from all others that no non Ethnic Black American can mark themselves as. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0952,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-1tkq-69j3,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,ONLY COUNT CITIZENS!  GET IT DONE!!,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0953,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-3c8q-v3r4,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Please see the attached letter from the Leadership Conference on Civil and Human Rights and undersigned organizations requesting an extension to the comment period for this proposed rule. ,,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0953/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0955,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-59qw-wsvj,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes would be catastrophic for all, including the citizens and permanent residents the administration seems intent on fixating on with these changes. If communities don&#39;t have enough support for infrastructure, services, and emergency preparedness to cover ALL residents, then everyone suffers.<br/><br/>- Excluding International students, work visa holders, recognized refugees and asylees, and other residents still need to take public transit; still need to go to hospitals; still need to go to school; still need to evacuate in emergency scenarios; etc. etc.<br/><br/>- The race/ethnicity question is not an undue burden- that is a deceitful claim. The decennial census is extremely short. Maintaining the privacy procedures that already existed would also eliminate any concerns of disclosure. Understanding health outcomes, workforce development, etc. relies on demographic information from this survey.<br/><br/>Trying to rush such disastrous changes also reflects poor judgment and shows the administration acting in poor faith. Stop these changes immediately and take a common sense approach before you destroy a crucially important resource for all Americans on both side of the aisle.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0954,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-3n51-cl4k,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Representatives for those who are not citizens does grave injustice to the American system. It weakens the rights and protections of those who &ldquo;have skin in the game&rdquo;. Many people living in the USA are here to just take advantage of the American tax payers obligated support of the system which is not fair to those payers. Some Constitutional guides/prescriptions were not undertaken with the concept of mass migration. Only citizens are supposed to vote yet others make a mockery of the system which is breaking the law. So why offer representation to those who do not respect the system (break the law), do not reward their bad behavior by diluting the governance reserved for citizens.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0956,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-621y-j5ck,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed modifications to the Census regarding the measurement of race and ethnicity should not move forward. The presence of such items does not impair the government&#39;s ability to apply &quot;race-blind&quot; policies should they so choose; they can ignore such data. However, given the racial and ethnic disparities that exist in the US today, race is not a useless category when it is still associated with enormous differences in health, wealth and opportunity. Failure to measure such constructs hampers our ability to observe these differences. <br/><br/>The same arguments apply to disallowing questions based on sexual orientation. Individuals that are not heterosexual evince large disparities that are linked to their experiences of exclusion and discrimination. By not asking these questions, we are ignoring these individuals and removing the opportunity to document such disparities in a nationally representative sample. <br/><br/>The arguments given by the government furthermore state that based on the Constitution, the Census is not required to measure these constructs, but in the same breath acknowledge that race and ethnicity has been measured continuously since the Census&#39;s beginning. The argument therefore that it should not be included based on the founder&#39;s intentions is invalid when this has been occurring since George Washington&#39;s presidency.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0958,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-6lib-rsmv,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"These changes come across as intended to disenfranchise both individuals and states.<br/><br/>Many important government services, non-profits, and private organizations rely on the Census to provide reliable and accurate information. Eliminating the race/ethnicity question and not counting all residents will not remove any problems, it will just hide them. Please don&#39;t make the proposed changes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0957,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-6ava-ag6d,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows:<br/><br/>&sect;&thinsp;60.4 - those categories of individuals sometimes referred to as removable aliens and foreign citizens, should not be included in apportion the seats in the U.S. House of Representatives, as;<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>The sole Constitutional purpose of the decennial census is to apportion the seats in the U.S. House of Representatives based on the number of qualifying persons.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Removable aliens and foreign citizens are not qualifying persons, and populations of such are subject to dramatic variation.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Counting unqualified persons rewards districts that intentionally violate immigration law.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Counting unqualified persons effectively displaces representation for millions of Americans, diluting representation and raises concern about vote equality and legislative accessibility.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>The census is sometimes used to determine how the wealth of the United States is spent when it comes to helping the domestic population of the country. This again rewards districts that violate immigration law and further erodes representation of legal citizens.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>Counting unqualified persons violates representative government principle and democratic equality (fidelity to the Constitution).<br/><br/>&sect;&thinsp;70.2 - the inclusion of certain demographic questions should be restricted<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>In that it violates the core values in our Declaration of Independence, in which we held the aspiration to the truth that we are all created equal.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>The more data that the Census Bureau publishes, the higher the risk that a particular respondent&#39;s data can be identified.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>The (potentially) sensitive nature of questions about race, ethnicity, and sexual orientation to the delight of statisticians and sociologists may be a particular encumbrance on response given that, under 13 U.S.C. 221, respondents are legally required to answer, &ldquo;to the best of their knowledge&rdquo;, and the failure to do so constitutes a federal crime.<br/>&bull;<span style='padding-left: 30px'></span>The census was not created for the purposes to serve as a convenient measuring stick for the dispensing of federal funds by ethnicity, race, or sexual orientation. Its purpose under the Constitution was to determine the apportionment of Representatives among the States.<br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0961,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-7h1j-ejyf,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The Proposed Residence Criteria&#39;s statements that &quot;illegal aliens (among others) should not be included in the apportionment count, as they are not true inhabitants, members of the body politic, or persons with a &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; in the United States due to their lack of a sufficient tie and allegiance to the United States&quot; is inconsistent reality, not to mention with deeply held American values of inclusion of immigrant communities. Persons without legal status are members of our communities, raising families, contributing to their communities, and often with profound ties and allegiances to the nation. This statement, and the proposed rule, is deeply dehumanizing, and is obviously a cynical ploy to concentrate political power. It will have real, measurable negative impacts on the many, many communities who will lose seats in Congress and funding tied to apportionment.<br/><br/>I strongly disagree with the proposed changes in criteria and regulations and urge you to reconsider.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0959,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-7533-gfbq,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The proposed changes to the census may make it harder to do genealogy research in the future by obscuring the true count of household individuals and their national origin when the results are released to the public. Also, the potential for privacy intrusions with obtaining potentially missing data is worrisome since this secondary data collection would make the census data more vulnerable to large-scale data breaches. I don&#39;t support the proposed changes at this time. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0960,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-7afj-0fc9,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a citizen, as a descendant of immigrants, and as an occasional user of Census data in my research, I urge the Commerce Department to rescind this proposal. First, I join countless others in asserting that your interpretation of the Enumeration Clause and &quot;citizens&quot; versus &quot;persons&quot; is incorrect. All persons, whether children, visa holders, foreign students, asylum seekers, and so on, represent, in my work, members of the denominator when establishing population rates. They also represent people who may wish to become full citizens, as America has always been a nation of immigrants, and they are potential users of infrastructure, payers of taxes, and so on. Second, the proposed residency definition is unclear (e.g., as to recent movers) and would appear to &quot;force&quot; snowbirds to be counted in their warm-weather home rather than that in which they spend more than half the year. Finally, as the the demographic questions, see my earlier point about constructing population rates: Making race/ethnicity-specific rates (deaths, crime, etc.) will become difficult if not impossible. The Census has asked about race and ethnicity since 1790. I see no justification for stopping now. For the purpose of a full and informative count and continuity with previous data collection, I urge the Commerce Department to rescind the propose changes.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0963,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-8a7e-3fnz,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a citizen of the USA and support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published.  I also support the language of section 70.2 as published.<br/><br/>The influx of millions of foreign nationals into our country during the Biden administration and having them counted toward congressional apportionment diluted the purpose of taking a census of citizens within our nation.  Non-citizens are not legally allowed to vote in US elections, so why should their presence affect apportionment of our congressional representation in Washington, D.C.?  <br/><br/>I applaud the efforts of the Census Bureau to count only those individuals who are in the USA LEGALLY.  My own wife is a foreign national from Indonesia and was awarded a green card nearly one year ago.  She is a law-abiding permanent resident, and when she was offered an opportunity to register to vote at the local DMV, she refused because she knew that it was unlawful for her to register to vote as a non-citizen.  Our laws should be enforced and common sense should be the norm, not the exception.<br/><br/>Thank you for exercising &quot;common sense&quot; with this effort.<br/><br/>Sincerely, <br/><br/>Jake D Huff<br/>Eugene, OR",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0962,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-7ir7-51m6,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The race, ethnicity, and other demographic variables data collected in the decennial census are critical to the function of governments at all levels. Without this information, I would argue that no governmental agency could effectively do their job. There is no re-identification risk from collecting these variables given that it represents entire communities, is abstracted from survey responses to represent entire geographic regions, and is not shared publicly at the individual response level. Any argument to say otherwise is poorly founded and being used to support an agenda aimed at dismantling progress towards an America where everyone is thriving. <br/><br/>While these changes seem minor in scope, they have massive repercussions that will ripple through all government agencies and reach every community within the United States. And that is not hyperbolic. <br/><br/>As a proud public servant, representing myself as a private citizen in my comment here, I know how deeply this would impact my ability to do my job, fundamentally alter how my community receives resources, and will cause chaos that will take many years to repair.<br/><br/>In the end, what I assume most Americans want is to thrive. In order to do that, we must know who exists in our neighborhood, town, state, and nation. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0964,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-9np7-4s9h,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I am a 56 year old male currently classified as African/Black American. This term is no longer an accurate term to be identified as. It erases my family history and contributions to the building of the United States of America. My documented family history goes back to at least to post civil war in Cass County, Texas. I am a descendent of slavery here in America. The distinction should be reclassified and the current term does not do that. Since 1980 the Black immigration has increased tremendously. The black immigrant story is not the black American experience and our classification should reflect that. Without it should be considered a form of paper genocide and the erasure of a population that contributed to the founding of this nation. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0965,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-a2dc-ydtb,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Collecting accurate data on race/ethnicity is a critical role of the Census, and is vital in understanding the demographics of our country. It provides vital statistics on social mobility, diversity and the ethnic make-up of our communities. To no longer collect this data would disrupt long-run data series that are vital in telling the story of our country, states and neighborhoods. It would be a disservice to our children, grandchildren and future Americans to stop collecting this data.<br/><br/>Regarding residency for apportionment, collecting data on persons living in the U.S. is the role of the Census. Specifically, the Census is constitutionally obligated to make a count of the &quot;whole number of persons in each State, excluding Indians not taxed&quot;. No mention is made to legal status as a resident. Thus, I cannot understand how it would be conscionable to exclude a person living in this country, legally or otherwise. They require just as many roads as I do. They require just as many firefighters as I do. They require just as much water, clean air and parks as I do. To exclude them from apportionment counts would not only disregard the constitution and the basic needs of human beings residing within the jurisdiction of the United States, but would also erode my access to said services, for which I send tax dollars to the government to provide.<br/><br/>Thus, I ask the Census Bureau to reconsider their changes to long-standing norms, continue to collect data on race/ethnicity, continue to include all persons in the count, and their commitment to providing a vital service to the American people. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0966,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-aq51-2i71,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I support the changes to the meaning of &ldquo;usual residence&rdquo; because &ldquo;where you live and sleep most of the time&rdquo; was very confusing (most of what time???).<br/><br/>Also, as a biracial woman I support the elimination of race and ethnicity questions. I never know what to pick and it makes me uncomfortable having to fill out a government form about it.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0967,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-dcc2-92fa,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,See attached file(s),,,,,,https://downloads.regulations.gov/USBC-2026-0628-0967/attachment_1.pdf,
USBC-2026-0628-0968,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-deip-ey2r,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,I support the residence criteria.,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0969,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-e0vk-lg7h,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Demographic questions on the census are essential to capture data for federal, state, and local programs that support the health and wellbeing of our nation. Without these data, we will not know where federal funds are most needed. Removing demographic questions is a direct threat to public health and will continue to exacerbate disparate health outcomes. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0970,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-edzd-fqzm,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,This is unamerican and honoring the late great Chairman Elijah Cummings - the answer is NO,,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0971,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-fczt-w5ob,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"My name is William B McDonough and I am a citizen of the USA. I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: 1. Regarding 60.4, I believe only US citizens should be counted in the census. I find it very difficult to believe that people in this country illegally should be counted. This needs to change. 2. Regarding 70.2, we need to stop counting people based on their race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation, this needs to stop. We&#39;re all Americans and should be counted as individuals not a part of a group or tribe. That just contributes to pitting one group versus another and has been increasing in this country for years. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0972,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-fj75-19gk,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"I&rsquo;m writing to say that I believe it is of dire importance to collect and record ALL census data, including race and ethnicity. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0973,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-fktp-72pg,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"&ldquo;My name is Susan M Smith, I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows.&rdquo;<br/>I am in favor of both rules, as illegal aliens should NOT be counted as part of the census. They are here illegally and should have no bearing on how federal dollars are distributed to the states. Foreign residents who are here legally should be counted, as they have followed the rules to become a part of the citizen numbers in the US. <br/>As to not asking about race, ethnicity and sexual orientation on the census, I completely agree. These demographics have no bearing on the number of people who will be counted. In reality, NOT focusing on these traits will more accurately portray the legal citizenship in the country, because citizens who fall into those categories will be more likely to answer without fearing reprisal or being identified as such. I believe that focusing on race(especially)is one of the reasons for a lot of the incivility that is occurring in the country. Most Americans get along with their fellow citizens, until some media personality or other bozo makes race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation an issue.  <br/><br/>",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0974,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-g0ue-lz23,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Hello. I am a citizen of the United States of America, and I am commenting as an individual. I am addressing sections 60.4 and 70.2 of the NPRM. I support the proposed language of section 60.4 as published. I further support the language of section 70.2 also as published. The reasons for my support are as follows: <br/>Regarding 60.4, the census affects apportionment in Congress, as well as how tax dollars are spent upon the domestic population of the nation. Non-citizens without authorization have no business in that count, no business having representation of any kind, and no business being present in the country beyond tourist or short term status.<br/>Regarding 70.2, In order to have a more cohesive union, questions about race, ethnicity, or sexual orientation have no business on the census. Sexual orientation or ethnicity are subsets of identity that should not be factors identifying an American citizen. All who are counted as legal residents, capable of voting, paying taxes and living under the protection of the United Stated military should be counted as just that, American citizens without granular detail that panders to special interests.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0976,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-hp9n-5v4v,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"  The proposal to drop demographic questions would be a major blow to learning and to this country&#39;s ability to better understand itself. It&#39;s inconceivable that the Census Bureau would suggest ending collection of variables that date to the first census. While there have been numerous changes to demographic questions over the past 230 years, we still have substantial data series that run from 150-230 years. <br/>  Ending that would be like deliberately setting fire to census data. We continue to suffer from the 1921 blaze which destroyed most of the 1890 records, and it&#39;s hard to fathom why the bureau would willingly choose to create that kind of gap in our data in 2030.<br/>  There is an enormous amount of analysis that draws on this demographic data. I have used it in my research, have trained students in its use, and have referred many interested individuals to this data. I urge you not to inflict deep harm on the country&#39;s analytic capacity by dropping the use of these variables.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0975,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-gvdl-yq87,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"This is only another example of ignoring those who work, pay taxes, use roads, work and spend in the communities in which they live. Business owners, schools, Healthcare there are people in these areas that matter to the greater good. And now our nation of bigots and morons want to make ither people simply disappear. I was absent the day our rule of law was eliminated and following our Constitution became optional. The intent behind the proposal is sinister, it&#39;s abhorrent and goes against everything this nation once strived to stand for. I do not support this proposed change. We must count all people living in each community. Period. ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0977,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-j68d-fag1,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The 14th Amendment clearly states &quot;all persons&quot; and not &quot;all Citizens.&quot; Therefore, ALL PERSONS must remain the precedent. Regarding the Community Survey date on race / ethnicity  and LGBTQIA+ it is essential that we count everyone from every group. Removing those categories does not make it neutral or just; instead, removing the mechanism for tracking the data will make it easier for discrimination and disparities. A government can easily erase people if they aren&#39;t being counted. Regarding gender, Intersex folx were completely erased in the USA now that the binary is back as official policy. We can&#39;t and shouldn&#39;t do that. Every year, more intersex babies are born and it&#39;s unclear why. Some regions have higher numbers than others. We must document it and keep track. For example, there is one town in the Dominican Republic that has unusually high numbers of intersex babies. It&#39;s not until around age 6/7 that the kid can be identified as a male due to delayed development. Elsewhere, it may be due to environmental factors. If we don&#39;t document it, we can&#39;t address it.<br/><br/>Since the 1930s, Mexico has been colorblind in all official systems, and for generations, the Afro-Mexican population has raised concerns about systemic bias. In fact, Mexico&#39;s National Survey on Discrimination (ENADIS) consistently revealed structural disparities rooted directly in skin tone; true socioeconomic neutrality remains unachieved despite the appearance of neutrality. Racism still exists in Mexico, despite having the appearance of neutrality. Race correlates strongly with class, and darker-skinned Mexicans are often economically vulnerable and culturally excluded. <br/><br/>In order to address the various inequalities in the USA, it is essential to know where it&#39;s happening and to whom. Rural Americans have a very different experience than Urbanites. Someone who is disabled and a POC will have a very different reality than someone who isn&#39;t a POC. People in highly skilled careers will likely differ from those who didn&#39;t finish high school. <br/><br/>We need to know all these categories, and then support policies / agendas that will help everyone with &quot;life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.&quot; ",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0978,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-jy71-3fix,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"As a researcher, I rely everyday on Census&#39;s data, and I rely upon the Census to provide accurate data. The accuracy of the decennial Census seems like it would be greatly harmed (particularly around the counting of immigrant populations) if the proposed changes were to take place. Furthermore, many other data products I use, which aren&#39;t connected to the Census, rely upon the Census as a ground truth (e.g., for use in creating weights to create a representative population). I cannot stress enough how much damage would be done both to my research but also to the broader research community if the proposed changes were to took place. Lastly, much of my work (and much of the work by other researchers) explicitly uses data around an individual&#39;s race and ethnicity to understand differential impacts and different life circumstances for individuals from different backgrounds. This entire line of research would be made impossible to conduct if the Census does not ask questions around race and ethnicity. I implore you to please reconsider these proposals and to keep the Census as it has been. It should be an enumeration of everyone (regardless of immigration status), it should not inquire about immigration status, and it should continue to ask about individual&#39;s race and ethnicity.",,,,,,,
USBC-2026-0628-0979,USBC,USBC-2026-0628,mu3-kfq3-3oo3,Public Submission,2026-09-17T04:00Z,false,,,Comment on FR Doc # 2026-18481,,,false,USBC-2026-0628-0001,,,,2026-09-15T04:00Z,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"The next census should include ethnicities across the entire spectrum of the African diaspora. That includes a category to recognize immigrants and their children from the following countries: Nigerians, Ghanaians, Ethiopians, Somalians, Jamaicans, Trinidadians, Cubans, Puerto Ricans, Dominicans, Haitians, and so on. But most importantly, there should be a place where black Americans can check their own disaggregated box by black Americans, I mean anyone who identifies under the following groups: foundational black Americans, Freedmen, Soulaan, ADOS, American descendants of slavery. The term African-American should be retired.",,,,,,,
